window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"MS-1":{"unit":"MS-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Embryology","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the musculoskeletal system from the embryo to the adult layout. It covers how somites build the vertebrae, ribs, back muscles, and dermis; why each vertebral body is assembled from two sclerotome halves; how signaling centers shape the limb bud; why the limbs rotate in opposite directions; how bone forms by intramembranous and endochondral ossification; and how joints and pharyngeal arch muscles arise. Board questions rarely ask for a date alone. They give an adult finding, such as a nerve supply, a dermatome pattern, a short-limbed skeleton, or an extra digit, and ask you to work back to the embryonic step that explains it.","prerequisites":["Gastrulation and the three germ layers, including paraxial, intermediate, and lateral plate mesoderm","Notochord and neural tube formation and the role of sonic hedgehog in the neural tube","Basic structure of a spinal nerve: roots, dorsal and ventral rami","Hyaline cartilage and bone histology: chondrocytes, osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteoid","Signaling families: FGF, Wnt, hedgehog, BMP, and HOX transcription factors"],"connections":[{"to":"Back and spinal cord anatomy","how":"Resegmentation explains why spinal nerves exit at disc level and why there are eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae."},{"to":"Upper and lower limb anatomy","how":"Limb rotation and the dorsal and ventral muscle masses predict which nerve supplies each compartment and why the lower limb dermatomes spiral."},{"to":"Bone physiology and metabolic bone disease","how":"The growth plate built during endochondral ossification is the site affected in achondroplasia and in disorders of linear growth."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy and the cranial nerves","how":"The pharyngeal arch of origin predicts the motor nerve of each muscle of mastication, facial expression, pharynx, and larynx."},{"to":"Teratology and medical genetics","how":"Thalidomide, HOX mutations, FGFR3 mutation, and RUNX2 loss show how a single signal or gene produces a predictable skeletal defect."}],"terms":[{"term":"Somite","def":"A paired, segmental block of paraxial mesoderm beside the neural tube that forms sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome."},{"term":"Sclerotome","def":"The ventromedial part of a somite that migrates around the notochord and neural tube to form vertebrae and ribs."},{"term":"Myotome","def":"The part of a somite that forms skeletal muscle; clinically, the group of muscles supplied by one spinal nerve segment."},{"term":"Dermatome","def":"Embryologically, the part of a somite that forms dermis of the back; clinically, the skin area supplied by one spinal nerve."},{"term":"Epaxial and hypaxial","def":"The dorsal (back muscle) and ventral (body wall and limb muscle) divisions of the myotome, supplied by dorsal and ventral rami."},{"term":"Resegmentation","def":"Formation of each vertebral body from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next."},{"term":"Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)","def":"Thickened ectoderm at the distal rim of the limb bud whose FGFs drive proximal to distal outgrowth."},{"term":"Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA)","def":"Posterior limb bud mesenchyme that secretes sonic hedgehog to pattern the anterior to posterior (thumb to little finger) axis."},{"term":"Preaxial border","def":"The cranial border of the early limb bud, which carries the radius and thumb or the tibia and big toe."},{"term":"Endochondral ossification","def":"Bone formation in which a hyaline cartilage model is formed first and then replaced by bone."},{"term":"Interzone","def":"A band of dense mesenchyme between two cartilage models that forms a joint instead of cartilage."},{"term":"Pharyngeal arch","def":"A paired bar of mesenchyme in the embryonic neck that contains neural crest skeleton, muscle, an artery, and its own cranial nerve."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C01","obj":"MS-1.1","title":"Somites and their derivatives","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Somites are paired blocks of paraxial mesoderm that form on each side of the neural tube and notochord. The first pair appears in the future occipital region at about day 20, and new pairs bud off in a cranial to caudal order at about three pairs per day until the end of week 5, giving about 42 to 44 pairs. The caudal-most pairs later regress. Each boundary forms on a timer: a clock of oscillating Notch and Wnt signaling in the presomitic mesoderm meets a receding FGF gradient, and a new somite separates each time the two coincide. This periodic origin is why vertebrae, ribs, back muscles, and spinal nerves are segmental."},{"type":"p","text":"Each somite then divides according to signals from its neighbors. Sonic hedgehog (Shh) from the notochord and the floor plate of the neural tube induces the ventromedial part to become sclerotome, which expresses PAX1, loses its epithelial arrangement, and migrates around the notochord and neural tube. Wnt signals from the dorsal neural tube and surface ectoderm keep the dorsolateral part as dermomyotome, which expresses PAX3 and gives rise to the myotome, the source of skeletal muscle, and the dermatome, the source of the dermis of the back."},{"type":"flow","title":"From paraxial mesoderm to adult derivatives","steps":["Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites from about day 20","Notochord and floor plate Shh induce ventromedial sclerotome","Sclerotome surrounds notochord and neural tube: vertebrae, ribs","Dorsal neural tube Wnt maintains dorsolateral dermomyotome","Dermomyotome forms myotome (muscle) and dermatome (back dermis)","Myotome splits into epaxial and hypaxial divisions"],"caption":"Signals from the notochord and neural tube decide which part of each somite becomes skeleton, muscle, or dermis."},{"type":"p","text":"Muscle keeps the nerve of the segment it came from. Each spinal nerve divides into a dorsal ramus, which enters the epaxial (dorsomedial) muscle mass, and a ventral ramus, which follows the hypaxial (ventrolateral) mass into the body wall and limbs. Epaxial muscle becomes the deep, intrinsic back muscles such as erector spinae and transversospinalis. Hypaxial muscle becomes the intercostal and abdominal wall muscles, the prevertebral and scalene muscles, and, after its precursor cells migrate into the limb buds, all limb muscle. The limb bones, tendons, and connective tissue come instead from lateral plate mesoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"This explains a classic distinction. The superficial muscles on the back, such as trapezius, latissimus dorsi, and the rhomboids, are not epaxial; they migrated onto the back and are supplied by ventral rami of the brachial plexus or, for trapezius, by the accessory nerve. Only the deep muscles of the vertebral column are supplied by dorsal rami. Because segmental innervation is kept, injury to one spinal nerve produces a predictable band of sensory loss (its dermatome) and weakness of a predictable muscle group (its myotome)."},{"type":"table","title":"Somite parts, adult derivatives, and nerve supply","cols":["Part","Adult derivatives","Nerve supply"],"rows":[["Sclerotome","Vertebral bodies and arches, ribs, annulus fibrosus, part of the occipital bone","Not applicable"],["Dermatome (embryonic)","Dermis of the skin of the neck and back","Cutaneous branches of dorsal rami"],["Epaxial myotome","Deep back muscles: erector spinae, transversospinalis, splenius, suboccipital","Dorsal rami"],["Hypaxial myotome","Limb, intercostal, abdominal wall, scalene, prevertebral, and infrahyoid muscles","Ventral rami"],["Occipital somite myotomes","Most tongue muscles","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]],"caption":"Limb dermis, bone, and tendon are not somite derivatives; they come from lateral plate mesoderm."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C02","obj":"MS-1.2","title":"Resegmentation and the intervertebral disc","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sclerotome cells that surround the notochord form the vertebral bodies, those that surround the neural tube form the vertebral arches, and in the thoracic region the costal processes grow out to become ribs. A vertebra does not come from a single sclerotome. Each sclerotome splits into a loosely packed cranial half and a densely packed caudal half, and the caudal half of one sclerotome fuses with the cranial half of the next to form one vertebral body. This is resegmentation. Cells at the junction between the two original halves do not join either body; they fill the space between adjacent bodies and form the annulus fibrosus of the intervertebral disc."},{"type":"p","text":"Resegmentation solves a mechanical problem. The myotomes do not shift, so after resegmentation each muscle segment attaches to two adjacent vertebrae and crosses the disc between them, which is what allows segmental muscles to move the spine. For the same reason the spinal nerve, which grew toward its own myotome, now lies at the level of the disc and leaves through the intervertebral foramen, and the intersegmental arteries, which ran between somites, now cross the middle of each vertebral body."},{"type":"flow","title":"Resegmentation of the sclerotomes","steps":["Sclerotome cells surround the notochord and neural tube","Each sclerotome splits into loose cranial and dense caudal halves","Caudal half fuses with the next cranial half: one vertebral body","Cells between the original halves form the annulus fibrosus","Notochord regresses in bodies, persists between them as nucleus pulposus","Myotomes now span discs; spinal nerves lie at disc level"],"caption":"Vertebrae end up offset by half a segment from the myotomes and spinal nerves."},{"type":"p","text":"The same shift explains spinal nerve numbering. The cranial half of the first cervical sclerotome fuses with the caudal half of the last occipital sclerotome and is incorporated into the occipital bone, so the first cervical nerve emerges between the skull and the atlas. There are therefore eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae: nerves C1 to C7 exit above the vertebra of the same number, C8 exits between C7 and T1, and from T1 downward each nerve exits below its numbered vertebra."},{"type":"p","text":"The notochord is squeezed out of the developing vertebral bodies, where it regresses, but persists and expands between them as the gelatinous nucleus pulposus, which the annulus fibrosus then encircles. The large notochordal cells largely disappear from the nucleus during childhood and are replaced by smaller chondrocyte-like cells. Notochordal rests left along the axis, most often in the sacrococcygeal region and the clivus at the skull base, can give rise to chordoma, a slow-growing malignant tumor of vacuolated (physaliferous) cells that express brachyury, a notochordal transcription factor."},{"type":"table","title":"Errors of vertebral development","cols":["Defect","Embryonic error","Result"],"rows":[["Hemivertebra","One lateral half of a vertebral body fails to form","Wedge-shaped vertebra and congenital scoliosis"],["Klippel-Feil syndrome","Failed segmentation of cervical somites","Fused cervical vertebrae, short neck, low posterior hairline, limited neck motion"],["Spina bifida occulta","Vertebral arches fail to fuse dorsally, usually at L5 or S1","Skin-covered defect, sometimes a tuft of hair; cord usually normal"],["Cervical rib","Costal process of C7 enlarges","May compress the lower brachial plexus or subclavian artery"],["Chordoma","Notochordal rest persists","Malignant tumor of the clivus or sacrum"]],"caption":"Each defect traces to one step: somite segmentation, sclerotome growth, arch fusion, costal process growth, or notochord regression."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A fetal ultrasound shows a hemivertebra at T8 in which only the left half of the body formed. Which way will the spine curve?","explain":"The left side of that segment has a vertebral half and the right side has none, so the left side is taller. The spine bends away from the taller side, producing a scoliosis convex to the left at that level, and the curve tends to worsen with growth because the left half keeps growing.","check":"The resegmentation flow and the defect table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C03","obj":"MS-1.3","title":"Limb bud signaling and limb defects","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Limb buds appear near the end of week 4 as outpocketings of the ventrolateral body wall, the upper limb first and the lower limb one to two days later. Each bud is a core of mesenchyme from the somatic layer of lateral plate mesoderm covered by surface ectoderm. Three signaling centers pattern its three axes, and HOX genes give each segment its identity."},{"type":"p","text":"At the distal rim, mesenchymal FGF10 induces the ectoderm to thicken into the apical ectodermal ridge (AER). The AER secretes FGFs, including FGF8 and FGF4, that keep the mesenchyme beneath it (the progress zone) dividing and undifferentiated. In the classic model, cells that leave this zone early form proximal structures and cells that leave late form distal ones, so the limb is laid down proximal to distal: arm or thigh, then forearm or leg, then hand or foot. Loss of the AER stops outgrowth, giving amelia (absent limb) or meromelia (partial absence)."},{"type":"p","text":"The zone of polarizing activity (ZPA), mesenchyme at the posterior (postaxial) border, secretes sonic hedgehog (Shh). Digit identity depends on how much Shh a cell receives and for how long: the little finger side receives the most, and the thumb forms without it. If Shh is also made at the anterior border, by a graft or by mutation of its limb enhancer (the ZRS), a second gradient produces extra anterior digits: preaxial polydactyly, sometimes a mirror-image duplication. The AER and ZPA sustain each other, which couples growth to pattern. The third center, WNT7A in the dorsal ectoderm, induces LMX1B in dorsal mesenchyme to make dorsal structures; LMX1B mutation causes nail-patella syndrome, with small or absent nails and patellae."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The AER and ZPA maintain each other","steps":["AER FGFs maintain Shh expression in the ZPA","ZPA Shh induces the BMP antagonist gremlin in mesenchyme","Gremlin blocks BMP, which would shut down the AER","AER keeps secreting FGFs, maintaining Shh"],"caption":"The loop ends as the bud grows and gremlin falls, so outgrowth and patterning stop together."},{"type":"p","text":"HOX genes are transcription factors expressed in nested domains along the limb. Genes at the 5 prime end of the HOXA and HOXD clusters (paralog groups 9 to 13) switch on progressively later and more distally, and HOX13 genes mark the hand and foot. HOXD13 mutation causes synpolydactyly, with fused digits and an extra digit in the web, and HOXA13 mutation causes hand-foot-genital syndrome. Digits separate by BMP-driven apoptosis between them; failure gives syndactyly."},{"type":"p","text":"Thalidomide shows that timing decides the defect. Exposure while the limb buds form and grow out, about days 24 to 36 after fertilization, causes limb reduction defects, most characteristically phocomelia, in which the hands or feet attach close to the trunk because the long bones are absent or rudimentary. The leading explanation is that thalidomide binds cereblon, part of an E3 ubiquitin ligase, redirecting it to degrade transcription factors such as SALL4 that limb outgrowth needs. Later exposure spares the limbs."},{"type":"table","title":"Limb signaling centers and their defects","cols":["Center","Signal","Axis or role","Defect when disrupted"],"rows":[["Apical ectodermal ridge","FGF8, FGF4","Proximal to distal outgrowth","Amelia or meromelia"],["Zone of polarizing activity","Sonic hedgehog","Anterior to posterior (digit identity)","Ectopic anterior Shh: preaxial polydactyly"],["Dorsal ectoderm","WNT7A, then LMX1B","Dorsal to ventral","Nail-patella syndrome (LMX1B)"],["Limb mesenchyme","HOXA and HOXD genes","Segment identity","Synpolydactyly (HOXD13); hand-foot-genital syndrome (HOXA13)"]],"caption":"Name the abnormal axis to find the responsible center and signal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a chick embryo, the AER is removed once early in outgrowth and, in a second embryo, late in outgrowth. How will the two limbs differ?","explain":"Early removal leaves only proximal structures, because outgrowth stops before the forearm and hand are laid down. Late removal loses only the most distal elements, such as the digits, because proximal segments were already laid down.","check":"The AER paragraph and the proximal to distal sequence."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C04","obj":"MS-1.4","title":"Limb rotation, compartments, and dermatomes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both limb buds start with the same layout. The cranial border is the preaxial border, which will carry the radius and thumb in the arm and the tibia and big toe in the leg; the caudal border is postaxial. Myoblasts gather into a dorsal muscle mass, which forms the extensors and is supplied by posterior division nerves of the plexus, and a ventral mass, which forms the flexors (and thigh adductors) and is supplied by anterior division nerves. Dermatomes run in segmental order, cranial segments preaxial and caudal segments postaxial."},{"type":"p","text":"In weeks 7 and 8 the limbs bend at the elbow and knee and rotate about their long axes in opposite directions. The upper limb rotates about 90 degrees laterally, so the elbow points backward, the extensors lie posteriorly, and the thumb lies laterally. The lower limb rotates about 90 degrees medially, so the knee points forward, the extensors lie anteriorly, the flexors posteriorly, and the big toe medially. The palm and the sole are therefore both ventral, flexor surfaces. Its cellular cause is not fully understood, but the result suits each limb's job: lateral rotation turns the palms forward for grasping, and medial rotation puts the knee extensors in front to straighten and lock the knee for weight bearing, with the sole on the ground."},{"type":"p","text":"Because muscles and nerves keep their embryonic surface, rotation predicts compartment and nerve. Posterior division nerves (axillary and radial in the arm; femoral, superior and inferior gluteal, and common fibular in the leg) supply the original dorsal, extensor surface, which is now the back of the arm but the front of the thigh and leg. Anterior division nerves (musculocutaneous, median, and ulnar; obturator and tibial) supply the original ventral surface, now the front of the arm but the medial and posterior thigh and the back of the leg. Dorsiflexion of the ankle is an extensor action, so it is performed by anterior leg muscles."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermatomes are mapped before rotation, so rotation twists them. In the upper limb the pattern stays readable: C5 and C6 run along the lateral (preaxial) side to the thumb, C7 reaches the middle finger, and C8 and T1 run along the medial (postaxial) side. In the lower limb, medial rotation carries the preaxial strip around to the front and medial side, so the bands run obliquely downward and medially in a spiral: L1 near the inguinal ligament, L2 and L3 over the anterior thigh, L4 over the knee and medial leg, L5 over the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot, S1 over the lateral border and sole of the foot, and S2 up the back of the leg and thigh."},{"type":"table","title":"Opposite rotations of the upper and lower limb","cols":["Feature","Upper limb","Lower limb"],"rows":[["Rotation in weeks 7 to 8","About 90 degrees lateral","About 90 degrees medial"],["Preaxial digit","Thumb, lateral","Big toe, medial"],["Extensor (dorsal mass) compartment","Posterior","Anterior"],["Flexor (ventral mass) compartment","Anterior","Posterior (adductors medial)"],["Posterior division nerves","Axillary, radial","Femoral, gluteal, common fibular"],["Preaxial dermatomes","C5, C6 on the lateral side","L4 on the medial leg"]],"caption":"Find the embryonic surface first, then apply the rotation."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting compartment and nerve from rotation","text":"Where should quadriceps lie, and which nerve supplies it? It extends the knee, so it is a dorsal mass muscle supplied by a posterior division nerve: femoral, L2 to L4. Medial rotation carried the dorsal surface forward, so quadriceps lies anteriorly. Triceps is also dorsal mass and posterior division (radial), but lateral rotation left the dorsal surface behind, so it lies posteriorly. The hamstrings flex the knee, so they are ventral mass muscles supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (anterior divisions) and lie posteriorly. The one exception, the short head of biceps femoris, is supplied by the common fibular division."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C05","obj":"MS-1.5","title":"Ossification and joint formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone forms by two routes, and the route decides which bones a disorder affects. In intramembranous ossification, mesenchymal cells condense, switch on the transcription factor RUNX2, and differentiate directly into osteoblasts that secrete osteoid, which then mineralizes. Woven bone spicules join into trabeculae, later remodeled into compact plates. This route builds the flat bones of the skull vault, most facial bones, much of the mandible, and most of the clavicle, the first bone to begin ossifying at about weeks 5 to 6. Skull bones grow at their edges, so the sutures and fontanelles let the vault enlarge with the brain."},{"type":"p","text":"In endochondral ossification, mesenchyme condenses and, under SOX9, forms a hyaline cartilage model covered by perichondrium. A cartilage model can grow from within by chondrocyte division, which bone cannot, so long bones, vertebrae, ribs, the pelvis, and the skull base use this route. The primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis, and by about week 12 primary centers are present in all the long bones of the limbs. Secondary centers appear in the epiphyses, mostly after birth; the distal femoral center is usually present at term. Between them, the epiphyseal growth plate keeps producing cartilage that is replaced from the diaphyseal side, lengthening the bone until the plates close after puberty under the influence of estrogen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Endochondral ossification of a long bone","steps":["Mesenchyme forms a hyaline cartilage model with perichondrium","Midshaft perichondrium becomes periosteum and lays a bone collar","Central chondrocytes hypertrophy, calcify matrix, release VEGF, and die","Periosteal bud vessels bring osteoprogenitors and osteoclast precursors","Primary ossification center forms in the diaphysis","Secondary centers form in the epiphyses, mostly after birth","Growth plates lengthen the bone, then close after puberty"],"caption":"Cartilage is not turned into bone; calcified cartilage is removed and bone replaces it."},{"type":"p","text":"Growth plate chondrocytes are held in balance by a feedback loop in which Indian hedgehog from prehypertrophic chondrocytes stimulates PTHrP near the bone ends, and PTHrP keeps chondrocytes dividing and delays their hypertrophy. FGFR3 signaling restrains chondrocyte proliferation. A disorder acting mainly on one route shows mainly in its bones, which makes the skeletal pattern diagnostic (see the STOP below)."},{"type":"table","title":"Intramembranous versus endochondral ossification","cols":["Feature","Intramembranous","Endochondral"],"rows":[["Template","None; mesenchyme becomes bone directly","Hyaline cartilage model"],["Key transcription factor","RUNX2","SOX9 for cartilage, then RUNX2"],["Main bones","Skull vault, facial bones, most of clavicle","Long bones, vertebrae, ribs, pelvis, skull base"],["Growth in length","At edges (sutures)","At growth plates"],["Classic disorder","Cleidocranial dysplasia (RUNX2)","Achondroplasia (FGFR3)"]],"caption":"RUNX2 is needed for osteoblasts in both routes, but its loss shows most clearly in intramembranous bones."},{"type":"p","text":"Joints form at interzones, bands of dense mesenchyme between adjacent cartilage models, from about week 6, and by the end of week 8 they resemble adult joints. Interzone cells express GDF5 and make no cartilage. Where the interzone becomes dense fibrous tissue, a fibrous joint forms, such as a suture; where it becomes cartilage, a cartilaginous joint forms, such as the pubic symphysis. In a synovial joint the central interzone cavitates, through cell death and hyaluronan secretion, to form the joint cavity, while the surrounding mesenchyme forms the capsule, synovial membrane, and ligaments, and intra-articular structures such as menisci also arise from the interzone. Cavitation depends on fetal movement, so disorders that immobilize the fetus leave stiff joints at birth (arthrogryposis)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"One child has absent clavicles, a wide anterior fontanelle, and extra teeth. Another has short arms and legs, a large skull vault with a flat midface, and a near-normal trunk length. Which ossification route fails in each?","explain":"The first has cleidocranial dysplasia: loss of one RUNX2 copy impairs osteoblast formation, most visibly in intramembranous bones. The second has achondroplasia: gain-of-function FGFR3 slows growth plate proliferation, so endochondral long bones and skull base are short while the intramembranous vault grows normally.","check":"The ossification comparison table and the growth plate paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-01-01-C06","obj":"MS-1.6","title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles and their nerves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pharyngeal arches are paired bars of mesenchyme that appear in weeks 4 and 5 on each side of the developing head and neck, separated externally by clefts and internally by pouches. Arches 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 develop in humans; the fifth is absent or rudimentary. Each arch is covered by ectoderm outside and endoderm inside and contains four components: a skeletal element and connective tissue from neural crest, an aortic arch artery, muscle from cranial paraxial mesoderm, and a cranial nerve that grows in from the adjacent hindbrain."},{"type":"p","text":"Each arch's muscle is supplied by the nerve of its own arch and keeps that nerve wherever it migrates, just as somite muscle keeps its spinal nerve. Temporalis spreads up to the temple and the facial muscles spread over the scalp and down the neck as platysma, yet they remain supplied by the nerves of arches 1 and 2. The arch nerves run V3, VII, IX, X, so the motor nerve of a head or neck muscle follows from its arch (table). The fourth arch is supplied by the superior laryngeal and pharyngeal branches of the vagus, and the sixth by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The right recurrent nerve hooks under the subclavian artery (fourth arch artery) and the left under the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum (sixth arch artery), because the distal right sixth arch artery regresses."},{"type":"table","title":"Pharyngeal arch muscles, nerves, and lesion signs","cols":["Arch","Muscles","Nerve","Sign of nerve lesion"],"rows":[["1st","Masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini","CN V3 (mandibular)","Weak chewing; jaw deviates toward the lesion on opening"],["2nd","Muscles of facial expression, buccinator, platysma, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of digastric","CN VII (facial)","Facial paralysis; hyperacusis from stapedius loss"],["3rd","Stylopharyngeus","CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","Weak elevation of the pharynx, rarely detectable alone"],["4th","Pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, levator veli palatini, palatoglossus","CN X (superior laryngeal and pharyngeal branches)","Dysphagia; uvula deviates away from the lesion; loss of high pitch with cricothyroid palsy"],["6th","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","CN X (recurrent laryngeal)","Hoarseness from vocal fold paralysis"]],"caption":"Arches 1, 2, and 3 use nerves V3, VII, and IX; arches 4 and 6 both use the vagus."},{"type":"p","text":"Lesion signs follow the muscle actions. With a V3 lesion, the intact lateral pterygoid pulls its condyle forward unopposed, so the jaw deviates toward the weak side. With a VII lesion, stapedius no longer damps ossicle movement, so sounds seem too loud. With a vagal lesion, the intact levator veli palatini pulls the uvula away from the lesion. Cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage to lengthen and tense the vocal folds, so its loss lowers vocal pitch range, while the sixth arch muscles open, close, and adjust the folds, so recurrent nerve injury causes hoarseness."},{"type":"p","text":"Two exceptions are often tested. The tongue muscles, except palatoglossus, come from occipital somites and are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), not an arch nerve. Stylopharyngeus is the only third arch muscle and is a longitudinal elevator, whereas the constrictors belong to the fourth arch and the vagus."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a lesion with the arch map","text":"A man cannot close his left eye or raise the left corner of his mouth, and loud sounds hurt his left ear. Chewing and jaw opening are normal, and his uvula is midline. Facial expression muscles and stapedius are second arch muscles, so the lesion is the left facial nerve, CN VII, proximal to the branch to stapedius in the facial canal. Normal chewing and jaw opening exclude a first arch (V3) lesion, and a midline uvula excludes a vagal (fourth arch) lesion. The posterior belly of digastric and stylohyoid are also second arch muscles, but their weakness is rarely noticed clinically."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"One rule ties this chapter together: a muscle keeps the nerve of the segment or arch where it formed, however far it migrates. Somite muscle keeps its spinal nerve and divides into epaxial (dorsal ramus) and hypaxial (ventral ramus) territories; limb muscle keeps the posterior or anterior division nerve of its dorsal or ventral mass even after rotation turns that surface to the front or back; and arch muscle keeps V3, VII, IX, or X. Clinical dermatome and myotome maps, the anterior thigh extensors, the medial big toe, and the vagal supply of the larynx all follow from this rule plus the movements of development."},{"type":"p","text":"Molecular biology and pathology explain the defects. A single signal or gene acting at one step gives a predictable lesion: FGF from the AER for limb length, Shh from the ZPA for digit pattern, LMX1B for dorsal structures, HOXD13 and HOXA13 for distal identity, FGFR3 for growth plate proliferation, and RUNX2 for osteoblasts. Teratogens such as thalidomide act only during the window when their target step is underway. Three limits apply: timing figures are approximate and differ among texts, the progress zone model of limb outgrowth is a simplification still debated, and the cause of limb rotation is described better than it is explained."}],"summary":["Somites form from paraxial mesoderm from about day 20; Shh from notochord and floor plate makes sclerotome (vertebrae, ribs), and Wnt maintains dermomyotome (myotome and back dermis).","Epaxial myotome forms the deep back muscles supplied by dorsal rami; hypaxial myotome forms body wall and limb muscles supplied by ventral rami; limb bone and tendon come from lateral plate mesoderm.","Each vertebral body forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next, so myotomes span discs, nerves exit at disc level, and there are eight cervical nerves but seven cervical vertebrae.","The notochord persists as the nucleus pulposus; its rests can form chordoma in the clivus or sacrum.","AER FGFs drive proximal to distal outgrowth; ZPA Shh sets digit identity, and ectopic anterior Shh causes preaxial polydactyly; WNT7A and LMX1B set the dorsal side; HOX genes set segment identity.","Thalidomide during limb outgrowth (about days 24 to 36) causes phocomelia.","The upper limb rotates laterally and the lower limb medially, so extensors are posterior in the arm and anterior in the thigh and leg, the thumb is lateral and the big toe medial, and lower limb dermatomes spiral.","Intramembranous ossification (skull vault, most of the clavicle) needs RUNX2 and no cartilage; endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model and lengthens bone at growth plates.","Joints form from interzone mesenchyme; synovial cavities form by cavitation, which requires fetal movement.","Arch muscles keep their nerves: first V3 (mastication), second VII (facial expression), third IX (stylopharyngeus), fourth and sixth X (pharynx and larynx)."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Axial Skeleton; Muscular System"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Limbs; Head and Neck (pharyngeal arches)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back; Upper Limb; Lower Limb (dermatomes and nerve supply of compartments)"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone (osteogenesis and bone growth)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"}]},"MS-10":{"unit":"MS-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01","title":"Neuromuscular Transmission, Reflexes, and Muscle Excitability","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the signal from motor neuron to muscle fiber: how acetylcholine is released and received at the neuromuscular junction, how spindles and tendon organs feed back on motor neurons, and how plasma calcium and potassium set the excitability of nerve and muscle. These mechanisms let you place weakness at the nerve, the junction, or the muscle. Board questions describe a pattern of weakness, a reflex finding, a biopsy, or an electrolyte value and ask you to name the site and mechanism. The recurring examples are myasthenia gravis, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, botulism, spinal muscular atrophy, and tetany from hypocalcemia.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential, the Nernst equation, and voltage-gated sodium channel gating","Chemical synapses, quantal release, and ligand-gated ion channels","Excitation-contraction coupling and skeletal muscle fiber types (earlier MS units)","Type II hypersensitivity and antibody-mediated injury","Spinal cord gray matter organization and the dermatome and myotome concept"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology","how":"Myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are antibody-mediated (type II) diseases, and Lambert-Eaton is often a paraneoplastic response to small cell lung cancer."},{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Clostridium botulinum toxin is a zinc protease that cleaves SNARE proteins, and tetanus toxin cleaves synaptobrevin, one of the same proteins, in inhibitory spinal interneurons."},{"to":"Neuroscience","how":"Reflex grading separates upper from lower motor neuron lesions because descending pathways set gamma motor neuron drive and spinal reflex gain."},{"to":"Endocrine and renal physiology","how":"Parathyroid hormone, vitamin D, magnesium, and renal potassium handling determine the electrolyte values that change nerve and muscle excitability."},{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology","how":"The same calcium and potassium effects alter the cardiac action potential, producing QT and T wave changes and arrhythmias."}],"terms":[{"term":"End plate potential (EPP)","def":"The local depolarization of the muscle membrane at the motor end plate produced by acetylcholine opening nicotinic receptor channels."},{"term":"Quantum","def":"The packet of acetylcholine contained in one synaptic vesicle; spontaneous release of a single quantum produces a miniature end plate potential."},{"term":"Safety factor","def":"The amount by which the normal end plate potential exceeds the depolarization needed to fire a muscle action potential."},{"term":"SNARE proteins","def":"Synaptobrevin on the vesicle and syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the plasma membrane, which zipper together to fuse vesicles with the membrane."},{"term":"Repetitive nerve stimulation","def":"An electrodiagnostic test that records the compound muscle action potential during a train of nerve shocks to detect decrement or increment."},{"term":"Intrafusal fiber","def":"A specialized muscle fiber inside the spindle capsule whose central region carries sensory endings and whose ends receive gamma motor neurons."},{"term":"Alpha-gamma coactivation","def":"Simultaneous firing of alpha and gamma motor neurons so the spindle stays taut and keeps signaling while the muscle shortens."},{"term":"Autogenic inhibition","def":"Inhibition of a muscle's own motor neurons by Ib input from its Golgi tendon organs when tension rises."},{"term":"Motor unit","def":"One alpha motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates, which share the same fiber type."},{"term":"Fiber type grouping","def":"Clusters of muscle fibers of the same histochemical type that replace the normal checkerboard pattern after denervation and reinnervation."},{"term":"Fasciculation","def":"A visible spontaneous twitch of a whole motor unit caused by ectopic firing of an irritable lower motor neuron or its axon."},{"term":"Tetany","def":"Involuntary sustained muscle contraction from spontaneous repetitive firing of peripheral nerves, classically from low ionized calcium."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01-C01","obj":"MS-10.1","title":"Neuromuscular transmission and where junction disorders act","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The neuromuscular junction is a chemical synapse built for reliability: normally every motor neuron action potential fires the muscle. Choline acetyltransferase makes acetylcholine from choline and acetyl-CoA, and sodium-coupled choline uptake from the cleft limits the rate of synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"When the action potential invades the terminal, depolarization opens P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels clustered at active zones opposite the postsynaptic folds. Calcium binds synaptotagmin on docked vesicles, which drives the final zippering of the SNARE complex: synaptobrevin (VAMP) on the vesicle pairs with syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the plasma membrane, pulling the two membranes together until they fuse. Release depends steeply on calcium (roughly with the third to fourth power of its concentration), so a modest fall in calcium entry produces a large fall in output. One impulse releases on the order of 100 or more quanta."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetylcholine crosses the cleft and binds the muscle-type nicotinic receptor, a pentamer of two alpha, one beta, one delta, and one epsilon subunit (gamma replaces epsilon in fetal and denervated muscle). Binding of two acetylcholine molecules, one at each of the two alpha subunit interfaces (alpha-delta and alpha-epsilon), opens a nonselective cation channel. Sodium has the larger driving force, so net current is inward. The resulting end plate potential is a graded local potential, normally several times larger than needed to reach threshold, and it spreads to voltage-gated sodium channels (Nav1.4) concentrated in the troughs of the junctional folds, which fire the muscle action potential."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetylcholinesterase, anchored in the basal lamina of the cleft, hydrolyzes acetylcholine to acetate and choline within milliseconds, so each nerve impulse produces one brief end plate potential. Inhibiting the enzyme (as organophosphates do) lets acetylcholine persist, causing fasciculations and then a depolarizing block in which perijunctional sodium channels stay inactivated and the muscle weakens."},{"type":"flow","title":"Neuromuscular transmission","steps":["Action potential invades motor nerve terminal","P/Q-type calcium channels open at active zones","Calcium binds synaptotagmin; SNARE complex fuses vesicles","Acetylcholine binds alpha subunits of nicotinic receptors","Cation influx produces the end plate potential","Nav1.4 channels fire the muscle action potential","Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine, ending the signal"],"caption":"Each step is a separate target: Lambert-Eaton syndrome acts at step 2, botulinum toxin at step 3, myasthenia gravis at step 4, and cholinesterase inhibitors at step 7."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing the defect","cols":["Step","Disorder or agent","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Presynaptic calcium entry","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","IgG against P/Q-type calcium channels","Fewer quanta per impulse; facilitation with repeated firing"],["Vesicle fusion","Botulinum toxin","Light chain protease cleaves SNARE proteins","Release fails at somatic and autonomic cholinergic synapses"],["Postsynaptic receptor","Myasthenia gravis","IgG blocks, internalizes, and lyses (via complement) nicotinic receptors","Smaller end plate potential; fatigue with use"],["Acetylcholine removal","Organophosphates (mechanistic example)","Acetylcholinesterase inhibition","Fasciculations, then depolarizing block and weakness"]],"caption":"Presynaptic disorders reduce how much acetylcholine is released; postsynaptic disorders reduce how well it is detected."},{"type":"p","text":"Botulism comes from Clostridium botulinum, a spore-forming, gram-positive anaerobic rod. Adults usually ingest preformed toxin in home-canned food, infants ingest spores (classically in honey) that colonize the gut, and wounds can be infected. The heavy chain binds cholinergic terminals and mediates entry; the light chain, a zinc-dependent protease, cleaves SNAP-25 (types A and E), synaptobrevin (type B), or syntaxin and SNAP-25 (type C). Because all cholinergic terminals are affected, the picture is a symmetric descending flaccid paralysis that begins in cranial nerves (ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia) with dilated poorly reactive pupils, dry mouth, constipation, and urinary retention, while sensation and alertness are preserved. Tetanus toxin also cleaves synaptobrevin but travels retrogradely to silence inhibitory spinal interneurons, causing rigidity instead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has ptosis, diplopia, and dysphagia. What single bedside finding best separates botulism from myasthenia gravis?","explain":"Botulinum toxin also blocks parasympathetic cholinergic terminals, so pupils are dilated; myasthenic antibodies target only the muscle nicotinic receptor, so pupils are normal.","check":"The botulism paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01-C02","obj":"MS-10.2","title":"Myasthenia gravis versus Lambert-Eaton syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both diseases are antibody-mediated (type II hypersensitivity), but one attacks detection and the other release, and the response to repeated effort follows from that difference."},{"type":"h","text":"Myasthenia gravis: too few receptors"},{"type":"p","text":"In most patients with generalized myasthenia gravis (about 85%), IgG antibodies bind the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor. They reduce functional receptors by complement-mediated destruction of the postsynaptic folds, by cross-linking that speeds receptor internalization, and by blocking the binding site. A minority of patients have antibodies to MuSK or LRP4, proteins that cluster receptors. The thymus is abnormal in most patients: thymic hyperplasia with germinal centers in about two thirds and thymoma in about 10 to 15%."},{"type":"p","text":"Release per impulse normally declines over the first few impulses of a train as the readily releasable vesicle pool is depleted. The safety factor normally hides this. With fewer receptors, each end plate potential starts closer to threshold, so as release falls, more fibers fail to fire. Strength therefore declines with use and worsens toward the end of the day. Extraocular and levator muscles are usually affected first, giving fluctuating ptosis and diplopia, followed by bulbar and proximal limb muscles; respiratory muscle failure is myasthenic crisis. Reflexes, sensation, and pupils are normal. Low-rate (2 to 3 Hz) repetitive nerve stimulation shows a decrement, and raising cleft acetylcholine with a short-acting cholinesterase inhibitor, or cooling the eyelid (cold slows the enzyme), briefly improves strength."},{"type":"h","text":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome: too little release"},{"type":"p","text":"In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, IgG antibodies against presynaptic P/Q-type calcium channels reduce calcium entry, so fewer quanta are released and the resting compound muscle action potential is small. About half of cases are paraneoplastic, nearly always with small cell lung cancer, whose neuroendocrine cells express the same channels and provoke the immune response. During rapid repeated firing, calcium enters faster than the terminal can clear it, residual calcium builds up, and release rises. Strength and reflexes therefore improve briefly after a few seconds of maximal effort (post-exercise facilitation), and high-rate stimulation (20 to 50 Hz) or brief exercise produces a large increment. Weakness is proximal and starts in the legs, reflexes are reduced or absent, and autonomic cholinergic failure causes dry mouth, constipation, and erectile dysfunction."},{"type":"table","title":"Myasthenia gravis versus Lambert-Eaton syndrome","cols":["Feature","Myasthenia gravis","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"],"rows":[["Antibody target","Postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (some MuSK, LRP4)","Presynaptic P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channel"],["Effect of repeated effort","Weakness worsens (fatigability)","Strength briefly improves (facilitation)"],["First muscles","Levator palpebrae and extraocular, then bulbar","Proximal legs"],["Reflexes","Normal","Reduced or absent; return after brief exercise"],["Autonomic signs","None; pupils normal","Dry mouth, constipation, erectile dysfunction"],["Associated condition","Thymic hyperplasia or thymoma","Small cell lung cancer (about half of cases)"],["Repetitive nerve stimulation","Decrement at 2 to 3 Hz","Small baseline response; large increment at high rates or after exercise"]],"caption":"The site of the antibody determines whether repeated activity uncovers or overcomes the defect."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a train of shocks gives opposite results","text":"Suppose (illustrative numbers) a normal end plate potential is 3 times threshold and release falls about 25% by the fourth shock of a 3 Hz train. Normally the fourth potential is still about 2.25 times threshold, so every fiber fires and the compound response is unchanged. In myasthenia gravis, suppose receptor loss shrinks the first potential to 1.2 times threshold in many fibers; a 25% fall leaves 0.9 times threshold, those fibers drop out, and the compound response decrements. In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, many fibers start below threshold, so the first response is small. At 50 Hz, residual calcium builds faster than it is cleared, release rises, and fibers that were silent are recruited, so the response increments."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01-C03","obj":"MS-10.3","title":"Muscle spindles, Golgi tendon organs, and reflex roots","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Motor neurons need constant information about muscle length and force. Two receptors supply it, and what each one reports follows from how it is mounted relative to the working (extrafusal) muscle fibers."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscle spindle is a capsule of small intrafusal fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers) lying in parallel with extrafusal fibers, so it is stretched whenever the muscle lengthens. Group Ia afferents, the primary endings, wrap the central region of every intrafusal fiber and fire in proportion to both length and rate of stretch. Group II afferents, the secondary endings, mainly report static length. The contractile ends of intrafusal fibers are innervated by gamma motor neurons. If only alpha motor neurons fired, the shortening muscle would let the spindle go slack and its signal would stop. Alpha-gamma coactivation shortens the intrafusal ends along with the muscle, keeping the spindle taut and sensitive over the whole range of movement. Gamma drive therefore sets the gain of the stretch reflex."},{"type":"p","text":"Tapping a tendon stretches the muscle briefly, and Ia afferents excite alpha motor neurons to the same muscle directly through a single synapse (the monosynaptic stretch reflex). Ia collaterals also excite inhibitory interneurons that relax the antagonist (reciprocal inhibition), so the joint can move."},{"type":"flow","title":"Stretch (deep tendon) reflex arc","steps":["Tendon tap briefly stretches the muscle and its spindles","Ia afferent fires through the dorsal root","Ia synapses directly on alpha motor neurons in the ventral horn","Alpha motor neuron contracts the same muscle","Ia collateral excites interneuron that inhibits the antagonist"],"caption":"A tendon reflex tests the sensory nerve, dorsal root, cord segment, alpha motor neuron, ventral root, junction, and muscle in one maneuver."},{"type":"p","text":"The Golgi tendon organ lies in series at the muscle-tendon junction, where collagen strands squeeze its endings as tension rises. Because it is in series, it responds best to active contraction rather than passive stretch. Its Ib afferents act through inhibitory interneurons on alpha motor neurons of the same muscle (autogenic inhibition, a disynaptic pathway), which helps regulate force and limits excessive tension."},{"type":"table","title":"Muscle spindle versus Golgi tendon organ","cols":["Feature","Muscle spindle","Golgi tendon organ"],"rows":[["Arrangement","In parallel with extrafusal fibers","In series at the muscle-tendon junction"],["Senses","Length and rate of change of length","Tension (force)"],["Afferent","Ia (length and velocity), II (length)","Ib"],["Central pathway","Monosynaptic excitation of homonymous alpha motor neurons","Disynaptic inhibition of homonymous alpha motor neurons"],["Effect on the same muscle","Contraction","Relaxation"],["Efferent control","Gamma motor neurons set sensitivity","None"]],"caption":"Spindles keep length near a set point; tendon organs regulate force."},{"type":"table","title":"Deep tendon reflexes and their roots","cols":["Reflex","Main root","Nerve","Muscle"],"rows":[["Biceps","C5 to C6","Musculocutaneous","Biceps brachii"],["Triceps","C7","Radial","Triceps brachii"],["Patellar","L4","Femoral","Quadriceps femoris"],["Achilles","S1","Tibial","Gastrocnemius and soleus"]],"caption":"Each reflex receives contributions from neighboring segments (for example patellar L2 to L4), but these are the roots usually tested."},{"type":"p","text":"Reflex grading localizes lesions. Damage anywhere along the arc (peripheral nerve, root, anterior horn cell, junction in Lambert-Eaton syndrome, or severe muscle loss) reduces or abolishes the reflex, so a lost Achilles reflex with lateral foot sensory loss points to an S1 radiculopathy. Upper motor neuron lesions remove descending inhibition of spinal circuits, raising reflex gain and producing hyperreflexia, spasticity, clonus, and an extensor plantar response."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During a voluntary contraction, what would the Ia afferent do if gamma motor neurons were silent?","explain":"The extrafusal fibers would shorten while the intrafusal fibers did not, the spindle would go slack, and Ia firing would pause, removing length information exactly when it is needed.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on alpha-gamma coactivation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01-C04","obj":"MS-10.4","title":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The motor unit is the key to this distinction. All fibers in a unit share one motor neuron and therefore one fiber type, and units interdigitate, so a normal ATPase or oxidative stain shows a checkerboard of type I and type II fibers. Neurogenic disease removes whole units at once; myopathic disease damages individual fibers scattered through every unit."},{"type":"h","text":"Neurogenic weakness"},{"type":"p","text":"When a lower motor neuron is lost, every fiber in its unit is denervated together. These fibers shrink into small angular fibers that appear in groups (grouped atrophy). Surviving neurons sprout and adopt orphaned fibers, which convert to the fiber type of their new neuron; the checkerboard is replaced by large patches of one type (fiber type grouping). If an enlarged unit is later lost, a large group of atrophic fibers of one type appears. The irritable neuron or axon fires spontaneously, producing visible fasciculations, and denervated single fibers fire spontaneously as fibrillations, seen only on electromyography. Weakness comes with early atrophy, reduced tone, and reduced reflexes. Fibers are lost without much membrane necrosis, so creatine kinase (CK) is normal or only mildly raised."},{"type":"flow","title":"Denervation and reinnervation","steps":["Lower motor neuron dies","All fibers of its unit atrophy together","Surviving neurons sprout to orphaned fibers","Adopted fibers convert to the new neuron's type","Checkerboard replaced by fiber type grouping"],"caption":"Grouping on biopsy records denervation followed by reinnervation."},{"type":"p","text":"Spinal muscular atrophy is the classic childhood neurogenic disorder. It is autosomal recessive, most often from homozygous deletion of exon 7 of SMN1 on chromosome 5q. The SMN protein assembles small nuclear ribonucleoproteins for the spliceosome, and anterior horn cells are especially vulnerable to its loss. The nearly identical SMN2 gene makes mostly an exon 7 skipped transcript but some full-length protein, so SMN2 copy number modifies severity. Type 1 (Werdnig-Hoffmann disease) presents before 6 months as a floppy infant with symmetric, proximal weakness, areflexia, and tongue fasciculations, with facial and eye movements relatively spared and alertness normal. SMA is an exception to the rule that neurogenic weakness is distal."},{"type":"h","text":"Myopathic weakness"},{"type":"p","text":"In myopathies the defect is in the fiber itself, so weakness is usually proximal and symmetric (difficulty rising from a chair or lifting the arms), fasciculations are absent, and reflexes persist until much muscle is lost. In necrotizing myopathies such as the dystrophies and inflammatory myopathies, damaged sarcolemma leaks CK, which is high. Biopsy shows necrosis, regeneration, internal nuclei, variation in fiber size, and in chronic cases endomysial fibrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II fiber atrophy is a nonspecific pattern seen with disuse, glucocorticoid excess, and cachexia. Glucocorticoids increase ubiquitin-proteasome proteolysis and reduce anabolic signaling, and fast type II fibers are the most sensitive. Because fibers shrink without necrosis, CK stays normal; proximal weakness with normal CK in a patient on long-term glucocorticoids suggests steroid myopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Neurogenic versus myopathic weakness","cols":["Feature","Neurogenic (lower motor neuron)","Myopathic"],"rows":[["Distribution","Often distal and asymmetric (SMA proximal)","Proximal and symmetric"],["Fasciculations","Present","Absent"],["Atrophy","Early and marked","Later, proportional to weakness"],["Reflexes","Reduced or absent","Preserved until late"],["CK","Normal or mildly raised","High in necrotizing myopathies; normal in steroid myopathy"],["Biopsy","Grouped atrophy, fiber type grouping","Necrosis, regeneration, internal nuclei, varied fiber size"],["Electromyography","Fibrillations; large, long-duration motor unit potentials with reduced recruitment","Small, short, polyphasic motor unit potentials with early recruitment"]],"caption":"Neurogenic disease removes whole motor units; myopathic disease damages fibers within every unit."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-10-01-C05","obj":"MS-10.5","title":"Calcium, potassium, and the excitability of nerve and muscle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Excitability depends on the resting membrane potential and on the threshold at which enough sodium channels open to start an action potential. Potassium mainly changes the resting potential; calcium mainly changes the threshold."},{"type":"h","text":"Hypocalcemia: a threshold that moves toward rest"},{"type":"p","text":"Extracellular calcium binds and screens fixed negative charges on the outer surface of the membrane and its sodium channels, which affect the voltage the channel gates sense. When ionized calcium falls, the channels activate at a more negative membrane potential, and threshold moves closer to the resting potential. Peripheral nerves then fire spontaneously and repetitively. This is why low calcium causes tetany, even though contraction itself requires calcium; the calcium for contraction comes from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe hypocalcemia can also cause laryngospasm, seizures, and a prolonged QT interval. Chvostek sign is a twitch of the ipsilateral facial muscles when the facial nerve is tapped just in front of the ear; it is also present in a minority of healthy people. Trousseau sign is carpal spasm (wrist and metacarpophalangeal flexion, interphalangeal extension, thumb adduction) when a blood pressure cuff is held above systolic pressure for 3 minutes; it is more specific. Only ionized calcium (about 1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L) acts on membranes. Total calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L), and alkalosis increases calcium binding to albumin, which explains tetany during hyperventilation with a normal total calcium. Hypercalcemia does the reverse, raising threshold and causing weakness, hyporeflexia, and a short QT interval."},{"type":"flow","title":"From low ionized calcium to tetany","steps":["Ionized calcium falls (hypoparathyroidism, alkalosis, low magnesium)","Less screening of negative surface charge","Sodium channels activate at more negative voltage","Threshold moves toward resting potential","Spontaneous repetitive firing of peripheral nerves","Paresthesias, carpopedal spasm, positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs"],"caption":"Hypocalcemia makes nerves hyperexcitable by changing threshold, not resting potential."},{"type":"h","text":"Potassium: why both directions cause weakness"},{"type":"p","text":"The resting potential of nerve and muscle lies close to the potassium equilibrium potential, which depends on the ratio of extracellular to intracellular potassium (normal plasma potassium about 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). In hypokalemia the membrane hyperpolarizes, moving further from threshold, so a larger stimulus is needed and weakness, cramps, and ileus follow; severe depletion can cause rhabdomyolysis. In hyperkalemia the membrane depolarizes. A small rise first brings the cell closer to threshold, but sustained depolarization closes the inactivation gates of sodium channels and keeps them closed, so fewer channels are available, action potentials fail, and an ascending flaccid weakness develops. In the heart this produces peaked T waves, a widened QRS complex, and arrhythmias."},{"type":"table","title":"Electrolyte effects on excitability","cols":["Disorder","Main change","Effect on excitability","Muscle result"],"rows":[["Hypocalcemia","Threshold moves toward rest","Increased","Tetany, cramps, Chvostek and Trousseau signs"],["Hypercalcemia","Threshold moves away from rest","Decreased","Weakness, hyporeflexia"],["Hypokalemia","Resting potential hyperpolarized","Decreased","Weakness, cramps, ileus"],["Hyperkalemia","Resting potential depolarized; sodium channels inactivated","Briefly increased, then decreased","Flaccid weakness, cardiac conduction failure"]],"caption":"Calcium mainly moves threshold; potassium mainly moves the resting potential."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating the potassium equilibrium potential","text":"At 37 °C (98.6 °F), EK = 61.5 mV times log(extracellular K / intracellular K). With intracellular potassium 140 and plasma potassium 4 mEq/L, log(4/140) is about minus 1.54, so EK is about minus 95 mV. At 2 mEq/L, log(2/140) is about minus 1.85, giving about minus 113 mV: the cell hyperpolarizes and is harder to excite. At 7 mEq/L, log(7/140) is about minus 1.30, giving about minus 80 mV: the cell depolarizes, and if the depolarization persists, sodium channels remain inactivated. The resting potential sits slightly positive to EK (small sodium leak) but moves the same way."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient hyperventilating during a panic attack develops perioral tingling and carpal spasm with a normal total calcium. Why?","explain":"Respiratory alkalosis makes albumin bind more calcium, so ionized calcium falls while total calcium is unchanged, and threshold moves toward rest.","check":"Compare with the hypocalcemia paragraphs and flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Localizing weakness uses every Part I discipline. Anatomy supplies the reflex roots and the nerve paths; physiology supplies the safety factor, spindle gain, and the electrical effects of calcium and potassium; immunology explains why myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are antibody diseases with thymic and tumor associations; microbiology explains why botulinum and tetanus toxins, which both cleave SNARE proteins, cause opposite clinical pictures; and pathology reads the muscle biopsy to separate whole motor unit loss from fiber necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The reasoning has limits. Mixed pictures occur: reflexes can fall late in severe myopathy, CK can be mildly raised in active denervation, and seronegative myasthenia gravis still produces fatigable weakness. Spinal muscular atrophy breaks the distal rule for neurogenic disease, and some myopathies do not raise CK at all. Use the whole pattern of distribution, fatigue, reflexes, autonomic signs, CK, and biopsy rather than any single finding."}],"summary":["Calcium entry through P/Q-type channels drives SNARE-mediated fusion of acetylcholine vesicles; acetylcholine opens nicotinic cation channels to produce an end plate potential that normally exceeds threshold by a wide safety factor.","Acetylcholinesterase in the basal lamina ends each signal; inhibiting it causes fasciculations and then depolarizing block.","Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins at all cholinergic terminals, producing descending flaccid paralysis with dilated pupils and autonomic failure.","Myasthenia gravis is postsynaptic receptor loss: fatigable ocular and bulbar weakness, normal reflexes and pupils, decrement at low-rate stimulation, thymic disease.","Lambert-Eaton syndrome is presynaptic calcium channel loss: proximal leg weakness, low reflexes, autonomic signs, facilitation with effort, and small cell lung cancer.","Spindles lie in parallel and sense length through Ia afferents, with gamma motor neurons setting their gain; tendon organs lie in series and sense tension through Ib afferents that inhibit the same muscle.","Biceps C5 to C6, triceps C7, patellar L4, Achilles S1; lower motor neuron lesions lower reflexes and upper motor neuron lesions raise them.","Neurogenic weakness shows fasciculations, grouped atrophy, and fiber type grouping with near-normal CK; myopathic weakness is proximal with high CK in necrotizing disease; type II atrophy with normal CK suggests disuse or glucocorticoids.","Hypocalcemia moves threshold toward rest, causing tetany with Chvostek and Trousseau signs; hypokalemia hyperpolarizes and hyperkalemia inactivates sodium channels, so both weaken muscle."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling; Motor Functions of the Spinal Cord; the Cord Reflexes"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Synaptic transmission and the neuromuscular junction; electrophysiology of the cell membrane"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, sections on neuromuscular junction disorders, denervation, and myopathies"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Spore-forming gram-positive bacilli, section on Clostridium botulinum and Clostridium tetani"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders, antibody-mediated diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Nervous system disorders, sections on myasthenia gravis; disorders of the parathyroids and calcium metabolism"}]},"MS-11":{"unit":"MS-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01","title":"Immune Responses in Musculoskeletal Disease","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how adaptive and innate immunity injure joints, muscle, and connective tissue. It covers rheumatoid arthritis from loss of tolerance to bone erosion, the clinical and serologic features that separate it from osteoarthritis and the other connective tissue diseases, the hypersensitivity type behind each disorder, crystal and bacterial inflammation in the joint, and arthritis that follows infection or vaccination. Board questions usually give a joint pattern, a synovial fluid result, or an autoantibody and ask for the disease or the effector mechanism. If you can name the cell, antibody, or cytokine doing the damage, the clinical picture follows logically.","prerequisites":["Synovial joint structure: synovium, articular cartilage, capsule, and subchondral bone (MS-1)","Bone remodeling and the RANKL, RANK, and osteoprotegerin system","MHC class I and II antigen presentation and CD4 and CD8 T cell function","Antibody classes, complement activation, and the four hypersensitivity types","Neuromuscular junction transmission and acetylcholine release"],"connections":[{"to":"Immunology: tolerance and hypersensitivity","how":"Every disease in this chapter is a failure of tolerance or an innate response placed in a particular tissue, classified by its effector mechanism."},{"to":"Biochemistry: purine metabolism","how":"Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown, and its limited solubility is what lets crystals form and trigger gout."},{"to":"Microbiology: streptococci, enteric bacteria, Chlamydia, and viruses","how":"Group A streptococci, enteric pathogens, Chlamydia trachomatis, parvovirus B19, and rubella each cause arthritis by a different immune route."},{"to":"Renal and pulmonary pathology","how":"Immune complexes in SLE injure glomeruli as well as synovium, and anti-Scl-70, anti-Jo-1, and anti-U1 RNP predict lung disease."},{"to":"Neuromuscular disorders (MS-10)","how":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome and inflammatory myopathy produce weakness through antibody and T cell attack on the neuromuscular junction and muscle fiber."}],"terms":[{"term":"Citrullination","def":"Conversion of arginine residues in proteins to citrulline by peptidylarginine deiminase, creating new epitopes that can break tolerance."},{"term":"Shared epitope","def":"A common amino acid sequence in the peptide binding groove of HLA-DR4 and related HLA-DRB1 alleles that binds citrullinated peptides well."},{"term":"Pannus","def":"Hyperplastic, inflamed synovium with granulation tissue that grows over and invades articular cartilage and bone."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"Receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa B ligand, a membrane and soluble signal that drives osteoclast formation and activation."},{"term":"Rheumatoid factor","def":"An autoantibody, usually IgM, directed against the Fc portion of IgG."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of antibodies or T cells raised against a microbial or tumor antigen with a structurally similar self antigen."},{"term":"Inflammasome","def":"A cytoplasmic protein complex, such as NLRP3, that activates caspase-1 to cleave pro-IL-1 beta and pro-IL-18 into active cytokines."},{"term":"Jaccoud arthropathy","def":"Nonerosive hand deformity from lax ligaments and tendons that can be passively corrected, seen in SLE."},{"term":"Sclerodactyly","def":"Thickened, tight skin of the fingers from excess collagen deposition in systemic sclerosis."},{"term":"Enthesitis","def":"Inflammation where tendons and ligaments insert into bone, typical of HLA-B27 associated spondyloarthritis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C01","obj":"MS-11.1","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis: from citrullinated peptides to bone erosion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic autoimmune synovitis that destroys cartilage and bone. It begins when genetic susceptibility meets an environmental trigger. The strongest genetic link is to HLA-DR4 and related HLA-DRB1 alleles that carry the shared epitope, a sequence in the peptide binding groove that binds citrullinated peptides with high affinity. Smoking is the best established environmental factor."},{"type":"p","text":"Citrullination is the key molecular event. Peptidylarginine deiminase (PAD) enzymes, released by stressed or dying cells such as those in a smoker's airway, convert arginine residues to citrulline in proteins like fibrinogen, vimentin, and type II collagen. Citrulline is neutral where arginine was positively charged, which changes how the peptide folds and fits into MHC class II. Shared epitope molecules present these altered peptides to CD4 T cells, tolerance fails, and the T cells help B cells produce anti-citrullinated protein antibodies (detected as anti-CCP) and rheumatoid factor. These antibodies often appear years before the joints swell."},{"type":"h","text":"Effector phase in the synovium"},{"type":"p","text":"In the joint, Th1 and Th17 CD4 T cells activate macrophages and synovial fibroblasts. Macrophages release TNF, IL-1, and IL-6. TNF and IL-1 induce endothelial adhesion molecules that recruit more leukocytes and stimulate fibroblasts and chondrocytes to secrete matrix metalloproteinases that digest cartilage collagen and proteoglycan. The normally thin synovial lining becomes a thick, villous mass of fibroblasts, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and new vessels. This pannus spreads across the cartilage surface and invades it."},{"type":"p","text":"Bone loss depends on osteoclasts. Activated T cells and synovial fibroblasts express RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their maturation, while TNF and IL-1 add to the signal. Osteoclasts resorb bone first at the joint margins, the bare areas where synovium touches bone not covered by cartilage, giving marginal erosions and periarticular osteopenia on radiographs. IL-6 travels to the liver and drives the acute phase response, raising CRP and fibrinogen (and so ESR), increasing hepcidin, and producing anemia of inflammation, fatigue, and low-grade fever. RA is therefore systemic, not only articular."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis pathogenesis","steps":["Smoking and other stress increase PAD-mediated citrullination","HLA-DR4 shared epitope presents citrullinated peptides to CD4 T cells","T cell help yields anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor","Th1 and Th17 cells activate macrophages and synovial fibroblasts","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 drive synovitis and pannus","Metalloproteinases degrade cartilage; RANKL activates osteoclasts","Marginal erosions, periarticular osteopenia, joint deformity"],"caption":"The chain from loss of tolerance to permanent joint damage. Each arrow is a step where a cytokine or cell type does the work."},{"type":"table","title":"Mediators in rheumatoid synovitis","cols":["Mediator","Main source","Effect in RA"],"rows":[["TNF","Macrophages, T cells","Adhesion molecules, cytokine cascade, MMP release, osteoclast support"],["IL-1","Macrophages","Cartilage breakdown by chondrocytes and fibroblasts, osteoclast activation"],["IL-6","Macrophages, fibroblasts","Acute phase response (CRP, hepcidin), B cell differentiation, systemic symptoms"],["IL-17","Th17 cells","Fibroblast activation, neutrophil recruitment, RANKL induction"],["RANKL","T cells, synovial fibroblasts","Osteoclast formation and bone erosion"],["Matrix metalloproteinases","Fibroblasts, chondrocytes","Digest type II collagen and proteoglycan"]],"caption":"Cytokines amplify the synovitis; MMPs and RANKL do the structural damage to cartilage and bone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If RANKL signaling were blocked while TNF and IL-1 remained high, which joint damage would slow most?","explain":"Bone erosion depends on osteoclasts, which need RANKL, whereas cartilage loss is driven mainly by metalloproteinases from fibroblasts and chondrocytes, so erosions would slow while synovitis and cartilage damage continued.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on bone loss and the mediator table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C02","obj":"MS-11.2","title":"Clinical picture of rheumatoid arthritis, contrast with osteoarthritis, and Felty syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"RA typically affects women about two to three times as often as men and begins in middle age. It produces a symmetric polyarthritis of the small joints of the hands and feet: the metacarpophalangeal (MCP), proximal interphalangeal (PIP), wrist, and metatarsophalangeal joints. The distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints are characteristically spared. Larger joints and the cervical spine are involved later, and synovitis at C1 to C2 can loosen the transverse ligament and cause atlantoaxial subluxation."},{"type":"p","text":"Morning stiffness lasting more than 1 hour reflects inflammation. Overnight, inflammatory fluid and mediators accumulate in the synovium and periarticular tissue while the joint is still; movement gradually disperses them. Joints are warm, boggy, and tender because the swelling is synovial tissue and effusion rather than bony overgrowth."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the deformities form"},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic synovitis stretches capsules, collateral ligaments, and tendon sheaths, and pannus erodes their bony attachments. At the MCP joints the extensor tendons slip toward the ulnar side, giving ulnar deviation with volar subluxation. Damage to the finger extensor mechanism and volar plate produces swan neck deformity (PIP hyperextension with DIP flexion) or boutonniere deformity (PIP flexion with DIP hyperextension). Rheumatoid nodules form over pressure points such as the extensor forearm; histologically they show central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by palisading epithelioid histiocytes, and they occur mainly in seropositive patients."},{"type":"table","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis versus osteoarthritis","cols":["Feature","Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteoarthritis"],"rows":[["Primary process","Autoimmune synovitis with pannus","Cartilage degeneration with failed repair"],["Joint pattern","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist, MTP; DIP spared","DIP, PIP, first CMC, hips, knees, spine; often asymmetric"],["Morning stiffness","Over 1 hour","Under about 30 minutes"],["Effect of activity","Stiffness improves with use","Pain worsens with use, eases with rest"],["Hand findings","Ulnar deviation, swan neck, boutonniere, soft swelling","Heberden (DIP) and Bouchard (PIP) nodes, hard bony swelling"],["Radiograph","Marginal erosions, periarticular osteopenia, uniform joint space loss","Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts, uneven joint space loss"],["Synovial fluid","Inflammatory, about 2,000 to 50,000 WBC/uL","Noninflammatory, under about 2,000 WBC/uL"],["Systemic features","Fatigue, nodules, anemia, high ESR and CRP, serositis","None"],["Serology","Anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor often positive","Negative"]],"caption":"The joint pattern, duration of stiffness, and radiograph separate inflammatory from degenerative arthritis."},{"type":"p","text":"Serology supports the diagnosis. Rheumatoid factor (RF) is IgM directed against the Fc portion of IgG; it is positive in about 70 to 80% of patients with RA but also appears in Sjogren syndrome, chronic infections such as hepatitis C and endocarditis, and in healthy older adults. Anti-CCP has similar or slightly lower sensitivity but specificity of about 95%, so it is the better confirmatory test and predicts more erosive disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Felty syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Felty syndrome is the triad of RA, splenomegaly, and neutropenia. It occurs in long-standing, seropositive, often nodular disease. Neutrophils are lost through antibody and immune complex coating with splenic removal, splenic sequestration, and suppressed granulopoiesis, so patients present with recurrent bacterial infections and leg ulcers. Some cases overlap with large granular lymphocyte expansion, which can produce the same picture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C03","obj":"MS-11.3","title":"Autoantibodies in rheumatic disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autoantibodies are the serologic fingerprint of a connective tissue disease. Each one reflects the self antigen to which tolerance was lost, and many also predict which organ is at risk. A sensitive test helps rule a disease out when negative; a specific test helps rule it in when positive."},{"type":"p","text":"The antinuclear antibody (ANA) is the screening test. It is positive in more than 95% of patients with SLE but also in many other connective tissue diseases and in a meaningful fraction of healthy people, especially older women, so a positive ANA alone diagnoses nothing. Specific antibodies are then ordered to identify the disease. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith both point to SLE: anti-Smith is the most specific but is found in only a minority of patients, while anti-dsDNA is more common, rises with disease activity, and correlates with lupus nephritis because DNA and anti-DNA complexes deposit in glomeruli."},{"type":"table","title":"Autoantibodies and their diseases","cols":["Antibody","Target antigen","Disease","Clue or organ risk"],"rows":[["Anti-CCP","Citrullinated peptides","Rheumatoid arthritis","Most specific for RA; can precede symptoms; predicts erosions"],["Rheumatoid factor","Fc of IgG","Rheumatoid arthritis","Sensitive but not specific; also Sjogren, hepatitis C, aging"],["ANA","Nuclear antigens","SLE and many others","Sensitive screen, low specificity"],["Anti-dsDNA","Double-stranded DNA","SLE","Tracks activity and lupus nephritis"],["Anti-Smith","Small nuclear ribonucleoprotein core proteins","SLE","Most specific for SLE, low sensitivity"],["Anti-Scl-70","DNA topoisomerase I","Diffuse systemic sclerosis","Interstitial lung disease"],["Anticentromere","Centromere proteins","Limited systemic sclerosis (CREST)","Pulmonary arterial hypertension"],["Anti-U1 RNP","U1 small nuclear ribonucleoprotein","Mixed connective tissue disease","High titer; Raynaud, puffy hands, overlap features"],["Anti-Jo-1","Histidyl-tRNA synthetase","Polymyositis and dermatomyositis","Interstitial lung disease, mechanic's hands"],["Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La)","Ribonucleoproteins","Sjogren syndrome","Maternal anti-SSA crosses placenta: neonatal lupus, congenital heart block"]],"caption":"Match the antibody to the disease, then use the clue column to predict organ involvement."},{"type":"p","text":"The two forms of systemic sclerosis are separated by antibody as well as by skin extent. Anti-Scl-70 (anti-topoisomerase I) marks diffuse disease, with skin thickening proximal to the elbows and knees and on the trunk, and a high risk of interstitial lung disease. Anticentromere antibody marks limited disease, also called CREST (calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, telangiectasia), with skin change confined to the face and distal limbs and a later risk of pulmonary arterial hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-U1 RNP in high titer defines mixed connective tissue disease, so the antibody and the overlap syndrome travel together. Anti-Jo-1 is the most common antisynthetase antibody in inflammatory myopathy and identifies a subset with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, fever, and rough, cracked fingertips called mechanic's hands. Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are typical of Sjogren syndrome but also occur in SLE, and RF is often positive in Sjogren syndrome as well."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading a serology panel","text":"A 38-year-old woman has symmetric MCP and wrist swelling. ANA is positive at a low titer, RF is positive, anti-CCP is negative, and anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are negative. A low-titer ANA is common in healthy women, and the specific SLE antibodies are negative, so SLE is not supported. RF is positive, but RF alone also occurs in Sjogren syndrome and chronic infection, so ask about dry eyes and mouth and consider hepatitis C. A negative anti-CCP does not exclude RA, because its sensitivity is only about 60 to 70%. Persistent symmetric small joint synovitis with a positive RF still supports RA; if anti-SSA is positive with sicca symptoms, Sjogren syndrome explains both."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A pregnant woman with no symptoms has anti-SSA antibodies. Which fetal organ is at risk, and why?","explain":"Maternal IgG anti-SSA crosses the placenta and injures the developing fetal AV node and conduction tissue, causing congenital heart block that can be permanent.","check":"Compare with the anti-SSA row of the autoantibody table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C04","obj":"MS-11.4","title":"Musculoskeletal features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and mixed connective tissue disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The connective tissue diseases all reach the joints, but each injures a different tissue, so the joint picture differs. Asking what the immune system is attacking (synovium, collagen and small vessels, or several targets at once) predicts whether the joint erodes, stiffens, or simply hurts."},{"type":"h","text":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"},{"type":"p","text":"Joint pain or arthritis is among the most common features of SLE, present in most patients at some point. The synovitis is driven by immune complex deposition and complement activation (type III hypersensitivity), producing mild synovial inflammation without the proliferative pannus of RA. Cartilage and bone are therefore not eroded, and the radiograph stays free of erosions. With time, lax capsules, ligaments, and tendons can let the fingers drift into ulnar deviation or swan neck positions that the examiner can push back into place; this reducible, nonerosive deformity is Jaccoud arthropathy. Myalgia is common, and an inflammatory myositis occurs in a minority. Osteonecrosis, especially of the femoral head, is more frequent in SLE, linked to glucocorticoid exposure, vasculitis, and antiphospholipid antibodies."},{"type":"h","text":"Systemic sclerosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) combines small vessel injury, autoimmunity, and fibrosis. Endothelial damage and intimal proliferation narrow digital arteries, and activated fibroblasts, stimulated largely by TGF-beta, deposit excess collagen in skin and deeper tissues. Raynaud phenomenon is usually the first sign: cold or emotion triggers vasospasm in already narrowed vessels, so the fingers turn white (ischemia), then blue (deoxygenated blood), then red (reactive hyperemia). Chronic ischemia causes digital pitting scars, ulcers, and resorption of the terminal phalangeal tufts (acro-osteolysis)."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibrosis of skin and tendon sheaths produces sclerodactyly, tight shiny skin that tethers the fingers, and fixed flexion contractures as the hand loses the ability to extend. In diffuse disease, fibrin and fibrosis in tendon sheaths can produce palpable tendon friction rubs, which signal more aggressive disease. Calcinosis cutis is the deposition of calcium hydroxyapatite in subcutaneous tissue, typically over fingertips and extensor surfaces, and can ulcerate and drain chalky material. Muscle can be involved by a fibrosing or inflammatory myopathy."},{"type":"h","text":"Mixed connective tissue disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Mixed connective tissue disease (MCTD) is an overlap syndrome defined by high-titer anti-U1 RNP together with features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis. Raynaud phenomenon is nearly universal and diffusely swollen, puffy hands are common early, and patients also have inflammatory arthritis, myositis with elevated creatine kinase, and esophageal dysmotility. Severe renal and central nervous system disease are less common than in SLE, while pulmonary arterial hypertension is the leading cause of death. Some patients later evolve toward one defined disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Musculoskeletal involvement in three connective tissue diseases","cols":["Feature","SLE","Systemic sclerosis","MCTD"],"rows":[["Main tissue injury","Immune complex synovitis","Microvascular injury and collagen fibrosis","Overlap of all three patterns"],["Arthritis","Nonerosive; Jaccoud reducible deformity","Arthralgia; contractures from skin and tendon fibrosis","Inflammatory arthritis, usually nonerosive"],["Hand signs","Reducible ulnar deviation","Sclerodactyly, calcinosis, digital ulcers, acro-osteolysis","Swollen puffy hands, Raynaud"],["Muscle","Myalgia; myositis uncommon","Fibrosing or inflammatory myopathy","Myositis with high CK"],["Raynaud phenomenon","Common","Nearly universal, often first sign","Nearly universal"],["Defining antibody","Anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith","Anti-Scl-70 (diffuse), anticentromere (limited)","High-titer anti-U1 RNP"]],"caption":"The joint findings follow the tissue each disease attacks."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C05","obj":"MS-11.5","title":"Hypersensitivity mechanisms in joint, nerve, and muscle disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypersensitivity types are defined by the effector mechanism, not by the organ. Type I is IgE and mast cell mediated; type II is antibody bound to an antigen fixed on a cell or in tissue; type III is circulating antigen-antibody complexes that deposit and activate complement; type IV is T cell mediated injury without antibody. To classify any disease, ask what is doing the damage and where the antigen sits."},{"type":"table","title":"Hypersensitivity types in musculoskeletal disease","cols":["Type","Effector","How tissue is injured","Musculoskeletal example"],"rows":[["II","IgG or IgM against a fixed tissue antigen","Complement, phagocytes, or blocked or altered receptor and channel function","Rheumatic fever polyarthritis; Lambert-Eaton syndrome; myasthenia gravis"],["III","Immune complexes","Deposit in synovium, vessels, glomeruli; complement draws neutrophils","SLE arthritis; serum sickness arthralgia"],["IV","CD4 or CD8 T cells","Cytokines activate macrophages, or cytotoxic T cells kill target cells","Polymyositis (CD8 T cells)"]],"caption":"Type I is omitted because it is not a primary mechanism of these joint and muscle diseases."},{"type":"h","text":"Type II: antibody against tissue"},{"type":"p","text":"Acute rheumatic fever follows group A streptococcal pharyngitis by about 2 to 4 weeks. Antibodies and T cells raised against the streptococcal M protein and cell wall cross-react with self proteins in heart, joints, brain, and skin, a process called molecular mimicry. The arthritis is a migratory polyarthritis of large joints such as knees, ankles, elbows, and wrists; each joint is inflamed for days and the arthritis heals without deformity, unlike the carditis, which can scar valves."},{"type":"p","text":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome shows that type II injury can alter function without killing cells. Small cell lung cancer, the trigger in about half of patients, is a neuroendocrine tumor that expresses voltage-gated calcium channels. Antibodies raised against tumor P/Q type channels cross-react with the same channels on presynaptic motor nerve terminals and reduce calcium entry, so fewer acetylcholine quanta are released per impulse. Patients have proximal weakness, reduced reflexes, and autonomic symptoms such as dry mouth. Repeated or sustained contraction lets calcium accumulate in the terminal, so strength and reflexes briefly improve after exercise, and high-frequency nerve stimulation produces an increasing muscle response. Myasthenia gravis is also type II, but its antibodies target the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor and weakness worsens with use."},{"type":"flow","title":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome as type II cross-reaction","steps":["Small cell lung cancer expresses voltage-gated calcium channels","Antibodies form against tumor P/Q type channels","Antibodies bind presynaptic calcium channels at motor terminals","Less calcium entry, fewer acetylcholine quanta released","Proximal weakness, hyporeflexia, autonomic symptoms","Repetitive activity builds calcium and transiently improves strength"],"caption":"Antibody against a fixed antigen changes channel function, which is type II even though the trigger is a tumor."},{"type":"h","text":"Type III and type IV"},{"type":"p","text":"In SLE, antibodies to nuclear antigens form complexes with material released from dying cells. The complexes deposit in synovium, vessel walls, and glomeruli, activate complement (lowering serum C3 and C4), and attract neutrophils, which is type III injury. In polymyositis, muscle fibers abnormally express MHC class I, and CD8 cytotoxic T cells invade the endomysium and kill nonnecrotic fibers, which is type IV injury and produces symmetric proximal weakness with high creatine kinase. Dermatomyositis differs: complement-mediated injury to capillaries causes perifascicular atrophy, with a perimysial and perivascular infiltrate rich in CD4 T cells and B cells."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome begins with a tumor and involves no immune complexes. Why is it still type II hypersensitivity?","explain":"The injury is caused by antibody binding an antigen fixed in tissue, the presynaptic calcium channel, and altering its function; the source of the original immunizing antigen does not change the effector mechanism.","check":"Compare with the hypersensitivity table and the Lambert-Eaton flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C06","obj":"MS-11.6","title":"Innate immune activation in the joint: gout and septic arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gout and septic arthritis show that a joint can be destroyed without autoimmunity. In both, innate pattern recognition, cytokines, and neutrophils produce intense inflammation within hours. Neither needs prior sensitization or antigen specificity."},{"type":"h","text":"Gout and the NLRP3 inflammasome"},{"type":"p","text":"Uric acid is the end product of purine metabolism in humans. Serum urate saturates at about 6.8 mg/dL at 37 °C (98.6 °F); above that level monosodium urate crystals can form, and solubility falls further at lower temperature, which helps explain why the cooler first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra) is a classic site. Crystals can sit in a joint silently until they are shed or newly formed, then trigger an attack."},{"type":"p","text":"The flare needs two signals. Priming through Toll-like receptors and NF-kappa B (from free fatty acids, for example) makes macrophages transcribe pro-IL-1 beta and NLRP3. When macrophages phagocytose urate crystals, the crystals damage lysosomes and cause potassium efflux, which assembles NLRP3 with the adaptor ASC and pro-caspase-1. Active caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1 beta into IL-1 beta. IL-1 beta activates endothelium and induces chemokines such as IL-8, drawing in neutrophils that ingest crystals and release more mediators, so the joint becomes hot, red, and exquisitely tender. Flares are self-limited partly because neutrophil extracellular traps aggregate crystals and degrade cytokines. Aspirated fluid shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals, often inside neutrophils."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Amplification loop in an acute gout flare","steps":["Urate crystals shed into the joint","Macrophages phagocytose crystals; NLRP3 assembles","Caspase-1 releases active IL-1 beta","Endothelial activation and chemokines recruit neutrophils","Neutrophils ingest crystals and release more mediators"],"caption":"Each neutrophil that ingests crystals adds to the inflammation until crystal aggregation and cytokine breakdown end the flare."},{"type":"h","text":"Septic arthritis"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria reach the joint usually by hematogenous spread, less often by direct inoculation. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause overall, and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is an important cause in sexually active young adults. Bacterial products activate Toll-like receptors on synovial cells, releasing TNF and IL-1 and recruiting huge numbers of neutrophils. Neutrophil elastase and other proteases, metalloproteinases from IL-1 stimulated chondrocytes, and bacterial toxins strip proteoglycan from cartilage within about 2 days and then break down collagen. The tense effusion also impairs nutrient diffusion into cartilage. Because articular cartilage is avascular and has little capacity for repair, this damage is permanent within days, which makes the hot, swollen, single joint a medical emergency until infection is excluded."},{"type":"table","title":"Synovial fluid in acute monoarthritis","cols":["Finding","Gout","Pseudogout (CPPD)","Septic arthritis"],"rows":[["Typical WBC/uL","About 2,000 to 50,000, can exceed 50,000","About 2,000 to 50,000","Usually over 50,000, neutrophil predominant"],["Crystals","Needle-shaped, negatively birefringent urate","Rhomboid, weakly positively birefringent calcium pyrophosphate","None, unless coexisting"],["Gram stain and culture","Negative","Negative","Often positive; gonococcus may culture poorly"],["Glucose","Normal or mildly low","Normal or mildly low","Often low"]],"caption":"White cell counts overlap; crystals and culture decide the diagnosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: crystals do not exclude infection","text":"A 62-year-old man with known gout has a hot right knee and a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F). Aspirate shows 85,000 WBC/uL, 92% neutrophils, and negatively birefringent needles; the Gram stain is negative. The crystals prove gout is present, but not that gout is the only process. A count above 50,000 with high neutrophil predominance and fever overlaps the septic range, a Gram stain misses many infections, and crystals and bacteria can coexist in the same joint. Because infection destroys cartilage within days, culture must exclude it before the flare is blamed on gout alone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-11-01-C07","obj":"MS-11.7","title":"Postinfectious and postvaccine arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Some arthritis follows an infection rather than being caused by live organisms growing in the joint. The joint fluid is sterile on culture, and the inflammation reflects the host response to a recent microbe. Timing, joint pattern, and host genetics identify the cause."},{"type":"h","text":"Reactive arthritis"},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive arthritis is a spondyloarthritis that develops about 1 to 4 weeks after a gastrointestinal infection with Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, or Campylobacter, or a genitourinary infection with Chlamydia trachomatis. It is associated with HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele. Bacterial antigens, and for Chlamydia even viable organisms that cannot be cultured, are carried to the synovium by monocytes. Proposed mechanisms include HLA-B27 presenting cross-reactive bacterial peptides to CD8 T cells and misfolding of HLA-B27 that drives IL-23 and IL-17 production; impaired clearance of the organism by HLA-B27 positive cells may also contribute."},{"type":"p","text":"The pattern is an asymmetric oligoarthritis of the lower limbs, often knees and ankles, with enthesitis (Achilles tendon, plantar fascia), dactylitis (sausage digits), and sometimes sacroiliitis. Extra-articular features include conjunctivitis, urethritis or cervicitis, and skin and mucosal lesions such as keratoderma blennorrhagicum. This differs from RA because inflammation centers on entheses and lower limb joints rather than on symmetric hand synovium, and RF and anti-CCP are negative."},{"type":"flow","title":"From enteric or chlamydial infection to reactive arthritis","steps":["Enteric or Chlamydia trachomatis infection","Bacterial antigens carried to synovium by monocytes","HLA-B27 background shapes T cell and IL-17 response","Sterile asymmetric lower limb arthritis and enthesitis, 1 to 4 weeks later"],"caption":"The joint is inflamed by the host response, so culture of the fluid is negative."},{"type":"h","text":"Viral and postvaccine arthritis"},{"type":"p","text":"Parvovirus B19 causes erythema infectiosum in children, but in adults, particularly women, it more often causes an acute symmetric polyarthritis of the MCP, PIP, wrist, and knee joints. Because this pattern mirrors early RA, it is a classic mimic, and RF can be transiently positive. Joint symptoms begin as specific IgM appears, consistent with immune complex formation, and usually resolve within weeks without erosion. Rubella infection produces a similar symmetric small joint arthritis in adult women around the time of the rash, through immune complexes and direct infection of synovium."},{"type":"p","text":"The live attenuated rubella vaccine reproduces this pattern on a smaller scale. Susceptible postpubertal women are most affected, developing arthralgia or, less often, arthritis about 1 to 3 weeks after vaccination that lasts days to weeks. Children and men rarely have joint symptoms. The replicating vaccine virus provokes the same response as natural infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Arthritis after infection or vaccination","cols":["Condition","Trigger and timing","Joint pattern","Host and mechanism"],"rows":[["Reactive arthritis","Salmonella, Shigella, Yersinia, Campylobacter, Chlamydia; 1 to 4 weeks after","Asymmetric lower limb oligoarthritis, enthesitis, dactylitis","HLA-B27; sterile, antigen-driven T cell and IL-17 response"],["Parvovirus B19 arthritis","Coincides with IgM antibody, about 2 to 3 weeks after exposure","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist, knee; mimics RA","Adults, mostly women; immune complexes; self-limited"],["Rubella arthritis","Around the rash","Symmetric small joints of hands, wrists, knees","Adult women; immune complexes and synovial infection"],["Postvaccine rubella arthralgia","About 1 to 3 weeks after vaccination","Transient arthralgia, occasionally arthritis","Susceptible adult women; response to replicating vaccine virus"]],"caption":"Symmetric small joint arthritis after a viral illness mimics RA; asymmetric lower limb arthritis with enthesitis points to reactive arthritis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Immunology supplies the classification and pathology supplies the joint picture. Adaptive immunity against a specific self antigen drives RA, SLE, systemic sclerosis, and myositis, and the effector (T cell, antibody against tissue, or immune complex) determines whether synovium proliferates, immune complexes deposit, or muscle fibers are killed. Innate immunity explains gout and septic arthritis, where crystals or bacteria trigger neutrophil inflammation without antigen specificity. Microbiology links the two through molecular mimicry in rheumatic fever and antigen persistence in reactive arthritis, while biochemistry explains citrullination and urate solubility, and bone biology explains why RANKL turns synovitis into erosion."},{"type":"p","text":"These categories have limits. Many diseases use more than one mechanism: RA involves T cells, autoantibodies, and immune complexes, and dermatomyositis does not fit neatly into one hypersensitivity type. Autoantibodies support a diagnosis but are not diagnostic alone, since ANA and RF occur in healthy people and anti-CCP is negative in about a third of patients with RA. Synovial fluid white cell counts overlap among gout, RA, and infection, so crystals and culture, not the count, settle the question."}],"summary":["RA begins when HLA-DR4 shared epitope molecules present citrullinated peptides, CD4 T cells break tolerance, and B cells make anti-CCP and rheumatoid factor.","TNF, IL-1, and IL-6 drive pannus and systemic inflammation; metalloproteinases degrade cartilage and RANKL-activated osteoclasts cause marginal erosions.","RA is a symmetric MCP, PIP, and wrist synovitis sparing the DIP with stiffness over 1 hour; osteoarthritis involves the DIP, worsens with use, and forms osteophytes.","Felty syndrome is RA with splenomegaly and neutropenia, causing recurrent bacterial infections.","Anti-CCP is the most specific test for RA, anti-Smith for SLE, anti-Scl-70 for diffuse and anticentromere for limited systemic sclerosis, anti-U1 RNP for MCTD, anti-Jo-1 for myositis, and anti-SSA and anti-SSB for Sjogren syndrome.","SLE arthritis is nonerosive with reducible deformity; systemic sclerosis causes Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly, calcinosis, and flexion contractures.","Rheumatic fever arthritis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome are type II, SLE arthritis is type III, and polymyositis is type IV with CD8 T cells killing MHC class I positive fibers.","Urate crystals activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and caspase-1 to release IL-1 beta, which recruits neutrophils; septic arthritis destroys avascular cartilage within days.","Reactive arthritis is sterile, HLA-B27 associated, asymmetric lower limb arthritis with enthesitis 1 to 4 weeks after enteric or chlamydial infection.","Parvovirus B19 and rubella cause self-limited symmetric small joint arthritis in adult women that mimics RA; rubella vaccine causes transient arthralgia in susceptible adult women."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Diseases of the Immune System (autoimmune diseases and hypersensitivity)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (section on joints)"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Hypersensitivity disorders and immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Inflammatory Rheumatic Diseases"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci; Parvoviruses; Rubella virus"}]},"MS-12":{"unit":"MS-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Pathogens","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the organisms that infect bone, joints, fascia, and muscle, and the virulence factors that let each one reach, attach to, and destroy those tissues. It moves from Staphylococcus aureus and Neisseria gonorrhoeae in bones and joints, through the setting-specific causes of osteomyelitis, to the toxin-driven destruction of gas gangrene and necrotizing fasciitis, and ends with Lyme arthritis and tuberculosis of the spine. Board questions usually give a setting (sickle cell disease, a nail through a sneaker, a dirty deep wound, a tick bite) and ask for the organism, its laboratory features, or the molecule causing the damage. If you can link each virulence factor to the lesion it produces, most of these questions answer themselves.","prerequisites":["Gram stain, bacterial cell wall structure, and basic identification tests (catalase, coagulase, oxidase, hemolysis)","Bone histology and blood supply, including the metaphysis and growth plate (MS-2)","Opsonization, complement, and phagocytosis (IM-7, IM-13)","Antigen presentation by MHC class II to CD4 T cells and Th1 granuloma formation (IM-5)","Limb muscle compartments and the attachments of psoas major (MS-5)"],"connections":[{"to":"Immune responses in musculoskeletal disease (MS-11)","how":"Reactive arthritis and antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis show joint inflammation that continues after, or without, live organisms in the joint."},{"to":"Infectious musculoskeletal diseases (MS-13-G)","how":"This chapter supplies the organisms and mechanisms behind the clinical patterns of osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, and myonecrosis taught there."},{"to":"Complement (IM-7)","how":"Terminal complement deficiency explains recurrent disseminated Neisseria infection, and M protein and protein A show two different ways bacteria defeat opsonization."},{"to":"Conduction system (CV-7) and cardiovascular pathogens (CV-17)","how":"Borrelia burgdorferi and Streptococcus pyogenes also injure the heart, through Lyme carditis with AV block and rheumatic fever."},{"to":"Hematology (HE-9-B)","how":"Sickle cell disease predisposes to Salmonella osteomyelitis through bone infarcts and functional asplenia, and clostridial alpha toxin causes intravascular hemolysis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Osteomyelitis","def":"Infection of bone and marrow, reaching bone through the blood, from a contiguous focus, or by direct inoculation."},{"term":"Septic arthritis","def":"Infection of a joint space, usually by bacteria, producing purulent synovial fluid that can destroy cartilage within days."},{"term":"Sequestrum","def":"A fragment of dead bone left within an infected area after its blood supply is lost."},{"term":"Involucrum","def":"A shell of new reactive bone laid down by periosteum that has been lifted off by pus."},{"term":"Adhesin","def":"A bacterial surface molecule that binds a specific host molecule, such as collagen or fibronectin, so the organism can attach to tissue."},{"term":"Biofilm","def":"A community of bacteria embedded in a self-made polysaccharide matrix on a surface, protected from antibodies, phagocytes, and many antibiotics."},{"term":"Antigenic variation","def":"Change in the structure of a surface antigen through gene recombination, so antibodies made against the old form no longer bind."},{"term":"Lecithinase","def":"A phospholipase C that hydrolyzes lecithin (phosphatidylcholine) in cell membranes; the alpha toxin of Clostridium perfringens."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A toxin that binds MHC class II and the T cell receptor beta chain outside the peptide groove, activating a large fraction of T cells at once."},{"term":"Myonecrosis","def":"Death of skeletal muscle, the defining lesion of clostridial gas gangrene."},{"term":"Pott disease","def":"Tuberculosis of the spine, with destruction and collapse of vertebral bodies."},{"term":"Cold abscess","def":"A collection of caseous material without the heat and redness of an acute pyogenic abscess, typical of tuberculosis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C01","obj":"MS-12.1","title":"Staphylococcus aureus in bone and joint infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus is a gram-positive coccus that grows in grapelike clusters. It is catalase positive, which separates staphylococci from streptococci, and coagulase positive, which separates it from coagulase-negative species such as S. epidermidis. Free coagulase activates prothrombin so that fibrinogen is converted to fibrin, and bound clumping factor coats the organism in fibrinogen, so the bacteria hide inside a fibrin shell. About a third of people carry S. aureus in the anterior nares, which gives it frequent access to the blood through minor skin breaks."},{"type":"h","text":"Why it is the leading bone and joint pathogen"},{"type":"p","text":"Its success rests on three abilities. First, surface adhesins anchored in the cell wall (often called MSCRAMMs, microbial surface components recognizing adhesive matrix molecules) bind collagen, fibronectin, and fibrinogen, which are exposed in bone matrix, cartilage, and synovium. Second, protein A binds the Fc region of IgG. The antibody is then held backward, with its Fc pointing at the bacterium instead of outward, so neutrophil Fc receptors cannot grab it and opsonization fails. Third, on dead bone and implanted hardware the organisms form a biofilm, a polysaccharide matrix in which slow-growing bacteria are shielded from antibodies, phagocytes, and many antibiotics. This is why infection on a device or a sequestrum tends to persist until the foreign material or dead bone is removed."},{"type":"table","title":"Staphylococcus aureus virulence factors and their effects in bone and joint","cols":["Factor","Mechanism","Consequence"],"rows":[["Collagen and fibronectin adhesins","Bind matrix proteins in bone, cartilage, synovium","Attachment and seeding during bacteremia"],["Protein A","Binds IgG by its Fc region","Blocks opsonization and phagocytosis"],["Coagulase and clumping factor","Convert or bind fibrinogen to fibrin","Fibrin coat hides bacteria; walls off abscesses"],["Biofilm","Polysaccharide matrix on dead bone or hardware","Persistent infection resistant to clearance"],["Cytolytic toxins and enzymes","Kill leukocytes and digest tissue","Pus, cartilage and bone destruction"]],"caption":"Each factor maps to a step: attachment, escape from opsonization, protection, and tissue damage."},{"type":"p","text":"In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis favors the metaphysis of long bones. Capillary loops beneath the growth plate turn into wide venous sinusoids where flow is slow and phagocytes are sparse, so circulating bacteria settle there. In infants younger than about 18 months, vessels still cross the growth plate, so infection can reach the epiphysis and joint. Where the metaphysis lies inside the joint capsule (hip and shoulder), osteomyelitis can rupture into the joint. In adults, hematogenous infection more often involves the vertebrae. S. aureus is also the most common cause of septic arthritis overall, which produces turbid synovial fluid, often above 50,000 cells per microliter, mostly neutrophils, whose proteases destroy cartilage within days."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acute hematogenous osteomyelitis in a child","steps":["Transient S. aureus bacteremia","Bacteria lodge in slow metaphyseal sinusoids and bind matrix","Neutrophils and bacterial enzymes form pus","Rising pressure and thrombosis cut blood supply","Dead bone remains as a sequestrum","Pus lifts periosteum, which forms an involucrum"],"caption":"From seeding to the chronic lesion; the sequestrum then harbors biofilm that resists clearance."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict whether an S. aureus strain lacking protein A would be cleared more easily from the blood of an immunized host.","explain":"Without protein A, opsonizing IgG binds by its Fab region with the Fc facing outward, so neutrophil Fc receptors can engage it and phagocytosis proceeds.","check":"Compare with the protein A row of the virulence table and the paragraph on adhesins and protein A."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C02","obj":"MS-12.2","title":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae and disseminated gonococcal infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus, the paired cocci having flattened adjacent sides, and in urethral or joint exudate it is often seen inside neutrophils. It is oxidase positive and ferments glucose but not maltose, which separates it from N. meningitidis (glucose and maltose). It is fastidious, growing best on chocolate agar or selective Thayer-Martin medium in increased CO2, and it has no polysaccharide capsule."},{"type":"h","text":"Virulence: attachment and escape from adaptive immunity"},{"type":"p","text":"Type IV pili attach the organism to nonciliated columnar epithelium of the urethra, cervix, rectum, and pharynx and resist flushing by urine. The pilin gene is expressed from one locus while many silent partial copies sit elsewhere in the genome, and recombination of a silent copy into the expression locus changes the pilus antigen. Opacity (Opa) proteins switch on and off by phase variation. Because antibodies against one variant miss the next, infection gives no lasting immunity, reinfection is common, and no vaccine exists. IgA1 protease cleaves secretory IgA1 in its hinge region, removing the main antibody defense of mucosa. Lipooligosaccharide (a short-chain endotoxin) drives inflammation, and sialylation of it helps resist complement."},{"type":"p","text":"Killing of Neisseria depends heavily on the membrane attack complex. People with deficiency of terminal complement components (C5 to C9) therefore get recurrent disseminated Neisseria infection. Dissemination is also more common in women, especially during menstruation and pregnancy, when cervical conditions favor bacteremia."},{"type":"table","title":"Two presentations of disseminated gonococcal infection","cols":["Feature","Arthritis-dermatitis syndrome","Purulent (suppurative) arthritis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Bacteremia seeds tendon sheaths, skin, and joints, possibly with immune complexes","Organisms settle and multiply in one joint"],["Joints","Migratory polyarthralgia; tenosynovitis of wrists, hands, ankles","Monoarthritis or oligoarthritis, often knee, wrist, or ankle"],["Skin","Few painless pustules on a red base, on the extremities","Usually absent"],["Cultures","Blood sometimes positive; joint fluid usually negative","Synovial fluid culture positive in about half or fewer"]],"caption":"The two forms often overlap in time; the first can evolve into the second."},{"type":"p","text":"The typical patient is a sexually active young adult, often with an asymptomatic genital infection, who develops fever, tender swollen tendon sheaths over the wrist or hand, and scattered pustules. Because the joint culture is often negative, the diagnosis rests on finding the organism at mucosal sites (urethra, cervix, rectum, pharynx) by culture or nucleic acid amplification, or in blood. In this age group gonococcus is a leading cause of acute septic arthritis, while S. aureus remains the most common cause across all ages."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C03","obj":"MS-12.3","title":"Matching osteomyelitis organisms to settings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"S. aureus is the most likely cause of osteomyelitis in almost every setting, so board questions test the exceptions. Each exception follows from how the organism reaches bone or why host defenses fail in that setting, and remembering the reason is more reliable than memorizing a list."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteomyelitis by setting","cols":["Setting","Organism and laboratory features","Why this organism"],"rows":[["Sickle cell disease","Salmonella (nontyphoidal): gram-negative rod, non-lactose fermenter, H2S producer","Bone infarcts give dead tissue to seed; functional asplenia impairs clearance; gut microinfarcts let bacteria enter blood"],["Injection drug use","Pseudomonas aeruginosa (S. aureus also common)","Contaminated water and equipment; vertebrae and sternoclavicular joint typical"],["Nail through a sneaker sole","Pseudomonas aeruginosa: gram-negative rod, oxidase positive, non-lactose fermenter, blue-green pigment","Moist foam insole harbors the organism; puncture inoculates calcaneus or metatarsal"],["Cat or dog bite","Pasteurella multocida: small gram-negative coccobacillus","Oral flora of cats and dogs; sharp cat teeth inoculate periosteum; cellulitis within about a day"],["Prosthetic joint or hardware","Staphylococcus epidermidis: gram-positive clusters, coagulase negative, novobiocin sensitive","Skin flora introduced at surgery; biofilm on foreign material; indolent course"],["Vertebrae with slow course and constitutional symptoms","Mycobacterium tuberculosis: acid-fast rod","Hematogenous seeding of vascular vertebral bodies (Pott disease)"]],"caption":"The setting predicts the organism because it determines the route of entry or the host defect."},{"type":"p","text":"Sickle cell disease shows how host defects shape the organism. Repeated splenic infarction leaves the patient functionally asplenic, which impairs clearance of encapsulated and opsonized bacteria from the blood. Vaso-occlusion in bowel wall lets enteric organisms such as Salmonella enter the circulation, and infarcted marrow and bone give them a poorly perfused, devitalized place to grow. Infection is often in the diaphysis and can be multifocal. Salmonella is the classic association and in many series the most frequent cause in these patients, but S. aureus still causes a large share. Bone infarction during a pain crisis can look very much like osteomyelitis, which is a common diagnostic problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is an obligate aerobe that thrives in moist environments, so it lives in the wet foam of an athletic shoe and is driven into the foot when a nail passes through the sole. Its grapelike odor and pyocyanin pigment help identify it in culture. Pasteurella multocida is part of normal oral flora in cats and dogs; infection begins as rapidly spreading cellulitis within about 24 hours of the bite, and cat bites, being deep punctures, more often reach bone or tendon sheath."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis is a low-virulence skin organism that becomes a pathogen when foreign material is present. It adheres to plastic and metal and builds a polysaccharide biofilm, so prosthetic joint infections it causes are often low grade, appear months after surgery, and present with pain and loosening rather than high fever. Contiguous spread from a foot ulcer in diabetes, by contrast, is usually polymicrobial."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reasoning from the setting","text":"A 9-year-old with sickle cell disease has fever and a tender, swollen thigh. Start with what is broken in this host. Functional asplenia weakens clearance of bacteria from the blood, gut microinfarcts let enteric gram-negative rods reach the circulation, and infarcted bone gives them dead tissue to colonize. That chain points to Salmonella, a gram-negative, non-lactose-fermenting rod that produces H2S. S. aureus remains possible, because it is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in general. Bone infarction from a pain crisis can mimic infection, so a culture of blood or bone, not imaging alone, separates infarct from osteomyelitis and identifies the organism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C04","obj":"MS-12.4","title":"Clostridium perfringens and gas gangrene","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Clostridium perfringens is a large, gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming rod. Its spores survive for years in soil and in the intestinal tract of humans and animals, so wounds contaminated with soil or feces receive them. Unlike most clostridia it is nonmotile, and spores are seldom seen in clinical specimens. On blood agar it gives a double zone of hemolysis: an inner zone of complete hemolysis from theta toxin and an outer zone of partial hemolysis from alpha toxin. On a Gram stain of wound fluid it appears as broad, boxcar-shaped rods with strikingly few neutrophils."},{"type":"h","text":"Alpha toxin and myonecrosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Germination needs a low oxidation-reduction potential. That exists in deep wounds with crushed or devitalized muscle, retained foreign material, or impaired blood supply, as in battlefield injury, open fractures, or compartment injury. Once growing, the organism secretes alpha toxin, a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that hydrolyzes phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) and sphingomyelin in the outer leaflet of plasma membranes. Muscle fibers, red cells, platelets, and endothelium lose membrane integrity and die. At lower concentrations the toxin activates platelets and leukocytes inside vessels, forming aggregates that occlude small vessels and hold neutrophils back from the tissue, which is why the lesion has so little inflammation. Theta toxin (perfringolysin O), a cholesterol-dependent pore-forming toxin, adds to the damage."},{"type":"p","text":"Dead, ischemic muscle widens the anaerobic zone, so the infection advances along muscle bundles at a rate measured in hours. Fermentation of tissue sugars releases hydrogen and carbon dioxide, which produce crepitus on palpation and gas in tissue planes on imaging. Spreading hemolysis causes hemoglobinuria and jaundice, and released toxin and tissue breakdown products cause shock and renal failure. The skin over the lesion turns pale, then bronze, with hemorrhagic bullae and a thin, foul, brownish discharge, and pain is severe and out of proportion to the visible wound."},{"type":"flow","title":"Gas gangrene","steps":["Deep contaminated wound with devitalized, poorly perfused muscle","Spores germinate in the low-oxygen tissue","Alpha toxin hydrolyzes membrane lecithin","Muscle, red cells, and endothelium die","Necrosis enlarges the anaerobic zone; spread accelerates","Fermentation gas gives crepitus; hemolysis and shock follow"],"caption":"Tissue destruction creates the conditions for further growth, so spread is self-reinforcing."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are commonly tested. C. perfringens food poisoning is a different disease, caused by an enterotoxin released when large numbers of vegetative cells, ingested in slowly cooled or reheated meat, sporulate in the gut, and it causes self-limited watery diarrhea. Spontaneous (nontraumatic) gas gangrene without a wound suggests Clostridium septicum, which is associated with colon cancer and neutropenia because organisms enter the blood through damaged bowel mucosa."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the Gram stain of fluid from a crepitant, rapidly spreading thigh wound in gas gangrene: many neutrophils or few?","explain":"Few. Alpha toxin lyses neutrophils that arrive and, at lower concentrations, makes platelets and leukocytes aggregate in vessels, so neutrophils do not reach the tissue in normal numbers.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on alpha toxin and myonecrosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C05","obj":"MS-12.5","title":"Streptococcus pyogenes, necrotizing fasciitis, and toxic shock","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus pyogenes (group A streptococcus) is a gram-positive coccus in chains. It is catalase negative, beta-hemolytic (complete hemolysis from streptolysins O and S), sensitive to bacitracin, and PYR positive. Its hyaluronic acid capsule resembles host connective tissue and is poorly immunogenic, which adds to its ability to evade phagocytes."},{"type":"h","text":"Resisting phagocytosis and spreading through tissue"},{"type":"p","text":"M protein is a coiled-coil fibrillar protein that projects from the surface. It binds host complement regulators such as factor H and C4b-binding protein and binds fibrinogen, so C3b deposition is limited and the bacterium is not opsonized. Antibody against the specific M type restores phagocytosis, which is why immunity is type specific and repeated infections occur. Hyaluronidase digests hyaluronic acid in connective tissue ground substance, streptokinase converts plasminogen to plasmin to dissolve fibrin that would wall off infection, and DNases thin pus by cleaving DNA from dead neutrophils. Together these let infection travel along the superficial fascia, the plane between subcutaneous fat and deep muscle fascia."},{"type":"p","text":"In necrotizing fasciitis this spread outpaces the skin findings. Bacteria and toxins thrombose the small perforating vessels that pass through the fascia to the skin, so the skin first looks only mildly red while pain is severe and out of proportion. Later the skin becomes dusky and blistered and then numb as cutaneous nerves die. Group A streptococcus alone causes monomicrobial (type II) disease, whereas type I disease is polymicrobial, with anaerobes, and more often has gas."},{"type":"h","text":"Superantigens and toxic shock"},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins (for example SpeA and SpeC) are superantigens. A conventional antigen is processed into a peptide that sits in the groove of an MHC class II molecule and activates only the rare T cells whose receptors fit it, a tiny fraction of all T cells. A superantigen is not processed; it clamps the outside of MHC class II to the variable region of the T cell receptor beta chain, activating every T cell with that beta chain family, up to about 20 percent of all T cells. The resulting cytokine flood produces shock."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a superantigen causes toxic shock","steps":["Pyrogenic exotoxin released from infected tissue","Toxin binds MHC class II outside the peptide groove","Same toxin binds T cell receptor beta chain","Up to about 20 percent of T cells activate","Massive IL-2, TNF, IFN-gamma, and IL-1 release","Fever, capillary leak, hypotension, multiorgan failure"],"caption":"Bypassing antigen processing converts a local infection into a systemic cytokine storm."},{"type":"table","title":"Streptococcal and staphylococcal toxic shock compared","cols":["Feature","Streptococcal toxic shock","Staphylococcal toxic shock"],"rows":[["Toxin","Pyrogenic exotoxins (SpeA, SpeC)","Toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 (TSST-1), enterotoxins"],["Usual source","Deep soft tissue infection such as necrotizing fasciitis","Tampon, nasal packing, wound with colonization"],["Blood cultures","Often positive","Usually negative"],["Diffuse rash with later desquamation","Less common","Typical"]],"caption":"Both are superantigen diseases; the streptococcal form usually has an invasive tissue focus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C06","obj":"MS-12.6","title":"Borrelia burgdorferi and Lyme arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Borrelia burgdorferi is a spirochete, a long, thin, helical bacterium that moves by periplasmic flagella lying between the inner and outer membranes. It stains poorly with the Gram stain, grows slowly in special liquid medium, and is rarely cultured in practice. In the United States it is carried by Ixodes scapularis (the black-legged or deer tick) in the Northeast, mid-Atlantic, and upper Midwest, and by Ixodes pacificus on the Pacific coast."},{"type":"h","text":"The transmission cycle"},{"type":"p","text":"Borrelia is rarely passed through tick eggs, so larvae hatch uninfected. They acquire the organism during their single blood meal on an infected white-footed mouse, the main reservoir, and keep it when they molt into nymphs. Nymphs feed the following late spring and summer; they are about the size of a poppy seed, often go unnoticed, and are the stage that transmits most human infections. Adult ticks feed and mate on white-tailed deer. Deer sustain the tick population but are not a competent reservoir for the bacterium."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Ixodes life cycle and Borrelia transmission","steps":["Eggs hatch into uninfected larvae","Larvae feed on infected white-footed mice","Infected larvae molt into nymphs","Nymphs feed on mice or humans, transmitting Borrelia","Nymphs molt into adults that feed on deer","Adult females lay eggs"],"caption":"About two years; mice infect larvae, nymphs infect people, and deer feed adults."},{"type":"p","text":"Transmission is delayed because the spirochetes start in the tick midgut, where they express outer surface protein A (OspA) to bind the gut wall. The blood meal triggers multiplication and a switch to OspC, and the organisms migrate to the salivary glands before entering the skin. For this reason risk is low if the tick is removed within about 36 hours of attachment. In the skin the organisms spread outward, producing the expanding erythema migrans rash, then disseminate through the blood to joints, heart, and nervous system. Antigenic variation of the surface lipoprotein VlsE helps them persist."},{"type":"table","title":"Stages of Lyme disease","cols":["Stage","Timing after bite","Main findings"],"rows":[["Early localized","Days to about a month","Erythema migrans, flulike symptoms"],["Early disseminated","Weeks to months","Multiple erythema migrans, facial nerve palsy (can be bilateral), lymphocytic meningitis, carditis with AV block, migratory joint pain"],["Late","Months to years","Intermittent or persistent arthritis of one or a few large joints, especially the knee"]],"caption":"Untreated patients may skip stages; late arthritis can be the first recognized sign."},{"type":"p","text":"Late Lyme arthritis follows invasion of the synovium. Spirochetal lipoproteins activate Toll-like receptor 2 on synovial cells and macrophages, and the adaptive response adds T cells and antibody, giving synovial hypertrophy and attacks of large effusions that are often less painful than their size suggests. Synovial fluid is inflammatory, commonly about 10,000 to 25,000 cells per microliter with neutrophil predominance. Spirochete DNA can be detected in joint fluid by PCR, and patients have high IgG antibody levels. In a minority, inflammation persists after the organism is eliminated (antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis), linked to certain HLA-DR alleles and to autoimmune responses in the synovium."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: timing of transmission","text":"A hiker in Connecticut removes an engorged nymphal tick and estimates it had been attached for 2 days. Reason through the tick first. Spirochetes sit in the midgut bound by OspA and must multiply, switch to OspC, and migrate to the salivary glands after feeding starts, a process that takes about 36 hours or more. Engorgement shows feeding long enough for that migration, so the risk of transmission is real. A flat nymph removed within a day carries a much lower risk. If the bacterium was transmitted, expect an expanding erythema migrans lesion within about 3 to 30 days, and, without treatment, possible arthritis of the knee months later."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-12-01-C07","obj":"MS-12.7","title":"Tuberculosis of the spine (Pott disease)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a slow-growing, obligately aerobic rod whose cell wall is rich in mycolic acids. These long-chain lipids resist decolorization by acid alcohol (acid fast on Ziehl-Neelsen stain) and protect the organism inside macrophages. Sulfatides help block phagosome-lysosome fusion, and cord factor (trehalose dimycolate) stimulates TNF release and granuloma formation. The spine is the most common site of skeletal tuberculosis, accounting for about half of cases."},{"type":"h","text":"Hematogenous seeding and granuloma formation"},{"type":"p","text":"During primary pulmonary infection, bacilli carried by macrophages enter lymph and blood and seed distant organs before cell-mediated immunity develops. The vertebral bodies are favored because they hold red marrow with a rich arterial supply, and the valveless paravertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus) offers a second route. The lower thoracic and upper lumbar spine are most often affected. The seeded focus may stay latent for years and reactivate when immunity wanes, and the chest radiograph often shows no active lung disease by then."},{"type":"p","text":"Dendritic cells present mycobacterial antigens to CD4 T cells, and IL-12 drives a Th1 response. Interferon-gamma activates macrophages, which become epithelioid cells and fuse into Langhans giant cells, and TNF holds the granuloma together. The center undergoes caseous necrosis, a cheesy, structureless necrosis that destroys the trabecular bone."},{"type":"flow","title":"From seeding to deformity in Pott disease","steps":["Bacilli seed the anterior vertebral body near the endplate","Caseating granulomas destroy trabecular bone","Infection spreads beneath the anterior longitudinal ligament and across the disc","Adjacent vertebrae and disc space collapse","Anterior wedge collapse with intact posterior elements gives a gibbus","Caseous material tracks down the psoas sheath"],"caption":"Destruction centers on the anterior column, so the spine angles forward."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the posterior elements usually survive, the collapsing anterior column tips the spine forward into a sharp angular kyphosis, the gibbus deformity. Retropulsed bone, granulation tissue, or abscess can compress the spinal cord and cause paraparesis (Pott paraplegia). The abscess is a cold abscess, lacking the heat and redness of pyogenic pus. The psoas major arises from the lumbar vertebral bodies and discs (T12 to L5), so caseous material from the lower thoracic and lumbar spine can track down its sheath, presenting as a groin mass or pain on hip extension."},{"type":"table","title":"Tuberculous versus pyogenic vertebral infection","cols":["Feature","Tuberculous (Pott disease)","Pyogenic (S. aureus)"],"rows":[["Course","Months; night sweats, weight loss, low fever","Days to weeks; often high fever"],["Disc","Relatively preserved early; destroyed later","Destroyed early"],["Extent","Several contiguous levels, skip lesions possible","Usually one disc and the two adjacent vertebrae"],["Paraspinal abscess","Large, calcified, cold, may track along psoas","Smaller, inflamed"],["Deformity","Gibbus common","Less common"],["Diagnosis","Biopsy: caseating granulomas, acid-fast bacilli, culture or PCR","Blood or biopsy culture"]],"caption":"The slow course and anterior spread under the longitudinal ligament distinguish tuberculous spondylitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does vertebral tuberculosis produce a sharp angular kyphosis rather than a smooth curve?","explain":"Destruction is concentrated in the anterior bodies of a few adjacent vertebrae while the posterior elements remain, so the spine hinges forward at one short segment.","check":"Compare with the flow figure and the paragraph on the gibbus deformity."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology explain where these organisms settle. Slow flow in metaphyseal sinusoids, the rich arterial and venous supply of vertebral bodies, infarcted bone in sickle cell disease, and the fascial planes of the limbs each create a site where a particular organism can grow. Immunology explains why some hosts fail: protein A and M protein defeat opsonization by different routes, terminal complement deficiency allows disseminated Neisseria, functional asplenia allows Salmonella, and the Th1 granuloma both contains and destroys tissue in tuberculosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows the virulence factor. Enzymes and toxins (lecithinase, hyaluronidase, streptokinase) cause rapid, spreading necrosis; superantigens cause shock through T cell activation rather than direct tissue damage; and biofilm and granulomas cause chronic infection that persists around dead bone or implants. The settings in this chapter are probabilities, not rules. S. aureus remains common in nearly every group, cultures can be negative in gonococcal and Lyme arthritis, and sterile inflammation, as in bone infarction or antibiotic-refractory Lyme arthritis, can mimic infection."}],"summary":["S. aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis and septic arthritis because matrix adhesins attach it to bone, protein A binds IgG Fc to block opsonization, and biofilm protects it on dead bone and hardware.","In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis seeds the slow-flowing metaphyseal sinusoids, leading to sequestrum and involucrum.","N. gonorrhoeae evades immunity by pilus antigenic variation and IgA1 protease; terminal complement deficiency predisposes to disseminated infection with tenosynovitis, pustules, and polyarthralgia or purulent monoarthritis.","Settings predict exceptions to S. aureus: Salmonella in sickle cell disease, Pseudomonas after a sneaker puncture or injection drug use, Pasteurella after animal bites, S. epidermidis on prostheses, and M. tuberculosis in the spine.","C. perfringens alpha toxin is a lecithinase that destroys cell membranes, causing myonecrosis, hemolysis, and gas gangrene with few neutrophils.","S. pyogenes M protein blocks complement opsonization; hyaluronidase and streptokinase let infection spread along fascia; pyrogenic exotoxins are superantigens that cause toxic shock.","Ixodes larvae acquire Borrelia from white-footed mice, nymphs transmit it to people after about 36 hours or more of attachment, and deer feed adult ticks.","Late Lyme arthritis is an intermittent large-joint arthritis, most often of the knee, months after infection.","Pott disease is hematogenous tuberculosis of the anterior vertebral bodies, with caseating granulomas, collapse into a gibbus, and cold psoas abscess."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Streptococci, clostridia, mycobacteria, and spirochetes (sections on S. pyogenes, C. perfringens, M. tuberculosis, and Borrelia)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (sections on osteomyelitis and infectious arthritis)"},{"book":"Abbas Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes (sections on extracellular and intracellular bacteria and superantigens)"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious Diseases"}]},"MS-13-A":{"unit":"MS-13-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Nutritional Deficiencies: Osteomalacia, Rickets, and Scurvy","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the three classic nutritional diseases of the skeleton. Osteomalacia and rickets are one defect, failed mineralization of osteoid, seen after and before growth plate closure. Scurvy is the opposite kind of defect: mineral handling is normal, but the collagen matrix itself is unstable because vitamin C is missing. Board questions usually give a lab panel, a radiograph description, or a child with bowed legs or painful limbs and ask you to name the defect, the missing step, and the reason each finding appears. If you can explain why phosphate, not calcium, usually limits mineralization, and why unhydroxylated collagen falls apart at body temperature, the findings follow.","prerequisites":["Vitamin D activation and the regulation of 1-alpha-hydroxylase (MS-8)","Hormonal control of serum calcium and phosphate by PTH, calcitriol, and FGF23 (MS-8)","Growth plate zones and endochondral ossification (MS-2)","Collagen synthesis steps, hydroxylases, and lysyl oxidase cross-linking (MS-6)","Osteoblast and osteoclast function in bone remodeling (MS-2)"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal biochemistry and nutrition (MS-8)","how":"The activation pathway and the PTH and FGF23 loops taught there explain every laboratory value in vitamin D deficiency and X-linked hypophosphatemia."},{"to":"Connective tissue and collagen (MS-6)","how":"Scurvy is a defect at the intracellular hydroxylation step of collagen synthesis, the pathway in which osteogenesis imperfecta and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome arise at other steps."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal malabsorption (GI units)","how":"Celiac disease, cystic fibrosis, cholestasis, and bariatric surgery reduce absorption of fat-soluble vitamin D through loss of micelles and chylomicrons."},{"to":"Renal physiology and chronic kidney disease","how":"The proximal tubule makes calcitriol and reabsorbs phosphate, so tubular disease and loss of renal mass cause mineralization defects."},{"to":"Hematology and hemostasis","how":"Scurvy bleeds from fragile vessels with normal platelets and coagulation tests, and it impairs nonheme iron absorption."}],"terms":[{"term":"Osteoid","def":"The unmineralized organic matrix of bone, mostly type I collagen, laid down by osteoblasts before hydroxyapatite is deposited in it."},{"term":"Mineralization","def":"Deposition of calcium phosphate as hydroxyapatite crystals within osteoid or calcified cartilage, which requires an adequate local supply of calcium and phosphate."},{"term":"Osteomalacia","def":"Defective mineralization of newly formed osteoid at remodeling sites in the adult skeleton, producing soft bone with wide osteoid seams."},{"term":"Rickets","def":"Defective mineralization of the growing skeleton, affecting both the growth plate cartilage and newly formed osteoid, before the plates close."},{"term":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism","def":"A raised PTH that is an appropriate response to a low or falling serum calcium, rather than a primary parathyroid disorder."},{"term":"25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol)","def":"The main circulating vitamin D metabolite made in the liver, whose serum level reflects body vitamin D stores."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormone made by renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which raises intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption."},{"term":"FGF23","def":"Fibroblast growth factor 23, an osteocyte hormone that causes phosphaturia and lowers calcitriol production."},{"term":"Looser zone","def":"A pseudofracture: a narrow lucent band running across part of the cortex, filled with unmineralized osteoid, typical of osteomalacia."},{"term":"Prolyl hydroxylase","def":"An iron-containing enzyme of the endoplasmic reticulum that converts proline in procollagen to hydroxyproline and needs ascorbate to keep its iron reduced."},{"term":"Perifollicular hemorrhage","def":"Small hemorrhages around hair follicles, mainly on the legs, caused by fragile capillaries in scurvy."},{"term":"Subperiosteal hemorrhage","def":"Bleeding beneath the periosteum, which lifts it from the bone; a painful feature of scurvy in children."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C01","obj":"MS-13-A.1","title":"How vitamin D deficiency and phosphate loss stop osteoid from mineralizing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone forms in two steps. Osteoblasts first secrete osteoid, a collagen-rich matrix, and mineral is then deposited in it as hydroxyapatite over the following days to weeks. Mineralization needs an adequate product of calcium and phosphate in the extracellular fluid, matrix vesicles from osteoblasts, and tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase, which destroys pyrophosphate, a natural inhibitor of crystal growth. When the calcium phosphate product is too low, osteoblasts keep making osteoid but it stays soft."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D deficiency lowers that product mainly through phosphate. Without calcitriol, the gut absorbs less calcium and phosphate. The small fall in ionized calcium raises PTH, which restores calcium by increasing bone resorption, distal tubular calcium reabsorption, and 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity. PTH also removes phosphate transporters from the proximal tubule, so phosphate is lost in the urine. The result is a near-normal calcium and a low phosphate, and the low phosphate is what fails mineralization. Primary phosphate loss through the kidney, as with excess FGF23 or proximal tubular injury, reaches the same end without vitamin D deficiency."},{"type":"flow","title":"From vitamin D deficiency to unmineralized osteoid","steps":["Low 25-hydroxyvitamin D limits calcitriol","Gut absorbs less calcium and phosphate","Ionized calcium falls slightly; PTH rises","PTH restores calcium from bone and kidney","PTH causes phosphaturia; serum phosphate falls","Calcium phosphate product too low to mineralize","Osteoid accumulates in wide unmineralized seams"],"caption":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism protects serum calcium at the cost of phosphate, which is why phosphate is usually the limiting ion."},{"type":"p","text":"The skeletal result depends on whether growth plates are open. In a child, the defect involves both the growth plate and new bone. Without enough phosphate, hypertrophic chondrocytes fail to undergo apoptosis, the cartilage is not mineralized or invaded by vessels, and the plate becomes thick and disorganized (MS-2); the metaphyseal bone formed beneath it is also poorly mineralized. This is rickets. After the plates close, only remodeling sites are affected: each osteon or trabecular packet refilled by osteoblasts stays partly unmineralized. This is osteomalacia. The amount of matrix is normal or increased, but the matrix is soft, so bones bend and develop insufficiency fractures."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of defective mineralization grouped by mechanism","cols":["Mechanism","Examples","Key distinguishing point"],"rows":[["Too little vitamin D","Low sun exposure, low intake, fat malabsorption","Low 25-hydroxyvitamin D, high PTH"],["Failed activation or action","Severe liver disease; CKD; inherited 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) or vitamin D receptor defects","Receptor defects give high calcitriol, often with alopecia"],["Renal phosphate loss","X-linked hypophosphatemia, tumor-induced osteomalacia (FGF23), Fanconi syndrome","Normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D, high urine phosphate"],["Calcium deficiency","Very low dietary calcium in some regions","25-hydroxyvitamin D may be normal"],["Failure to clear inhibitor","Hypophosphatasia (ALPL loss of function)","Low alkaline phosphatase, the reverse of other causes"]],"caption":"Every cause either lowers the calcium phosphate product at bone or leaves pyrophosphate uncleared."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with severe vitamin D deficiency has a normal serum calcium. Why can bone still fail to mineralize?","explain":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism keeps calcium normal by drawing on bone and kidney, but PTH also causes phosphaturia. Serum phosphate falls, so the calcium phosphate product at the osteoid is too low even though calcium looks normal.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on why phosphate is the limiting ion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C02","obj":"MS-13-A.2","title":"Risk factors for vitamin D deficiency and the step each one blocks","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each risk factor acts at one point in the supply and activation pathway (MS-8): skin synthesis, intake and absorption, hepatic 25-hydroxylation, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation, or catabolism. Placing a risk factor on that pathway predicts what the laboratory panel will show."},{"type":"p","text":"Skin synthesis supplies most vitamin D for many people, so anything that reduces ultraviolet B (UVB) reaching the epidermis matters. High latitude in winter, indoor living, covering clothing, sunscreen, and older age (less 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin) all reduce production. Melanin absorbs UVB, so darker skin needs considerably longer exposure to make the same amount, and people with darker skin living at high latitude carry a high risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Infants depend on stores transferred across the placenta and then on diet. Human milk contains little vitamin D, so an exclusively breastfed infant who is not supplemented and has little sun exposure, especially one born to a vitamin D deficient mother, is the classic rickets case. The risk is greatest in infants with darker skin."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D is fat soluble. It is absorbed in bile acid micelles and leaves the enterocyte in chylomicrons, so any cause of fat malabsorption, such as cholestasis, pancreatic insufficiency, celiac disease, Crohn disease of the ileum, or bariatric surgery, causes deficiency. Liver disease reduces 25-hydroxylation only when severe, and cholestatic disease acts mainly by reducing bile flow and absorption. Kidney disease reduces calcitriol by loss of proximal tubule mass, and nephrotic syndrome adds urinary loss of 25-hydroxyvitamin D bound to its binding protein."},{"type":"p","text":"Some anticonvulsants, notably phenytoin, phenobarbital, and carbamazepine, induce hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes, chiefly CYP3A4, through the pregnane X receptor. These enzymes convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D and calcitriol to inactive polar metabolites, so stores fall despite normal intake. Rifampin acts the same way, and glucocorticoids oppose calcitriol action on intestinal calcium absorption. Obesity lowers serum levels because the vitamin is sequestered in adipose tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors placed on the vitamin D pathway","cols":["Risk factor","Step affected","Mechanism"],"rows":[["High latitude, winter, indoor life, sunscreen","Skin synthesis","Less UVB reaches 7-dehydrocholesterol"],["Darker skin","Skin synthesis","Melanin absorbs UVB"],["Older age","Skin synthesis","Less 7-dehydrocholesterol in epidermis"],["Unsupplemented exclusive breastfeeding","Intake","Human milk is low in vitamin D; maternal stores may be low"],["Fat malabsorption","Absorption","No micelles or chylomicrons for a fat-soluble vitamin"],["Severe liver disease","25-hydroxylation","Less hepatic CYP2R1 activity; cholestasis also cuts absorption"],["Chronic kidney disease","1-alpha-hydroxylation","Loss of proximal tubule mass; high FGF23"],["Nephrotic syndrome","Loss","Urinary loss of binding protein with bound vitamin D"],["Phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine","Catabolism","P450 induction inactivates vitamin D metabolites"],["Obesity","Distribution","Sequestration in adipose tissue"]],"caption":"Kidney disease lowers calcitriol, while the other factors lower 25-hydroxyvitamin D."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C03","obj":"MS-13-A.3","title":"Osteomalacia: bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones, and the laboratory pattern","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteomalacia develops slowly and is often mistaken for osteoporosis or a muscle disease. The findings follow from soft, unmineralized bone and from low phosphate."},{"type":"p","text":"Bone pain is diffuse and aching, worst in the lower back, pelvis, hips, and legs, with tenderness when the sternum, ribs, or tibiae are pressed. The pain is thought to come from unmineralized osteoid that hydrates and expands beneath the richly innervated periosteum. Proximal muscle weakness, often with a waddling gait and difficulty rising from a chair, is partly explained by low phosphate, which limits ATP and creatine phosphate in muscle, and partly by loss of calcitriol action on vitamin D receptors in muscle fibers. Muscle enzymes are usually normal, which helps separate it from an inflammatory myopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Looser zones (pseudofractures) are the characteristic radiographic sign. They are narrow lucent bands, often bilateral and symmetric, running perpendicular to the cortex and partway across the bone, typically in the medial femoral neck, pubic rami, ribs, lateral border of the scapula, and proximal ulna. They are insufficiency fractures that have healed with unmineralized osteoid instead of bone. Bone density is low on densitometry, which cannot distinguish osteomalacia from osteoporosis. Bone biopsy after double tetracycline labeling shows wide osteoid seams and a prolonged mineralization lag time."},{"type":"p","text":"The laboratory pattern in vitamin D deficiency follows directly from C01. 25-hydroxyvitamin D is low, often below about 12 ng/mL (30 nmol/L). PTH is high. Calcium is low or low normal because PTH defends it, and urine calcium is low. Phosphate is low from phosphaturia and poor absorption. Alkaline phosphatase is high because osteoblasts are active, laying down osteoid that does not mineralize. Calcitriol is often normal or even high, since PTH drives 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which is why it is not a test of vitamin D status."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory patterns in adult bone disease","cols":["Test (approximate adult range)","Vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia","FGF23-mediated osteomalacia","Hypophosphatasia","Osteoporosis"],"rows":[["Calcium (8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL)","Low or normal","Normal","Normal or high","Normal"],["Phosphate (2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL)","Low","Low, with high urine phosphate","Normal or high","Normal"],["Alkaline phosphatase (about 40 to 130 U/L)","High","High or normal","Low","Normal"],["PTH (about 10 to 65 pg/mL)","High","Normal or mildly high","Normal or low","Normal"],["25-hydroxyvitamin D (sufficient above about 20 ng/mL)","Low","Normal","Normal","Normal"],["Calcitriol","Normal, high, or low","Low or inappropriately normal","Normal","Normal"]],"caption":"Osteoporosis is less bone of normal composition, so its labs are normal; osteomalacia is matrix that has not mineralized. Reference ranges vary by laboratory."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading an osteomalacia panel","text":"A 70-year-old woman who rarely goes outdoors has hip pain and trouble climbing stairs. Calcium is 8.6 mg/dL, phosphate 2.0 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase 240 U/L, PTH 140 pg/mL, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D 7 ng/mL. Start with the low 25-hydroxyvitamin D: stores are depleted. The high PTH explains why calcium is low normal rather than low, and the PTH-driven phosphaturia explains the low phosphate. The high alkaline phosphatase shows active osteoblasts making osteoid that cannot mineralize. Osteoporosis alone would give normal values throughout, and primary hyperparathyroidism would give a high, not low normal, calcium."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C04","obj":"MS-13-A.4","title":"Rickets: skeletal signs, radiographs, and X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rickets appears where bone grows fastest, so the signs depend on age. In the first year the skull and ribs grow rapidly; once the child walks, the weight-bearing long bones take the strain. Each sign is either an expanded growth plate, accumulated soft osteoid, or soft bone bending under load."},{"type":"table","title":"Rickets findings and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","What is seen","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Craniotabes","Skull bones dent inward under pressure and spring back","Thin, poorly mineralized membranous bone; usually in infants under about 6 months"],["Frontal bossing, wide fontanelle","Square, prominent forehead; delayed fontanelle closure","Excess unmineralized osteoid on the skull vault"],["Rachitic rosary","Smooth, rounded knobs along the costochondral junctions","Thickened, unmineralized growth cartilage at the rib ends"],["Harrison sulcus","Horizontal groove along the lower ribs","Diaphragm pulls inward on soft ribs"],["Wrist and ankle widening","Thick wrists and ankles","Widened metaphyses and growth plates"],["Genu varum (or valgum)","Bowed legs after walking begins","Soft long bones bend under body weight"],["Dental and growth changes","Delayed tooth eruption, enamel defects, short stature","Poor mineralization of teeth and slow linear growth"],["Radiograph","Widened physis; frayed, cupped, splayed metaphyses","Disorganized cartilage and unmineralized osteoid at the metaphysis"]],"caption":"The radiographic changes are clearest at the distal radius and ulna and around the knee, where growth is fastest."},{"type":"p","text":"Some bowing is normal: toddlers have physiologic genu varum until about 18 to 24 months, followed by physiologic valgus. Bowing that persists, worsens, or comes with metaphyseal changes points to rickets. Nutritional rickets also brings the consequences of low calcium and low phosphate: hypotonia, delayed walking, a protuberant abdomen, and, when calcium finally falls, tetany or seizures, especially in young infants."},{"type":"h","text":"X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets"},{"type":"p","text":"X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH) is the most common inherited form of rickets. Loss-of-function mutations in PHEX, an endopeptidase expressed in osteoblasts and osteocytes, raise FGF23 production by a mechanism not fully defined. FGF23 acts on the proximal tubule through FGF receptor 1 and its coreceptor alpha-Klotho. It removes the sodium phosphate cotransporters NaPi-IIa and NaPi-IIc, causing phosphaturia, and it suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase while inducing 24-hydroxylase, so calcitriol is low or inappropriately normal despite low phosphate. The gut therefore cannot compensate."},{"type":"flow","title":"How PHEX loss causes hypophosphatemic rickets","steps":["PHEX loss of function in osteocytes","FGF23 production rises","Proximal tubule loses NaPi-IIa and NaPi-IIc","Phosphaturia; serum phosphate falls","Calcitriol stays low or inappropriately normal","Gut phosphate absorption does not increase","Rickets or osteomalacia without hypocalcemia"],"caption":"Calcium is normal because the primary defect is phosphate wasting, not loss of vitamin D."},{"type":"p","text":"Because calcium stays normal and PTH is normal or only mildly raised, XLH lacks tetany and has little myopathy. The leg bowing, disproportionate short stature, and spontaneous dental abscesses (from defective dentin) dominate, and adults develop painful enthesopathy and osteomalacia. Inheritance is X-linked dominant: an affected father passes it to all daughters and no sons. Autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets, from FGF23 mutations that resist cleavage, and tumor-induced osteomalacia produce the same biochemistry."},{"type":"table","title":"Nutritional rickets versus X-linked hypophosphatemia","cols":["Feature","Nutritional (vitamin D deficiency)","X-linked hypophosphatemia"],"rows":[["Primary defect","Low vitamin D supply","Excess FGF23, renal phosphate wasting"],["Calcium","Low or normal","Normal"],["Phosphate","Low","Low, with high urine phosphate"],["PTH","High","Normal or mildly high"],["25-hydroxyvitamin D","Low","Normal"],["Calcitriol","Variable","Low or inappropriately normal"],["Alkaline phosphatase","High","High"],["Tetany, myopathy","Possible","Absent; dental abscesses instead"],["Family history","Usually none","X-linked dominant pattern"]],"caption":"Normal calcium and 25-hydroxyvitamin D with low phosphate point to a phosphate-wasting disorder."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a child with XLH not develop tetany, although the bones are as soft as in nutritional rickets?","explain":"Tetany comes from low ionized calcium. In XLH the defect is renal phosphate loss driven by FGF23, so calcium absorption and PTH regulation of calcium are intact and calcium stays normal. Mineralization fails only because phosphate is low.","check":"The XLH flow and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C05","obj":"MS-13-A.5","title":"How vitamin C deficiency produces unstable collagen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Humans cannot make ascorbate because the gene for L-gulonolactone oxidase, the last enzyme of the synthetic pathway, is inactive. Vitamin C must therefore come from the diet, mainly fresh fruit and vegetables, and it is easily destroyed by heat and storage. Body stores last only weeks to a few months, so scurvy appears soon after intake stops."},{"type":"p","text":"In the rough endoplasmic reticulum, newly made pro-alpha chains are modified before they fold. Prolyl 4-hydroxylase converts proline in the Y position of the Gly-X-Y repeat to 4-hydroxyproline, prolyl 3-hydroxylase makes a smaller amount of 3-hydroxyproline, and lysyl hydroxylase converts lysine to hydroxylysine. These enzymes use Fe2+, O2, and alpha-ketoglutarate. Their iron is sometimes oxidized to Fe3+ during the reaction cycle, and ascorbate reduces it back to Fe2+. Without ascorbate the enzymes inactivate themselves and hydroxylation fails (MS-6)."},{"type":"p","text":"Hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix by fixing the proline ring in the shape the helix requires and by adding water-mediated hydrogen bonds. Underhydroxylated chains form helices that melt below body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F), so they cannot fold stably. Most are retained in the endoplasmic reticulum and degraded, and secretion of collagen falls. Lack of hydroxylysine reduces glycosylation and the hydroxylysine-derived cross-links formed by lysyl oxidase, so the collagen that is secreted makes weak fibrils."},{"type":"flow","title":"From ascorbate deficiency to fragile tissue","steps":["Ascorbate is absent","Hydroxylase iron stays oxidized; enzymes inactivate","Proline and lysine are underhydroxylated","Triple helix is unstable at body temperature","Chains are degraded; less collagen is secreted","Fewer hydroxylysine cross-links weaken fibrils","Fragile vessels, poor healing, thin osteoid"],"caption":"The defect is quantitative and qualitative: less collagen reaches the matrix, and what reaches it is weak."},{"type":"p","text":"The tissues that fail first are those that depend on continuous collagen synthesis. Capillaries rely on type IV collagen in their basement membrane and on supporting perivascular connective tissue, both of which need hydroxylated collagen, so they rupture under ordinary pressure and bleeding appears in skin, gums, muscle, and joints. Wound healing depends on new type III and then type I collagen; wounds gain little tensile strength, and old scars, whose collagen is remodeled continuously, can break down. In bone, osteoblasts produce too little osteoid, which is the reverse of rickets: there the osteoid is plentiful but unmineralized, while in scurvy mineral is available but there is little matrix to receive it."},{"type":"p","text":"Ascorbate also serves dopamine beta-hydroxylase and two enzymes of carnitine synthesis, which helps explain early fatigue, and it reduces dietary Fe3+ to Fe2+ for absorption, which links scurvy to iron deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-01-C06","obj":"MS-13-A.6","title":"Scurvy: findings, risk groups, and how it differs from a bleeding disorder","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy begins with nonspecific fatigue, malaise, irritability, and aching limbs after about one to three months of very low intake. Specific signs follow as collagen-dependent tissues fail, and nearly every one can be traced to fragile vessels or weak connective tissue."},{"type":"table","title":"Scurvy findings and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","What is seen","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Perifollicular hemorrhage","Red to purple spots around hair follicles, mainly on the legs","Fragile capillaries rupture where hydrostatic pressure is highest"],["Corkscrew hairs","Coiled, fragmented hairs with follicular hyperkeratosis","Abnormal hair keratinization, thought to involve defective disulfide bonding"],["Gum disease","Swollen, spongy, purple gums that bleed; loose teeth","Fragile gingival vessels and weak periodontal collagen; spared where no teeth are present"],["Poor wound healing","Wounds fail to close; old scars may reopen","Too little stable new collagen"],["Ecchymoses, muscle bleeds, hemarthrosis","Large bruises, painful swollen joints","Capillary rupture under ordinary mechanical stress"],["Subperiosteal hemorrhage (children)","Severe limb pain, refusal to move the legs","Bleeding lifts the loosely attached periosteum from bone"],["Anemia","Fatigue, pallor","Blood loss, poor nonheme iron absorption, often coexisting folate deficiency"]],"caption":"Skin, gums, and joints bleed because their vessels lack collagen support, not because clotting fails."},{"type":"p","text":"In a child, scurvy presents with painful legs. The infant lies still with the hips and knees flexed and cries when the legs are handled, a picture called pseudoparalysis. Subperiosteal hemorrhage is painful because it strips the periosteum. Fractures through the weak metaphysis and a scorbutic rosary at the costochondral junctions may also occur; unlike the smooth rachitic rosary, the scorbutic rosary is angular, because the sternum sinks back behind the rib ends, and often tender. Radiographs show thin cortices and a dense line of calcified cartilage at the metaphysis with a lucent band of poor bone beneath it, where fractures occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk follows diet. Adults at risk eat few fruits and vegetables: older adults living alone on a limited diet, people with alcohol use disorder, people with food insecurity or very restrictive eating (including children with autism and selective eating), and people with malabsorption or on dialysis, which removes water-soluble vitamins. Smoking raises the requirement through oxidative turnover. Infant scurvy, usually after about 6 months of age, occurs when formula or breast milk is replaced by heat-treated cow milk or other foods without vitamin C."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on the clinical picture and a low plasma ascorbate, below about 0.2 mg/dL (11 micromol/L). Platelet count, prothrombin time, and partial thromboplastin time are normal. That combination, mucocutaneous and deep bleeding with normal coagulation tests, separates scurvy from thrombocytopenia, vitamin K deficiency, and hemophilia, each of which can also cause hemarthrosis."},{"type":"example","title":"Painful legs in a 14-month-old","text":"A toddler fed mainly boiled cow milk and crackers will not stand, screams when the legs are moved, and has swollen, bleeding gums around erupted teeth. Platelets, PT, and PTT are normal. Rickets is unlikely to explain this picture: it causes bowing and wide wrists but not acute pain on handling, gum bleeding, or subperiosteal hemorrhage. A coagulation defect is excluded by the normal tests. The diet lacks vitamin C, boiling destroys what little milk contains, and fragile vessels bleed beneath the periosteum and in the gums, so scurvy fits every finding."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with alcohol use disorder has a hemarthrosis, perifollicular hemorrhages, and normal PT, PTT, and platelets. Why are the tests normal if he bleeds so easily?","explain":"Those tests measure platelets and clotting factors, which are intact. The defect is in the vessel wall and its supporting collagen, which ruptures under ordinary stress; clotting at the site works normally once bleeding starts.","check":"The mechanism table and the diagnosis paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These three disorders show the two separate requirements for strong bone: a sound collagen matrix and mineral deposited within it. Scurvy fails the first: hydroxylation, a biochemical step inside the endoplasmic reticulum, is lost, so matrix is scarce and weak while mineral supply is normal. Rickets and osteomalacia fail the second: the matrix is normal or abundant, but the calcium phosphate product, set by the gut, kidney, liver, skin, and the PTH and FGF23 loops, is too low. Osteoporosis differs from both, being too little bone of normal composition, which is why its laboratory panel is normal. Pathology, anatomy, and biochemistry meet at the growth plate, where rickets and scurvy produce opposite metaphyseal changes."},{"type":"p","text":"The limits matter for exam reasoning. Serum calcium is defended by PTH and is a late and insensitive marker, so phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, PTH, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D carry the diagnosis. Thresholds for vitamin D sufficiency differ between expert bodies, and children have higher normal alkaline phosphatase and phosphate than adults, so values must be read against age-specific ranges. Scurvy is uncommon in high-income settings but persists in predictable risk groups, and its bleeding with normal coagulation tests is the distinction most often tested."}],"summary":["Mineralization needs an adequate calcium phosphate product and alkaline phosphatase to clear pyrophosphate; osteoblasts keep making osteoid when mineral is lacking.","In vitamin D deficiency, secondary hyperparathyroidism holds calcium near normal but causes phosphaturia, so low phosphate is what usually stops mineralization.","The same defect causes rickets when growth plates are open and osteomalacia after they close.","Risk factors act at skin synthesis, intake and absorption, hepatic or renal hydroxylation, or catabolism; inducing anticonvulsants speed inactivation of vitamin D metabolites.","Osteomalacia: diffuse bone pain, proximal weakness, Looser zones, low phosphate, high alkaline phosphatase and PTH, low 25-hydroxyvitamin D, calcium low or normal.","Rickets: craniotabes, frontal bossing, rachitic rosary, wide wrists, bowed legs, and widened, cupped, frayed metaphyses.","X-linked hypophosphatemia: PHEX loss raises FGF23, causing phosphaturia and low calcitriol with normal calcium and normal 25-hydroxyvitamin D.","Vitamin C keeps prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase iron reduced; without it collagen helices are unstable, less collagen is secreted, and cross-linking is weak.","Scurvy: perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, bleeding gums, poor healing, hemarthrosis, and subperiosteal hemorrhage in children, with normal platelets, PT, and PTT.","Rickets has plentiful unmineralized osteoid; scurvy has too little osteoid with normal mineral supply."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases, sections on vitamin D and vitamin C"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on rickets and osteomalacia"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid glands, vitamin D, and phosphate regulation"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins, collagen synthesis and scurvy"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the parathyroids and calcium and phosphorus metabolism"}]},"MS-13-B":{"unit":"MS-13-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02","title":"Inflammatory Musculoskeletal Diseases","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the inflammatory, crystal, and overuse disorders of joints, entheses, bursae, tendons, and muscle: the HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies, gout and pseudogout, the inflammatory myopathies, polymyalgia rheumatica, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, fibromyalgia, and the common soft tissue lesions. Board questions usually turn on a small number of discriminating findings: which joints are involved, whether the enthesis or the synovium is the target, crystal shape and birefringence, whether CK and strength are abnormal, and which infection or metabolic state came first. If you can explain the tissue being attacked and why, each pattern of findings follows.","prerequisites":["Synovial joint structure, articular cartilage, and the enthesis (MS-4, MS-5)","Skeletal muscle fiber types and the significance of creatine kinase","MHC class I and II antigen presentation, complement, and T cell subsets","Purine synthesis, salvage, and degradation","Upper and lower limb anatomy of the shoulder, elbow, wrist, and heel"],"connections":[{"to":"Rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis (MS-13 joint chapters)","how":"Spondyloarthropathies and crystal arthritis are defined largely by how they differ from erosive synovial RA and degenerative OA."},{"to":"Immunology (IM)","how":"HLA-B27, the IL-23 and IL-17 axis, complement-mediated vascular injury, and the NLRP3 inflammasome explain the main diseases here."},{"to":"Biochemistry and renal physiology (UR)","how":"Serum urate depends on purine turnover, HGPRT salvage, and proximal tubular urate transport."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and pulmonary systems (CV, PU)","how":"Aortitis with aortic regurgitation, giant cell arteritis, and restrictive or interstitial lung disease are the main extra-articular threats."},{"to":"Microbiology (MS pathogens)","how":"Chlamydia and enteric pathogens trigger reactive arthritis, and Staphylococcus aureus causes septic bursitis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Enthesis","def":"The site where a tendon, ligament, or joint capsule inserts into bone, the primary target of the spondyloarthropathies."},{"term":"Seronegative spondyloarthropathy","def":"An inflammatory arthritis of the spine and entheses in which rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies are absent, often associated with HLA-B27."},{"term":"Syndesmophyte","def":"A thin vertical bony bridge formed by ossification of the outer annulus fibrosus and adjacent ligament, joining neighboring vertebral bodies."},{"term":"Sacroiliitis","def":"Inflammation of the sacroiliac joints, seen on imaging as erosion, sclerosis, and eventually fusion."},{"term":"Birefringence","def":"The property of a crystal to split polarized light; its sign (positive or negative) and color under a compensator identify urate versus calcium pyrophosphate."},{"term":"Tophus","def":"A deposit of urate crystals surrounded by macrophages and foreign body giant cells in soft tissue, cartilage, or bone."},{"term":"NLRP3 inflammasome","def":"A cytoplasmic protein complex in macrophages that activates caspase-1 to release IL-1 beta when it senses crystals or other danger signals."},{"term":"Perifascicular atrophy","def":"Atrophy of muscle fibers at the edge of each fascicle, the biopsy hallmark of dermatomyositis caused by capillary loss."},{"term":"Central sensitization","def":"Increased responsiveness of central pain pathways so that normal input is felt as pain (allodynia) and painful input is amplified (hyperalgesia)."},{"term":"Tendinosis","def":"Chronic degenerative tendinopathy with disorganized collagen and new vessels but few inflammatory cells."},{"term":"Dactylitis","def":"Diffuse swelling of an entire finger or toe (sausage digit) from tenosynovitis, synovitis, and soft tissue edema together."},{"term":"Chondrocalcinosis","def":"Radiographic calcification of hyaline cartilage or fibrocartilage, most often from calcium pyrophosphate deposition."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C01","obj":"MS-13-B.1","title":"Ankylosing spondylitis: enthesitis, sacroiliitis, and spinal fusion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ankylosing spondylitis (AS) is the prototype seronegative spondyloarthropathy: a chronic inflammatory disease of the axial skeleton that begins at the sacroiliac joints and the entheses of the spine. About 90% of patients of European descent carry HLA-B27, an MHC class I allele, yet only a small fraction of B27 carriers ever develop AS, so the gene confers susceptibility rather than disease. Onset is usually in the late teens to the 30s, and men are affected about two to three times as often as women and tend to have more severe radiographic progression."},{"type":"p","text":"The exact role of HLA-B27 is unsettled. Leading explanations are presentation of arthritogenic microbial or self peptides to CD8 T cells, misfolding of the B27 heavy chain that triggers an unfolded protein response, and abnormal B27 heavy chain dimers recognized by innate receptors. These converge on the IL-23 and IL-17 axis and on TNF, which drive inflammation at entheses, where mechanical stress concentrates."},{"type":"p","text":"The key distinction from rheumatoid arthritis is what follows inflammation. In RA, pannus erodes bone and the joint is destroyed with periarticular bone loss. In AS, an early erosive phase at the enthesis is followed by repair that overshoots: fibrous tissue replaces the inflamed attachment and then ossifies. At the vertebral corners this produces syndesmophytes, thin vertical bridges that grow from the outer annulus fibrosus to the next vertebra. When many levels fuse, the radiograph shows a bamboo spine, and the sacroiliac joints become eroded, sclerotic, and finally fused, usually bilaterally."},{"type":"flow","title":"From enthesitis to bamboo spine","steps":["HLA-B27 susceptibility plus mechanical stress at entheses","IL-23, IL-17, and TNF drive enthesitis and sacroiliitis","Bone erodes at ligament and annulus insertions","Repair lays down fibrous tissue that ossifies","Syndesmophytes bridge adjacent vertebral bodies","Fused, rigid, osteoporotic bamboo spine"],"caption":"Inflammation, erosion, and then excessive new bone formation, which is why AS fuses joints while RA destroys them."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammatory back pain explains itself through this biology. Pain and stiffness arise from inflamed entheses that stiffen with rest and loosen with movement, so symptoms are worst in the morning and in the second half of the night, last more than 30 minutes on rising, and improve with exercise but not with rest. Mechanical back pain behaves the opposite way. As the costovertebral and costotransverse joints are involved, chest expansion falls (under about 2.5 cm at the fourth intercostal space), and lumbar flexion is lost, measured by the modified Schober test. Advanced disease produces loss of lumbar lordosis and fixed thoracic kyphosis. Hips and shoulders are the joints outside the spine most often involved."},{"type":"table","title":"Inflammatory versus mechanical back pain","cols":["Feature","Inflammatory (AS)","Mechanical"],"rows":[["Age at onset","Usually under 40","Any age, often older"],["Onset","Insidious, over more than 3 months","Often sudden, after strain"],["Morning stiffness","More than 30 minutes","Brief"],["Exercise","Improves pain","Worsens pain"],["Rest","Does not help; night pain","Relieves pain"],["Inflammatory markers","ESR and CRP may be raised","Normal"]],"caption":"Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP are negative in AS, which is the meaning of seronegative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C02","obj":"MS-13-B.2","title":"Extra-articular complications of ankylosing spondylitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The inflammation of AS is not confined to the spine. Sites outside the skeleton that share fibrous attachments, collagen-rich walls, or the same immune milieu are also involved."},{"type":"p","text":"Acute anterior uveitis (iritis) is the most common extra-articular feature, occurring in about a third of patients over their lifetime. It is typically unilateral, recurrent, and may alternate between eyes, presenting with a painful red eye, photophobia, and blurred vision. Inflammation of the iris and ciliary body releases cells and protein into the anterior chamber; untreated attacks can leave posterior synechiae (adhesions of iris to lens), cataract, or glaucoma. Painful acute uveitis in AS contrasts with the painless chronic uveitis of oligoarticular JIA (C14)."},{"type":"p","text":"Aortitis affects the aortic root. Inflammation centered on the adventitia and vasa vasorum causes intimal proliferation and fibrosis that dilates the root and thickens and shortens the aortic cusps, so the valve fails to coapt and aortic regurgitation results: a diastolic decrescendo murmur and wide pulse pressure. Fibrosis can extend below the valve into the membranous septum and the base of the anterior mitral leaflet, injuring the AV node or His bundle and producing heart block."},{"type":"p","text":"Lung function becomes restrictive mainly from the chest wall. Fusion of the costovertebral joints and thoracic kyphosis fix the rib cage, so vital capacity and total lung capacity fall while the FEV1 to FVC ratio stays normal or high and diffusing capacity is preserved; the diaphragm takes over most ventilation. A smaller number develop apical fibrobullous disease of the upper lobes."},{"type":"p","text":"Finally, the fused spine is both rigid and osteoporotic, because inflammation and immobility reduce bone mass inside the vertebral bodies even as bone forms at their margins. A rigid column behaves like a long bone, so minor trauma such as a fall from standing can fracture it, most often in the lower cervical spine. These fractures pass through the whole fused segment, are unstable, and carry a high risk of spinal cord injury. Chronic inflammation can also cause secondary (AA) amyloidosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Extra-articular disease in AS explained by mechanism","cols":["Complication","Tissue injured","Mechanism","Finding"],"rows":[["Anterior uveitis","Iris and ciliary body","HLA-B27 associated inflammation","Unilateral painful red eye, photophobia"],["Aortic regurgitation","Aortic root and cusps","Adventitial and vasa vasorum inflammation, fibrosis","Diastolic decrescendo murmur, wide pulse pressure"],["Heart block","Membranous septum, AV node, His bundle","Fibrosis extending below the valve","Prolonged PR to complete block"],["Restrictive lung disease","Costovertebral joints, upper lobes","Fixed rib cage; apical fibrosis","Low VC and TLC, normal FEV1/FVC"],["Spinal fracture","Fused osteoporotic spine","Rigid column cannot absorb force","Lower cervical fracture after minor trauma"]],"caption":"Each complication follows from the tissue involved."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 45-year-old man with long-standing AS falls from standing and has neck pain but a normal neurological examination. Why should this be treated as a possible unstable fracture?","explain":"The fused, osteoporotic spine acts as a single rigid lever. Force that a mobile spine would absorb across many discs concentrates at one level and fractures through the entire column, often in the lower cervical spine, so the fracture is unstable and the cord can be injured later.","check":"The paragraph on vertebral fractures and the bone changes in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C03","obj":"MS-13-B.3","title":"Bursitis by site and cause","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A bursa is a closed sac lined by synovium-like cells and containing a thin film of fluid that lets tendon, muscle, or skin glide over bone. Bursae are either subcutaneous (between skin and a bony prominence, such as the prepatellar and olecranon bursae) or deep (beneath tendons, such as the subacromial and trochanteric bursae). Bursitis is inflammation of this lining, which then secretes excess fluid and swells. The location of a bursa predicts its cause: superficial bursae suffer pressure and skin breaks, and deep bursae suffer friction from overused tendons."},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated pressure or friction produces aseptic bursitis. Prepatellar bursitis follows prolonged kneeling and gives a fluctuant swelling in front of the patella with a preserved knee range of motion, because the joint itself is not involved. Olecranon bursitis follows leaning on the elbow, direct trauma, or gout and rheumatoid arthritis, and presents as a soft swelling over the tip of the elbow. Subacromial (subdeltoid) bursitis accompanies supraspinatus impingement under the acromion (C11). Trochanteric bursitis causes lateral hip pain worse when lying on that side or climbing stairs, often together with gluteus medius and minimus tendinopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Septic bursitis almost always involves the superficial prepatellar or olecranon bursae, because a small skin break lets organisms in directly. Staphylococcus aureus causes most cases, followed by streptococci. The bursa is warm, red, and very tender, often with cellulitis around it, and fever may be absent. Aspirated fluid separates the causes: septic fluid has a high white cell count with neutrophils and organisms on Gram stain or culture, gouty bursitis shows urate crystals, and traumatic bursitis is often bloody."},{"type":"table","title":"Common bursitis sites","cols":["Bursa","Location","Usual cause","Key finding"],"rows":[["Prepatellar","Between skin and patella","Repeated kneeling; skin break if septic","Swelling in front of patella, knee motion preserved"],["Olecranon","Over the olecranon process","Leaning, trauma, gout; skin break if septic","Soft swelling at the elbow tip"],["Subacromial (subdeltoid)","Between acromion and supraspinatus tendon","Overhead overuse, impingement","Painful arc on abduction"],["Trochanteric","Over the greater trochanter","Overuse, gait abnormality","Lateral hip pain lying on the side"]],"caption":"Superficial bursae are the ones that become septic; deep bursae are usually inflamed by overuse."},{"type":"table","title":"Septic versus aseptic bursitis","cols":["Feature","Septic","Aseptic"],"rows":[["Usual organism or cause","Staphylococcus aureus after a skin break","Pressure, friction, crystals"],["Skin","Warm, red, often cellulitis","Little warmth or redness"],["Pain","Marked tenderness","Mild"],["Aspirate","High neutrophil count, Gram stain or culture positive","Low count; crystals in gout; blood after trauma"]],"caption":"Warmth and erythema over a superficial bursa raise suspicion of infection, but aspiration decides."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C04","obj":"MS-13-B.4","title":"Fibromyalgia: a disorder of central pain processing","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibromyalgia is chronic widespread pain without damage or inflammation in the painful tissues. The abnormality lies in how the nervous system processes pain. It affects about 2 to 4% of adults, more often women, and usually begins in middle adulthood. Because the muscles and joints are structurally normal, ESR, CRP, CK, and muscle biopsy are normal, and the absence of objective inflammation is part of the picture rather than evidence against it."},{"type":"p","text":"The mechanism is central sensitization. Repeated input to dorsal horn neurons raises their excitability through glutamate acting on NMDA receptors (the basis of temporal summation, or wind-up), so the same stimulus produces a growing response. Descending inhibitory pathways from the brainstem, which use serotonin and norepinephrine to damp dorsal horn transmission, are less effective. Cerebrospinal fluid substance P is raised, and functional imaging shows that a given pressure activates pain-processing regions at lower intensity than in controls. The results are allodynia, in which light touch or pressure hurts, and hyperalgesia, in which painful stimuli hurt more than they should."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Self-sustaining loop of central sensitization","steps":["Ongoing input or stress activates dorsal horn neurons","Glutamate and NMDA receptors raise neuronal excitability","Descending serotonin and norepinephrine inhibition is weak","Normal input is felt as widespread pain","Pain fragments sleep and raises stress","Poor sleep and stress lower pain thresholds further"],"caption":"Sleep disturbance and pain reinforce each other, which is why both are central to the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Clinically, pain is widespread (both sides of the body, above and below the waist, and axial), present for at least 3 months, and accompanied by fatigue, unrefreshing sleep, and cognitive difficulty often called fibro fog. Many patients also have irritable bowel syndrome, migraine or tension headache, temporomandibular disorder, depression, or anxiety, conditions that share altered central processing. Older criteria required tenderness at 11 of 18 tender points; current criteria score the extent of pain and the severity of symptoms instead."},{"type":"p","text":"The diagnostic reasoning relies on what is normal. Strength is normal, distinguishing it from myositis. CK is normal, excluding muscle necrosis. ESR and CRP are normal, separating it from polymyalgia rheumatica, which has girdle-limited pain in older adults with high inflammatory markers. Hypothyroidism, which can cause diffuse aches and fatigue, is excluded with thyroid function tests."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C05","obj":"MS-13-B.5","title":"Hyperuricemia: urate production and excretion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Uric acid is the end product of purine degradation in humans, because humans lack uricase, the enzyme that converts urate to the far more soluble allantoin in most other mammals. At physiological pH, uric acid exists mostly as the urate anion, which saturates plasma as monosodium urate at about 6.8 mg/dL (about 400 micromol/L) at 37°C (98.6°F). Above this level, called hyperuricemia, crystals can form, and solubility falls further in cooler tissue such as the toes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Purine degradation to uric acid","steps":["AMP is deaminated to IMP, then inosine","Inosine becomes hypoxanthine","GMP becomes guanosine, then guanine","Guanine is deaminated to xanthine","Xanthine oxidase: hypoxanthine to xanthine","Xanthine oxidase: xanthine to uric acid"],"caption":"Xanthine oxidase catalyzes both final steps. HGPRT normally salvages hypoxanthine and guanine back to IMP and GMP before they are degraded."},{"type":"p","text":"Serum urate reflects a balance between production and excretion. About two thirds of daily urate is excreted by the kidney and one third by the gut. Urate is freely filtered, then mostly reabsorbed in the proximal tubule by URAT1 and GLUT9 while a smaller amount is secreted by organic anion transporters, leaving fractional excretion near 10%. URAT1 exchanges filtered urate for organic anions from inside the cell, so anything that loads those anions increases urate reabsorption, and volume contraction raises proximal reabsorption generally."},{"type":"p","text":"Underexcretion causes about 90% of gout. Chronic kidney disease lowers filtration. Thiazide and loop diuretics cause volume contraction that enhances proximal reabsorption and compete with urate for tubular secretion. Lactate from alcohol and ketoacids from fasting are exchanged for urate through URAT1, and low-dose aspirin reduces urate secretion. Lead nephropathy and cyclosporine also reduce excretion."},{"type":"p","text":"Overproduction accounts for the rest. In tumor lysis, the nucleic acids of dying cells are degraded to urate, and high cell turnover in myeloproliferative disorders and severe psoriasis does the same more slowly. In Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, an X-linked recessive deficiency of hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT), the salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine fails, so they are degraded instead, and the PRPP they would have consumed accumulates and stimulates PRPP amidotransferase, the committed step of de novo purine synthesis. Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency (von Gierke disease) raises urate both by overproduction and by underexcretion."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of hyperuricemia","cols":["Category","Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Underexcretion","Chronic kidney disease","Reduced filtration of urate"],["Underexcretion","Thiazide and loop diuretics","Volume contraction increases proximal reabsorption; compete for secretion"],["Underexcretion","Alcohol, ketoacidosis","Lactate and ketoacids exchanged for urate via URAT1"],["Underexcretion","Low-dose aspirin","Reduces tubular urate secretion"],["Overproduction","Tumor lysis, myeloproliferative disease","Degradation of nucleic acids from dying cells"],["Overproduction","Lesch-Nyhan (HGPRT deficiency)","Salvage fails; excess PRPP drives de novo synthesis"],["Both","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","PRPP excess, ATP breakdown, lactate competing for excretion"]],"caption":"Underexcretion is far more common than overproduction."},{"type":"example","title":"Why glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency raises urate in three ways","text":"Without glucose-6-phosphatase, the liver cannot release free glucose, so glucose-6-phosphate accumulates. First, excess glucose-6-phosphate enters the pentose phosphate pathway, producing ribose-5-phosphate and therefore PRPP, which drives de novo purine synthesis. Second, phosphate trapped in sugar phosphates lowers free hepatic phosphate, so ATP cannot be regenerated; ADP and AMP accumulate and AMP is degraded through IMP and hypoxanthine to urate. Third, fasting hypoglycemia drives lactic acidosis, and lactate is exchanged for urate in the proximal tubule, reducing excretion. Two routes increase production and one reduces excretion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C06","obj":"MS-13-B.6","title":"Gout: clinical, crystal, and radiographic findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most people with hyperuricemia never develop gout; crystals must form in a joint and be recognized by the innate immune system. Gout typically progresses from asymptomatic hyperuricemia, through acute attacks separated by symptom-free intercritical periods, to chronic tophaceous gout. It affects men after puberty and women mainly after menopause, because estrogen increases renal urate excretion."},{"type":"p","text":"An acute attack follows a rise, or any abrupt change, in serum urate that causes crystals to form or to shed from deposits on cartilage. Alcohol (especially beer, which also contains purines), meals rich in organ meat or shellfish, dehydration, surgery, and acute illness are typical triggers. Serum urate may be normal during the attack itself, so a normal level does not exclude gout. The first metatarsophalangeal joint is the site of about half of first attacks (podagra), because it is cool and repeatedly stressed, both of which favor crystal formation. The attack starts suddenly, often overnight, and the joint becomes hot, red, swollen, and too painful to touch, peaking within a day."},{"type":"flow","title":"How urate crystals cause an acute attack","steps":["Urate crystals form or shed into the joint","Macrophages phagocytose crystals","NLRP3 inflammasome activates caspase-1","Caspase-1 cleaves pro-IL-1 beta to active IL-1 beta","Endothelial activation recruits neutrophils","Neutrophils release mediators: hot, red, swollen joint"],"caption":"Gout is an autoinflammatory response driven by the innate immune system, not by autoantibodies."},{"type":"p","text":"Synovial fluid analysis is definitive. Urate crystals are needle-shaped and strongly negatively birefringent under compensated polarized light: they appear yellow when their long axis lies parallel to the slow axis of the compensator and blue when perpendicular. The fluid is inflammatory, with white cell counts commonly between about 2,000 and 100,000 per microliter, mostly neutrophils that often contain crystals."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic disease produces tophi: chalky deposits of urate surrounded by macrophages and foreign body giant cells, a granulomatous reaction, in the helix of the ear, olecranon bursa, Achilles tendon, and around finger joints. Urate dissolves in water-based formalin, so tissue for crystal identification is fixed in alcohol. Tophi in bone produce punched-out erosions with sclerotic borders and overhanging edges, while joint space is preserved until late, unlike RA. In the kidney, uric acid stones form because uric acid (pKa about 5.5) is poorly soluble in acidic urine; they are radiolucent on plain film. Urate deposits in the medullary interstitium cause chronic urate nephropathy, and massive urate release in tumor lysis precipitates in tubules and causes acute kidney injury."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in gout and their basis","cols":["Finding","Basis"],"rows":[["Podagra","Cool, stressed first MTP joint lowers urate solubility"],["Needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals","Monosodium urate crystal optics"],["Tophi","Urate masses walled by macrophages and giant cells"],["Punched-out erosions with overhanging edges","Tophi eroding bone with reactive new bone at the margin"],["Radiolucent uric acid stones","Uric acid precipitates in acidic urine"]],"caption":"Each finding follows from urate chemistry or the tissue reaction to crystals."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C07","obj":"MS-13-B.7","title":"Dermatomyositis and polymyositis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The idiopathic inflammatory myopathies share symmetric proximal muscle weakness, raised CK, and myofiber necrosis, but they differ in what the immune system attacks. Weakness of the shoulder and hip girdles shows up as difficulty climbing stairs, rising from a chair, or lifting the arms to comb hair. Involvement of the striated muscle of the pharynx and upper esophagus causes dysphagia and aspiration, and myocarditis can occur. Distal strength, sensation, and reflexes are preserved early, which separates myopathy from neuropathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Dermatomyositis is a vasculopathy. Type I interferon signaling and deposition of the complement membrane attack complex (C5b-9) injure endomysial capillaries, which drop out. The fibers at the periphery of each fascicle sit at the watershed of the remaining blood supply, so they become ischemic and atrophic, producing perifascicular atrophy. Inflammatory cells, mainly CD4 T cells, B cells, and plasmacytoid dendritic cells, gather around vessels and in the perimysium rather than invading fibers. The same vascular injury affects skin: a heliotrope rash (violet discoloration of the upper eyelids, often with periorbital edema), Gottron papules (violaceous scaly papules over the knuckles), and photodistributed erythema of the upper chest (V sign) and upper back (shawl sign). Dermatomyositis also occurs in children, often with calcinosis."},{"type":"p","text":"In adults, dermatomyositis is associated with internal cancer, most often ovarian, lung, gastrointestinal, and pancreatic carcinoma, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma in Southeast Asian populations. Risk is highest around the time of diagnosis, consistent with a paraneoplastic immune response to antigens shared by tumor and regenerating muscle. Antibodies to TIF1-gamma mark the highest risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Polymyositis is a direct cytotoxic attack. Muscle fibers abnormally express MHC class I, and CD8 T cells in the endomysium surround and invade non-necrotic fibers and kill them with perforin and granzymes. There is no rash, and the cancer association is weaker. Polymyositis is now diagnosed less often, because many cases once given this name turn out to be inclusion body myositis or necrotizing myopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Antibodies to aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, of which anti-Jo-1 (anti-histidyl tRNA synthetase) is the most common, define the antisynthetase syndrome in either disease: myositis with interstitial lung disease, Raynaud phenomenon, nonerosive arthritis, fever, and mechanic's hands (cracked, hyperkeratotic skin on the radial fingers). Interstitial lung disease is a leading cause of death in these patients."},{"type":"table","title":"Dermatomyositis versus polymyositis","cols":["Feature","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis"],"rows":[["Primary target","Endomysial capillaries","Muscle fibers"],["Effector","Complement (C5b-9), type I interferon","CD8 T cells, MHC class I on fibers"],["Inflammation location","Perivascular and perimysial","Endomysial, invading fibers"],["Biopsy hallmark","Perifascicular atrophy, capillary loss","CD8 T cells invading non-necrotic fibers"],["Skin","Heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, V and shawl signs","None"],["Cancer association","Strong in adults","Weaker"],["Children","Juvenile form occurs","Rare"]],"caption":"Both have proximal weakness, high CK, and possible anti-Jo-1 interstitial lung disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C08","obj":"MS-13-B.8","title":"Biopsy and laboratory findings that separate myositis, polymyalgia rheumatica, and steroid myopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Patients with inflammatory myositis, polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR), and glucocorticoid (steroid) myopathy all complain of trouble with the shoulders and hips, so the work-up must separate true weakness from pain-limited movement, and muscle destruction from muscle atrophy. Three questions do most of the work: Is strength actually reduced? Is CK raised? Is there inflammation in the muscle?"},{"type":"p","text":"CK is a cytoplasmic enzyme of skeletal muscle that leaks into blood only when the sarcolemma is breached. In inflammatory myositis, fibers are necrotic, so CK is raised, often to 10 or more times the upper limit of normal, along with aldolase, AST, ALT, and LDH. Electromyography shows a myopathic pattern with spontaneous fibrillation from irritable fibers, and MRI shows muscle edema that can guide biopsy. Biopsy shows inflammatory infiltrates, necrotic and regenerating fibers, and the pattern specific to the subtype (C07). CK can be normal in dermatomyositis limited to the skin or when muscle is already atrophic late in disease."},{"type":"p","text":"In PMR, the inflammation is in bursae, tendon sheaths, and synovium around the girdle joints, not in muscle (C09). Strength is normal when pain is accounted for, CK is normal, muscle biopsy is normal, and ESR and CRP are high."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucocorticoids cause a myopathy by catabolism rather than inflammation. They reduce muscle protein synthesis by inhibiting IGF-1 and mTOR signaling and increase protein breakdown through the ubiquitin-proteasome system. Fast glycolytic type II fibers are most affected. The result is painless proximal weakness, worse in the legs, with normal CK because fibers shrink rather than die, and a biopsy showing type II fiber atrophy without inflammation or necrosis. Endogenous excess in Cushing syndrome does the same."},{"type":"table","title":"Separating the three disorders","cols":["Test","Inflammatory myositis","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Steroid myopathy"],"rows":[["Strength","Reduced, proximal","Normal (pain limits effort)","Reduced, proximal, legs more"],["Pain","Mild or absent","Prominent, with morning stiffness","Absent"],["CK and aldolase","High","Normal","Normal"],["ESR and CRP","Normal or high","High","Not raised by the myopathy"],["EMG","Myopathic with fibrillations","Normal","Mildly myopathic or normal"],["Biopsy","Infiltrates, necrosis, regeneration","Normal muscle","Type II fiber atrophy, no inflammation"]],"caption":"High CK means fibers are dying; normal CK with weakness points to atrophy, and normal CK with normal strength points away from muscle."},{"type":"example","title":"A myositis patient who gets weaker on glucocorticoids","text":"A woman with polymyositis improved over two months on high-dose prednisone, and her CK fell from about 30 times normal to normal. Now her hip weakness is worse and she cannot rise from a chair, yet she has no muscle pain. Two explanations compete: a relapse of myositis or steroid myopathy. A relapse would destroy fibers again, so CK would rise and MRI would show new muscle edema. Her CK is still normal and there is no edema, which fits fiber atrophy without necrosis. The most likely cause is steroid myopathy; a biopsy, if done, would show type II fiber atrophy without inflammatory infiltrates."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C09","obj":"MS-13-B.9","title":"Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polymyalgia rheumatica is an inflammatory disorder of adults over 50, most often in their 70s, women about twice as often as men, and most common in people of northern European descent. It presents with bilateral aching and stiffness of the shoulders, neck, and hip girdle, worst in the morning and after rest, often lasting more than 45 minutes. Patients struggle to turn over in bed, get up, or raise their arms. Fatigue, low-grade fever, weight loss, and malaise reflect systemic inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Despite the name, the muscle itself is not inflamed. Ultrasound and MRI show subacromial and subdeltoid bursitis, biceps tenosynovitis, glenohumeral synovitis, and trochanteric bursitis. That explains the laboratory picture: ESR is typically above 40 mm/h and CRP and IL-6 are raised, often with a normocytic anemia of inflammation, but CK and muscle biopsy are normal. Strength is normal when pain is controlled, which is the key difference from myositis (C08)."},{"type":"p","text":"PMR and giant cell arteritis (GCA) are best viewed as parts of one disease spectrum. About 15 to 20% of people with PMR have GCA, and about 40 to 50% of people with GCA have PMR symptoms. GCA is a granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries, especially branches of the external carotid such as the superficial temporal artery, plus the ophthalmic artery and the aorta. Dendritic cells in the adventitia activate CD4 T cells that produce IFN-gamma (Th1) and IL-17 (Th17); macrophages and multinucleated giant cells fragment the internal elastic lamina, and the intima thickens until the lumen narrows. The patchy distribution (skip lesions) means a short biopsy can be falsely negative."},{"type":"flow","title":"Giant cell arteritis to vision loss","steps":["Granulomatous inflammation of the ophthalmic artery branches","Intimal hyperplasia narrows the posterior ciliary arteries","Optic nerve head becomes ischemic","Transient monocular blurring (amaurosis fugax) may warn","Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Sudden painless, usually permanent, monocular vision loss"],"caption":"The second eye can be affected within days, which is why visual symptoms in GCA are an emergency."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms of GCA follow from narrowed arteries: new headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication from ischemic masseters during chewing, and visual disturbance. Large vessel disease can cause arm claudication, unequal arm blood pressures, and late thoracic aortic aneurysm. In a patient with PMR, any of these symptoms means GCA must be considered urgently."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 72-year-old with shoulder and hip stiffness, ESR 85 mm/h, and normal CK develops pain in the jaw when chewing. What has changed, and what is at risk?","explain":"Jaw claudication means the masseter is ischemic during use, which points to giant cell arteritis narrowing branches of the external carotid. The same process in the posterior ciliary arteries can infarct the optic nerve head and cause permanent vision loss.","check":"The GCA paragraph and the vision loss flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C10","obj":"MS-13-B.10","title":"Reactive arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile inflammatory arthritis that appears about 1 to 4 weeks after an infection elsewhere in the body. The triggers are genitourinary infection with Chlamydia trachomatis or enteric infection with Shigella (especially S. flexneri), Salmonella, Yersinia, or Campylobacter jejuni. It is a spondyloarthropathy: it targets entheses, joints of the lower limb, the eye, and mucous membranes, and it is associated with HLA-B27, which also predicts more severe and chronic disease, although many patients are B27 negative."},{"type":"p","text":"The joint is sterile, so synovial fluid cultures are negative, but it is not free of bacterial material. Chlamydia can persist in synovium in a viable but nonculturable form, and enteric bacteria deliver lipopolysaccharide and other antigens to the joint inside phagocytes. A T cell response to these persisting antigens, shaped by HLA-B27 and possibly by molecular mimicry between bacterial and self peptides, drives inflammation. This is why the arthritis follows the infection rather than accompanying it, and why culture of the joint fails."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic triad is arthritis, conjunctivitis, and urethritis, although all three occur together in only a minority. The arthritis is an asymmetric oligoarthritis of large weight-bearing joints such as the knees and ankles, often with dactylitis and enthesitis at the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia, and sometimes asymmetric sacroiliitis. Conjunctivitis is usually early and sterile; anterior uveitis can occur later. Urethritis or cervicitis can be sterile and can follow an enteric trigger. Mucocutaneous lesions include keratoderma blennorrhagicum, hyperkeratotic pustular plaques on the soles and palms that resemble pustular psoriasis histologically, circinate balanitis, and painless oral ulcers. Most episodes resolve within about 3 to 12 months, but a proportion become recurrent or chronic."},{"type":"table","title":"Reactive arthritis compared with disseminated gonococcal infection","cols":["Feature","Reactive arthritis","Disseminated gonococcal infection"],"rows":[["Timing","1 to 4 weeks after infection","During active infection"],["Joint fluid","Sterile, culture negative","Often culture positive (septic)"],["Joints","Asymmetric knees and ankles, enthesitis","Migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, or monoarthritis"],["Skin","Keratoderma blennorrhagicum on soles and palms","Few pustules on the limbs"],["Eye","Conjunctivitis, anterior uveitis","Usually none"],["HLA-B27","Increases risk and severity","No association"]],"caption":"Both follow a sexually transmitted infection, but only gonococcal arthritis contains live organisms in the joint."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C11","obj":"MS-13-B.11","title":"Tendinopathy: tendinosis, epicondylitis, and supraspinatus impingement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A tendon is made of tightly packed, parallel type I collagen fibrils produced by sparse tenocytes, with little ground substance and a poor blood supply. This structure resists tension well but heals slowly. Tendinopathy is the general term for pain and impaired function in an overused tendon. Despite the old label tendinitis, chronic tendinopathy is mainly degenerative and is better called tendinosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Repeated loading creates microtears faster than tenocytes can repair them. The failed healing response leaves disorganized, thinner collagen with more type III collagen, increased proteoglycan and water (mucoid degeneration), rounded and abnormal tenocytes, and ingrowth of small vessels accompanied by nerve fibers. Inflammatory cells are scarce in established disease. The new nerve fibers and local mediators such as glutamate and substance P likely explain the pain, and the weakened matrix explains the risk of rupture."},{"type":"p","text":"Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow) affects the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle, mainly the extensor carpi radialis brevis. Repetitive gripping and wrist extension load this origin, so pain is reproduced by resisted wrist extension and by gripping. Medial epicondylitis (golfer's elbow) affects the common flexor-pronator origin on the medial epicondyle, mainly pronator teres and flexor carpi radialis, and pain is reproduced by resisted wrist flexion and forearm pronation; the ulnar nerve lies just behind the medial epicondyle and can be irritated."},{"type":"p","text":"Supraspinatus impingement is explained by anatomy. The supraspinatus tendon passes through the subacromial space, between the humeral head and the coracoacromial arch (acromion, coracoacromial ligament, and coracoid process), with the subacromial bursa above it. Near its insertion on the greater tubercle is a relatively hypovascular zone. During abduction from about 60 to 120 degrees, the greater tubercle and tendon pass under the arch and are compressed, producing a painful arc; below and above that range the tendon is clear of the arch. Overhead activity, a hooked acromion, and acromioclavicular osteophytes narrow the space. Chronic impingement can progress to a rotator cuff tear, which weakens active abduction while passive motion remains full."},{"type":"table","title":"Tendinopathies of the elbow and shoulder","cols":["Disorder","Structure","Provoking test","Typical activity"],"rows":[["Lateral epicondylitis","Extensor carpi radialis brevis origin","Resisted wrist extension, gripping","Racquet sports, repetitive gripping"],["Medial epicondylitis","Common flexor-pronator origin","Resisted wrist flexion and pronation","Golf, throwing"],["Supraspinatus impingement","Supraspinatus tendon under the acromion","Painful arc about 60 to 120 degrees","Overhead work and sports"]],"caption":"Pain arises where the loaded tendon attaches to bone or passes under a bony arch."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C12","obj":"MS-13-B.12","title":"De Quervain tenosynovitis, plantar fasciitis, and Achilles tendinopathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"De Quervain tenosynovitis affects the first dorsal compartment of the wrist, where the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons share a fibro-osseous tunnel over the radial styloid. Repetitive thumb abduction and wrist ulnar deviation, typical of lifting an infant, cause the retinaculum and tendon sheath to thicken, narrowing the tunnel so the tendons glide painfully. Histology shows thickening and myxoid change more than acute inflammation. It is most common in women aged 30 to 50 and in late pregnancy and the postpartum period. Pain and tenderness lie over the radial styloid, and the Finkelstein test, with the thumb flexed into the palm and the wrist deviated toward the ulna, stretches both tendons through the narrowed compartment and reproduces the pain."},{"type":"p","text":"Plantar fasciitis is degeneration of the plantar fascia (aponeurosis) at its origin on the medial tubercle of the calcaneus. The fascia supports the longitudinal arch, and dorsiflexion of the toes during push-off winds it tight (the windlass mechanism), so repetitive loading stresses its origin. Overnight the foot rests in plantar flexion and the fascia shortens, so the first steps in the morning stretch it suddenly and cause sharp medial heel pain that eases as the tissue warms up. Risk factors include obesity, prolonged standing, running, flat or high arches, and a tight Achilles tendon that limits ankle dorsiflexion. A calcaneal spur is often present but is an association, not the cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Achilles tendinopathy most often affects the midportion of the tendon, about 2 to 6 cm above its insertion, where blood supply is poorest, causing pain and fusiform thickening; insertional disease occurs at the calcaneus. Rupture produces a sudden pop, as if kicked in the heel, with a palpable gap and loss of plantar flexion when the calf is squeezed (Thompson test). Fluoroquinolones raise the risk of tendinopathy and rupture. They are toxic to tenocytes, increase matrix metalloproteinase activity, generate oxidative stress, and reduce collagen synthesis. Risk is higher over age 60, with concurrent systemic glucocorticoids, with kidney disease, and after organ transplantation, and it can appear within days of exposure and in both tendons. Achilles enthesitis is also a feature of the spondyloarthropathies (C10)."},{"type":"table","title":"Wrist and foot soft tissue disorders","cols":["Disorder","Structure","Key finding","Risk"],"rows":[["De Quervain tenosynovitis","Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis in the first dorsal compartment","Pain over radial styloid, positive Finkelstein test","Repetitive thumb use, pregnancy, new parents"],["Plantar fasciitis","Plantar fascia at medial calcaneal tubercle","Heel pain with the first morning steps","Obesity, standing, running, tight Achilles"],["Achilles tendinopathy","Midportion or insertion of the Achilles tendon","Pain and thickening; rupture with positive Thompson test","Running, age, fluoroquinolones, glucocorticoids"]],"caption":"All three are degenerative overuse lesions at sites of high mechanical load."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C13","obj":"MS-13-B.13","title":"Psoriatic arthritis versus rheumatoid arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Psoriatic arthritis develops in roughly a quarter to a third of people with psoriasis. Skin disease usually comes first, but in about 15% the arthritis appears before the rash, so the nails, scalp, umbilicus, and gluteal cleft should be examined in any unexplained inflammatory arthritis. Like the other spondyloarthropathies, it is seronegative and centers on the enthesis, and it shares the IL-23 and IL-17 pathway with psoriatic skin. HLA-Cw6 is linked mainly to the skin disease, whereas HLA-B27 is linked to axial (spinal and sacroiliac) involvement."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinctive features follow from enthesitis. The distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint lies next to the nail, and the extensor tendon enthesis at the DIP joint is anatomically continuous with the nail bed. Inflammation of this nail and enthesis unit explains why DIP arthritis travels with nail pitting and onycholysis. Dactylitis, a sausage-shaped digit, arises when flexor tenosynovitis, synovitis, and soft tissue edema involve the whole finger or toe at once. Patterns vary: DIP-predominant arthritis, asymmetric oligoarthritis, symmetric polyarthritis that resembles RA, axial disease, and the rare destructive arthritis mutilans."},{"type":"p","text":"Radiographs show both erosion and new bone, the combination that marks spondyloarthropathy. Erosion of the head of a phalanx tapers it to a point, which sits in a widened, cupped base of the adjacent phalanx, the pencil-in-cup deformity. Fluffy periosteal new bone forms next to the joint, and periarticular bone density is usually preserved. Rheumatoid arthritis, by contrast, is a symmetric synovitis of the MCP, PIP, and wrist joints that spares the DIP joints, is associated with HLA-DR4 and with rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies, and produces marginal erosions with periarticular osteopenia and no new bone."},{"type":"table","title":"Psoriatic arthritis versus rheumatoid arthritis","cols":["Feature","Psoriatic arthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"rows":[["Primary target","Enthesis, then synovium","Synovium"],["Distribution","Often asymmetric; DIP involved","Symmetric MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP spared"],["Digit","Dactylitis (sausage digit)","Fusiform PIP swelling, ulnar deviation, swan neck"],["Nails and skin","Pitting, onycholysis, psoriatic plaques","Rheumatoid nodules"],["Spine","Sacroiliitis and spondylitis, HLA-B27 linked","Cervical (atlantoaxial) involvement"],["Serology","RF and anti-CCP usually negative","RF and anti-CCP positive in most"],["Radiograph","Pencil-in-cup, new bone, preserved density","Marginal erosions, periarticular osteopenia"],["HLA","HLA-Cw6 (skin), HLA-B27 (axial)","HLA-DR4 (shared epitope)"]],"caption":"DIP involvement with nail disease and new bone formation points to psoriatic arthritis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient has swelling of the DIP joints of two fingers, pitting of those nails, and no rash. Why does nail pitting make psoriatic arthritis more likely than osteoarthritis or RA?","explain":"The DIP extensor enthesis is continuous with the nail bed, so enthesitis at the DIP joint also disturbs the nail matrix. RA spares the DIP joint, and the Heberden nodes of osteoarthritis are bony enlargements without nail disease.","check":"The nail and enthesis paragraph and the comparison table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C14","obj":"MS-13-B.14","title":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) is arthritis that begins before the 16th birthday, lasts at least 6 weeks, and has no other identified cause such as infection, malignancy, or trauma. It is a group of diseases rather than one, classified by the number of joints involved in the first 6 months and by systemic and serological features. Arthritis in a growing child has consequences that adult disease does not: inflammation increases blood flow to the nearby growth plate, so an affected knee can grow faster and produce a leg length difference, while long-standing disease can close growth plates early."},{"type":"p","text":"Oligoarticular JIA, involving four or fewer joints, is the most common form, about half of cases. It typically affects girls aged about 1 to 5 years and involves large joints such as a knee or ankle, often with little pain. Antinuclear antibody (ANA) is positive in most. Its most dangerous feature is chronic anterior uveitis, which is silent: unlike the acute painful uveitis of AS, it causes no pain, redness, or photophobia, and a young child does not report gradual blurring. Undetected, it causes posterior synechiae, band keratopathy, cataract, glaucoma, and permanent vision loss. ANA positivity and young age at onset identify the children at highest risk, and because there are no symptoms, only slit-lamp examination detects it."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic JIA, about 10% of cases, behaves as an autoinflammatory disease driven by the innate cytokines IL-1 and IL-6 rather than by autoantibodies, so ANA and RF are usually negative. It presents with a quotidian fever that spikes once or twice daily to 39°C (102.2°F) or higher and returns to normal or below between spikes, for at least 2 weeks. An evanescent salmon-pink macular rash appears with the fever spikes. Lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and serositis are common, and arthritis may appear only later. Laboratory tests show leukocytosis, thrombocytosis, anemia, and very high ferritin and inflammatory markers. Its serious complication is macrophage activation syndrome, in which uncontrolled macrophage activation causes falling blood counts, a falling ESR (as fibrinogen is consumed), and extreme ferritin."},{"type":"table","title":"Main JIA subtypes","cols":["Subtype","Joints","Typical patient","Key features"],"rows":[["Oligoarticular","4 or fewer, large joints","Girls aged about 1 to 5","ANA positive, silent chronic anterior uveitis, leg length difference"],["Polyarticular, RF negative","5 or more","Children of any age, girls more","Symmetric large and small joints"],["Polyarticular, RF positive","5 or more, small joints","Adolescent girls","Resembles adult RA, erosive, anti-CCP"],["Systemic","Variable, may start later","Any age, boys and girls equally","Quotidian fever, salmon rash, serositis, high ferritin"],["Enthesitis-related","Lower limb, sacroiliac","Boys over about 6","HLA-B27, enthesitis, acute painful uveitis"]],"caption":"Psoriatic JIA and undifferentiated arthritis complete the classification."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C15","obj":"MS-13-B.15","title":"Pseudogout (calcium pyrophosphate deposition) versus gout","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (CPPD) disease results when calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals form in articular cartilage. Chondrocytes generate extracellular inorganic pyrophosphate (PPi), partly through the ANKH transporter and the enzyme ENPP1, and PPi combines with calcium in the cartilage matrix. Crystals form when the product of calcium and PPi concentrations rises, or when the enzymes that degrade PPi are inhibited. Age is the main risk, so CPPD is common after 60; when it appears in a younger person, a metabolic cause should be sought."},{"type":"table","title":"Metabolic associations of CPPD and their mechanism","cols":["Condition","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High calcium raises the calcium and PPi product"],["Hemochromatosis","Iron inhibits pyrophosphatase and damages cartilage"],["Hypomagnesemia","Magnesium is a pyrophosphatase cofactor and increases crystal solubility"],["Hypophosphatasia","Low alkaline phosphatase leaves PPi unhydrolyzed"]],"caption":"Each condition raises local PPi or calcium in cartilage."},{"type":"p","text":"When crystals shed from cartilage into the joint space, they activate the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1 beta release in the same way as urate, so acute pseudogout looks like gout: a hot, swollen, painful joint, sometimes with fever. The knee is most often involved, followed by the wrist, and attacks commonly follow surgery, trauma, or acute medical illness. The calcified cartilage itself appears on radiographs as chondrocalcinosis, a thin line of calcification within the hyaline cartilage or the fibrocartilage of the knee menisci, the triangular fibrocartilage of the wrist, and the pubic symphysis. CPPD can also produce a chronic degenerative arthropathy in joints that osteoarthritis usually spares, such as the second and third MCP joints in hemochromatosis."},{"type":"p","text":"CPPD crystals are rhomboid or short rods and weakly positively birefringent, appearing blue when their long axis is parallel to the slow axis of the compensator. Urate crystals are needle-shaped and strongly negatively birefringent, appearing yellow in the same orientation."},{"type":"table","title":"Gout versus pseudogout","cols":["Feature","Gout","Pseudogout (CPPD)"],"rows":[["Crystal","Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate"],["Shape","Needle","Rhomboid or rod"],["Birefringence","Strongly negative: yellow when parallel","Weakly positive: blue when parallel"],["Typical joint","First MTP, ankle, knee","Knee, wrist"],["Imaging","Punched-out erosions, tophi","Chondrocalcinosis"],["Typical patient","Middle-aged men, postmenopausal women","Older adults of either sex"],["Associations","Alcohol, diuretics, kidney disease, high cell turnover","Hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypomagnesemia, hypophosphatasia"]],"caption":"Both activate the same inflammasome; the crystal under polarized light settles the diagnosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-02-C16","obj":"MS-13-B.16","title":"Adhesive capsulitis and ganglion cysts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder) is inflammation followed by fibrosis of the glenohumeral joint capsule, especially the rotator interval, coracohumeral ligament, and inferior axillary recess. Early synovial inflammation gives way to proliferation of fibroblasts and myofibroblasts that lay down dense collagen, a process similar to Dupuytren contracture. The capsule thickens, contracts, and adheres, and joint volume falls sharply. It most often affects people aged 40 to 60, women more than men, and runs through a painful freezing phase, a stiff frozen phase, and a slow thawing phase over about 1 to 3 years."},{"type":"p","text":"Diabetes is the strongest risk factor, raising the risk several fold and producing more severe and bilateral disease. Chronic hyperglycemia forms advanced glycation end products that cross-link collagen and stiffen connective tissue, the same mechanism behind limited joint mobility in the hand. Hypothyroidism and prolonged immobilization after injury or surgery are other associations."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the problem is a shrunken capsule, motion is limited mechanically in every direction, and both active and passive range of motion are lost, with external rotation lost first and most. This finding separates it from rotator cuff disease: in a cuff tear, the patient cannot actively lift the arm, but the examiner can move it passively through a full range, and in impingement the range is full with a painful arc. Radiographs are normal, which helps exclude glenohumeral osteoarthritis."},{"type":"p","text":"A ganglion cyst is the most common soft tissue mass of the hand and wrist. It arises from myxoid degeneration of connective tissue next to a joint capsule or tendon sheath, where fibroblasts produce hyaluronic acid rich mucin that collects in pools that coalesce. The wall is compressed collagen with no synovial or epithelial lining, so it is a pseudocyst, often connected to the joint by a stalk that may act as a one-way valve. About two thirds arise on the dorsal wrist over the scapholunate ligament, with others on the volar wrist or the flexor tendon sheaths of the fingers. The mass is firm, smooth, transilluminates, and varies in size with activity; most are painless, though some compress nearby nerves."},{"type":"table","title":"Shoulder motion in three common disorders","cols":["Disorder","Active motion","Passive motion","Key association"],"rows":[["Adhesive capsulitis","Lost in all planes","Lost, external rotation most","Diabetes, immobilization"],["Rotator cuff tear","Weak or lost abduction","Preserved","Age, chronic impingement"],["Supraspinatus impingement","Full but painful from about 60 to 120 degrees","Full","Overhead activity"]],"caption":"Loss of passive motion means the joint capsule itself is restricted."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Most disorders in this chapter are best organized by the tissue under attack. The spondyloarthropathies (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and enthesitis-related JIA) target the enthesis through HLA-B27 associated IL-23 and IL-17 inflammation, so they share sacroiliitis, dactylitis, anterior uveitis, a negative RF, and new bone formation. RA targets synovium and destroys bone. Gout and pseudogout are innate immune responses to crystals through the NLRP3 inflammasome, so their attacks look alike and are separated by chemistry: purine metabolism and renal urate handling for gout, cartilage pyrophosphate and mineral metabolism for CPPD. In the myopathies, the target is capillaries in dermatomyositis and the muscle fiber itself in polymyositis, while PMR inflames periarticular structures and leaves muscle intact."},{"type":"p","text":"Two laboratory values carry much of the reasoning, with limits. CK measures fiber necrosis, not weakness, so it is normal in PMR, steroid myopathy, and fibromyalgia for different reasons, and can be normal in early or skin-limited dermatomyositis. ESR measures systemic inflammation, not its site, and is high in PMR, GCA, and active spondyloarthropathy but normal in fibromyalgia and overuse lesions. Tendinopathy, plantar fasciitis, de Quervain disease, ganglion cysts, and adhesive capsulitis are local degenerative or fibrotic processes, so they produce no systemic signs, but their anatomy (which tendon, which arch, which capsule) predicts the examination findings."}],"summary":["Ankylosing spondylitis is HLA-B27 associated enthesitis and sacroiliitis in young men; repair ossifies entheses into syndesmophytes and a bamboo spine, and back pain improves with exercise.","AS complications follow the tissue involved: painful anterior uveitis, aortic root fibrosis with aortic regurgitation and heart block, chest wall restriction, and unstable fractures of a fused osteoporotic spine.","Superficial bursae (prepatellar, olecranon) suffer pressure and skin breaks and become septic, mostly from Staphylococcus aureus; deep bursae (subacromial, trochanteric) are inflamed by overuse.","Fibromyalgia is central sensitization: widespread pain for at least 3 months with fatigue and unrefreshing sleep, mostly in women, with normal ESR, CRP, CK, and strength.","Hyperuricemia arises mostly from renal underexcretion; overproduction comes from tumor lysis, HGPRT deficiency, and glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency; xanthine oxidase makes urate from hypoxanthine and xanthine.","Gout crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent, trigger IL-1 beta through NLRP3, and cause podagra, tophi, punched-out erosions, and radiolucent uric acid stones.","Dermatomyositis is complement-mediated capillary injury with perifascicular atrophy, skin signs, and cancer risk; polymyositis is endomysial CD8 T cell attack; anti-Jo-1 marks interstitial lung disease.","High CK with weakness means myositis; normal CK with weakness suggests steroid myopathy (type II fiber atrophy); normal CK and strength with high ESR suggests PMR, which travels with giant cell arteritis.","Reactive arthritis is sterile, HLA-B27 associated arthritis with conjunctivitis and urethritis 1 to 4 weeks after Chlamydia or enteric infection; psoriatic arthritis involves DIP joints, nails, and dactylitis with pencil-in-cup deformity.","Pseudogout crystals are rhomboid and weakly positive with chondrocalcinosis; adhesive capsulitis loses active and passive motion; oligoarticular JIA hides silent uveitis and systemic JIA spikes daily fevers."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, section on joints (spondyloarthropathies, gout, CPPD, ganglion cyst)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on inflammatory myopathies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on giant cell arteritis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Nucleotide Metabolism, purine degradation and salvage"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Innate immunity and the inflammasome; MHC molecules and disease associations"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb and Lower Limb, sections on the rotator cuff, elbow, wrist compartments, and plantar fascia"}]},"MS-13-C":{"unit":"MS-13-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-03","title":"Metabolic Bone Disease: Osteopetrosis, Bone Laboratory Patterns, and Renal Osteodystrophy","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers bone diseases that arise when the cells or hormones that control bone turnover fail. Osteopetrosis shows what happens when osteoclasts cannot resorb bone: the skeleton becomes dense yet brittle, crowds out the marrow, and traps the cranial nerves in narrow foramina. The chapter then shows how serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and parathyroid hormone (PTH) separate the common metabolic bone diseases, and closes with renal osteodystrophy, in which chronic kidney disease drives secondary hyperparathyroidism and defective mineralization. Board questions usually give a lab panel, a radiograph description, or an infant with dense bones and cytopenias and ask for the mechanism. A few rules about PTH, calcitriol, and osteoblast activity explain the lab patterns.","prerequisites":["Bone cells, the bone remodeling unit, and endochondral ossification (MS-2)","RANKL, RANK, and osteoprotegerin signaling between osteoblasts and osteoclasts (MS-8)","PTH, calcitriol, FGF23, and calcitonin in calcium and phosphate balance (EN-5, EN-7)","Renal handling of phosphate and acid excretion by the proximal and distal tubule (UR-6, UR-7)","Hematopoiesis in the marrow and its fetal sites in the liver and spleen"],"connections":[{"to":"Nutritional bone disease (MS-13-A)","how":"Osteomalacia and rickets supply the vitamin D deficiency lab pattern and the mineralization defect that also appears in renal osteodystrophy."},{"to":"Degenerative bone disease (MS-13-E)","how":"Osteoporosis and Paget disease are the normal-lab and isolated high alkaline phosphatase patterns used in the lab comparison."},{"to":"Endocrine hyperfunction (EN-12-A)","how":"Primary, secondary, and tertiary hyperparathyroidism share osteitis fibrosa cystica but differ in calcium and phosphate."},{"to":"Glomerular disease and chronic kidney disease (UR-12-A)","how":"Loss of nephrons causes the phosphate retention and calcitriol deficiency that start renal osteodystrophy."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Marrow obliteration in osteopetrosis is a myelophthisic process with extramedullary hematopoiesis, like marrow replacement by fibrosis or tumor."}],"terms":[{"term":"Osteoclast","def":"A multinucleated bone-resorbing cell formed by fusion of monocyte-macrophage precursors under the control of M-CSF and RANKL."},{"term":"Ruffled border","def":"The folded osteoclast membrane facing bone, through which acid and proteases are secreted into the sealed resorption space."},{"term":"Osteopetrosis","def":"A group of inherited disorders in which osteoclasts are absent or cannot resorb bone, producing dense, brittle bone that fills the marrow cavity."},{"term":"Extramedullary hematopoiesis","def":"Blood cell production outside the bone marrow, mainly in the liver and spleen, when the marrow cannot support it."},{"term":"Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)","def":"An enzyme on the osteoblast surface (and in liver and other tissues) whose serum level rises when osteoblasts are active."},{"term":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism","def":"Increased PTH secretion that is an appropriate response to low calcium, high phosphate, or low calcitriol rather than a primary gland disorder."},{"term":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","def":"Autonomous PTH secretion with hypercalcemia from parathyroid glands that were stimulated for years by secondary hyperparathyroidism."},{"term":"FGF23","def":"Fibroblast growth factor 23, an osteocyte hormone that increases renal phosphate excretion and suppresses calcitriol synthesis."},{"term":"Renal osteodystrophy","def":"The bone disease of chronic kidney disease, ranging from high-turnover osteitis fibrosa cystica to low-turnover and mineralization defects."},{"term":"Osteitis fibrosa cystica","def":"PTH-driven bone disease with osteoclastic resorption, marrow fibrosis, cysts, and brown tumors."},{"term":"Brown tumor","def":"A reactive, non-neoplastic mass of osteoclast-like giant cells, fibrous tissue, hemorrhage, and hemosiderin in hyperparathyroidism."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-03-C01","obj":"MS-13-C.1","title":"Osteopetrosis: failure of osteoclast resorption","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is continuously remodeled: osteoclasts remove old bone and osteoblasts replace it. During growth, osteoclasts also carve the marrow cavity out of calcified cartilage, shape the metaphyses, and enlarge the skull foramina. Osteopetrosis (marble bone disease) is a group of inherited disorders in which osteoclasts are absent or cannot resorb bone, so formation continues while removal fails."},{"type":"p","text":"An osteoclast attaches to bone through integrins (mainly alphaVbeta3) and forms a sealing zone that isolates a resorption lacuna. It then acidifies this space to about pH 4.5 to dissolve hydroxyapatite, and secretes cathepsin K to digest the exposed collagen."},{"type":"flow","title":"How an osteoclast dissolves bone","steps":["Sealing zone isolates the resorption lacuna","Carbonic anhydrase II forms H+ and HCO3- from CO2 and water","Basolateral exchanger exports HCO3- and imports Cl-","Ruffled border H+-ATPase (TCIRG1 subunit) pumps H+ into lacuna","ClC-7 (CLCN7) moves Cl- alongside, forming HCl","Acid dissolves hydroxyapatite; cathepsin K digests type I collagen"],"caption":"Every step must work; a defect in the proton supply, the pump, or the chloride channel stops resorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Each step is a point of failure. Loss of the proton pump subunit or of ClC-7 leaves osteoclasts present, often in increased numbers, but unable to acidify the lacuna (osteoclast-rich osteopetrosis). Mutations in RANKL or RANK stop osteoclasts from forming at all (osteoclast-poor osteopetrosis). Osteoclast number on biopsy therefore does not define the disease; resorptive function does."},{"type":"table","title":"Molecular causes of osteopetrosis","cols":["Gene (protein)","Defect","Osteoclasts","Inheritance and features"],"rows":[["TCIRG1 (a3 subunit of the V-type H+-ATPase)","No proton pumping","Present, nonfunctional","Autosomal recessive; about half of severe infantile cases"],["CLCN7 (ClC-7 chloride transporter)","Lacuna cannot be acidified","Present, nonfunctional","Autosomal dominant adult form; also a severe recessive form"],["CA2 (carbonic anhydrase II)","No proton supply","Present, nonfunctional","Autosomal recessive; renal tubular acidosis, cerebral calcification"],["TNFSF11 (RANKL) or TNFRSF11A (RANK)","No osteoclast differentiation","Absent","Autosomal recessive; RANKL defect lies in osteoblast-lineage cells"]],"caption":"Most forms leave osteoclasts in place but disabled; RANKL and RANK defects prevent their formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Carbonic anhydrase II also supplies H+ in renal tubule cells, both for bicarbonate reclamation in the proximal tubule and for H+ secretion by distal intercalated cells. Its deficiency therefore combines osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis that has proximal and distal features, a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis, along with cerebral calcification and often intellectual disability."},{"type":"p","text":"Dense bone is brittle because quality, not quantity, determines strength. Without resorption, calcified cartilage and woven primary spongiosa are never replaced by lamellar bone, so the medullary cavity fills with disorganized, highly mineralized tissue. Microcracks from daily loading are not removed, because targeted repair begins with osteoclasts. Bones fracture transversely, like chalk. Radiographs show diffuse sclerosis without a clear medullary cavity, club-shaped (Erlenmeyer flask) distal femora, bone-within-bone images, and in the adult form dense vertebral endplates (sandwich vertebrae)."},{"type":"example","title":"One enzyme, two organs","text":"A 7-year-old with two low-trauma fractures and diffusely dense bones has Na+ 138, Cl- 113, and HCO3- 16 mEq/L, arterial pH 7.31, and urine pH 6.6. The anion gap is 138 minus (113 plus 16), or 9 mEq/L, so this is a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. A urine pH above 5.5 despite acidemia shows that the distal tubule cannot secrete H+ normally. Dense bones point to failed osteoclast acidification. The single defect that explains both is carbonic anhydrase II deficiency, because both the osteoclast and the renal tubule cell need this enzyme to generate H+ for secretion."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-03-C02","obj":"MS-13-C.2","title":"Complications of osteopetrosis and the infantile versus adult forms","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The complications of osteopetrosis follow from what osteoclasts normally do during growth: make space and shape bone. Severity depends on how completely resorption fails. The autosomal recessive infantile (malignant) form shows the full picture within months of birth, while the autosomal dominant adult form (Albers-Schönberg disease) is often found through fractures or an incidental radiograph."},{"type":"flow","title":"From failed resorption to pancytopenia and hepatosplenomegaly","steps":["Osteoclasts fail to hollow out the medullary cavity","Bone and calcified cartilage fill the marrow space","Marrow failure: anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia","Hematopoiesis shifts to the liver and spleen","Hepatosplenomegaly; the enlarged spleen sequesters cells","Cytopenias worsen; infection and bleeding follow"],"caption":"Marrow obliteration is a myelophthisic process, and splenic sequestration adds to the marrow failure."},{"type":"p","text":"Hematopoietic marrow needs a cavity. When none forms, blood cell production moves back to the fetal sites, the liver and spleen, which enlarge. The blood smear may be leukoerythroblastic, with nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes, as in other causes of marrow replacement. The large spleen also sequesters red cells and platelets, deepening the anemia and thrombocytopenia. Neutropenia predisposes to infection, which with anemia and bleeding is a major cause of death in the infantile form."},{"type":"p","text":"The skull foramina normally widen by resorption of their margins as the nerves grow. In osteopetrosis they stay narrow, so the nerves are compressed. The optic nerve (CN II) is most often affected, giving progressive vision loss with optic atrophy; the facial nerve (CN VII) gives facial palsy; and the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) gives hearing loss. Thickening of the skull base can also narrow the foramen magnum and cause hydrocephalus."},{"type":"p","text":"Fractures follow minor trauma and heal slowly, because remodeling of the callus needs osteoclasts. Dense jaw bone with poor blood supply and delayed tooth eruption make osteomyelitis of the mandible a characteristic complication. Infants may develop hypocalcemia with tetany or seizures because calcium is locked in a skeleton that cannot be resorbed, and PTH rises in response. Serum calcium, phosphate, and ALP are otherwise usually normal, which separates osteopetrosis from the mineral disorders in C03."},{"type":"table","title":"Infantile and adult osteopetrosis compared","cols":["Feature","Infantile form","Adult form"],"rows":[["Inheritance","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant"],["Typical genes","TCIRG1, CLCN7; CA2 in a milder variant","CLCN7"],["Onset","Infancy","Late childhood to adulthood; may be silent"],["Marrow","Pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly","Usually normal"],["Cranial nerves","Blindness, deafness, facial palsy common","Uncommon"],["Other","Growth failure, macrocephaly, hypocalcemic seizures","Fractures, hip osteoarthritis, jaw osteomyelitis"],["Course","Often fatal in childhood","Usually normal life span"]],"caption":"Both forms make brittle bone; only the infantile form usually obliterates the marrow and nerve foramina."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can an infant with osteopetrosis be hypocalcemic when the skeleton holds more calcium than normal?","explain":"Serum calcium is defended minute to minute partly by PTH-driven osteoclastic release from bone. If osteoclasts cannot resorb, that calcium cannot be mobilized, so a growing infant with high mineral demand becomes hypocalcemic despite a large skeletal store.","check":"The osteoclast flow in C01 and the hypocalcemia sentence in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-03-C03","obj":"MS-13-C.3","title":"Reading calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and PTH in bone disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four serum tests answer most questions about metabolic bone disease: calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase (ALP), which reflects osteoblast activity, and PTH, which shows whether the parathyroid glands cause the problem or are responding to it."},{"type":"p","text":"Three rules generate every pattern. First, PTH raises serum calcium (osteoclastic resorption, distal tubular calcium reabsorption, and stimulation of renal calcitriol synthesis) and lowers serum phosphate by inhibiting proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption. When PTH excess is the primary problem, calcium is high and phosphate low; when PTH rises to defend a falling calcium (secondary hyperparathyroidism), calcium is low or low-normal. Second, phosphate separates the two common secondary causes: in vitamin D deficiency the healthy kidney wastes phosphate under high PTH and gut absorption is poor, so phosphate is low, whereas the failing kidney cannot excrete phosphate, so it is high. Third, ALP comes from active osteoblasts, so it rises when bone formation is increased or when osteoblasts lay down osteoid that fails to mineralize, and it stays normal in osteoporosis, where bone is lost slowly."},{"type":"table","title":"Serum patterns in metabolic bone disease","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","ALP","PTH","Why"],"rows":[["Osteoporosis","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal","Bone mass lost; mineral regulation intact"],["Osteopetrosis","Normal (low in some infants)","Normal","Normal","Normal (high if hypocalcemic)","Resorption fails; regulation intact"],["Osteomalacia or rickets (vitamin D deficiency)","Low or low-normal","Low","High","High","Low gut absorption; PTH wastes phosphate"],["Paget disease","Normal","Normal","High, often very high","Normal","Focal chaotic remodeling, active osteoblasts"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low or low-normal","Normal or high","High or inappropriately normal","Autonomous PTH; ALP high with bone disease"],["Renal osteodystrophy","Low or normal","High","High","High, often very high","Phosphate retention, low calcitriol; ALP low or normal in adynamic bone"]],"caption":"Read calcium and PTH together first, then use phosphate and ALP to separate the causes."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reasoning through a bone lab panel","steps":["Read albumin-corrected calcium and PTH together","High calcium with high or normal PTH: primary hyperparathyroidism","Low calcium with high PTH: secondary; check phosphate","Low phosphate: vitamin D deficiency (osteomalacia, rickets)","High phosphate: chronic kidney disease (renal osteodystrophy)","Normal calcium and PTH: check ALP","Isolated high ALP: Paget disease; all normal: osteoporosis"],"caption":"A normal PTH in the face of hypercalcemia is inappropriate, because hypercalcemia should suppress it."},{"type":"table","title":"Approximate adult reference ranges","cols":["Test","About"],"rows":[["Total calcium","8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L)"],["Phosphate","2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL (0.8 to 1.45 mmol/L)"],["ALP","40 to 130 U/L (higher in growing children)"],["Intact PTH","10 to 65 pg/mL"],["25-hydroxyvitamin D","Deficient below about 20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L)"]],"caption":"Ranges vary by laboratory; judge borderline values against the reporting laboratory."},{"type":"p","text":"Three cautions prevent common errors. Total calcium falls when albumin is low, so either correct it (add about 0.8 mg/dL for each 1 g/dL that albumin is below 4 g/dL) or measure ionized calcium. ALP also comes from liver, so a high ALP with a high gamma-glutamyl transferase suggests a hepatic source, and children normally have a high ALP from active growth plates. Finally, osteoporosis by definition has normal labs; an abnormal panel in a patient with low bone mass points to a secondary cause such as hyperparathyroidism or vitamin D deficiency."},{"type":"example","title":"Three panels, three diseases","text":"Patient A: calcium 11.4 mg/dL, phosphate 2.1 mg/dL, ALP 150 U/L, PTH 120 pg/mL. Calcium and PTH are both high, so the gland is the cause: primary hyperparathyroidism, with low phosphate from PTH phosphaturia. Patient B: calcium 8.4, phosphate 2.0, ALP 220, PTH 140. Calcium is slightly low and PTH high, so PTH is responding; low phosphate means the kidney is working and wasting phosphate, so this is vitamin D deficiency with osteomalacia. Patient C: calcium 8.2, phosphate 6.8, ALP 190, PTH 480. Again PTH is responding, but phosphate is high because the kidney cannot excrete it: renal osteodystrophy."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-03-C04","obj":"MS-13-C.4","title":"Renal osteodystrophy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Renal osteodystrophy is the bone disease of chronic kidney disease (CKD). It is one part of the CKD mineral and bone disorder, which also includes the abnormal laboratory values and vascular calcification. Its hormonal changes begin early, with PTH often rising once GFR falls below about 60 mL/min per 1.73 m2, long before bone symptoms appear."},{"type":"p","text":"The first change is phosphate retention. As nephrons are lost, each remaining nephron must excrete more phosphate, and osteocytes respond by secreting FGF23, which with its coreceptor Klotho reduces proximal phosphate reabsorption and suppresses renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase. Loss of renal mass removes more of this enzyme, so calcitriol falls early, gut calcium absorption drops, and serum calcium falls. Three signals then drive the parathyroids: low ionized calcium sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor, high phosphate acting directly on the glands, and loss of calcitriol, which normally restrains PTH gene transcription and parathyroid cell proliferation through the vitamin D receptor. The result is secondary hyperparathyroidism with diffuse, later nodular, gland hyperplasia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Chronic kidney disease to bone disease","steps":["Falling GFR retains phosphate; osteocytes raise FGF23","FGF23 and lost renal mass reduce 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Low calcitriol cuts gut calcium absorption; calcium falls","Low calcium, high phosphate, low calcitriol stimulate the parathyroids","Parathyroid hyperplasia; PTH rises (secondary hyperparathyroidism)","PTH drives osteoclast resorption: osteitis fibrosa cystica","Low calcitriol and acidosis impair mineralization"],"caption":"Phosphate retention starts the chain; high-turnover resorption and defective mineralization are its two skeletal results."},{"type":"p","text":"Sustained PTH acts on osteoblast-lineage cells, which raise RANKL and recruit osteoclasts, so turnover becomes high. Osteoclasts tunnel into trabeculae, the marrow next to them is replaced by fibrous tissue, and woven bone is laid down rapidly, raising ALP. This is osteitis fibrosa cystica. Where resorption is severe, cysts form, and hemorrhage with osteoclast-like giant cells, fibroblasts, and hemosiderin produces brown tumors, which are reactive rather than neoplastic and resemble giant cell tumor of bone under the microscope."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings of osteitis fibrosa cystica and CKD bone disease","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Subperiosteal resorption, radial side of middle phalanges of index and middle fingers","Most specific radiographic sign of PTH excess"],["Resorption of distal phalangeal tufts","PTH-driven osteoclastic resorption"],["Salt and pepper skull","Mottled trabecular resorption of the skull vault"],["Loss of the lamina dura around teeth","Resorption of the dense bone lining tooth sockets"],["Brown tumors and bone cysts","Focal intense resorption with hemorrhage; may fracture"],["Rugger jersey spine","Endplate sclerosis from excess trabecular bone in CKD"],["Vascular and soft tissue calcification","High phosphate and calcium-phosphate product"]],"caption":"The first five are shared with primary hyperparathyroidism; the last two are characteristic of CKD."},{"type":"p","text":"Defective mineralization often coexists. Low calcitriol, chronic metabolic acidosis (bone mineral is consumed as a buffer), and historically aluminum exposure in dialysis patients cause unmineralized osteoid to accumulate (osteomalacia). At the other extreme, adynamic bone disease has very low turnover with few osteoblasts and osteoclasts, usually when PTH is relatively low. Many patients have a mixed lesion, with bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, and fractures; children show growth failure and rickets-like changes."},{"type":"p","text":"After years of stimulation, nodular parathyroid tissue expresses fewer calcium-sensing and vitamin D receptors and secretes PTH autonomously. Calcium then becomes high with very high PTH (tertiary hyperparathyroidism), which can persist after kidney transplantation."},{"type":"stop","predict":"PTH lowers phosphate in primary hyperparathyroidism. Why is phosphate high in renal osteodystrophy even though PTH is higher still?","explain":"PTH lowers serum phosphate by reducing tubular reabsorption, which only works if phosphate is being filtered. With a low GFR, the filtered load is too small for phosphaturia to keep up with intake, so phosphate is retained despite maximal PTH and FGF23.","check":"The second rule in C03 and the phosphate retention paragraph in this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteopetrosis and renal osteodystrophy sit at opposite ends of one system. Osteoclasts are hematopoietic cells of the monocyte-macrophage lineage that depend on RANKL from osteoblast-lineage cells, so osteopetrosis links bone biology to hematology (myelophthisic marrow failure), renal physiology (carbonic anhydrase II and tubular acidosis), and neuroanatomy (cranial nerves in their foramina). In renal osteodystrophy the opposite happens: osteoclasts are driven too hard by PTH, and the kidney, parathyroid, gut, and osteocyte are linked through calcitriol, FGF23, and phosphate."},{"type":"p","text":"The lab table works because each disease disturbs a different control point, but it has limits. Values overlap near reference limits, mixed disease is common (vitamin D deficiency in CKD, osteoporosis with hyperparathyroidism), and ALP reflects only osteoblast activity, not bone mass. Lab values point to a mechanism; bone density, radiographs, and sometimes bone biopsy define the skeletal lesion."}],"summary":["Osteoclasts resorb bone by sealing a lacuna and secreting HCl (carbonic anhydrase II, V-type H+-ATPase, ClC-7) and cathepsin K.","Osteopetrosis is failed osteoclast resorption; bone is dense but brittle because woven bone and microcracks are never remodeled.","Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency combines osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis and cerebral calcification, because both cell types need it to make H+.","Marrow obliteration causes pancytopenia, extramedullary hematopoiesis, and hepatosplenomegaly; narrow foramina compress CN II, VII, and VIII.","The autosomal recessive infantile form is severe and often fatal in childhood; the autosomal dominant adult form causes fractures and jaw osteomyelitis.","PTH raises calcium and lowers phosphate; ALP tracks osteoblast activity.","Osteoporosis has normal labs, Paget disease an isolated high ALP, and primary hyperparathyroidism high calcium with low phosphate.","Low calcium with high PTH is secondary hyperparathyroidism: low phosphate means vitamin D deficiency, high phosphate means CKD.","In CKD, phosphate retention, high FGF23, and low calcitriol drive secondary hyperparathyroidism, osteitis fibrosa cystica, and defective mineralization.","Long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism can become autonomous (tertiary) with hypercalcemia."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (osteopetrosis, hyperparathyroidism, renal osteodystrophy)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Calcium and phosphate homeostasis: the parathyroid glands, vitamin D, and bone"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone: osteoclasts and bone remodeling"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the parathyroids and calcium and phosphorus metabolism; chronic kidney disease"}]},"MS-13-D":{"unit":"MS-13-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04","title":"Congenital Musculoskeletal Diseases","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers inherited defects in the structural proteins that hold the musculoskeletal system together: fibrillin-1 in Marfan syndrome, dystrophin in Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy, type I collagen in osteogenesis imperfecta, types V and III collagen in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, and the growth plate receptor FGFR3 in achondroplasia. For each disorder the notes trace the path from gene to protein to the tissues that depend most on that protein, because that path explains the findings. Board vignettes give a body build, an eye or skin sign, a creatine kinase level, or a fracture pattern and ask for the gene, the inheritance, the complication that threatens life, or the look-alike condition that must be excluded.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance: autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and X-linked recessive patterns, penetrance, and expressivity","Collagen synthesis: Gly-X-Y repeat, hydroxylation, triple helix formation, and fibril assembly (MS-6, MS-8)","Skeletal muscle fiber structure: sarcolemma, basal lamina, and the contractile apparatus (MS-2, MS-7)","Endochondral and intramembranous ossification and the zones of the growth plate (MS-1)","Layers of the aortic wall and the role of elastic fibers in elastic arteries"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal genetics (MS-9)","how":"The dominant negative effect in collagen, the dystrophin reading frame rule, and the FGFR3 paternal age effect are developed there and applied clinically here."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"Cystic medial degeneration of the aortic root in Marfan syndrome and arterial fragility in vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome are causes of aneurysm and dissection."},{"to":"Amino acid metabolism (biochemistry)","how":"Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency in the methionine pathway explains homocystinuria, the main look-alike of Marfan syndrome."},{"to":"Genetic skin conditions (IN-13-E)","how":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is also classified as a genetic skin disease because skin hyperextensibility and fragility are its defining signs."},{"to":"Cardiomyopathy and respiratory failure","how":"Dystrophin loss in cardiac muscle causes dilated cardiomyopathy, and weakness of the diaphragm and chest wall causes restrictive ventilatory failure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Fibrillin-1","def":"A large cysteine-rich glycoprotein that polymerizes into extracellular microfibrils, scaffolds elastic fibers, and stores latent TGF-beta."},{"term":"Ectopia lentis","def":"Displacement of the lens from its normal position because the ciliary zonules that suspend it are weak or broken."},{"term":"Cystic medial degeneration","def":"Fragmentation of elastic lamellae, loss of smooth muscle cells, and pooling of proteoglycan in the aortic media, which weakens the wall."},{"term":"Arachnodactyly","def":"Abnormally long, slender fingers and toes."},{"term":"Dystrophin","def":"A large rod-shaped protein under the sarcolemma that links actin filaments to the dystroglycan complex and, through it, to the basal lamina."},{"term":"Gowers sign","def":"Rising from the floor by turning prone and pushing the hands up the thighs, a sign of weak hip and knee extensors."},{"term":"Pseudohypertrophy","def":"Enlargement of a muscle, classically the calf, caused by fat and fibrous tissue replacing lost muscle fibers."},{"term":"Dominant negative effect","def":"A mutant protein that incorporates into a complex with normal protein and disables it, so one bad allele inactivates more than half of the product."},{"term":"Dentinogenesis imperfecta","def":"Defective dentin formation that leaves teeth discolored, translucent, and prone to rapid wear and enamel loss."},{"term":"Wormian bones","def":"Small accessory bones within the cranial sutures, seen in excess when skull ossification is abnormal."},{"term":"Rhizomelia","def":"Disproportionate shortening of the proximal limb segments, the humerus and femur."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C01","obj":"MS-13-D.1","title":"Marfan syndrome: fibrillin-1, TGF-beta, and the skeletal and ocular findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibrillin-1 is a large cysteine-rich glycoprotein secreted by fibroblasts and smooth muscle cells. It polymerizes into microfibrils (about 10 nm wide) that do three jobs. They form the scaffold on which tropoelastin is laid down to make elastic fibers, so they are abundant in the aortic media, ligaments, and joint capsules. They act as structural cables in their own right, most importantly in the ciliary zonules that suspend the lens. They also bind latent TGF-beta through latent TGF-beta binding proteins, which keeps this growth factor stored in an inactive form within the matrix."},{"type":"p","text":"Marfan syndrome is caused by mutations in FBN1 on chromosome 15 and is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait. Most mutations are missense changes whose abnormal monomers enter microfibrils and disrupt them (a dominant negative effect); others lower the amount of fibrillin made. The result is twofold. Microfibrils are mechanically weak, and they can no longer hold latent TGF-beta in the matrix, so more of it is activated, which raises local signaling, increases matrix metalloproteinase activity, and further degrades the matrix. Loeys-Dietz syndrome, caused by mutations in TGF-beta receptor genes, produces similar aortic disease, which supports a central role for this pathway."},{"type":"p","text":"Penetrance is high, but expressivity varies widely, even within a family, and the single gene affects many organs (pleiotropy). About one quarter of cases arise from new mutations, so a negative family history does not exclude the diagnosis. Prevalence is about 1 in 5,000."},{"type":"h","text":"Skeletal and ocular findings"},{"type":"p","text":"Long bones overgrow, so patients are tall with limbs out of proportion to the trunk: arm span exceeds height (ratio above about 1.05) and the upper to lower body segment ratio is reduced. Overgrowth of the ribs pushes the sternum in or out (pectus excavatum or carinatum). Lax ligaments and capsules give joint hypermobility, and the spine often curves (scoliosis). The cause of overgrowth is not fully settled; altered growth factor signaling around the growth plate is the leading explanation. Because the ciliary zonules are made of fibrillin microfibrils, they stretch and break, and the lens usually shifts upward and outward (superotemporal ectopia lentis), typically in both eyes."},{"type":"table","title":"Marfan findings and their mechanism","cols":["Finding","How it is recognized","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Tall stature, long limbs","Arm span greater than height; low upper to lower segment ratio","Overgrowth of long bones"],["Arachnodactyly","Thumb tip passes the ulnar border of the fist (thumb sign); thumb and fifth finger overlap around the opposite wrist (wrist sign)","Overgrowth of tubular bones of the hands and feet"],["Pectus excavatum or carinatum","Sunken or protruding sternum","Rib overgrowth"],["Joint hypermobility, flat feet, scoliosis","Lax joints, collapsed arches, spinal curve","Weak microfibrils in ligaments and capsules"],["Upward lens dislocation, myopia","Superotemporal ectopia lentis, often bilateral","Zonules are fibrillin microfibrils"],["Spontaneous pneumothorax","Sudden chest pain and dyspnea in a tall young adult","Rupture of apical lung blebs"],["Dural ectasia, skin striae","Widened lumbosacral dural sac; striae without weight change","Weak connective tissue in dura and dermis"]],"caption":"Every finding follows from tissue that depends on fibrillin microfibrils for strength or from excess TGF-beta signaling."},{"type":"p","text":"The revised Ghent criteria make aortic root dilation and ectopia lentis the two cardinal features; together they establish the diagnosis even without a family history, and aortic root dilation with a pathogenic FBN1 variant also suffices."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C02","obj":"MS-13-D.2","title":"Marfan cardiovascular disease and the homocystinuria look-alike","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The aortic root, at the sinuses of Valsalva, takes the full pulsatile force of each ejection and depends on fibrillin-scaffolded elastic lamellae to absorb it. In Marfan syndrome the media shows cystic medial degeneration: elastic lamellae fragment, smooth muscle cells are lost, and pools of proteoglycan collect between them. The weakened root dilates, and by the law of Laplace (wall stress is proportional to pressure times radius divided by wall thickness) each increase in radius raises wall stress further, so dilation tends to progress."},{"type":"flow","title":"From FBN1 mutation to aortic dissection","steps":["FBN1 mutation yields defective fibrillin-1 microfibrils","Elastic lamellae lose their scaffold; latent TGF-beta is released","Excess TGF-beta signaling and proteases degrade the aortic media","Aortic root dilates under pulsatile stress","Annular stretch causes aortic regurgitation","Intimal tear lets blood split the media: dissection"],"caption":"Dilation raises wall stress (Laplace), so root enlargement is self-reinforcing until regurgitation or dissection occurs."},{"type":"p","text":"Three complications follow. Stretching of the aortic annulus pulls the cusps apart, causing aortic regurgitation, heard as an early diastolic decrescendo murmur with a wide pulse pressure. A tear in the intima lets blood dissect along the weakened media, usually starting in the ascending aorta; rupture into the pericardium causes tamponade, and dissection is the leading cause of death. Pregnancy raises the risk because cardiac output and blood volume rise. The mitral leaflets undergo myxomatous change, become redundant, and prolapse into the left atrium in systole, producing a midsystolic click, sometimes followed by a late systolic murmur of mitral regurgitation."},{"type":"h","text":"Homocystinuria versus Marfan syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"Classic homocystinuria is an autosomal recessive deficiency of cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS), the pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6) dependent enzyme that joins homocysteine and serine to form cystathionine on the way to cysteine. Homocysteine and methionine accumulate in plasma, homocystine spills into urine, and cysteine falls. Homocysteine injures endothelium and promotes platelet activation, so arterial and venous thrombosis occur at young ages. Homocysteine is proposed to disrupt the disulfide bonds of cysteine-rich fibrillin, which would explain the tall, long-limbed build and lens dislocation, but the lens usually shifts downward and inward (inferonasal). Intellectual disability and osteoporosis are common."},{"type":"table","title":"Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria compared","cols":["Feature","Marfan syndrome","Classic homocystinuria"],"rows":[["Inheritance and defect","Autosomal dominant, FBN1 (fibrillin-1)","Autosomal recessive, CBS deficiency"],["Lens dislocation","Upward (superotemporal)","Downward (inferonasal)"],["Intelligence","Normal","Often impaired"],["Main vascular threat","Aortic root dilation and dissection","Arterial and venous thrombosis"],["Laboratory","Normal homocysteine","High plasma homocysteine and methionine; urinary homocystine"],["Bone","Tall, joint hypermobility","Tall build with osteoporosis"]],"caption":"A tall patient with a dislocated lens plus thrombosis or intellectual disability points to homocystinuria."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: tall teenager with a dislocated lens","text":"A tall 14-year-old with long fingers has bilateral lens dislocation and needs special education. Last year he had a deep vein thrombosis. Step 1: tall build plus ectopia lentis suggests a fibrillin problem, so both Marfan syndrome and homocystinuria are on the list. Step 2: intellectual disability and venous thrombosis have no mechanism in Marfan syndrome, but both follow from high homocysteine. Step 3: predict the lens shifted downward and the plasma homocysteine and methionine are high. Step 4: predict the inheritance is autosomal recessive, so parents are usually unaffected carriers, unlike the affected parent in about three quarters of Marfan cases. The deficient enzyme is cystathionine beta-synthase."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients with Marfan syndrome have the same blood pressure, but one has an aortic root twice as wide. Which has the higher wall stress?","explain":"The wider root. By the law of Laplace, wall stress rises in proportion to radius at a given pressure, and a thinned media raises the stress still further, so the larger root is closer to dissection.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph and the flow figure in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C03","obj":"MS-13-D.3","title":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy: absent dystrophin and progressive fiber loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dystrophin is a 427 kDa rod-shaped protein that lines the inner face of the sarcolemma. Its amino terminus binds actin filaments, and its carboxyl terminus binds beta-dystroglycan, a membrane protein linked to alpha-dystroglycan, which in turn binds laminin in the basal lamina. Together with the sarcoglycans, this dystrophin-glycoprotein complex transmits contractile force from the cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix and protects the membrane from mechanical stress during contraction. It also anchors neuronal nitric oxide synthase, which helps dilate arterioles in working muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"The DMD gene on Xp21 is the largest known human gene (about 2.2 million base pairs, 79 exons), which gives it a high new mutation rate; about one third of Duchenne cases are new mutations. Duchenne muscular dystrophy is X-linked recessive and affects about 1 in 3,500 to 5,000 male births. Large deletions account for most cases, and mutations that shift the reading frame or create a stop codon leave muscle with essentially no dystrophin (see C04 and MS-9)."},{"type":"flow","title":"From absent dystrophin to muscle loss","steps":["Frameshift or nonsense mutation: no functional dystrophin","Actin no longer anchored to dystroglycan and basal lamina","Contraction tears the sarcolemma; CK leaks out","Calcium enters and activates proteases (calpains)","Fiber necrosis, then regeneration by satellite cells","Regeneration fails; fat and fibrous tissue replace muscle","Progressive weakness, cardiomyopathy, respiratory failure"],"caption":"Repeated cycles of membrane injury, necrosis, and repair exhaust regeneration over years."},{"type":"p","text":"Boys are normal at birth, and walking may be slightly late. Weakness appears before age 5 and begins in the proximal muscles of the pelvic girdle. The child falls often, walks with a waddling gait, struggles with stairs, and may walk on his toes with an exaggerated lumbar lordosis. Gowers sign appears because the gluteus maximus and quadriceps cannot extend the hips and knees: the child rolls prone and climbs up his own thighs with his hands. The calves enlarge and feel firm, but this pseudohypertrophy is fat and fibrous tissue, not stronger muscle. Most boys lose the ability to walk before about 13 years."},{"type":"p","text":"Creatine kinase (CK) leaks from torn fibers, so serum CK is markedly elevated from birth, often 10 to 100 times the upper limit of normal, and falls later as muscle mass disappears. Biopsy shows variation in fiber size, necrotic and regenerating fibers, endomysial fibrosis, and fat. Cardiac muscle also needs dystrophin, so fibrosis begins in the posterobasal left ventricle and progresses to dilated cardiomyopathy and arrhythmias by the late teens. Weak diaphragm and intercostal muscles, worsened by scoliosis after loss of walking, give a restrictive pattern with falling vital capacity, nocturnal hypoventilation, and eventually respiratory failure, which with cardiomyopathy causes death in early adulthood. Brain dystrophin isoforms explain the cognitive impairment seen in a minority, and smooth muscle involvement can slow gut motility. Female carriers are usually unaffected but may have raised CK, mild weakness, or cardiomyopathy when X-inactivation is skewed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C04","obj":"MS-13-D.4","title":"Becker versus Duchenne: truncated dystrophin and how to tell them apart","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Becker muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the same DMD gene, but the muscle still makes some dystrophin. The reading frame rule explains the difference. A deletion that removes a number of nucleotides not divisible by 3 shifts the reading frame, creates a premature stop codon, and the mRNA is degraded by nonsense-mediated decay, so no protein is made (Duchenne). A deletion of a multiple of 3 nucleotides keeps the frame, so the protein is shorter in the middle (rod domain) but keeps its actin-binding amino terminus and its dystroglycan-binding carboxyl terminus, and it works partially (Becker). The rule predicts the phenotype in about 90% of cases; the size of a deletion matters far less than whether the frame is kept."},{"type":"p","text":"Becker is less common (about 1 in 18,000 male births) and milder. Onset is later, often between ages 5 and 15 and sometimes in adulthood, progression is slower, and patients usually walk beyond age 16, often into adult life, with survival into middle age. Calf pseudohypertrophy, cramps, exercise-induced muscle pain, and myoglobinuria may occur. CK is elevated, though usually less than in Duchenne at the same age. Cardiomyopathy can be out of proportion to the skeletal weakness and may be the presenting or fatal problem."},{"type":"table","title":"Duchenne and Becker compared","cols":["Feature","Duchenne","Becker"],"rows":[["Mutation type","Frameshift or nonsense","In-frame deletion or duplication"],["Dystrophin protein","Absent","Shorter and/or reduced, partly functional"],["Onset","Before age 5","Usually 5 to 15 years or later"],["Loss of walking","Before about 13 years","After 16 years, often adulthood or never"],["Survival","Early adulthood","Often middle age or later"],["CK","Markedly elevated (10 to 100 times normal)","Elevated, usually less"],["Muscle immunostaining","No sarcolemmal dystrophin (rare revertant fibers)","Faint, patchy, or discontinuous staining"],["Immunoblot (Western blot)","Absent or trace","Abnormal size and/or reduced amount"],["Heart","Dilated cardiomyopathy by late teens","Cardiomyopathy, may exceed skeletal weakness"]],"caption":"Both are X-linked recessive; the amount and quality of dystrophin, not the gene, separates them."},{"type":"h","text":"Separating them in the laboratory"},{"type":"p","text":"Genetic testing of blood is usually done first. Deletion and duplication analysis detects most cases, and sequencing finds point mutations in the rest. Knowing the exact exons involved shows whether the reading frame is kept, which predicts Duchenne or Becker. When a muscle biopsy is done, immunostaining with antibodies against dystrophin shows the protein at the fiber surface. In Duchenne the sarcolemma is unstained in nearly all fibers; a few revertant fibers may stain because a second somatic change restored the frame in those fibers. In Becker the staining is present but faint, patchy, or discontinuous. An immunoblot measures size and quantity: no band in Duchenne, and a band of abnormal size or reduced intensity in Becker. Age at loss of walking is the clinical tiebreaker when tests are borderline, with an intermediate group between 13 and 16 years."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A boy has a deletion of three exons that together contain 312 nucleotides. Predict his dystrophin staining and phenotype.","explain":"312 is divisible by 3, so the frame is kept. He should make a shorter, partly working dystrophin, with faint or patchy sarcolemmal staining and a Becker phenotype, despite the large deletion.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph and the comparison table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C05","obj":"MS-13-D.5","title":"Osteogenesis imperfecta: defective type I collagen in every tissue that uses it","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type I collagen is the main organic component of bone, and it is also the dominant collagen of the sclera, dentin, tendons, ligaments, and dermis. Each molecule is a triple helix of two alpha-1(I) chains, encoded by COL1A1 on chromosome 17, and one alpha-2(I) chain, encoded by COL1A2 on chromosome 7. Glycine must occupy every third position (Gly-X-Y) because only glycine is small enough to sit at the center of the helix. In bone, collagen fibrils are the template on which hydroxyapatite is deposited: the mineral gives stiffness and the collagen gives toughness, so poor collagen makes bone brittle as well as thin."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta is a group of disorders of type I collagen, and most cases (about 85% to 90%) are autosomal dominant mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2. Two mechanisms set the severity (MS-9). A null allele halves the output of normal collagen, giving the mild form (type I). A glycine substitution produces a chain that joins trimers with normal chains and kinks the helix, so most molecules are abnormal (a dominant negative effect); these give moderate to lethal forms. Slowed helix folding exposes chains to excess hydroxylation and glycosylation, and much of the abnormal procollagen is retained or degraded in the endoplasmic reticulum. Rare autosomal recessive forms involve proteins that modify or process collagen."},{"type":"flow","title":"From glycine substitution to fracture","steps":["Missense mutation replaces a glycine in the Gly-X-Y repeat","Mutant chains join normal chains in the triple helix","Helix kinks and folds slowly; chains are overmodified","Abnormal procollagen is degraded or forms faulty fibrils","Scant, poorly organized matrix mineralizes abnormally","Thin, brittle bone fractures with minimal trauma"],"caption":"One mutant allele can damage most collagen molecules, which is why substitutions cause more severe disease than null alleles."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings map onto the tissues rich in type I collagen. Bones fracture with little force, beginning in infancy or even before birth in severe forms, and fractures become less frequent after puberty. Repeated fractures and soft bone cause bowing, short stature, vertebral compression, and scoliosis, and skull films show excess wormian bones. A thin sclera lets the dark choroid show through, giving blue or gray sclerae. Hearing loss usually begins in adolescence or early adult life; it is conductive at first because of ossicle fractures and fixation of the stapes footplate, and it often becomes mixed later. Dentin is largely type I collagen, so dentinogenesis imperfecta leaves teeth gray-blue or amber and translucent, with enamel that chips off the weak dentin. Lax ligaments give joint hypermobility, and thin skin bruises easily. Serum calcium, phosphate, and alkaline phosphatase are usually normal, which separates the disease from rickets and other mineral disorders."},{"type":"table","title":"Main clinical types of osteogenesis imperfecta (Sillence)","cols":["Type","Usual molecular basis","Severity and course","Sclerae","Other features"],"rows":[["I","Null COL1A1 allele, half the normal collagen","Mild, nondeforming; most common","Distinctly blue","Hearing loss common; near normal height"],["II","Glycine substitution (dominant negative)","Perinatal lethal; fractures in utero","Dark blue","Crumpled long bones, beaded ribs, small chest"],["III","Glycine substitution or recessive gene","Progressively deforming; most severe in survivors","Variable, may lighten with age","Very short stature, dentinogenesis imperfecta common"],["IV","Glycine substitution","Moderate, variable deformity","Normal or gray","Dentinogenesis imperfecta common"]],"caption":"Severity rises from type I to type III among survivors; type II is lethal around birth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C06","obj":"MS-13-D.6","title":"Osteogenesis imperfecta versus nonaccidental trauma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiple fractures in an infant raise two main possibilities: inflicted injury and a bone fragility disorder. Physical abuse is far more common than osteogenesis imperfecta, which occurs in about 1 in 15,000 to 20,000 births, so the reasoning must rest on specific findings rather than on the number of fractures alone. Both can also coexist."},{"type":"p","text":"Fractures from abuse reflect forces an infant cannot generate alone. Squeezing the chest from front to back levers the rib heads over the transverse processes and breaks the posterior ribs. Shaking or yanking a limb shears the immature bone just under the growth plate, producing classic metaphyseal lesions, seen as corner or bucket handle fractures. Fractures at different stages of healing (fresh lines beside mature callus) show more than one event. Injury beyond the skeleton strengthens the case: bruises in a baby who is not yet mobile, patterned bruises, subdural hematoma, retinal hemorrhages, and a history that changes or does not fit the child's developmental stage."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta leaves evidence in bone quality and in other type I collagen tissues. Blue sclerae, dentinogenesis imperfecta, wormian bones, generalized osteopenia, bowing, short stature, joint laxity, and a family history of easy fractures or early hearing loss all favor it. Each clue has limits. Healthy young infants often have bluish sclerae, and type IV disease may have white ones. Teeth cannot be judged before they erupt at about 6 months. A negative family history does not exclude the disease, because new mutations are common. COL1A1 and COL1A2 testing detects most dominant cases, and normal calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, and vitamin D help exclude metabolic bone disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Clues that separate inflicted injury from osteogenesis imperfecta","cols":["Clue","Favors nonaccidental trauma","Favors osteogenesis imperfecta"],"rows":[["Fracture pattern","Classic metaphyseal lesions, posterior rib, scapular, sternal, or spinous process fractures","Long bone shaft fractures, vertebral compression, bowing"],["Healing stages","Several ages that do not match the history","Can also be multiple, but with a fragility history and minor trauma"],["Bone density and skull","Normal density, no excess wormian bones","Osteopenia, thin cortices, excess wormian bones"],["Sclerae","White (after early infancy)","Blue or gray, especially type I"],["Teeth","Normal","Dentinogenesis imperfecta"],["Soft tissue and brain","Bruises in a non-mobile infant, subdural hematoma, retinal hemorrhage","Easy bruising possible, no inflicted pattern"],["Family history","Noncontributory","Fractures, blue sclerae, or early hearing loss in relatives"]],"caption":"Fracture location and associated findings carry more weight than fracture count."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a 4-month-old with three fractures","text":"A 4-month-old has a femoral shaft fracture after a reported fall from a sofa. Skeletal survey shows healing posterior fractures of the left 5th and 6th ribs and a corner fracture of the distal tibial metaphysis. Sclerae are white, bone density looks normal, and the family has no fracture history. Step 1: a 4-month-old cannot roll forcefully enough to break a femur. Step 2: posterior rib fractures and a classic metaphyseal lesion are highly specific for squeezing and shaking. Step 3: healing rib fractures mean an earlier, unexplained event. Step 4: no osteopenia, wormian bones, or blue sclerae. The findings favor nonaccidental trauma; teeth have not erupted, so dentition cannot help."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why are classic metaphyseal lesions so specific for abuse, while a midshaft long bone fracture is not?","explain":"The metaphyseal lesion needs a shearing or torsional force across the fragile zone below the growth plate, which a young infant cannot produce alone and which shaking or yanking supplies. A shaft fracture can follow a real accidental fall or minimal force in fragile bone, so it does not discriminate.","check":"Compare with the second paragraph and the table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C07","obj":"MS-13-D.7","title":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome: collagen defects in skin, joints, and hollow organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of inherited disorders of collagen structure or processing (the 2017 classification lists 13 types) that share three features: skin hyperextensibility, joint hypermobility, and tissue fragility. Which feature dominates depends on which collagen is affected and where that collagen matters most. Type V collagen is a minor fibrillar collagen that co-assembles with type I and controls when fibrils start and how thick they grow, so its loss disorganizes skin and ligament. Type III collagen forms thin, pliable fibrils in tissues that must stretch: arterial walls, bowel, uterus, and skin."},{"type":"p","text":"The classic type is autosomal dominant, usually from COL5A1 or COL5A2 mutations that reduce type V collagen. Electron microscopy shows collagen fibrils of irregular, variable diameter, many abnormally large, with cauliflower-like outlines in cross section. The skin is soft and velvety and stretches far but springs back when released, unlike cutis laxa, where loose skin hangs in folds. It splits with minor trauma and heals as wide, thin, atrophic scars over the knees, shins, and forehead. Joints are hypermobile and dislocate, and easy bruising reflects poorly supported small vessels, while platelet counts and clotting tests are usually normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The vascular type is autosomal dominant from COL3A1 mutations, often glycine substitutions that act as dominant negatives. It is the most dangerous form. Skin is thin and translucent with a visible venous pattern and bruises extensively, but it is not markedly stretchy, and hypermobility is usually limited to the small joints. Because type III collagen supports arteries, bowel, and uterus, these organs rupture spontaneously: arterial dissection or rupture, perforation of the colon (often sigmoid), and uterine rupture in pregnancy, which shortens life expectancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Selected Ehlers-Danlos types","cols":["Type","Gene and defect","Inheritance","Defining features"],"rows":[["Classic","COL5A1 or COL5A2, type V collagen","Autosomal dominant","Hyperextensible velvety skin, atrophic scars, joint hypermobility, bruising"],["Vascular","COL3A1, type III collagen","Autosomal dominant","Thin translucent skin, extensive bruising, arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture"],["Hypermobile","Gene not identified","Autosomal dominant pattern","Generalized hypermobility, dislocations, chronic joint pain; most common type"],["Kyphoscoliotic","PLOD1, lysyl hydroxylase deficiency","Autosomal recessive","Infant hypotonia, progressive kyphoscoliosis, fragile sclera"],["Dermatosparaxis","ADAMTS2, procollagen N-proteinase deficiency","Autosomal recessive","Severely fragile, sagging skin"]],"caption":"Classic and vascular types are the ones tested most; the enzyme defects show that faulty collagen processing gives the same picture as faulty collagen chains."},{"type":"p","text":"Three distinctions recur on exams. Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan syndrome share hypermobility, but only Marfan gives tall stature, lens dislocation, and aortic root disease from a fibrillin defect. Osteogenesis imperfecta is a type I collagen disorder of bone, whereas Ehlers-Danlos mainly affects soft tissues. Bruising in Ehlers-Danlos comes from fragile vessel support, not from a platelet or coagulation defect."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-04-C08","obj":"MS-13-D.8","title":"Achondroplasia: FGFR3 and short-limbed dwarfism","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Long bones lengthen by endochondral ossification at the growth plate, where chondrocytes proliferate in columns, enlarge (hypertrophy), and are replaced by bone. Fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3), a receptor tyrosine kinase on these chondrocytes, acts as a brake on both proliferation and hypertrophy. Bones that form by intramembranous ossification, such as the skull vault, and the periosteal growth that widens bones do not depend on this process."},{"type":"p","text":"Achondroplasia is the most common skeletal dysplasia (about 1 in 25,000 births). It is autosomal dominant with full penetrance, and almost all cases carry the same gain of function mutation (G380R, in the transmembrane domain) that makes the receptor overactive, so it signals even without ligand. The brake is always on, so the proliferative and hypertrophic zones are short and endochondral bones grow little. About 80% of cases are new mutations, linked to older paternal age (MS-9); homozygous inheritance from two affected parents is usually lethal in infancy. Other FGFR3 activating mutations give a lethal form (thanatophoric dysplasia) and a milder one (hypochondroplasia), so the strength of receptor activation sets severity."},{"type":"flow","title":"From FGFR3 mutation to rhizomelic dwarfism","steps":["Activating FGFR3 mutation signals without ligand","Growth plate chondrocytes proliferate and hypertrophy less","Endochondral bones grow short; proximal long bones most affected","Intramembranous skull vault grows normally; skull base stays small","Short limbs, near normal trunk, large head, narrow foramen magnum"],"caption":"Only bone made by endochondral ossification is restrained, which explains the disproportion."},{"type":"p","text":"The body is disproportionate. The proximal limb segments (humerus and femur) are shortest, called rhizomelic shortening, while the trunk is near normal in length. The head is large (macrocephaly) with frontal bossing because the vault grows normally, whereas the endochondral skull base is short, giving midface hypoplasia, a depressed nasal bridge, and a small foramen magnum. Intelligence is normal, although motor milestones are delayed by hypotonia and the heavy head. This disproportion separates achondroplasia from proportionate short stature, such as growth hormone deficiency or hypothyroidism."},{"type":"table","title":"Achondroplasia findings and mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Rhizomelic limb shortening, normal trunk length","Long bones depend most on growth plate lengthening"],["Macrocephaly with frontal bossing","Intramembranous vault grows normally over a short skull base"],["Midface hypoplasia, depressed nasal bridge","Short endochondral skull base and facial skeleton"],["Trident hand (gap between middle and ring fingers)","Short tubular bones of similar length in the hand"],["Genu varum (bowed legs)","Fibula relatively longer than tibia, plus lax knee ligaments"],["Foramen magnum stenosis in infancy","Small endochondral skull base can compress the cervicomedullary junction"],["Lumbar spinal stenosis in adults","Short pedicles and narrowing interpedicular distance down the lumbar spine"],["Recurrent otitis media, conductive hearing loss","Short, horizontal eustachian tubes in a small midface"],["Normal intelligence","Brain development is unaffected"]],"caption":"Adult height is about 130 cm (4 ft 3 in) in men and 125 cm (4 ft 1 in) in women."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disorder in this chapter follows one rule: a defective structural protein fails first and most visibly in the tissue that relies on it most. Fibrillin microfibrils support the aortic root and the lens zonules, dystrophin protects muscle membranes that contract hardest (pelvic girdle, heart, diaphragm), type I collagen gives bone, sclera, dentin, and ossicles their strength, type III collagen supports hollow organs and arteries, and FGFR3 acts only where bone grows from cartilage. Inheritance follows the protein's role. Defects in structural proteins that assemble with normal partners often act as dominant negatives and are usually autosomal dominant, whereas enzyme deficiencies such as cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency or lysyl hydroxylase deficiency are usually recessive, and a large X-linked gene like DMD has a high new mutation rate."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology meet at the complications that cause death: aortic dissection in Marfan syndrome and vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, restrictive respiratory failure and dilated cardiomyopathy in Duchenne, and cervicomedullary compression in infants with achondroplasia. Single clues have limits: a negative family history does not exclude a dominant disorder with frequent new mutations, Becker disease can present as cardiomyopathy alone, and a Marfan-like build with thrombosis or intellectual disability points to a metabolic cause rather than a fibrillin mutation."}],"summary":["Marfan syndrome is autosomal dominant FBN1 (fibrillin-1) disease; weak microfibrils and excess TGF-beta signaling cause tall stature, arachnodactyly, arm span greater than height, pectus deformity, hypermobility, and upward lens dislocation.","Cystic medial degeneration dilates the Marfan aortic root, causing aortic regurgitation and dissection, the main cause of death; mitral valve prolapse is also common.","Homocystinuria (autosomal recessive CBS deficiency) mimics Marfan build but gives downward lens dislocation, intellectual disability, osteoporosis, and thrombosis, with high homocysteine.","Duchenne is X-linked recessive with absent dystrophin: onset before age 5, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy from fat and fibrosis, CK 10 to 100 times normal, and death from cardiomyopathy or respiratory failure.","Becker comes from in-frame DMD mutations that leave a truncated, partly working dystrophin; onset is later, walking persists past 16, and cardiomyopathy may dominate.","Dystrophin staining is absent in Duchenne and faint or patchy in Becker; deletion and duplication testing shows whether the reading frame is kept.","Osteogenesis imperfecta is defective type I collagen (COL1A1 or COL1A2): fractures with minimal trauma, blue sclerae, conductive hearing loss, dentinogenesis imperfecta, and joint laxity; glycine substitutions act as dominant negatives.","Posterior rib fractures, classic metaphyseal lesions, and fractures of differing ages favor abuse; blue sclerae, abnormal teeth, wormian bones, osteopenia, and family history favor osteogenesis imperfecta.","Classic Ehlers-Danlos (type V collagen) gives stretchy skin, atrophic scars, hypermobility, and bruising; vascular Ehlers-Danlos (type III collagen) causes arterial, bowel, and uterine rupture.","Achondroplasia is an autosomal dominant FGFR3 gain of function, mostly new mutations with older paternal age, giving rhizomelic shortening, normal trunk, macrocephaly, frontal bossing, and normal intelligence."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases, sections on Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles, section on muscular dystrophies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors, sections on osteogenesis imperfecta and achondroplasia"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Blood Vessels, section on aortic dissection"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins; amino acid metabolism section on homocystinuria"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Genetic diseases, section on osteogenesis imperfecta"}]},"MS-13-E":{"unit":"MS-13-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05","title":"Degenerative Musculoskeletal Diseases","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers degenerative diseases of cartilage, bone, disc, and fascia: osteoarthritis, osteoporosis, Paget disease of bone, and avascular necrosis, followed by the age-specific hip and knee disorders of childhood and adolescence, intervertebral disc disease, and Dupuytren contracture. Each is a failure of tissue maintenance. Cartilage matrix breaks down faster than chondrocytes rebuild it, osteoclasts outpace osteoblasts, remodeling becomes chaotic, or a region with little collateral flow loses its blood supply. Board questions usually give the patient's age, the joint or bone involved, and a lab or imaging clue, then ask for the mechanism. Knowing which cell, matrix, or vessel fails in each disease lets you predict the findings instead of memorizing them.","prerequisites":["Bone remodeling: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, osteocytes, and the RANK, RANKL, and osteoprotegerin system (MS-2, MS-6)","Hyaline cartilage composition: chondrocytes, type II collagen, and aggrecan (MS-2, MS-6)","Calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D regulation (MS-8)","Hip joint anatomy and the blood supply of the femoral head (MS-3, MS-4)","Vertebral column, intervertebral discs, and spinal nerve root exit (MS-3, MS-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Metabolic musculoskeletal diseases (MS-13-C)","how":"Osteomalacia and hyperparathyroidism also lower bone density on imaging, but unlike osteoporosis they change serum calcium, phosphate, PTH, or alkaline phosphatase."},{"to":"Inflammatory musculoskeletal diseases (MS-13-B)","how":"Rheumatoid arthritis and crystal arthritis are separated from osteoarthritis by stiffness duration, joint pattern, and synovial fluid white cell count."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal neoplasms (MS-13-F)","how":"Paget disease is the classic precursor of secondary osteosarcoma in older adults."},{"to":"Hematology (sickle cell disease)","how":"Sickled red cells occlude marrow sinusoids, producing bone infarcts and avascular necrosis of the femoral and humeral heads."},{"to":"Endocrine system","how":"Estrogen, glucocorticoids, and thyroid hormone shift the balance between bone resorption and formation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Aggrecan","def":"The large cartilage proteoglycan whose negatively charged glycosaminoglycan chains hold water and let cartilage resist compression."},{"term":"Eburnation","def":"Polishing of exposed subchondral bone into a smooth, ivory-like surface after the overlying cartilage is lost."},{"term":"Osteophyte","def":"A bony outgrowth at the margin of a joint formed by endochondral ossification of reactive cartilage."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"Receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa B ligand, made by osteoblasts, stromal cells, and osteocytes, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives osteoclast formation and activity."},{"term":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG)","def":"A soluble decoy receptor secreted by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK."},{"term":"Peak bone mass","def":"The greatest bone mass a person attains, reached in about the third decade of life."},{"term":"T-score","def":"The number of standard deviations by which a patient's bone mineral density differs from the mean of healthy young adults of the same sex."},{"term":"Mosaic pattern","def":"The jigsaw arrangement of irregular cement lines in Paget bone, left by repeated, disorganized cycles of resorption and formation."},{"term":"Osteonecrosis (avascular necrosis)","def":"Death of bone and marrow cells caused by loss of blood supply, also called bone infarction."},{"term":"Apophysis","def":"A secondary ossification center at a tendon attachment that adds shape but not length to a bone."},{"term":"Radiculopathy","def":"Pain, sensory loss, weakness, or reflex change in the distribution of a single compressed or irritated spinal nerve root."},{"term":"Myofibroblast","def":"A fibroblast that contains alpha smooth muscle actin, so it both secretes collagen and contracts."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C01","obj":"MS-13-E.1","title":"Osteoarthritis pathogenesis and risk factors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Articular cartilage is avascular and aneural hyaline cartilage nourished by synovial fluid. Its type II collagen network restrains aggrecan, whose chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains carry fixed negative charges that draw in water. Under load, water is squeezed out and the swollen matrix pushes back; when the load lifts, water returns. This lets cartilage spread force across the joint and keeps friction near zero. Sparse chondrocytes maintain the matrix, but slowly, because they have no blood supply and little capacity to divide."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoarthritis (OA), also called degenerative joint disease, begins when mechanical stress or aging injures chondrocytes. The cells first proliferate into clusters and try to repair, then shift to a catabolic state. They release interleukin 1 beta, tumor necrosis factor, and nitric oxide, which induce matrix metalloproteinases (especially MMP-13, a collagenase) and aggrecanases (ADAMTS-4 and ADAMTS-5). Aggrecan is lost first, so the cartilage softens; then the collagen network is cleaved and vertical clefts form, called fibrillation. Fragments break off into the joint as loose bodies (joint mice), and full-thickness loss eventually exposes bone. Aging adds chondrocyte senescence and advanced glycation end products that stiffen collagen and make it brittle."},{"type":"p","text":"Bone reacts once cartilage no longer cushions it. Subchondral bone thickens (sclerosis), and bone rubbing on bone is polished smooth (eburnation). Synovial fluid forced through cartilage defects and microfractures, together with focal bone necrosis, produces subchondral cysts. At the joint margins, growth factors such as TGF-beta stimulate new cartilage that ossifies by the endochondral route into osteophytes. Mild synovitis is common, but OA is not driven by systemic autoimmunity, and the joint does not fuse."},{"type":"flow","title":"Osteoarthritis pathogenesis","steps":["Mechanical stress and aging injure chondrocytes","Chondrocytes release IL-1, MMPs, and aggrecanases","Aggrecan lost, cartilage softens and loses resilience","Collagen cleaved, cartilage fibrillates and cracks","Load falls on bone: sclerosis, eburnation, cysts","Marginal osteophytes form, motion is limited"],"caption":"Matrix breakdown outpaces repair, then bone remodels in response to unbuffered load."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors and how they act","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Age","Cumulative load, chondrocyte senescence, glycation of collagen"],["Obesity","Higher load on knees and hips; adipokines such as leptin may explain added hand OA"],["Prior joint injury","Meniscal or ligament tears and fractures through the joint change load distribution"],["Mechanical load","Repetitive occupational or athletic loading of specific joints"],["Malalignment","Varus knee concentrates load on the medial compartment"],["Female sex, genetics","More hand and knee OA in women, especially after menopause; heritable susceptibility"]],"caption":"Secondary OA follows a predisposing joint disease such as hip dysplasia, avascular necrosis, slipped capital femoral epiphysis, hemochromatosis, or calcium pyrophosphate deposition."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C02","obj":"MS-13-E.2","title":"Clinical and radiographic findings of osteoarthritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every OA finding follows from mechanical cartilage failure. Cartilage has no nerves, so pain comes from the structures around it: subchondral bone with raised intraosseous pressure and microfractures, periosteum lifted by osteophytes, an irritated synovium, and a stretched capsule and ligaments. Pain is therefore worse with use and weight bearing and eases with rest; in advanced disease it may also occur at rest and at night."},{"type":"p","text":"Stiffness after inactivity is brief, usually under 30 minutes, and is often called gelling. It is short because the synovium is not filled with an inflammatory exudate. In rheumatoid arthritis (RA), inflammatory synovitis causes morning stiffness lasting longer than an hour. Examination shows bony enlargement, crepitus as roughened surfaces grind, reduced range of motion, and at most a small cool effusion."},{"type":"p","text":"The joint pattern follows load. OA involves the knees (most often the medial compartment), hips, lumbar and cervical spine, first metatarsophalangeal joint, and in the hand the distal interphalangeal (DIP), proximal interphalangeal (PIP), and first carpometacarpal joints. Heberden nodes at the DIP joints and Bouchard nodes at the PIP joints are osteophytes, so they are hard and bony, unlike the soft, spindle-shaped PIP swelling of RA. The metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and wrists are usually spared. Involvement is asymmetric because each joint has its own load history."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory tests are normal: erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are not raised, and rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies are negative. Synovial fluid is clear and viscous with fewer than about 2,000 white cells per microliter. Radiographs show the cartilage loss indirectly as joint space narrowing that is uneven within the joint, plus osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and subchondral cysts. There is no periarticular osteopenia and no marginal erosion, because there is no inflammatory pannus. Radiographic severity correlates only loosely with pain."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteoarthritis compared with rheumatoid arthritis","cols":["Feature","Osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"rows":[["Driver","Mechanical cartilage failure","Autoimmune synovitis with pannus"],["Morning stiffness","Under 30 minutes","Over 1 hour"],["Pain pattern","Worse with use, better with rest","Worse after rest, eases with activity"],["Hand joints","DIP, PIP, first CMC; MCP and wrist spared","MCP, PIP, wrist; DIP usually spared"],["Distribution","Asymmetric, weight bearing","Symmetric, small joints"],["Synovial fluid WBC","Under about 2,000 per microliter","About 2,000 to 50,000 per microliter"],["Radiograph","Uneven narrowing, osteophytes, sclerosis, cysts","Even narrowing, periarticular osteopenia, marginal erosions"],["Systemic features","None","Fatigue, anemia, raised ESR and CRP"]],"caption":"Stiffness duration, hand joint pattern, and synovial fluid cell count separate the two most often."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C03","obj":"MS-13-E.3","title":"Osteoporosis pathogenesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteoporosis is reduced bone mass with normal mineralization. The bone that remains has a normal ratio of mineral to osteoid; there is simply less of it, with thin cortices and thin, disconnected trabeculae. This distinguishes it from osteomalacia, in which osteoid accumulates because it cannot be mineralized. Loss of architecture, not only loss of mass, lowers strength, so fractures follow minor trauma."},{"type":"p","text":"Adult bone is continually remodeled by basic multicellular units in which osteoclasts resorb a packet of bone and osteoblasts refill it. Bone mass at any age equals peak bone mass, reached in about the third decade, minus later losses. Peak mass is set largely by genetics, with contributions from calcium and vitamin D intake, physical activity, and sex hormones during puberty. A low peak leaves less reserve to lose. From about the fourth decade, each remodeling cycle in both sexes refills slightly less than was removed."},{"type":"p","text":"Estrogen loss at menopause accelerates this imbalance, with the fastest loss in the first several years. Osteoblasts, stromal cells, and osteocytes produce RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their fusion, activation, and survival. Osteoprotegerin (OPG) acts as a decoy receptor that binds RANKL. Estrogen suppresses RANKL, supports OPG, limits interleukin 1, interleukin 6, and TNF production, and promotes osteoclast apoptosis. Without it, more remodeling units are activated, each resorbs more than is replaced, and trabecular bone, with the largest surface area, thins first. Age-related (senile) osteoporosis adds reduced osteoblast number and responsiveness, less physical loading, and lower calcium absorption with mild secondary hyperparathyroidism, so cortical bone is also lost. Estrogen derived from testosterone also protects male bone."},{"type":"flow","title":"Postmenopausal osteoporosis","steps":["Estrogen falls at menopause","RANKL rises, osteoprotegerin falls, IL-1, IL-6, TNF rise","More osteoclasts form and survive longer","Resorption exceeds formation in each remodeling unit","Trabecular bone thins and loses connections first","Fragility fractures of vertebrae, distal radius, then hip"],"caption":"Estrogen loss shifts the RANKL to osteoprotegerin balance toward resorption."},{"type":"table","title":"Secondary causes and their mechanisms","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Glucocorticoids","Inhibit osteoblasts and induce osteoblast and osteocyte apoptosis; raise RANKL, lower OPG; reduce gut calcium absorption"],["Hyperthyroidism","Thyroid hormone increases remodeling rate, with resorption exceeding formation"],["Immobilization","Loss of mechanical loading; osteocytes raise sclerostin and RANKL"],["Alcohol","Direct osteoblast toxicity, hypogonadism, poor nutrition, falls"],["Smoking","Lowers estrogen levels and impairs osteoblast function"]],"caption":"Glucocorticoid excess is the most common cause of secondary osteoporosis and predominantly depresses bone formation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C04","obj":"MS-13-E.4","title":"Interpreting osteoporosis findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteoporosis is silent until a bone breaks, and the fracture sites follow the type of bone lost. Vertebral bodies and the distal radius are rich in trabecular bone, so they fail early, often in the 50s and 60s. Vertebral compression fractures, most common in the mid thoracic spine and at the thoracolumbar junction, collapse the anterior body into a wedge; many are painless and are discovered as height loss or as thoracic kyphosis (dowager hump). A fall on an outstretched hand breaks the distal radius (Colles fracture). Hip fractures of the femoral neck or intertrochanteric region involve both cortical and trabecular bone, occur later, usually after a fall, and carry the greatest morbidity and mortality."},{"type":"p","text":"Serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase (ALP), and PTH are normal. Mineral regulation is intact, and the slow imbalance in remodeling draws on a skeletal calcium reserve far larger than the extracellular pool, so homeostasis holds plasma values steady. ALP may rise briefly while a fracture heals. Abnormal values point to another diagnosis or a secondary cause, as the table shows."},{"type":"table","title":"Serum findings in diseases that lower bone density","cols":["Disease","Calcium","Phosphate","ALP","PTH"],"rows":[["Osteoporosis","Normal","Normal","Normal","Normal"],["Osteomalacia (vitamin D deficiency)","Low or normal","Low","High","High"],["Paget disease of bone","Normal","Normal","High, often markedly","Normal"],["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","Normal or high","High"]],"caption":"Approximate adult reference ranges: total calcium about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL, phosphate about 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL, ALP about 40 to 130 U/L (higher in growing adolescents; varies by laboratory)."},{"type":"p","text":"Plain radiographs detect reduced density only after roughly 30% or more of bone mass is lost, so dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) is used to measure bone mineral density at the lumbar spine, total hip, and femoral neck. The T-score compares the patient with the mean of healthy young adults of the same sex, in standard deviations. A Z-score compares the patient with people of the same age and sex and is used for children, premenopausal women, and younger men, in whom a low value suggests a secondary cause."},{"type":"table","title":"WHO categories by DEXA T-score","cols":["T-score","Category"],"rows":[["Minus 1.0 or higher","Normal"],["Between minus 1.0 and minus 2.5","Osteopenia (low bone mass)"],["Minus 2.5 or lower","Osteoporosis"],["Minus 2.5 or lower plus a fragility fracture","Severe (established) osteoporosis"]],"caption":"A hip or vertebral fragility fracture establishes clinical osteoporosis regardless of the T-score."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a DEXA report","text":"A 70-year-old woman has T-scores of minus 2.2 at the femoral neck and minus 1.4 at L1 to L4, with normal calcium, phosphate, and ALP. Step 1: classify by the lowest valid site, here the femoral neck, so she has osteopenia. Step 2: question the spine value. Osteophytes, facet sclerosis, and aortic calcification from age-related degeneration add mineral to the spine measurement and falsely raise it, so the hip is more reliable in older adults. Step 3: if her lateral spine film shows a wedge compression fracture after minimal trauma, the diagnosis becomes osteoporosis despite a T-score above minus 2.5. Step 4: normal labs fit primary osteoporosis; a high ALP would redirect you to osteomalacia or Paget disease."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why are serum calcium and ALP normal in a woman who has lost a third of her trabecular bone?","explain":"The mineral that remains is normal, PTH and vitamin D regulation are intact, and the slow net loss is buffered by a large skeletal reserve; ALP reflects osteoblast activity, which is not increased.","check":"Compare the paragraph on normal labs and the serum findings table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C05","obj":"MS-13-E.5","title":"Paget disease of bone: mechanism and laboratory findings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Paget disease of bone (osteitis deformans) is a localized disorder of remodeling in which excessive osteoclast activity is followed by rapid, disorganized osteoblastic repair. It affects adults, usually over 55 and rarely before 40, is more common in people of northern European descent, and most often involves the pelvis, spine, skull, femur, and tibia. Most patients have several bones involved (polyostotic). Many are asymptomatic and are found because of a raised ALP or an incidental radiograph."},{"type":"p","text":"The primary abnormality is in the osteoclast. Pagetic osteoclasts are enlarged, carry many more nuclei than normal (up to about 100), and are hyperresponsive to RANKL and vitamin D. Mutations in SQSTM1, which encodes p62, a scaffold protein in the RANK to NF-kappa B pathway, occur in many familial and some sporadic cases and increase osteoclast signaling. A slow paramyxovirus infection of osteoclasts has been proposed but is not proven."},{"type":"flow","title":"Phases of Paget disease","steps":["Lytic phase: giant osteoclasts resorb bone","Marrow replaced by loose, highly vascular fibrous tissue","Mixed phase: osteoblasts rapidly lay down woven bone","Woven bone remodeled into irregular lamellar bone","Sclerotic phase: thick, dense, disorganized bone","Burned-out phase: quiescent mosaic lamellar bone"],"caption":"Phases can coexist in different bones or regions of one bone."},{"type":"p","text":"Because resorption and formation repeat in short, random cycles, the final bone is lamellar bone crossed by irregular cement lines in a jigsaw or mosaic pattern. It is thicker and larger than normal but its collagen is not aligned along lines of stress, so it bends and breaks under loads normal bone tolerates. On imaging, the lytic front in a long bone advances as a sharp, flame-shaped edge, the skull shows a well-defined lytic area (osteoporosis circumscripta) and later a patchy cotton wool appearance, and affected bones show coarse trabeculae, cortical thickening, and enlargement. Radionuclide bone scans show intense uptake."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory findings reflect high turnover with balanced mineral handling. Serum ALP, a marker of osteoblast activity, is raised, often markedly, while calcium, phosphate, and PTH are normal because resorption and formation remain coupled. Resorption markers such as urinary hydroxyproline and N-telopeptide are also raised. Hypercalcemia appears mainly when a patient is immobilized, because formation then falls while resorption continues."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C06","obj":"MS-13-E.6","title":"Complications of Paget disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each complication of Paget disease follows from three properties of pagetic bone: it is enlarged, it is mechanically weak, and it is highly vascular. Enlargement compresses neighboring nerves and narrows foramina and canals. Weakness causes bowing and fracture. Vascularity shunts blood and makes the bone feel warm. Lifelong high cell turnover increases the risk of malignant transformation."},{"type":"table","title":"Complications and their mechanisms","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Bone pain, warmth","Microfractures, hypervascular bone, periosteal stretch; often worse at night"],["Enlarging skull, headache","Calvarial thickening increases hat size and causes frontal bossing"],["Hearing loss","Temporal bone involvement of ossicles and cochlear capsule, or CN VIII compression in the internal acoustic meatus"],["Other cranial and spinal nerve compression","Narrowed foramina; vertebral enlargement causes spinal stenosis; basilar invagination can compress the brainstem"],["Bowing","Weak bone deforms under load: anterolateral femur, anterior tibia"],["Fractures","Transverse (chalkstick) fractures and fissure fractures on the convex side of long bones"],["Secondary osteoarthritis","Deformed bone alters joint mechanics near the hip and knee"],["High-output heart failure","Hypervascular bone acts as arteriovenous shunts, lowering systemic vascular resistance"],["Osteosarcoma","Secondary sarcoma in about 1% overall, higher with extensive disease"]],"caption":"Complications concentrate in heavily involved bones; extensive polyostotic disease carries the highest risk of heart failure and sarcoma."},{"type":"p","text":"High-output heart failure needs extensive disease. Blood flow through hypervascular pagetic bone and the overlying skin can rise many times above normal, and these low-resistance channels behave like arteriovenous fistulas. Total peripheral resistance falls, cardiac output must rise to maintain arterial pressure, and the volume load can exhaust an older heart, especially one already damaged by coronary or valve disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteosarcoma normally peaks in adolescence. A second peak in older adults is largely due to Paget disease and prior radiation. In an older patient with known Paget disease, new or worsening localized pain, a growing mass, or a lytic destructive lesion with soft tissue extension suggests sarcoma, which carries a poor prognosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Predict the change in systemic vascular resistance and cardiac output in a patient with Paget disease involving the pelvis, spine, skull, and both femurs.","explain":"Hypervascular bone forms low-resistance arteriovenous channels, so systemic vascular resistance falls and cardiac output rises to keep arterial pressure, which can progress to high-output failure.","check":"Compare the paragraph on high-output heart failure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C07","obj":"MS-13-E.7","title":"Avascular necrosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis) is infarction of bone and marrow after its blood supply is interrupted. It occurs where bone is covered largely by articular cartilage and fed by end arteries with little collateral flow, so the subchondral region of a convex joint surface is most vulnerable. The femoral head is the most common site. In adults it depends mainly on retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery, which run up the femoral neck beneath the synovial reflection; the artery of the ligament of the head (from the obturator artery) contributes little after childhood. Other sites are the scaphoid, talus, humeral head, and lunate."},{"type":"p","text":"Fracture causes AVN by tearing these vessels. A displaced femoral neck fracture is intracapsular and disrupts the retinacular arteries, while an intertrochanteric fracture lies outside the capsule and seldom does. In the scaphoid, branches of the radial artery enter the distal pole and flow backward to the proximal pole, so a waist fracture leaves the proximal pole without supply."},{"type":"table","title":"Nontraumatic causes and proposed mechanisms","cols":["Cause","Proposed mechanism"],"rows":[["Glucocorticoids (most common nontraumatic)","Marrow fat cells enlarge and raise intraosseous pressure; fat emboli; osteocyte apoptosis"],["Alcohol","Marrow fat accumulation and fat emboli, as with glucocorticoids"],["Sickle cell disease","Sickled red cells occlude marrow sinusoids"],["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Vasculitis, antiphospholipid thrombosis, and glucocorticoid therapy"],["Decompression sickness","Nitrogen bubbles occlude marrow vessels"],["Others","Gaucher disease (marrow packing), radiation, hypercoagulable states"]],"caption":"Many nontraumatic cases share the same end point: raised pressure inside a closed marrow cavity or occlusion of its small vessels."},{"type":"flow","title":"Course of femoral head osteonecrosis","steps":["Blood flow to subchondral bone is interrupted","Marrow and osteocytes die; lacunae empty","Living margin becomes hyperemic and fibrotic","Creeping substitution resorbs dead trabeculae","Weakened subchondral plate fractures (crescent sign)","Articular surface collapses and flattens","Secondary osteoarthritis develops"],"caption":"Repair removes dead bone faster than new bone can carry load, which explains collapse months after the infarct."},{"type":"p","text":"Dead trabeculae keep their mineral, so radiographs are normal early. MRI is the most sensitive test, showing marrow edema and a double-line sign at the boundary between dead and living bone. Later radiographs show sclerosis, a subchondral lucent crescent, and collapse. Patients have deep groin, buttock, or thigh pain on weight bearing, with limited internal rotation of the hip."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 78-year-old woman falls and sustains a displaced femoral neck fracture; her neighbor has an intertrochanteric fracture. Which hip is at greater risk of AVN, and why?","explain":"The femoral neck fracture, because it is intracapsular and tears the retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery; the intertrochanteric fracture is extracapsular and leaves them intact.","check":"Compare the paragraph on fracture and femoral head blood supply."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C08","obj":"MS-13-E.8","title":"Pediatric hip disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three childhood hip disorders are separated mainly by age, because each reflects the weakest structure of the hip at that stage of growth. In the newborn it is a shallow socket and a lax capsule, in the young child it is the blood supply of the femoral head epiphysis, and in the adolescent it is the growth plate (physis)."},{"type":"p","text":"Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) is abnormal development of the acetabulum and femoral head, ranging from a shallow socket to frank dislocation. The acetabulum deepens only when a well-seated femoral head presses into it, so a head that is unstable or out of place leaves the socket shallow. Risk factors are female sex (maternal relaxin and estrogen increase ligament laxity), breech position, first pregnancy, oligohydramnios, and family history. The Barlow test adducts the flexed hip and pushes posteriorly to dislocate an unstable hip; the Ortolani test abducts the hip and lifts the greater trochanter to reduce a dislocated hip with a palpable clunk. Ultrasound is used in early infancy because the femoral head is cartilage until its ossification center appears at about 4 to 6 months. Older infants show limited abduction, a shorter thigh on the affected side (Galeazzi sign), and later a limp. Untreated DDH leads to early osteoarthritis."},{"type":"p","text":"Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic osteonecrosis of the femoral head epiphysis in children, usually boys about 4 to 8 years old. Between infancy and adolescence the epiphysis depends on a limited retinacular supply, and the growth plate blocks flow from the metaphysis. The child has a painless or mildly painful limp, sometimes with knee pain, and limited abduction and internal rotation. The head then fragments, revascularizes, and remodels; younger children remodel better."},{"type":"p","text":"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) occurs in obese adolescents during the growth spurt, more often in boys. Shear force fails the physis through its hypertrophic zone, and the femoral neck displaces anteriorly and upward while the epiphysis stays in the acetabulum, so the epiphysis appears to slip posteriorly and medially off the neck (ice cream off a cone). The hip is externally rotated and loses internal rotation, and hip pain may be referred to the knee through the obturator nerve. SCFE in a child outside the usual age range or in a thin child suggests hypothyroidism, growth hormone deficiency or therapy, or renal osteodystrophy. Unstable slips can cause AVN."},{"type":"table","title":"Pediatric hip disorders compared","cols":["Feature","DDH","Legg-Calve-Perthes","SCFE"],"rows":[["Age","Newborn, infant","About 4 to 8 years","About 10 to 16 years"],["Typical patient","Girl, breech, firstborn","Small, active boy","Obese adolescent"],["Weak point","Shallow acetabulum, lax capsule","Epiphyseal blood supply","Physis during growth spurt"],["Key finding","Positive Barlow or Ortolani","Painless limp, flattened head","Externally rotated leg, knee pain"],["Imaging","Ultrasound, then radiograph","Radiograph, MRI early","Frog-leg lateral radiograph"],["Late risk","Early osteoarthritis","Deformed head, osteoarthritis","AVN, osteoarthritis"]],"caption":"Hip disease often presents as knee pain in children, so the hip must be examined in any limping child."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C09","obj":"MS-13-E.9","title":"Disc degeneration, herniation, and spondylosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each intervertebral disc has a central nucleus pulposus, a notochord-derived gel of proteoglycan, water, and type II collagen, surrounded by the annulus fibrosus, concentric lamellae of fibrocartilage with type I collagen in the outer rings. The nucleus converts axial load into hoop tension in the annulus. With age the nucleus loses proteoglycan and water, the disc loses height, and the annulus develops fissures. A sudden or repetitive load, often flexion with rotation, can then push nucleus material through an annular tear."},{"type":"p","text":"Herniations are usually posterolateral, because the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrow and reinforces only the midline. Most lumbar herniations occur at L4 to L5 or L5 to S1, where load and motion are greatest. Each lumbar root exits the foramen beneath the pedicle of its own vertebra, above the level of the disc below. A posterolateral L4 to L5 herniation therefore misses the exiting L4 root and compresses the L5 root as it descends toward its foramen. A far lateral herniation into the foramen compresses the exiting root instead (L4). In the cervical spine, roots exit above their own vertebra, so the C5 to C6 disc also affects the lower-numbered root, C6. Pain arises from compression and from inflammation provoked by extruded nucleus material, and the outer annulus itself is innervated. A large central herniation can compress the cauda equina, causing saddle anesthesia and urinary retention."},{"type":"flow","title":"From disc degeneration to radiculopathy","steps":["Nucleus loses proteoglycan and water","Disc height falls; annulus develops fissures","Nucleus extrudes posterolaterally beside the PLL","Traversing root compressed and inflamed","Dermatomal pain, weakness, and reflex loss"],"caption":"Posterolateral L4 to L5 herniation produces an L5 radiculopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Lumbosacral radiculopathies","cols":["Root (usual disc)","Weakness","Sensory loss","Reflex"],"rows":[["L4 (L3 to L4)","Knee extension","Medial leg","Patellar reduced"],["L5 (L4 to L5)","Foot dorsiflexion, great toe extension, hip abduction","Lateral leg, dorsum of foot","No reliable reflex"],["S1 (L5 to S1)","Foot plantar flexion","Lateral foot, sole","Achilles reduced"]],"caption":"A positive straight leg raise (pain radiating below the knee at about 30 to 70 degrees) tensions the L5 and S1 roots."},{"type":"table","title":"Spondylosis, spondylolysis, and spondylolisthesis","cols":["Term","Meaning","Typical setting"],"rows":[["Spondylosis","Degenerative disc, facet, and osteophyte change","Older adults; may cause foraminal or canal stenosis"],["Spondylolysis","Defect (stress fracture) of the pars interarticularis","Adolescent athletes with repeated extension, usually L5"],["Spondylolisthesis","Forward slip of a vertebra on the one below","Isthmic L5 on S1 after spondylolysis; degenerative L4 on L5 in older adults"]],"caption":"Spondylosis is the wear, spondylolysis the break, spondylolisthesis the slip."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a lumbar herniation","text":"A 42-year-old man has back pain radiating down the lateral leg to the top of the foot, weak great toe extension, and normal knee and ankle reflexes. Step 1: the sensory and motor pattern matches L5. Step 2: normal patellar (L4) and Achilles (S1) reflexes fit, because L5 has no reliable reflex. Step 3: a posterolateral herniation compresses the traversing root, which is the lower root of the pair, so the disc is L4 to L5. Step 4: if imaging instead showed a far lateral L4 to L5 herniation, the exiting L4 root would be affected and the patellar reflex would fall."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C10","obj":"MS-13-E.10","title":"Adolescent knee disorders","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Growing adolescents have two vulnerable structures at the knee: apophyses, which are cartilage-rich secondary ossification centers at tendon attachments, and the articular cartilage and subchondral bone of the joint surfaces. Rapid growth tightens muscles and tendons, and sports add repeated load, so these structures fail under traction or shear."},{"type":"p","text":"Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle. Repeated quadriceps contraction during running and jumping pulls through the patellar ligament on the immature tubercle and produces small avulsions with reactive new bone. Athletic adolescents, often around 10 to 15 years old during the growth spurt, have pain and a tender bony prominence below the patella that is worse with kneeling, jumping, and stairs. It resolves when the apophysis fuses, sometimes leaving a permanent bump or a separate ossicle."},{"type":"p","text":"Chondromalacia patellae is softening and fibrillation of the articular cartilage on the back of the patella, usually in adolescents and young adults, more often female. Abnormal patellar tracking and repetitive patellofemoral loading concentrate stress on the cartilage. Anterior knee pain worsens with stairs, squatting, and prolonged sitting with the knees flexed, when patellofemoral pressure is high, and crepitus may be felt. Its cartilage breakdown resembles early osteoarthritis but is localized to the patella and occurs in the young."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteochondritis dissecans is separation of a fragment of subchondral bone with its overlying cartilage, most often from the lateral aspect of the medial femoral condyle. Repetitive microtrauma and a fragile subchondral blood supply are the leading explanations. A stable fragment causes activity-related pain and effusion; a detached fragment becomes a loose body that causes catching or locking. Lesions in children with open physes heal more readily than those in adults."},{"type":"table","title":"Adolescent knee disorders compared","cols":["Disorder","Site","Mechanism","Key clue"],"rows":[["Osgood-Schlatter disease","Tibial tubercle apophysis","Traction by the patellar ligament","Tender bump below the patella in a jumping athlete"],["Chondromalacia patellae","Patellar articular cartilage","Cartilage softening from patellofemoral overload","Anterior knee pain with stairs or long sitting"],["Osteochondritis dissecans","Subchondral bone and cartilage, medial femoral condyle","Fragment separation from microtrauma and poor blood supply","Effusion, catching, or locking"]],"caption":"Location answers most questions: below the patella, behind the patella, or within the femoral condyle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-05-C11","obj":"MS-13-E.11","title":"Dupuytren contracture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dupuytren contracture is a superficial fibromatosis of the palmar aponeurosis, the dense fascial sheet that lies under the palmar skin, superficial to the flexor tendons. Fibroblasts in the fascia differentiate into myofibroblasts, which contain alpha smooth muscle actin, contract like smooth muscle, and secrete collagen, initially type III. Transforming growth factor beta drives this differentiation. Proliferating myofibroblasts first form firm nodules in the palm, then longitudinal cords along the fascial bands. As the cords contract and are stabilized by new collagen, they shorten and pull the fingers into flexion at the metacarpophalangeal joints and then the proximal interphalangeal joints."},{"type":"p","text":"The ring finger is most often affected, followed by the little finger, and both hands are often involved. The contracture is usually painless, although early nodules may be tender. Because the flexor tendons themselves are normal, the finger cannot be fully extended passively or actively, and the patient cannot lay the palm flat on a table. Cords can also displace the digital neurovascular bundles."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk is highest in men over about 50 of northern European ancestry, with a family history consistent with autosomal dominant inheritance of variable penetrance. Diabetes mellitus, heavy alcohol use, smoking, and epilepsy are associated. The same myofibroblast process in other sites produces plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose disease) and penile fibromatosis (Peyronie disease), which may coexist with Dupuytren contracture."},{"type":"table","title":"Flexed ring and little fingers: distinguishing causes","cols":["Condition","Structure affected","Distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Dupuytren contracture","Palmar fascia (myofibroblast cords)","Palpable cords and nodules; fixed flexion; tendons normal"],["Trigger finger","Flexor tendon at the A1 pulley","Catching or locking that releases with a snap"],["Ulnar claw hand","Ulnar nerve lesion paralyzing medial lumbricals","MCP hyperextension with IP flexion; sensory loss and intrinsic weakness"]],"caption":"Dupuytren contracture flexes the MCP joint; ulnar claw hand hyperextends it."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteoporosis and Paget disease are both disorders of remodeling, and the coupling of osteoclasts to osteoblasts predicts their labs when they are set beside osteomalacia. In osteoporosis, resorption slightly exceeds formation but both are within normal limits, so ALP is normal. In Paget disease, both are greatly increased and remain coupled, so ALP rises while calcium stays normal. In osteomalacia, osteoid is made but not mineralized, so ALP and PTH rise, phosphate falls, and calcium is low or low normal. Avascular necrosis adds anatomy: the same retinacular arteries explain why femoral neck fractures, Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, and unstable SCFE all threaten the femoral head, while sickle cell disease, lupus, and decompression sickness reach bone through the circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Cartilage, disc, and fascia fail through matrix biology. Osteoarthritis and disc degeneration both begin with proteoglycan and water loss that removes a tissue's ability to bear load, and both later cause osteophytes that can narrow joints and foramina. Clinical correlation has limits: radiographic OA and MRI disc bulges are common in people without symptoms, so a finding must match the patient's pain pattern and neurologic signs before it explains them."}],"summary":["Osteoarthritis is mechanical cartilage failure: catabolic chondrocytes degrade aggrecan and type II collagen, and bone responds with sclerosis, eburnation, cysts, and osteophytes.","Osteoarthritis causes use-related pain, stiffness under 30 minutes, DIP and PIP bony nodes, normal labs, and uneven joint space narrowing without erosions.","Osteoporosis is less bone of normal composition; estrogen loss raises RANKL and lowers osteoprotegerin, and trabecular bone thins first.","Osteoporosis labs are normal; DEXA T-score of minus 2.5 or lower defines osteoporosis, and minus 1 to minus 2.5 defines osteopenia.","Paget disease starts with giant hyperactive osteoclasts, ends with thick, weak mosaic bone, and shows an isolated high ALP.","Paget complications follow from enlarged, weak, hypervascular bone: nerve compression, hearing loss, fractures, high-output heart failure, and osteosarcoma.","Avascular necrosis is bone infarction in end-arterial subchondral bone; the femoral head, fed by the medial circumflex femoral artery, is most common, and MRI detects it first.","Age separates pediatric hip disorders: DDH in newborns, Legg-Calve-Perthes at about 4 to 8 years, SCFE in obese adolescents.","Posterolateral lumbar disc herniation compresses the traversing lower root, so L4 to L5 affects L5.","Osgood-Schlatter is tibial tubercle traction apophysitis; Dupuytren contracture is myofibroblast shortening of palmar fascia that flexes the ring and little fingers."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb: hip joint and blood supply of the femoral head; knee joint"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: intervertebral discs, spinal nerve roots, and disc herniation"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb: palmar aponeurosis and blood supply of the scaphoid"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Section on metabolic bone disease and osteoporosis"}]},"MS-13-F":{"unit":"MS-13-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06","title":"Musculoskeletal Neoplasms","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers tumors of bone, cartilage, and soft tissue: the benign bone-forming and cartilage-forming lesions, the primary sarcomas (osteosarcoma, chondrosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, leiomyosarcoma), the giant cell tumors, metastatic disease in bone, and hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy. Board questions on these tumors almost always supply the same five clues: the patient's age, the bone and the region within it, the x-ray pattern, the matrix or cell the tumor makes, and the gene or marker that confirms it. The chapter explains why each clue points where it does, so you can reason from growth plate biology, bone remodeling, and driver mutations instead of memorizing a list.","prerequisites":["Regions of a long bone (epiphysis, physis, metaphysis, diaphysis) and endochondral ossification","Bone remodeling: osteoblasts, osteoclasts, RANKL, osteoprotegerin, and PTH","Tumor suppressor genes, oncogenes, chromosomal translocations, and the two-hit hypothesis","Hematogenous versus lymphatic spread of malignant tumors","Calcium homeostasis: PTH, PTH-related protein (PTHrP), and vitamin D"],"connections":[{"to":"Degenerative musculoskeletal disease (MS-13-E)","how":"Paget disease drives intense bone turnover and is the main soil for osteosarcoma in older adults."},{"to":"Infectious musculoskeletal disease (MS-13-G)","how":"Ewing sarcoma shares fever, a high ESR, and periosteal reaction with osteomyelitis, so biopsy separates them."},{"to":"Blood and lymph neoplasms (HE-9-D)","how":"Multiple myeloma is the most common primary malignancy in bone but arises from plasma cells and makes purely lytic lesions."},{"to":"Reproductive dysplasia and neoplasms (RE-13-J)","how":"Prostate carcinoma is the classic osteoblastic metastasis, and the uterus is a main site of leiomyosarcoma."},{"to":"Neoplasms of the pulmonary system and upper respiratory tract (PU-14-F)","how":"Lung cancer causes lytic metastases and hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy, and the lung is the usual metastatic target of sarcomas."}],"terms":[{"term":"Sarcoma","def":"A malignant tumor of mesenchymal origin, such as bone, cartilage, muscle, fat, fibrous tissue, or vessels."},{"term":"Osteoid","def":"Unmineralized bone matrix, mainly type I collagen, made by osteoblasts; osteoid made by malignant cells defines osteosarcoma."},{"term":"Nidus","def":"The small central focus of osteoid and woven bone in osteoid osteoma, surrounded by reactive sclerotic bone."},{"term":"Periosteal reaction","def":"New bone laid down by periosteum that an underlying process has lifted; its pattern reflects how fast that process grows."},{"term":"Codman triangle","def":"A wedge of reactive bone at the edge of a lesion where lifted periosteum meets the cortex, a sign of aggressive growth."},{"term":"Small round blue cell tumor","def":"A tumor of small primitive cells with scant cytoplasm and dark nuclei on H and E stain, such as Ewing sarcoma, neuroblastoma, lymphoma, and rhabdomyosarcoma."},{"term":"Oncometabolite","def":"A metabolite that accumulates because of a mutant enzyme and promotes tumor growth, such as 2-hydroxyglutarate from mutant IDH1 or IDH2."},{"term":"Rhabdomyoblast","def":"A malignant cell showing skeletal muscle differentiation, with eccentric eosinophilic cytoplasm and sometimes cross-striations."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"Receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa B ligand, the membrane protein on osteoblast-lineage cells that drives osteoclast formation and activity."},{"term":"Pathologic fracture","def":"A fracture through bone weakened by disease, under a force that normal bone would withstand."},{"term":"Paraneoplastic syndrome","def":"Signs and symptoms caused by a tumor's products or the immune response to it, not by local invasion or metastasis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C01","obj":"MS-13-F.1","title":"Chondrosarcoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chondrosarcoma is a malignant tumor whose cells make cartilage matrix but no osteoid. The distinction matters because osteosarcoma may also contain malignant cartilage; if the tumor cells make any osteoid directly, the tumor is classified as osteosarcoma. Chondrosarcoma is the second most common matrix-producing primary malignant bone tumor, about half as frequent as osteosarcoma, and it affects adults, usually in their 40s or older, men more often than women."},{"type":"p","text":"It favors the trunk and the proximal limbs: pelvis, shoulder girdle (proximal humerus and scapula), proximal femur, and ribs. This is nearly the reverse of enchondroma, which favors the small bones of the hands and feet, so a cartilage tumor in the pelvis of an adult is regarded as malignant until proven otherwise."},{"type":"h","text":"Primary and secondary tumors"},{"type":"p","text":"Most chondrosarcomas are conventional (hyaline) tumors in the medullary cavity. Primary tumors arise de novo, while secondary tumors arise in a preexisting enchondroma or in the cartilage cap of an osteochondroma (C07). Transformation is rare in a solitary benign lesion and much more likely when there are many lesions, as in Ollier disease, Maffucci syndrome, and hereditary multiple osteochondromas, because every lesion is another chance for further genetic damage. Signs of transformation are new pain in a previously silent lesion, growth after skeletal maturity, cortical destruction, and a soft tissue mass."},{"type":"h","text":"IDH mutations"},{"type":"p","text":"About half of conventional central chondrosarcomas, and most enchondromas, carry a mutation in IDH1 (cytosolic) or IDH2 (mitochondrial). The normal enzyme converts isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate. The mutant enzyme gains a new activity and reduces alpha-ketoglutarate to D-2-hydroxyglutarate, which inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate-dependent enzymes such as the TET DNA demethylases and histone demethylases. DNA and histones become hypermethylated, and chondrocytes stay immature and proliferative. Because benign enchondromas carry the same mutation, it is an early event; malignant progression needs more changes, such as loss of CDKN2A. The same oncometabolite operates in some gliomas and in acute myeloid leukemia."},{"type":"flow","title":"How mutant IDH promotes cartilage tumors","steps":["Point mutation in IDH1 or IDH2","Mutant enzyme converts alpha-ketoglutarate to 2-hydroxyglutarate","2-hydroxyglutarate inhibits TET and histone demethylases","DNA and histones become hypermethylated","Chondrocyte differentiation is blocked; proliferation continues","Enchondroma, then chondrosarcoma after further mutations"],"caption":"An early metabolic mutation reshapes the epigenome; additional hits such as CDKN2A loss convert a benign lesion into chondrosarcoma."},{"type":"p","text":"On x-ray, chondrosarcoma is a lytic lesion with ring-and-arc calcification of cartilage lobules, deep scalloping of the inner cortex, and cortical thickening or destruction. Behavior follows histologic grade: low-grade tumors grow slowly and rarely metastasize, while high-grade and dedifferentiated tumors spread through the blood, mainly to the lungs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C02","obj":"MS-13-F.2","title":"Ewing sarcoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ewing sarcoma is a malignant tumor of primitive small round cells that arises mostly in bone and less often in soft tissue. It is the second most common bone sarcoma of childhood after osteosarcoma. Most patients are 10 to 20 years old, boys slightly more often than girls, and it is much more common in people of European ancestry than of African ancestry. It arises in the diaphysis of long bones, especially the femur, and in the flat bones of the pelvis."},{"type":"h","text":"Molecular driver"},{"type":"p","text":"About 85% of tumors carry t(11;22)(q24;q12), which fuses EWSR1 on chromosome 22 to FLI1 on chromosome 11; most of the rest fuse EWSR1 to ERG, another ETS family gene, on chromosome 21. The chimeric protein joins the EWSR1 activation domain to the FLI1 DNA-binding domain. It binds GGAA repeat sequences, switches on genes that drive proliferation, and represses genes of differentiation. The cell of origin is uncertain, probably a primitive mesenchymal cell. Finding the fusion by FISH or RT-PCR confirms the diagnosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From translocation to tumor in Ewing sarcoma","steps":["t(11;22) joins EWSR1 to FLI1","Chimeric EWSR1-FLI1 transcription factor forms","Fusion protein binds GGAA repeats across the genome","Growth genes activated, differentiation genes repressed","Primitive small round cells proliferate in marrow"],"caption":"A translocation creates an aberrant transcription factor that locks primitive cells in a proliferative state."},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopically the tumor is sheets of uniform small round cells with scant clear cytoplasm rich in glycogen (PAS positive), sometimes forming Homer Wright rosettes. The cells show strong membranous CD99, which is sensitive but not specific, since lymphoblastic lymphoma and other small round cell tumors can also express it. The differential diagnosis includes neuroblastoma, lymphoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, and small cell osteosarcoma."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor permeates the marrow quickly, producing moth-eaten lytic destruction and a large soft tissue mass. It lifts the periosteum in successive waves, and each wave lays down a thin shell of reactive bone, giving a layered onion-skin periosteal reaction. Patients have pain and swelling, often with fever, anemia, leukocytosis, and a high ESR, because tumor necrosis and cytokines such as IL-6 produce a systemic inflammatory response. This picture closely mimics osteomyelitis, which also causes periosteal reaction. Ewing sarcoma spreads through the blood to the lungs, other bones, and bone marrow."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 12-year-old has thigh pain, fever, a high ESR, and a lytic lesion in the femoral shaft with layered periosteal new bone. Two weeks of antibiotics do not help. What would the biopsy most likely show?","explain":"Sheets of small round blue cells with membranous CD99 and an EWSR1-FLI1 fusion. Ewing sarcoma causes fever and inflammatory markers through tumor necrosis and cytokines, and its diaphyseal site and onion-skin reaction fit; osteomyelitis would show neutrophils, necrotic bone, and organisms.","check":"The presentation paragraph and the location of Ewing sarcoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C03","obj":"MS-13-F.3","title":"Osteoid osteoma and osteoblastoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteoid osteoma is a benign bone-forming tumor less than 2 cm across. It affects teenagers and young adults, males about twice as often as females, and usually arises in the cortex of long bones of the lower limb, especially the femur and tibia. The lesion is a nidus of interlacing trabeculae of woven bone and osteoid lined by plump osteoblasts in a loose, vascular stroma. The surrounding host bone responds by laying down dense reactive bone, which can be much thicker than the nidus itself. On x-ray or CT, a small round lucent nidus, sometimes with a mineralized center, sits inside a zone of cortical sclerosis."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the pain responds to NSAIDs"},{"type":"p","text":"The osteoblasts of the nidus produce large amounts of prostaglandin E2 through cyclooxygenase (COX), and the nidus contains nerve fibers. Prostaglandin E2 sensitizes nociceptors and causes local vasodilation and edema. The result is severe, well-localized pain that is classically worse at night and relieved by aspirin or other NSAIDs, which inhibit COX and remove the source of the prostaglandin."},{"type":"p","text":"When osteoid osteoma arises in the posterior elements of a vertebra, it can cause painful scoliosis. Reflex spasm of the paraspinal muscles on the side of the lesion pulls the spine into a curve that is concave toward the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoblastoma has nearly the same histology, so size and site separate the two. It is larger than 2 cm, favors the posterior elements of the vertebrae (lamina and pedicle), causes a dull aching pain that does not respond to NSAIDs, provokes less reactive sclerosis, and can enlarge and behave in a locally aggressive way."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteoid osteoma versus osteoblastoma","cols":["Feature","Osteoid osteoma","Osteoblastoma"],"rows":[["Size","Less than 2 cm","Greater than 2 cm"],["Usual site","Cortex of femur and tibia","Posterior elements of vertebrae"],["Pain","Severe, worse at night","Dull, aching"],["Response to NSAIDs","Relieved, because the nidus makes prostaglandin E2","Not relieved"],["Reactive sclerosis","Marked, surrounds the nidus","Less prominent"],["Growth","Self-limited","Can enlarge and be locally aggressive"]],"caption":"Shared histology (woven bone, osteoid, and osteoblasts in vascular stroma); size, site, and NSAID response distinguish them."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C04","obj":"MS-13-F.4","title":"Osteosarcoma: definition, age, and site","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteosarcoma is a malignant mesenchymal tumor in which the neoplastic cells make osteoid. It is the most common primary malignant tumor of bone once multiple myeloma is set aside; myeloma is more common, but it is a plasma cell neoplasm that happens to live in marrow rather than a tumor of bone-forming cells. Osteosarcoma has a bimodal age distribution. About 75% of patients are younger than 20 years, and a second peak occurs in older adults, who usually have a predisposing condition such as Paget disease of bone or prior radiation. Males are affected somewhat more often."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the knee metaphysis"},{"type":"p","text":"Most osteosarcomas arise in the metaphysis of long bones, about 60% around the knee (distal femur and proximal tibia), followed by the proximal humerus. These growth plates contribute the most to limb length, so their metaphyses hold the largest population of dividing osteoblastic cells during the adolescent growth spurt, and each division is a chance for a mutation to be copied. The same logic explains the older peak: Paget disease drives intense, disorganized remodeling with high osteoblast turnover, and radiation damages the DNA of cells that keep dividing. In older adults the tumor arises in the pagetic or irradiated bone itself, which is often axial or flat bone."},{"type":"h","text":"Pathology and presentation"},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly the tumor is bulky, gritty, and gray-white with hemorrhage and necrosis. It destroys the cortex and extends into soft tissue; the growth plate and articular cartilage are only relative barriers, so joint invasion is less common. Microscopically, pleomorphic cells with hyperchromatic nuclei, bizarre giant cells, and atypical mitoses make lace-like malignant osteoid. Some tumors also make cartilage or fibrous tissue, but osteoid made directly by the tumor cells defines the diagnosis. Conventional osteosarcoma is intramedullary and high grade. Patients present with a painful, enlarging mass and sometimes a pathologic fracture, and serum alkaline phosphatase is often raised, reflecting osteoblastic activity."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteosarcoma in adolescents and older adults","cols":["Feature","Adolescent (primary)","Older adult (secondary)"],"rows":[["Share of cases","About 75% occur before age 20","Smaller second peak"],["Setting","Usually no prior bone disease; germline RB1 or TP53 in some","Paget disease, prior radiation, bone infarcts"],["Usual site","Metaphysis around the knee, proximal humerus","Affected bone, often axial or flat bones"],["Why cells are at risk","Rapid physeal growth and osteoblast division","High remodeling turnover or radiation DNA damage"]],"caption":"Both peaks arise where osteoblastic cells divide most, whether from normal growth or from disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C05","obj":"MS-13-F.5","title":"Osteosarcoma: imaging, genetics, and metastasis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"On x-ray, osteosarcoma is a large destructive lesion with mixed lytic and sclerotic areas, poorly defined margins, cortical breakthrough, and a soft tissue mass. Two periosteal signs are tested. A Codman triangle forms where the tumor has broken through the cortex and lifted the periosteum: at the edge, where the periosteum still meets the cortex at an angle, it lays down a wedge of reactive bone, while centrally the tumor grows too fast for new bone to form. It marks an aggressive process, not osteosarcoma specifically, since Ewing sarcoma, metastases, and osteomyelitis can produce it. A sunburst pattern is made of spicules of mineralized tumor osteoid and reactive bone radiating perpendicular to the cortex into the soft tissue mass. Because the soft tissue component itself mineralizes, the sunburst points strongly toward a bone-forming malignancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Periosteal reactions and what they mean","cols":["Pattern","Growth it reflects","Typical example"],"rows":[["Solid, thick","Slow; periosteum has time to make mature bone","Osteoid osteoma, healing fracture"],["Lamellated (onion skin)","Rapid growth in repeated waves","Ewing sarcoma, osteomyelitis"],["Codman triangle","Aggressive breakthrough lifting the periosteum","Osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, others"],["Sunburst (spiculated)","Aggressive, with mineralizing tumor in soft tissue","Osteosarcoma"]],"caption":"The slower a lesion grows, the more organized the periosteal bone; aggressive patterns indicate growth rate, not a single diagnosis."},{"type":"h","text":"RB1 and TP53"},{"type":"p","text":"The RB protein, when hypophosphorylated, binds E2F transcription factors and holds the cell in G1. The p53 protein senses DNA damage and halts the cycle through p21 or triggers apoptosis. Losing RB removes the G1 checkpoint, and losing p53 lets damaged cells keep dividing and accumulate chromosomal chaos, a hallmark of osteosarcoma. Germline RB1 mutation (hereditary retinoblastoma) raises osteosarcoma risk many times over, especially within radiation fields, and germline TP53 mutation (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) also predisposes. Sporadic tumors show the same pathways disrupted somatically: RB1 loss, TP53 mutation or MDM2 amplification, and CDKN2A deletion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hereditary retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma","steps":["Germline RB1 mutation inactivates one allele in every cell","Second hit in a retinal cell causes childhood retinoblastoma","Second hit in a dividing osteoblastic cell removes the G1 checkpoint","Cells enter S phase without control and gather mutations","Osteosarcoma, often in adolescence or a radiation field"],"caption":"The two-hit model: every cell starts with one hit, so a single somatic event in a dividing bone cell is enough."},{"type":"h","text":"Metastasis to lung"},{"type":"p","text":"Like most sarcomas, osteosarcoma invades thin-walled vessels early and spreads through the blood rather than the lymphatics. Venous blood from the limbs returns to the right heart, so the pulmonary capillaries are the first bed that tumor emboli reach. The lungs are therefore the most common metastatic site, and many patients have microscopic lung metastases at diagnosis; other bones are next."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning through a bone tumor film","text":"A 16-year-old boy has 2 months of knee pain. X-ray shows a destructive lesion of the distal femoral metaphysis with a Codman triangle and radiating spicules in a soft tissue mass. Step 1, age: a teenager points to osteosarcoma or Ewing sarcoma. Step 2, site: metaphysis favors osteosarcoma, while Ewing sarcoma favors the diaphysis. Step 3, imaging: a Codman triangle says aggressive; a sunburst says the soft tissue mass is mineralizing, which means bone matrix. Step 4, confirmation: biopsy should show pleomorphic cells making osteoid. Step 5, prediction: hematogenous spread makes the lung the first metastatic site. Ask too about family history of retinoblastoma, which would suggest germline RB1."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C06","obj":"MS-13-F.6","title":"Rhabdomyosarcoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignant tumor of cells showing skeletal muscle differentiation, and it is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of children and adolescents. It often arises where there is little or no skeletal muscle, such as the bladder and vagina, which suggests it comes from primitive mesenchymal cells that switch on the muscle program rather than from mature muscle. The tumor cell is the rhabdomyoblast, with eccentric eosinophilic cytoplasm containing thick and thin filaments; elongated strap cells and occasional cross-striations may be seen."},{"type":"table","title":"Subtypes of rhabdomyosarcoma","cols":["Subtype","Patients and sites","Histology","Genetics","Prognosis"],"rows":[["Embryonal (most common)","Children under about 10; head and neck (orbit, nasopharynx), genitourinary tract","Spindle and round cells in loose myxoid stroma","Loss of heterozygosity at 11p15; RAS pathway mutations","Intermediate"],["Botryoid (embryonal variant)","Infants and young children; under the mucosa of vagina or bladder","Grape-like polyps with a dense layer of tumor cells under the epithelium (cambium layer)","As embryonal","Best"],["Alveolar","Adolescents; deep muscles of the extremities","Round cells lining fibrous septa like lung alveoli","PAX3-FOXO1 t(2;13) or PAX7-FOXO1 t(1;13)","Worse"],["Pleomorphic","Adults; deep soft tissue of the limbs","Large bizarre cells","Complex karyotype","Poor"]],"caption":"Age, site, and fusion status separate the subtypes; the fusion-positive alveolar type behaves most aggressively in children."},{"type":"h","text":"Markers and the alveolar fusion"},{"type":"p","text":"Desmin is the intermediate filament of all muscle, so it confirms muscle lineage but not which kind. Myogenin (MYF4) and MYOD1 are transcription factors that drive skeletal muscle differentiation, and they appear as nuclear staining, so they identify skeletal muscle specifically. Alveolar tumors typically show strong, diffuse myogenin."},{"type":"p","text":"PAX3 and PAX7 are transcription factors of embryonic muscle precursor cells. In the alveolar type, a translocation joins the DNA-binding domain of PAX3 or PAX7 to the potent activation domain of FOXO1. The chimeric protein activates PAX target genes far more strongly than normal, which keeps cells proliferating as precursors that cannot complete differentiation. This explains the aggressive behavior of fusion-positive tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms follow the site. An orbital tumor causes proptosis, a nasopharyngeal tumor causes nasal obstruction and bleeding, and bladder or vaginal tumors cause hematuria, vaginal bleeding, or a grape-like mass protruding from the vagina. Unlike most sarcomas, rhabdomyosarcoma often reaches regional lymph nodes, especially the alveolar type, in addition to spreading through blood to the lungs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C07","obj":"MS-13-F.7","title":"Benign cartilage tumors: osteochondroma and enchondroma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Osteochondroma is the most common benign bone tumor. It is found in adolescents and young adults, males about three times as often as females. It arises from the metaphysis next to the growth plate of long bones, especially around the knee, and grows away from the nearest joint. The lesion is a bony stalk capped by hyaline cartilage, and the cortex and marrow of the stalk are continuous with those of the host bone, which is the key imaging feature. The cap behaves like a growth plate: endochondral ossification at its base builds the stalk, and growth stops when the physes close at skeletal maturity."},{"type":"p","text":"About 85% are solitary. Multiple lesions occur in hereditary multiple osteochondromas, an autosomal dominant disorder caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in EXT1 or EXT2; in both settings the cap chondrocytes have lost both copies. EXT1 and EXT2 encode glycosyltransferases that build heparan sulfate chains on proteoglycans. Heparan sulfate shapes the diffusion and signaling of Indian hedgehog in the growth plate, so without it chondrocytes lose their orientation and a clone grows sideways out of the physis. Patients with the hereditary form have many lesions, short stature, and limb deformities."},{"type":"p","text":"Enchondroma is a benign nodule of hyaline cartilage inside the medullary cavity, probably derived from growth plate cartilage that was never replaced by bone. It appears from the 20s to the 50s and is most common in the short tubular bones of the hands and feet. It is usually solitary and painless, found incidentally or after a pathologic fracture of a finger. The x-ray shows a well-defined central lucency with ring-and-arc calcification, a thin intact cortex, and no periosteal reaction. Most enchondromas carry IDH1 or IDH2 mutations (C01)."},{"type":"p","text":"Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome are forms of enchondromatosis, with multiple enchondromas that often favor one side of the body. They are not inherited; they result from a postzygotic, mosaic IDH1 or IDH2 mutation, which explains the patchy distribution. Maffucci syndrome adds soft tissue hemangiomas and a risk of other tumors such as brain gliomas. Both carry a substantial risk of chondrosarcoma because of the number of lesions."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteochondroma versus enchondroma","cols":["Feature","Osteochondroma","Enchondroma"],"rows":[["Location","Surface; metaphysis near the knee","Medullary cavity; hand and foot bones"],["Structure","Bony stalk with a cartilage cap, marrow continuous with host bone","Lobules of hyaline cartilage within marrow"],["Age","Adolescents and young adults","20s to 50s"],["Genetics","EXT1 or EXT2","IDH1 or IDH2"],["Multiple form","Hereditary multiple osteochondromas (autosomal dominant)","Ollier disease; Maffucci syndrome with hemangiomas (mosaic, not inherited)"],["Malignant change","Rare if solitary; higher in the hereditary form","Rare if solitary; substantial in Ollier and Maffucci"]],"caption":"Both are benign hyaline cartilage tumors linked to growth plate cartilage; either can give rise to secondary chondrosarcoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C08","obj":"MS-13-F.8","title":"Giant cell tumor of bone and tenosynovial giant cell tumor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Giant cell tumor of bone is a benign but locally aggressive tumor of adults aged about 20 to 40 years, slightly more often women. It arises after the growth plate has closed, in the epiphysis, and extends into the metaphysis up to the subchondral bone beneath the joint. About half occur around the knee, and the distal radius and proximal humerus are next. The x-ray shows an eccentric, expansile, purely lytic lesion with a thin shell of reactive bone and no matrix mineralization, the soap-bubble appearance. Because it lies against the joint, it can cause pain that mimics arthritis, and it may present with pathologic fracture."},{"type":"h","text":"Who does the damage"},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor contains two cell populations. The neoplastic cells are mononuclear stromal cells of osteoblast lineage, most of which carry a mutation in the histone gene H3-3A (formerly H3F3A, G34W). They express RANKL, which binds RANK on monocyte-macrophage precursors and drives them to fuse into large, non-neoplastic osteoclast-like giant cells with many nuclei. These giant cells resorb the bone. The mechanism is confirmed by RANKL blockade with an antibody such as denosumab, which makes the giant cells disappear. The tumor often recurs locally and rarely sends slow-growing deposits to the lung."},{"type":"p","text":"Tenosynovial giant cell tumor is a benign neoplasm of the synovium of joints, bursae, and tendon sheaths, usually in adults aged about 20 to 50. The localized type is a small, firm nodule on a tendon sheath, most often of the fingers and hand. The diffuse type, formerly called pigmented villonodular synovitis, involves a large joint, most often the knee, as brown villous synovium that causes recurrent bloody effusions and can erode bone."},{"type":"p","text":"Most tumors carry a translocation, often t(1;2) joining CSF1 to the COL6A3 promoter, that causes overexpression of colony-stimulating factor 1. Only a minority of cells carry it. The secreted CSF1 recruits macrophages bearing the CSF1 receptor, which make up most of the mass, so the tumor is largely reactive cells gathered by a few neoplastic ones. Repeated bleeding loads these macrophages with hemosiderin, explaining the brown color, foam cells, and multinucleated giant cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Two giant cell tumors compared","cols":["Feature","Giant cell tumor of bone","Tenosynovial giant cell tumor"],"rows":[["Tissue","Epiphysis of long bone","Synovium, bursa, or tendon sheath"],["Typical patient","Adult 20 to 40, closed growth plates","Adult; fingers (localized) or knee (diffuse)"],["Neoplastic cell","Mononuclear stromal cell with H3-3A mutation","Synovial cell overexpressing CSF1"],["Signal to reactive cells","RANKL recruits osteoclast-like giant cells","CSF1 recruits macrophages"],["Key finding","Soap-bubble lytic lesion to subchondral bone","Brown, hemosiderin-rich mass; bloody effusions"]],"caption":"In both, a small neoplastic population secretes a signal that recruits the reactive cells forming most of the tumor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C09","obj":"MS-13-F.9","title":"Metastases to bone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metastases are the most common malignant tumors in bone, far outnumbering primary bone sarcomas. Two facts explain this. Carcinomas are much more common than sarcomas, which make up only about 1% of adult cancers. And red marrow is fertile soil: it receives high blood flow through slow sinusoids with fenestrated endothelium, and its matrix stores growth factors that tumor cells can use. Metastases therefore settle in bones that keep red marrow in adults, the vertebrae, pelvis, ribs, skull, and proximal femur and humerus, and are uncommon beyond the elbow and knee. The valveless vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus) connects pelvic and breast veins to the spine, so a rise in abdominal pressure can carry prostate or breast cancer cells straight to the vertebrae. In adults most bone metastases come from prostate, breast, kidney, lung, and thyroid; in children, from neuroblastoma, Wilms tumor, and rhabdomyosarcoma."},{"type":"h","text":"Lytic, blastic, and mixed lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"The pattern depends on which bone cell the tumor drives. Lytic tumors secrete PTHrP and cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF, which raise RANKL on osteoblasts and stromal cells; osteoclasts then resorb bone and release stored TGF-beta and IGF, which stimulate the tumor further. This loop is called the vicious cycle. Prostate cancer instead secretes osteoblast stimulants such as endothelin-1 and bone morphogenetic proteins, so osteoblasts lay down dense woven bone around the deposits."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The vicious cycle of osteolytic metastasis","steps":["Tumor cells in marrow secrete PTHrP and cytokines","Osteoblasts and stromal cells raise RANKL","Osteoclasts form and resorb bone","Matrix releases TGF-beta, IGF, and calcium","Growth factors stimulate tumor growth and more PTHrP"],"caption":"Bone destruction feeds the tumor, and the tumor drives more bone destruction."},{"type":"table","title":"Patterns of bone metastasis by primary site","cols":["Primary","Pattern","Notes"],"rows":[["Prostate","Osteoblastic (sclerotic)","Endothelin-1 and BMPs; lumbar spine and pelvis via Batson plexus"],["Breast","Mixed, mostly lytic","Most common source of bone metastasis in women"],["Kidney (renal cell)","Lytic, often expansile","Hypervascular deposits"],["Thyroid","Lytic","Follicular carcinoma spreads through blood to bone"],["Lung","Mostly lytic","Non-small cell carcinoma; can be mixed"],["Multiple myeloma (comparison)","Purely lytic, punched-out","Primary plasma cell tumor; osteoblasts suppressed"]],"caption":"Blastic lesions reflect stimulated osteoblasts; lytic lesions reflect RANKL-driven osteoclasts."},{"type":"h","text":"Complications and laboratory findings"},{"type":"p","text":"Lytic lesions remove load-bearing cortical bone, so pathologic fracture is common, especially in the femur and spine, where vertebral collapse can compress the spinal cord. Blastic lesions also fracture, because the new woven bone is poorly organized. Hypercalcemia results from local osteolysis and from tumor PTHrP acting on bone and kidney; in either case PTH is suppressed by negative feedback. Alkaline phosphatase rises with osteoblast activity, so it is high in blastic disease. A technetium-99m phosphonate bone scan shows sites of active new bone formation, so blastic lesions show strongly while purely lytic lesions may be missed. Marrow replacement can cause a leukoerythroblastic blood picture."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why might a bone scan miss the lesions of multiple myeloma yet show those of prostate cancer clearly?","explain":"The tracer binds where osteoblasts are mineralizing new bone. Prostate deposits stimulate osteoblasts, while myeloma suppresses them and activates osteoclasts, leaving purely lytic holes with little new bone.","check":"The table of patterns and the laboratory paragraph."},{"type":"example","title":"Two patients with bone lesions and different calcium","text":"Patient A, a 70-year-old man, has back pain, sclerotic lumbar vertebrae, high alkaline phosphatase, and normal calcium. Osteoblasts are building bone, consuming calcium and releasing alkaline phosphatase, which fits prostate cancer. Patient B, a 62-year-old smoker, has lytic rib lesions, serum calcium 13.5 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5), and low PTH. Osteoclasts are releasing calcium faster than the kidney can excrete it, and tumor PTHrP may add a systemic effect; the high calcium suppresses PTH. The opposite PTH result, high calcium with high PTH, would point to primary hyperparathyroidism instead."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C10","obj":"MS-13-F.10","title":"Soft tissue sarcomas by lineage, and spread to lung","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Soft tissue sarcomas are classified by the normal tissue the tumor cells resemble, not by where they arise. Under the light microscope many sarcomas look alike, as sheets of spindle or round cells, so immunohistochemistry identifies the lineage by the proteins the cells make. Intermediate filaments mark broad families: vimentin is found in mesenchymal cells generally, and desmin in muscle of every type. Contractile proteins and lineage transcription factors then narrow the answer to skeletal or smooth muscle."},{"type":"table","title":"Muscle lineage markers","cols":["Marker","What it is","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Leiomyosarcoma"],"rows":[["Vimentin","Mesenchymal intermediate filament","Positive","Positive"],["Desmin","Muscle intermediate filament","Positive","Usually positive"],["Myogenin, MYOD1","Skeletal muscle transcription factors (nuclear)","Positive","Negative"],["Smooth muscle actin","Smooth muscle contractile protein","Usually negative or focal","Positive"],["h-caldesmon","Smooth muscle actin-binding protein","Negative","Positive"]],"caption":"Desmin says muscle; myogenin says skeletal muscle; smooth muscle actin and h-caldesmon say smooth muscle."},{"type":"h","text":"Leiomyosarcoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Leiomyosarcoma is a malignant tumor of smooth muscle cells that occurs in adults, usually from middle age onward. Common sites are the retroperitoneum, the uterus (where it is the most common sarcoma), the walls of large veins such as the inferior vena cava, and the deep soft tissue of the limbs. The cells form fascicles of spindle cells with eosinophilic fibrillar cytoplasm and blunt-ended, cigar-shaped nuclei. Because benign leiomyomas share this appearance, malignancy is judged by nuclear atypia, high mitotic activity, and coagulative tumor necrosis. Uterine leiomyosarcoma usually arises on its own rather than from a leiomyoma, and it is typically a single soft, hemorrhagic, necrotic mass in a postmenopausal woman, in contrast to the multiple firm, whorled leiomyomas of reproductive age. Its karyotype is complex, and TP53 and RB1 are often lost."},{"type":"h","text":"Why sarcomas spread to the lung"},{"type":"p","text":"Carcinomas arise in epithelium beside a dense lymphatic network and usually reach regional lymph nodes first. Sarcomas arise in mesenchyme, where tumor cells grow around and into thin-walled vessels early, so they spread mainly through the blood. Venous blood from the limbs, retroperitoneum, and uterus returns through the systemic veins to the right heart, making the pulmonary capillaries the first bed tumor emboli meet. The lung is therefore the usual first metastatic site of sarcomas, often as multiple rounded nodules. Exceptions follow the same logic: sarcomas drained by the portal vein, such as those of the bowel wall, seed the liver first, and a few sarcomas, including rhabdomyosarcoma and synovial sarcoma, often reach lymph nodes."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-06-C11","obj":"MS-13-F.11","title":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy (HPOA) is a syndrome of digital clubbing, periosteal new bone formation along the shafts of tubular bones (radius, ulna, tibia, fibula, and the metacarpals and metatarsals), and a symmetric arthritis or arthralgia of the neighboring joints, such as the wrists, knees, and ankles. The secondary form is a paraneoplastic syndrome, most often of non-small cell lung carcinoma, especially adenocarcinoma, and also of pleural tumors. Non-malignant causes include cyanotic congenital heart disease, cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, and lung abscess. A rare primary form, pachydermoperiostosis, is inherited through mutations in HPGD or SLCO2A1, genes needed to take up and degrade prostaglandin E2."},{"type":"h","text":"Mechanism"},{"type":"p","text":"The leading explanation centers on platelets and growth factors. Megakaryocytes released from the marrow normally lodge in the pulmonary capillaries, where they fragment into platelets. When they bypass this filter, through right-to-left shunting or abnormal tumor vessels, large megakaryocytes and platelet clumps impact in the small vessels of the fingertips and the periosteum. There they release platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), which promote vessel growth, edema, and fibroblast and osteoblast proliferation. Tumors also secrete VEGF, and prostaglandin E2 rises when the lung fails to clear it, as the primary form demonstrates. The findings often regress after the tumor is removed."},{"type":"flow","title":"Proposed mechanism of hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","steps":["Lung tumor or shunt disrupts pulmonary capillary filtering","Megakaryocytes and platelet clumps reach systemic circulation","They lodge in distal digital and periosteal vessels","Release of PDGF and VEGF; prostaglandin E2 raised","Vascular proliferation, edema, fibroblast activity","Clubbing and periosteal new bone formation"],"caption":"A widely accepted but not fully proven model linking intrathoracic disease to changes in the distal limbs."},{"type":"p","text":"In clubbing, the soft tissue under the nail bed thickens, so the angle between the nail plate and the proximal nail fold rises to more than 180 degrees, the nail bed feels spongy, and the normal diamond-shaped gap between opposed fingernails disappears (Schamroth sign). X-rays show a smooth line of new periosteal bone along the shafts, separated from the cortex by a thin lucent line, usually symmetric. A bone scan shows symmetric linear uptake along the cortices. Joint fluid is usually non-inflammatory. Uncomplicated COPD does not cause clubbing, so new clubbing in a smoker suggests lung cancer."},{"type":"table","title":"Paraneoplastic syndromes of lung cancer","cols":["Syndrome","Usual tumor type","Mediator"],"rows":[["Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy","Non-small cell, especially adenocarcinoma","Proposed PDGF, VEGF, prostaglandin E2"],["Hypercalcemia","Squamous cell carcinoma","PTHrP"],["SIADH","Small cell carcinoma","ADH"],["Cushing syndrome","Small cell carcinoma","ACTH"],["Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Small cell carcinoma","Antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels"]],"caption":"HPOA is the musculoskeletal member of this group and, unlike the endocrine syndromes, is most linked to non-small cell tumors."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal bone biology predicts most bone tumors. Tumors arise where cells divide: osteosarcoma in growing metaphyses and remodeling pagetic bone, osteochondroma and enchondroma from growth plate cartilage that grew sideways or was left behind. Location follows marrow and blood flow, so metastases seek red marrow and Ewing sarcoma permeates the diaphyseal marrow. The RANKL system that normally couples osteoblasts to osteoclasts is hijacked by giant cell tumor of bone and by lytic metastases, and the same signal explains hypercalcemia. Periosteal reactions record growth rate, which is why the same pattern can appear in infection and malignancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Molecular pathology confirms the diagnosis and explains behavior. Fusion genes (EWSR1-FLI1, PAX3 or PAX7 to FOXO1, CSF1 rearrangement) create abnormal transcription factors or growth signals; loss of RB1 and TP53 links osteosarcoma to hereditary retinoblastoma and Li-Fraumeni syndrome; and IDH mutations tie cartilage tumors to gliomas and leukemia through a shared oncometabolite. Markers are tools with limits: CD99 and desmin are sensitive but not specific, and imaging signs such as the Codman triangle signal aggressiveness rather than a single diagnosis, so tissue diagnosis remains the final step."}],"summary":["Location narrows bone tumors: epiphysis for giant cell tumor, metaphysis for osteosarcoma and osteochondroma, diaphysis for Ewing sarcoma, medulla of hand bones for enchondroma, cortex for osteoid osteoma.","Osteosarcoma is defined by tumor cells making osteoid, peaks in adolescents at the knee and in older adults with Paget disease or radiation, and is linked to RB1 and TP53 loss.","A Codman triangle signals aggressive periosteal lifting; a sunburst signals mineralizing tumor in soft tissue; onion skin signals growth in repeated waves.","Ewing sarcoma is a CD99-positive small round blue cell tumor driven by EWSR1-FLI1 from t(11;22), and its fever and high ESR mimic osteomyelitis.","Chondrosarcoma affects older adults in the pelvis and shoulder girdle, may arise from enchondroma or osteochondroma, and often carries IDH1 or IDH2 mutations that produce 2-hydroxyglutarate.","Osteoid osteoma is a nidus under 2 cm whose prostaglandin E2 causes night pain relieved by NSAIDs; osteoblastoma is larger, vertebral, and not NSAID-responsive.","Osteochondroma (EXT1 or EXT2) is a stalk with a cartilage cap continuous with host marrow; enchondroma is intramedullary cartilage, multiple in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome.","In giant cell tumor of bone, RANKL from neoplastic stromal cells recruits the osteoclast-like giant cells; in tenosynovial giant cell tumor, CSF1 recruits macrophages.","Metastases are the most common malignant tumors in bone: prostate is blastic, kidney, thyroid, and lung are lytic, and breast is mixed; lysis causes fracture and hypercalcemia with suppressed PTH.","Desmin marks muscle, myogenin skeletal muscle, and smooth muscle actin smooth muscle; sarcomas spread through blood to the lung, and HPOA is a paraneoplastic sign of lung cancer."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on tumor suppressor genes, oncometabolites, and paraneoplastic syndromes"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, section on endochondral ossification and the epiphyseal plate"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Tricarboxylic acid cycle, isocitrate dehydrogenase"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, section on paraneoplastic syndromes"}]},"MS-13-G":{"unit":"MS-13-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07","title":"Infectious Musculoskeletal Diseases","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how bacteria and one parasite reach joints, bone, fascia, and muscle, why each tissue responds as it does, and how laboratory and imaging findings reflect those responses. It covers septic arthritis and synovial fluid analysis, necrotizing fasciitis, hematogenous and contiguous osteomyelitis, dry, wet, and gas gangrene, pyomyositis, and trichinellosis. Board questions usually give a host (a child, a person with diabetes, an injection drug user, a patient with sickle cell disease) and a tissue, then ask for the organism, the route of spread, the key toxin, or the meaning of a lab value. Anatomy of the blood supply explains most of the answers.","prerequisites":["Patterns of necrosis: coagulative, liquefactive, and caseous (general pathology)","Acute inflammation: neutrophil recruitment, proteases, and cytokines such as IL-1 and TNF","Long bone anatomy: epiphysis, physis, metaphysis, diaphysis, and periosteum (MS bone units)","Gram stain, oxygen requirements, and major virulence factors of staphylococci, streptococci, and clostridia","Synovial joint structure and the composition of articular cartilage"],"connections":[{"to":"Microbiology","how":"Toxins such as Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin and streptococcal superantigens explain tissue destruction and shock."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Superantigens, complement deficiency in recurrent Neisseria infection, and Th2 responses with eosinophilia in helminth infection all appear in this chapter."},{"to":"Endocrine system (diabetes mellitus)","how":"Neuropathy, vascular disease, and impaired neutrophil function make diabetes the leading host factor for foot osteomyelitis, gangrene, and type I necrotizing fasciitis."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Sickle cell disease favors Salmonella osteomyelitis, and clostridial alpha toxin causes intravascular hemolysis."},{"to":"Rheumatology (crystal and inflammatory arthritis)","how":"Synovial fluid analysis separates septic arthritis from gout, pseudogout, and rheumatoid arthritis, which can coexist with infection."}],"terms":[{"term":"Septic arthritis","def":"Infection of a joint space, most often bacterial, that produces purulent synovial fluid and rapid cartilage destruction."},{"term":"Osteomyelitis","def":"Infection of bone and marrow, reached through the blood, from a neighboring infection, or by direct inoculation."},{"term":"Metaphysis","def":"The flared region of a long bone between the shaft and the growth plate, where blood flow slows in looping vessels."},{"term":"Sequestrum","def":"A fragment of dead bone separated from living bone by infection and loss of blood supply."},{"term":"Involucrum","def":"A sleeve of new reactive bone laid down by lifted periosteum around a sequestrum."},{"term":"Brodie abscess","def":"A localized, walled-off bone abscess surrounded by sclerotic bone, typical of subacute osteomyelitis."},{"term":"Necrotizing fasciitis","def":"Rapidly spreading infection of the subcutaneous fascia that thromboses perforating vessels and kills the overlying tissue."},{"term":"Superantigen","def":"A toxin that links MHC class II on antigen-presenting cells to the T cell receptor outside the peptide groove, activating many T cells at once."},{"term":"Gangrene","def":"Clinical term for necrosis of a limb or organ part, called dry when coagulative and wet when infection makes it liquefactive."},{"term":"Alpha toxin (C. perfringens)","def":"A phospholipase C (lecithinase) that hydrolyzes membrane phospholipids, lysing muscle cells, red cells, and platelets."},{"term":"Pyomyositis","def":"Bacterial abscess within skeletal muscle, usually Staphylococcus aureus in a large muscle of the thigh, buttock, or iliopsoas."},{"term":"Nurse cell","def":"A skeletal muscle cell remodeled by an encysting Trichinella larva into a capsule with its own new capillary network."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C01","obj":"MS-13-G.1","title":"Septic arthritis: routes, organisms, and risk factors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most septic arthritis is hematogenous. The synovial membrane is richly vascular, its capillaries are fenestrated, and the synovial lining has no limiting basement membrane, so bacteria carried in the blood during a transient bacteremia cross easily into the joint. Synovial fluid then supports their growth. Less often, organisms are inoculated directly by joint injection, arthroscopy or surgery, a penetrating injury, or an animal or human bite, or spread from adjacent osteomyelitis. Infection is usually monoarticular, and the knee is the joint most often involved."},{"type":"p","text":"Once bacteria are in the joint, neutrophils pour in. Their proteases, reactive oxygen species, and the cytokines IL-1 and TNF, together with bacterial toxins, activate matrix metalloproteinases in chondrocytes and synovium that degrade proteoglycan and then collagen. Articular cartilage has no blood supply and little capacity for repair, so matrix lost within the first few days is lost permanently. A tense effusion can also compress the blood supply of the bone end; in a child's hip this can cause avascular necrosis of the femoral head."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus is the most common cause overall. In sexually active young adults, Neisseria gonorrhoeae is the classic cause and produces disseminated gonococcal infection, either a purulent monoarthritis or a triad of migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis (wrists, hands, ankles), and sparse pustular skin lesions. Gonococci are fastidious, so joint fluid cultures are often negative and cultures of the urethra, cervix, pharynx, or rectum are more often positive. Recurrent disseminated Neisseria infection points to deficiency of the terminal complement components C5 to C9, which form the membrane attack complex."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk factors either damage the joint or impair host defense. Rheumatoid arthritis combines abnormal, inflamed synovium with immunosuppressive therapy, and its flare can hide infection. Prosthetic joints carry foreign material on which bacteria form biofilm that resists phagocytosis. Injection drug use causes repeated bacteremia and favors unusual joints such as the sternoclavicular and sacroiliac joints. Diabetes impairs neutrophil function. Older age, chronic kidney disease, gout, and joint trauma also raise risk."},{"type":"table","title":"Likely organism by host and setting","cols":["Host or setting","Most likely organisms","Reason"],"rows":[["Most adults and children","Staphylococcus aureus","Common bacteremia; adhesins bind collagen and fibronectin"],["Sexually active young adult","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Bacteremic spread from mucosal infection"],["Child under about 4 years","S. aureus; Kingella kingae","K. kingae colonizes the oropharynx of young children"],["Neonate","S. aureus, group B streptococci, gram-negative bacilli","Maternal and nursery flora"],["Prosthetic joint","Coagulase-negative staphylococci, S. aureus","Biofilm on implant surfaces"],["Injection drug use","S. aureus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Skin flora and contaminated equipment"],["Tick exposure months earlier; knee effusion","Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme arthritis)","Inflammatory arthritis, not purulent"]],"caption":"The host usually narrows the organism before any test result is available."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C02","obj":"MS-13-G.2","title":"Synovial fluid analysis in the acute hot swollen joint","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal synovial fluid is a plasma ultrafiltrate to which synovial fibroblast-like (type B) cells add hyaluronic acid and lubricin. Hyaluronic acid makes the fluid viscous, and the fluid is clear and nearly acellular (under about 200 WBC/mm3). Arthrocentesis is the key test in a hot swollen joint because it separates three categories that look alike at the bedside."},{"type":"p","text":"As inflammation increases, neutrophils enter and their enzymes depolymerize hyaluronic acid, so the fluid becomes thinner, cloudier, and more cellular. White cell count and neutrophil percentage rise along a continuum, so the cutoffs are guides rather than absolute lines. Neutrophils and bacteria consume glucose, so in septic fluid glucose falls well below the serum level. A Gram stain is positive in only about half of nongonococcal infections and fewer gonococcal ones, so a negative stain does not exclude infection; culture is the definitive test."},{"type":"table","title":"Synovial fluid categories","cols":["Feature","Noninflammatory","Inflammatory","Septic"],"rows":[["Appearance","Clear, yellow, viscous","Cloudy, less viscous","Opaque, purulent"],["WBC per mm3","Under 2,000","About 2,000 to 50,000","Usually over 50,000, often over 100,000"],["Neutrophils","Under 25%","Over 50%","Usually over 75% to 90%"],["Glucose","Near serum","Normal to mildly low","Low"],["Gram stain, culture","Negative","Negative","Often positive"],["Typical causes","Osteoarthritis, trauma","Rheumatoid arthritis, gout, pseudogout, reactive arthritis, Lyme arthritis","Bacterial infection"]],"caption":"Counts overlap at the edges: gonococcal and partially treated infections can fall below 50,000, and severe gout can exceed it."},{"type":"p","text":"Crystals are identified by compensated polarized light microscopy. Monosodium urate crystals of gout are needle shaped and strongly negatively birefringent, so they appear yellow when aligned parallel to the slow axis of the red compensator. Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals of pseudogout are rhomboid and weakly positively birefringent, so they appear blue when parallel. Crystals prove a crystal arthropathy but do not exclude infection, because the two can coexist and an infected joint can shed crystals; a very high count is treated as possible infection until culture results return. A bloody effusion suggests trauma, fracture, a bleeding disorder such as hemophilia, or pigmented villonodular synovitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Crystals under polarized light","cols":["Crystal","Disease","Shape","Birefringence","Color parallel to slow axis"],"rows":[["Monosodium urate","Gout","Needle","Strongly negative","Yellow"],["Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate","Pseudogout (CPPD disease)","Rhomboid or rod","Weakly positive","Blue"]],"caption":"Color reverses when the crystal is turned perpendicular to the compensator axis."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a joint aspirate","text":"A 70-year-old man with known gout has a hot, swollen knee and a temperature of 38.6 degrees C (101.5 degrees F). The aspirate is opaque, with 85,000 WBC/mm3, 92% neutrophils, glucose much lower than serum, a negative Gram stain, and needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals. The crystals confirm urate, but they do not explain the picture. A count above 50,000 with over 90% neutrophils and low glucose places the fluid in the septic range, and Gram stains are negative in about half of septic joints. The correct reading is possible septic arthritis coexisting with gout until culture is negative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C03","obj":"MS-13-G.3","title":"Necrotizing fasciitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Necrotizing fasciitis is infection of the subcutaneous tissue and superficial fascia that spreads along fascial planes faster than the skin changes reveal. The superficial fascia is relatively poorly vascularized and offers little resistance to spread. Perforating vessels pass through it to supply the overlying skin, and as bacteria and their toxins thrombose these vessels, the skin above becomes ischemic. Cutaneous nerves running in the same plane are first inflamed and later destroyed."},{"type":"flow","title":"From fascial infection to visible necrosis","steps":["Organisms enter through a minor wound or surgical site","Enzymes and toxins spread along the superficial fascia","Perforating vessels to the skin thrombose","Skin becomes ischemic: dusky, then bullae, then necrosis","Cutaneous nerves die: early pain, later anesthesia","Toxins and cytokines cause sepsis or toxic shock"],"caption":"The deep infection leads; the skin findings lag behind it."},{"type":"p","text":"This sequence explains the clinical signs. Pain is out of proportion to the exam because the deep infection is extensive while the skin still looks only mildly red. Edema and tenderness extend beyond the margin of erythema. As ischemia progresses, the skin turns gray or violaceous and forms hemorrhagic bullae, and later the area may become numb as nerves die. Gas-producing organisms cause crepitus. Liquefied fascia gives thin gray fluid, foul when anaerobes are present, rather than true pus. Systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, confusion) and laboratory signs such as marked leukocytosis, hyponatremia, and a raised creatine kinase when muscle is involved reflect the bacterial burden and toxin load."},{"type":"p","text":"Type I is polymicrobial: anaerobes (Bacteroides, Peptostreptococcus, sometimes Clostridium) with facultative organisms such as Enterobacteriaceae and non-group A streptococci. Aerobes consume oxygen and lower the redox potential, which lets anaerobes grow, and mixed fermentation produces gas. It occurs in diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, obesity, and after abdominal or perineal surgery. Fournier gangrene is type I infection of the perineum and genitalia, spreading along Colles fascia, Scarpa fascia, and the dartos layer. Type II is monomicrobial Streptococcus pyogenes (group A), sometimes with S. aureus, and can strike healthy people of any age after minor trauma, varicella lesions, or injection drug use. Its virulence factors include antiphagocytic M protein, hyaluronidase, streptokinase, the cysteine protease SpeB, and the pyrogenic exotoxins SpeA and SpeC. These exotoxins are superantigens: they bind MHC class II and the T cell receptor variable beta region outside the peptide groove, activating up to about 20% of T cells and releasing IL-2, TNF, and IL-1, which produces streptococcal toxic shock syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"Type I versus type II necrotizing fasciitis","cols":["Feature","Type I","Type II"],"rows":[["Organisms","Mixed anaerobes and facultative bacteria","Streptococcus pyogenes, with or without S. aureus"],["Typical host","Diabetes, vascular disease, after surgery","Often healthy, after minor injury"],["Typical site","Perineum (Fournier), trunk, feet","Limbs"],["Gas and crepitus","Common","Uncommon"],["Toxic shock","Less typical","Common, from superantigens"]],"caption":"Both types share pain out of proportion, rapid spread, bullae, and systemic toxicity."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can a patient with necrotizing fasciitis have severe pain on the first day but a numb, dusky area two days later?","explain":"Early on, inflamed cutaneous nerves in the fascial plane signal intense pain while the skin still has its blood supply. As perforating vessels thrombose, both skin and nerves die, so the area darkens and loses sensation.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on clinical signs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C04","obj":"MS-13-G.4","title":"Routes of osteomyelitis: metaphyseal seeding in children, contiguous and vertebral disease in adults","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bacteria reach bone by three routes: hematogenous seeding, contiguous spread from an adjacent soft tissue infection, and direct inoculation through an open fracture, surgery, or a puncture. Age decides which route dominates, because bone blood supply changes with growth."},{"type":"p","text":"In children, hematogenous osteomyelitis localizes to the metaphysis of rapidly growing long bones, most often the distal femur and proximal tibia. Branches of the nutrient artery run to the growth plate and turn back in sharp loops that empty into wide venous sinusoids. Flow there slows and becomes turbulent, the sinusoidal lining has gaps, and phagocytic activity is low, so circulating bacteria such as S. aureus lodge and multiply. Minor trauma that causes a small hematoma in this region may add a nidus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the child's metaphysis is seeded","steps":["Transient bacteremia carries S. aureus to bone","Nutrient artery branches loop at the growth plate","Flow slows in wide metaphyseal venous sinusoids","Gaps in the lining and few phagocytes let bacteria lodge","Abscess forms; pus spreads through the cortex"],"caption":"The growth plate usually stops spread toward the epiphysis after infancy."},{"type":"p","text":"The growth plate shapes what happens next. In infants under about 18 months, vessels still cross the physis, so infection can reach the epiphysis and the joint. In older children the avascular physis acts as a barrier, so pus spreads outward through the thin metaphyseal cortex and lifts the loosely attached periosteum. Where the metaphysis lies inside the joint capsule (proximal femur, proximal humerus, radial neck, and distal lateral tibia), pus can enter the joint and cause septic arthritis. After the physis closes in adults, long bone hematogenous infection is uncommon, and when it occurs it can reach the epiphysis and joint."},{"type":"p","text":"In adults, hematogenous osteomyelitis is mostly vertebral, usually lumbar, seeded from urinary tract infection, endocarditis, intravascular catheters, or injection drug use. Adult intervertebral discs are avascular, so bacteria settle in the richly supplied vertebral endplate, then destroy the disc and cross to the neighboring endplate. The result is spondylodiscitis: two adjacent vertebral bodies and the disc between them, often with back pain, fever, and risk of epidural or psoas abscess. Children's discs still have a blood supply, so isolated discitis is possible. Tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease) favors the lower thoracic spine, often spares the disc early, spreads under the anterior longitudinal ligament to several levels, forms cold paraspinal abscesses, and can collapse vertebrae into an angular gibbus."},{"type":"p","text":"Contiguous osteomyelitis is typical of the diabetic foot. Sensory neuropathy removes protective pain, motor neuropathy deforms the foot and creates pressure points over the metatarsal heads, and peripheral artery disease and poor neutrophil function slow healing. An ulcer deepens until it reaches bone; bone felt with a sterile probe at the ulcer base strongly suggests osteomyelitis. These infections are polymicrobial."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteomyelitis by setting","cols":["Setting","Route and site","Typical organisms"],"rows":[["Child","Hematogenous; long bone metaphysis","S. aureus; K. kingae under about 4 years"],["Neonate","Hematogenous; may cross physis to joint","S. aureus, group B streptococci, Escherichia coli"],["Adult","Hematogenous; lumbar vertebrae and disc","S. aureus, gram-negative bacilli from urinary source"],["Diabetic foot ulcer","Contiguous; forefoot and calcaneus","Polymicrobial: S. aureus, streptococci, gram-negatives, anaerobes"],["Sickle cell disease","Hematogenous; often multiple, diaphyseal sites","Salmonella, S. aureus"],["Nail through a sneaker sole","Direct inoculation; calcaneus or metatarsals","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],["Cat or dog bite","Direct inoculation","Pasteurella multocida"],["Spine, endemic exposure","Hematogenous; thoracic vertebrae","Mycobacterium tuberculosis (Pott disease)"]],"caption":"S. aureus remains common in every group, so the special organisms are additions, not replacements."},{"type":"p","text":"In sickle cell disease, bone infarcts provide dead tissue, bowel microinfarcts allow Salmonella into the blood, and functional asplenia impairs clearance of bacteria from the blood, so Salmonella osteomyelitis is characteristic."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C05","obj":"MS-13-G.5","title":"Osteomyelitis: pathology, laboratory and imaging findings, and complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is rigid, so inflammation inside it has nowhere to expand. Neutrophils and edema raise pressure in the marrow cavity and Haversian canals, compressing vessels and causing ischemic necrosis within about 48 hours. Pus that reaches the surface lifts the periosteum, which in children is loosely attached, and strips away the outer blood supply to the cortex. Bone that has lost both its medullary and periosteal supply dies; histologically its lacunae are empty of osteocytes."},{"type":"flow","title":"From abscess to chronic osteomyelitis","steps":["Pus raises pressure inside rigid bone","Intraosseous vessels collapse; bone becomes ischemic","Pus lifts the periosteum, cutting the cortical supply","Dead bone separates as a sequestrum","Lifted periosteum lays down an involucrum","Avascular sequestrum harbors bacteria; sinus tracts drain"],"caption":"Dead bone without blood flow cannot be reached by neutrophils, which is why infection persists."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoclasts at the margin of dead bone resorb the interface and free the fragment as a sequestrum. Meanwhile the lifted periosteum, still alive, lays down a shell of reactive woven bone, the involucrum, around the infected segment. Openings in the involucrum through which pus escapes are called cloacae, and pus that continues to the skin forms a chronic draining sinus tract. Because the sequestrum has no blood flow, bacteria in its canals and in biofilm are protected from neutrophils and circulating antibody, so chronic infection can smolder for years and flare after quiescent periods."},{"type":"p","text":"A Brodie abscess is the subacute form: a small abscess, usually in the tibial metaphysis of a child or young adult, walled off by a rim of sclerotic reactive bone. It causes localized aching pain, often worse at night, with few systemic signs, and appears radiographically as a lucent lesion with a sclerotic margin."},{"type":"table","title":"Tests in suspected osteomyelitis","cols":["Test","Typical finding","What it means"],"rows":[["ESR and CRP","Elevated","Sensitive markers of inflammation, not specific; CRP rises and falls faster than ESR"],["White cell count","Often normal, especially in chronic disease","A normal count does not exclude infection"],["Blood cultures","Positive in about half of hematogenous cases","Identify the organism without a bone sample"],["Plain radiograph","Normal early; lysis and periosteal reaction after about 10 to 14 days","About 30% to 50% of bone mineral must be lost before lysis shows"],["MRI","Marrow edema early: low T1, high T2 and STIR signal","Most sensitive imaging; shows extent and abscesses"],["Bone biopsy with culture and histology","Neutrophils, necrotic bone, organisms","Definitive diagnosis"]],"caption":"Imaging shows inflammation; only bone culture proves the organism."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications follow from the pathology. Chronic osteomyelitis with a draining sinus develops in a minority of patients, especially with diabetes, vascular disease, or retained hardware. Squamous cell carcinoma (Marjolin ulcer) can arise in the epithelium lining a chronic sinus tract after years to decades of repeated injury and repair; a change in drainage, bleeding, or pain is the warning sign. Other complications include pathologic fracture through weakened bone, growth disturbance and limb length discrepancy when the physis is damaged, septic arthritis by extension, bacteremia with endocarditis, epidural abscess in vertebral disease, and reactive systemic AA amyloidosis from years of chronic inflammation, in which the liver produces serum amyloid A that deposits in the kidney and other organs."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does chronic osteomyelitis often relapse even after the patient's inflammatory markers have returned to normal?","explain":"Bacteria survive in the avascular sequestrum and in biofilm, where neutrophils, antibody, and circulating drugs cannot reach them. With little active inflammation, ESR and CRP can fall while viable organisms remain and later reactivate.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on the sequestrum."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C06","obj":"MS-13-G.6","title":"Dry versus wet gangrene","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gangrene is a clinical term, not a separate type of necrosis. It describes necrosis of a limb, usually the lower leg, that has lost its blood supply. Which pattern of necrosis it shows depends on whether protein denaturation or enzymatic digestion dominates."},{"type":"p","text":"Dry gangrene follows slowly progressive arterial occlusion, most often atherosclerotic peripheral artery disease. Ischemia and acidosis denature structural proteins and also the cell's own enzymes, so dead cells cannot digest themselves and the tissue keeps its outline: coagulative necrosis. Without blood flow the tissue dries, mummifies, and turns black as hemoglobin breaks down. Living tissue at the edge mounts acute inflammation, forming a sharp line of demarcation between dead and viable tissue. Because there is little moisture and few bacteria, spread is slow and systemic toxicity is minimal."},{"type":"p","text":"Wet gangrene develops when bacteria infect the dead tissue, or when venous outflow is also blocked so the tissue stays swollen and moist. Neutrophils recruited by the infection release hydrolytic enzymes, and bacterial enzymes add to them, so the tissue is digested: liquefactive necrosis superimposed on the coagulative pattern. The limb is swollen, moist, foul smelling, and poorly demarcated, the infection spreads along tissue planes, and bacteremia and sepsis can follow."},{"type":"table","title":"Dry and wet gangrene compared","cols":["Feature","Dry gangrene","Wet gangrene"],"rows":[["Cause","Arterial occlusion alone","Arterial or venous compromise plus bacterial infection"],["Necrosis","Coagulative","Liquefactive on top of coagulative"],["Appearance","Black, dry, shriveled","Swollen, moist, discolored, foul"],["Demarcation","Sharp line","Poor, spreading"],["Course","Slow, little systemic illness","Rapid, risk of sepsis"]],"caption":"Infection converts a stable, demarcated lesion into a spreading, toxic one."},{"type":"p","text":"Diabetes raises the risk of both forms through several converging mechanisms. Atherosclerosis is accelerated and tends to involve the smaller infrapopliteal arteries diffusely, which limits collateral flow. Microangiopathy with basement membrane thickening and hyaline arteriolosclerosis impairs nutrient and oxygen exchange. Sensory neuropathy lets pressure injuries go unnoticed, autonomic neuropathy dries and cracks the skin, and hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil adhesion, chemotaxis, phagocytosis, and oxidative killing while glucose-rich tissue supports bacterial growth. A small wound in an ischemic, insensate foot therefore progresses readily from ulcer to infection to wet gangrene. Smoking, hypertension, and dyslipidemia add to peripheral artery disease in people without diabetes, and gangrene typically begins in the toes, the most distal territory."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C07","obj":"MS-13-G.7","title":"Gas gangrene (clostridial myonecrosis)","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gas gangrene is rapidly progressive necrosis of skeletal muscle caused by toxin-producing clostridia, most often Clostridium perfringens (type A). Clostridia are large, gram-positive, spore-forming anaerobic rods found in soil and the intestinal tract. The classic setting is a deep, contaminated wound with devitalized muscle: crush and battlefield injuries, compound fractures, gunshot wounds, and contaminated surgical wounds, especially of the bowel or biliary tract. Dead tissue, foreign bodies, and impaired blood supply lower the oxygen tension and redox potential, which lets spores germinate. Clostridium septicum causes spontaneous gas gangrene without trauma by spreading from the bowel through the blood, and is associated with colon cancer and neutropenia."},{"type":"p","text":"The main virulence factor is alpha toxin, a phospholipase C (lecithinase) that hydrolyzes phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin in cell membranes. It lyses muscle cells, red cells, platelets, and leukocytes, and it activates platelets and endothelium so that small vessels thrombose. Theta toxin (perfringolysin O), a cholesterol-dependent pore-forming toxin, adds to cell lysis. Both toxins cause leukocytes to aggregate within vessels at the edge of the lesion, so the necrotic muscle contains strikingly few neutrophils. Necrosis lowers oxygen tension further, so the infection advances along muscle bundles at a rate that can be measured in hours."},{"type":"flow","title":"How alpha toxin produces the clinical picture","steps":["Spores germinate in anaerobic, devitalized muscle","Alpha toxin hydrolyzes membrane phospholipids","Muscle cells lyse; microvessels thrombose","Ischemia extends the anaerobic zone; spread accelerates","Fermentation produces gas in tissue: crepitus","Red cells lyse; toxins depress the heart: shock"],"caption":"Tissue destruction and the anaerobic environment feed each other."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical features follow this mechanism. Incubation is short, usually under 3 days. Severe pain at the wound, out of proportion to its appearance, is often the first symptom. Fermentation of muscle glycogen and glucose produces hydrogen and carbon dioxide, giving crepitus on palpation and gas within muscle on imaging. The skin changes from pale and tense to a bronze, then purple or black color, with hemorrhagic bullae and a thin, watery, brownish discharge. Necrotic muscle is pale and does not contract or bleed. Gram stain of the discharge shows large boxcar-shaped gram-positive rods with few neutrophils, and spores are rarely seen in tissue. In culture, C. perfringens shows a double zone of hemolysis on blood agar and lecithinase activity on egg yolk agar that antitoxin inhibits (the Nagler reaction)."},{"type":"p","text":"Systemic toxicity develops quickly. Alpha toxin causes intravascular hemolysis with anemia, hemoglobinuria, and jaundice; it depresses myocardial contractility and, with the cytokines released from dying tissue, causes hypotension and shock. Acute kidney injury follows from hemoglobin and myoglobin pigment and hypoperfusion. Gas in tissue is not unique to clostridia: type I necrotizing fasciitis also produces gas, but it spreads in fascia, while clostridial myonecrosis destroys muscle itself."},{"type":"example","title":"Fascia or muscle?","text":"Two days after a crush injury to the thigh with a deep, soil-contaminated wound, a man has severe pain, crepitus, bronze skin with bullae, a thin brown discharge, dark urine, and a falling hematocrit. Gas plus rapid spread could suggest necrotizing fasciitis, so reason from the clues. The deep, devitalized wound gives an anaerobic site for spores. Dark urine with falling hematocrit points to intravascular hemolysis, a hallmark of alpha toxin. A Gram stain showing large gram-positive rods with very few neutrophils fits clostridia, whose toxins keep leukocytes out of the lesion. The diagnosis is clostridial myonecrosis from C. perfringens."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does gas gangrene contain so few neutrophils when ordinary bacterial infections produce pus?","explain":"Alpha and theta toxins lyse leukocytes and make them aggregate inside vessels at the margin of the lesion, and thrombosed vessels cannot deliver them, so the dead muscle has little inflammatory infiltrate.","check":"The paragraph on alpha toxin and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-07-C08","obj":"MS-13-G.8","title":"Infective myositis: pyomyositis and trichinellosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Healthy skeletal muscle resists bacterial seeding even during bacteremia, so infection usually needs a predisposing factor. Pyomyositis is a primary abscess within a large skeletal muscle, most often the quadriceps, gluteal muscles, or iliopsoas. Staphylococcus aureus causes most cases, with group A streptococci less often. Once called tropical pyomyositis, it now occurs in temperate regions, especially with HIV infection, diabetes, malignancy, immunosuppression, injection drug use, and recent muscle injury or strenuous exercise, which may give circulating bacteria a damaged site to seed."},{"type":"p","text":"The course has three stages. In the invasive stage the muscle is firm, swollen, and only mildly tender, with low fever. In the suppurative stage, about 10 to 21 days later, an abscess forms, with fever, marked tenderness, and pus on aspiration. In the late stage, untreated infection causes bacteremia and septic shock. Because the abscess is deep, the overlying skin may look nearly normal. Creatine kinase is often normal or only mildly elevated because the necrosis is localized, unlike in diffuse myositis. Ultrasound, CT, or MRI shows the abscess. An iliopsoas abscess can also be secondary, spreading from vertebral osteomyelitis, Pott disease, Crohn disease, or appendicitis, and causes flank pain with pain on hip extension."},{"type":"p","text":"Trichinellosis (trichinosis) is caused by the nematode Trichinella spiralis and related species. People ingest larvae encysted in undercooked meat, classically pork and wild game such as bear, wild boar, and walrus. Gastric acid and pepsin free the larvae, which invade the small intestinal epithelium and mature into adults within about a week. Females are viviparous and release newborn larvae for several weeks; these enter lymphatics and blood and invade striated muscle, especially active, well perfused muscles such as the diaphragm, masseter, tongue, extraocular, intercostal, and limb muscles. Each larva enters a single muscle cell and reprograms it into a nurse cell with a collagen capsule and a new capillary network, where it coils and can survive for years before calcifying. Larvae also enter cardiac muscle and the brain but do not encyst there."},{"type":"flow","title":"Life cycle of Trichinella spiralis in humans","steps":["Encysted larvae eaten in undercooked pork or game","Stomach acid frees larvae; they invade small bowel","Adults mate; females release newborn larvae","Larvae spread through lymph and blood","Larvae invade striated muscle cells","Nurse cell encapsulates a coiled larva; later calcifies"],"caption":"Humans are dead-end hosts: the cycle continues only when another animal eats the encysted muscle."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms follow the two phases. The intestinal phase, in the first week, brings nausea, diarrhea, and abdominal pain, and may be mild. The muscle phase, beginning about the second week, brings fever, myalgia, muscle tenderness and weakness, periorbital and facial edema, conjunctival hemorrhages, and subungual splinter hemorrhages. These findings reflect an immune and vascular reaction to migrating larvae and their antigens, with inflammation of small vessels and capillary leak. Tissue invasion by a helminth drives a Th2 response: IL-5 produces marked eosinophilia, which peaks around weeks 2 to 4, and IL-4 increases IgE. Muscle cell injury raises creatine kinase and lactate dehydrogenase. Myocarditis, encephalitis, and pneumonitis are the serious complications, and myocarditis is the main cause of death."},{"type":"table","title":"Pyomyositis and trichinellosis compared","cols":["Feature","Pyomyositis","Trichinellosis"],"rows":[["Agent","S. aureus (most cases)","Trichinella spiralis larvae"],["Acquisition","Hematogenous seeding, often of injured muscle","Undercooked pork or wild game"],["Distribution","Single large muscle, localized abscess","Many muscles, diffuse"],["Key findings","Firm swelling, then fluctuant abscess, fever","Myalgia, periorbital edema, splinter hemorrhages"],["Blood","Neutrophilia; CK often near normal","Eosinophilia; CK and LDH elevated"],["Serious complication","Sepsis","Myocarditis"]],"caption":"Neutrophils mark the bacterial abscess; eosinophils mark the migrating helminth."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy of the blood supply ties this chapter together. Fenestrated synovial capillaries without a basement membrane make the joint easy to seed; looping metaphyseal vessels make the growing child's long bone the target of hematogenous osteomyelitis, while avascular adult discs push infection into the vertebral endplate; perforating vessels through the fascia explain why skin necrosis lags behind necrotizing fasciitis; and arterial occlusion turns a toe into dry gangrene. Pathology then describes the response: liquefactive necrosis where neutrophils and bacterial enzymes digest tissue, coagulative necrosis where ischemia alone kills, and the avascular sequestrum that lets chronic bone infection persist."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and immunology explain the speed and severity. Staphylococcal adhesins and biofilm favor bone, joints, and implants; streptococcal superantigens cause toxic shock; clostridial phospholipase destroys membranes and keeps neutrophils out; and a tissue-invading helminth produces eosinophilia instead of pus. Host factors, particularly diabetes, sickle cell disease, and terminal complement deficiency, explain who is affected. The limits of testing matter as much as the findings: Gram stains miss many joint infections, radiographs lag by about two weeks, and inflammatory markers are sensitive but not specific, so culture of joint fluid or bone remains the definitive evidence."}],"summary":["Septic arthritis is mostly hematogenous because synovium is highly vascular with no basement membrane; S. aureus is most common and N. gonorrhoeae is classic in sexually active young adults.","Neutrophil proteases and IL-1 driven metalloproteinases destroy avascular cartilage within days, and lost cartilage is not replaced.","Synovial fluid: noninflammatory under 2,000 WBC/mm3, inflammatory about 2,000 to 50,000, septic usually over 50,000 with over 75% neutrophils and low glucose; crystals do not exclude infection.","Urate crystals are needle shaped and negatively birefringent; calcium pyrophosphate crystals are rhomboid and weakly positively birefringent.","Necrotizing fasciitis spreads along fascia and thromboses perforating vessels, so pain is out of proportion to the skin; type I is polymicrobial with gas, type II is group A streptococcus with superantigen toxic shock.","Childhood hematogenous osteomyelitis seeds the metaphysis, where looping vessels slow flow; adult hematogenous disease is vertebral and involves two vertebrae and the disc, and diabetic foot disease spreads from contiguous ulcers.","Raised pressure and periosteal stripping create a sequestrum and involucrum; MRI is the most sensitive imaging, and chronic sinus tracts can develop squamous cell carcinoma.","Dry gangrene is coagulative necrosis from arterial occlusion with sharp demarcation; wet gangrene adds infection and liquefactive necrosis and can cause sepsis.","Clostridium perfringens alpha toxin (lecithinase) lyses muscle, red cells, and platelets, producing crepitus, bronze skin, hemolysis, shock, and few neutrophils.","Pyomyositis is an S. aureus abscess in a large muscle; trichinellosis from undercooked pork or game causes myalgia, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia as larvae encyst in muscle."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (osteomyelitis and infectious arthritis)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell Injury, Cell Death, and Adaptations (patterns of tissue necrosis, gangrene)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"The Staphylococci; The Streptococci; Spore-Forming Gram-Positive Bacilli: Bacillus and Clostridium Species; Neisseriae"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology (tissue nematodes, Trichinella)"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes (superantigens, immunity to helminths)"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, sections on blood supply and the epiphyseal plate; joints and synovial membrane"}]},"MS-13-H":{"unit":"MS-13-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08","title":"Musculoskeletal Trauma","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains what happens to bone, ligament, tendon, and muscle after injury. It covers how a fracture heals and why healing stalls, which fractures threaten a blood supply or a nerve, how soft tissue injuries differ, and three complications of crushed or ischemic muscle: compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis, and myositis ossificans. Board questions usually describe an injury and ask what structure is at risk, what the laboratory or examination finding means, or why a complication appears when it does. Each answer comes from anatomy (where vessels and nerves run), physiology (pressure and perfusion), or cell biology (how tissue repairs).","prerequisites":["Bone histology, osteoblasts, osteoclasts, and endochondral and intramembranous ossification (MS-1, MS-2)","Dense regular connective tissue and collagen types I, II, and III (MS-6)","Blood supply and nerves of the wrist, elbow, and hip (MS-3, MS-5)","Capillary hydrostatic pressure and fluid exchange across the capillary wall","Acute inflammation and repair by regeneration or scar"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal neoplasms (MS-13-F)","how":"Myositis ossificans and osteosarcoma both make bone, and the direction of maturation is what separates a reactive lesion from a malignant one."},{"to":"Degenerative musculoskeletal diseases (MS-13-E)","how":"Osteoporosis sets up hip, wrist, and vertebral fractures, and avascular necrosis of the femoral head leads to secondary osteoarthritis."},{"to":"Tubulointerstitial disease (UR-12-B)","how":"Myoglobin from rhabdomyolysis causes acute tubular injury through casts, heme toxicity, and vasoconstriction."},{"to":"Hemodynamic disorders and the lung","how":"Fat embolism is a form of nonthrombotic embolism that can progress to acute respiratory distress."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal biochemistry and contraction (MS-7, MS-8)","how":"ATP depletion and calcium overload explain why muscle cells die in crush injury and extreme exertion."}],"terms":[{"term":"Callus","def":"The mass of repair tissue (granulation tissue, cartilage, and woven bone) that bridges and splints a healing fracture."},{"term":"Woven bone","def":"Immature bone with randomly oriented collagen fibers, laid down quickly during repair and later replaced by lamellar bone."},{"term":"Delayed union","def":"A fracture that heals more slowly than expected for its site and type but is still progressing."},{"term":"Nonunion","def":"A fracture in which healing has stopped without bony bridging; a nonunion with persistent motion can form a false joint (pseudarthrosis)."},{"term":"Avascular necrosis","def":"Death of bone and marrow from loss of blood supply, also called osteonecrosis."},{"term":"Fat embolism syndrome","def":"Respiratory, neurologic, and skin dysfunction 1 to 3 days after fat droplets enter the circulation, usually from long bone fracture."},{"term":"Sprain","def":"Stretch or tear of a ligament."},{"term":"Strain","def":"Stretch or tear of a muscle or tendon, most often at the myotendinous junction."},{"term":"Compartment syndrome","def":"Ischemia of muscle and nerve caused by rising tissue pressure inside a closed fascial compartment."},{"term":"Volkmann ischemic contracture","def":"Permanent flexion deformity of the wrist and fingers after forearm flexor muscle necrosis and fibrosis."},{"term":"Rhabdomyolysis","def":"Breakdown of skeletal muscle that releases creatine kinase, myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate into the blood."},{"term":"Heterotopic ossification","def":"Formation of mature bone in soft tissue where bone does not normally exist; myositis ossificans is the post-traumatic form in muscle."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C01","obj":"MS-13-H.1","title":"Fracture healing and why it fails","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is one of the few adult tissues that heals by regeneration rather than scar: a well-healed fracture is replaced by true bone and can regain its original strength. Most fractures heal indirectly (secondary healing), repeating the sequence of skeletal development. Only when fragments are fixed rigidly in direct contact does bone heal directly (primary healing), with osteoclasts tunneling across the line and osteoblasts filling the tunnels, and no visible callus."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary healing starts when torn vessels in the bone, marrow, and periosteum bleed into the gap. The clot is a fibrin scaffold, and platelets and macrophages release growth factors (PDGF, TGF-beta, and bone morphogenetic proteins, BMPs) that recruit mesenchymal progenitor cells from the inner (cambium) layer of the periosteum and the marrow. Bone at the fracture ends loses its supply and dies back a short distance, and osteoclasts remove it. Within the gap, oxygen is low and the fragments move slightly, conditions that push progenitors to become chondrocytes, so the gap fills with granulation tissue and fibrocartilage (soft callus). Under the periosteum away from the gap, where oxygen is better, osteoblasts form woven bone directly by intramembranous ossification. As capillaries invade the soft callus, cartilage calcifies and is replaced by woven bone through endochondral ossification (hard callus), and the fracture becomes clinically stable. Remodeling then replaces woven with lamellar bone, removes excess callus, and aligns trabeculae with the loads the bone carries."},{"type":"flow","title":"Secondary fracture healing","steps":["Torn vessels form hematoma; fibrin scaffold and growth factors","Inflammation; dead bone ends resorbed by osteoclasts","Progenitors form granulation tissue and fibrocartilage (soft callus)","Vessels invade; cartilage replaced by woven bone (hard callus)","Woven bone remodeled to lamellar bone along stress lines"],"caption":"Approximate timing: hematoma and inflammation in the first days to a week, soft callus by about 1 to 3 weeks, hard callus over weeks to about 3 months, remodeling over months to years."},{"type":"p","text":"Every stage depends on new blood vessels and on the right amount of motion. Delayed union means healing is slower than expected; nonunion means it has stopped. Two patterns explain the causes. With too much motion and good biology, cartilage and fibrous tissue keep forming but never ossify, producing abundant callus that does not bridge (hypertrophic nonunion). With poor biology, little callus forms at all (atrophic nonunion)."},{"type":"table","title":"Factors that delay union","cols":["Factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Poor blood supply","Few vessels reach the gap, so soft callus cannot be converted to bone; worst in bones with a single or retrograde supply and with periosteal stripping"],["Instability","Excess motion tears ingrowing capillaries and keeps the gap fibrous or cartilaginous"],["Infection","Bacteria and neutrophil enzymes destroy matrix; dead bone (sequestrum) cannot be revascularized"],["Smoking","Nicotine constricts vessels and inhibits osteoblasts; carbon monoxide lowers oxygen delivery"],["Diabetes mellitus","Microvascular disease, impaired angiogenesis, advanced glycation end products, and weaker osteoblast function"],["Malnutrition","Too little protein for matrix, vitamin C for collagen hydroxylation, or calcium and vitamin D for mineralization"]],"caption":"Glucocorticoid excess and advanced age slow healing by similar effects on progenitor cells and blood flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C02","obj":"MS-13-H.2","title":"Complications of specific fractures","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The complication of a fracture follows from what lies next to the bone and how blood enters it. Avascular necrosis threatens any fragment whose vessels enter from the other side of the fracture line; nerve and artery injury threatens structures lying directly against a displaced fragment."},{"type":"p","text":"The scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal bone, usually after a fall on an outstretched hand with the wrist extended. Branches of the radial artery enter at the waist and distal pole, and the proximal pole receives its blood by retrograde flow through the bone. A waist fracture therefore cuts off the proximal pole, which can die and fail to unite. Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox is the key sign, and the first radiograph may be normal because an undisplaced crack becomes visible only after bone along the fracture margins is resorbed."},{"type":"p","text":"The adult femoral head is supplied mainly by retinacular arteries, branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery that run up the femoral neck inside the joint capsule. A displaced intracapsular (femoral neck) fracture tears them, so the head may necrose and later collapse. The artery of the ligament of the head contributes little in adults. Extracapsular intertrochanteric fractures leave the retinacular vessels intact and rarely cause necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The supracondylar humerus fracture is the classic elbow fracture of children, typically about 5 to 7 years old, after a fall on an outstretched arm. In the usual extension type the distal fragment shifts posteriorly and the sharp proximal fragment is driven forward into the brachial artery and median nerve, which lie in front of the distal humerus on the brachialis. The anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve is often affected, weakening flexion of the thumb and index fingertip. Arterial injury and swelling can cause forearm compartment syndrome (C04). With medial displacement of the distal fragment the radial nerve is at risk, and the uncommon flexion type endangers the ulnar nerve."},{"type":"table","title":"Fracture, structure at risk, and result","cols":["Fracture","Structure at risk","Result"],"rows":[["Scaphoid waist","Retrograde supply to proximal pole","Proximal pole avascular necrosis, nonunion"],["Displaced femoral neck","Retinacular branches of medial circumflex femoral artery","Femoral head necrosis and collapse"],["Supracondylar humerus (extension type)","Brachial artery, median and anterior interosseous nerves","Hand ischemia, nerve palsy, compartment syndrome"],["Femoral or tibial shaft, pelvis","Marrow fat entering torn veins","Fat embolism syndrome"]],"caption":"Anatomy predicts the complication: retrograde or capsular blood supply, and neurovascular structures adjacent to the fragment."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat droplets enter the circulation in most patients with long bone or pelvic fractures because marrow fat is forced into torn venules held open by rigid bone. Only a small proportion develop fat embolism syndrome, and the 1 to 3 day delay reflects its second, biochemical stage: lipase breaks fat into free fatty acids that injure endothelium, and platelets aggregate on the droplets. Pulmonary capillary injury causes hypoxemia and can progress to acute respiratory distress. Deformable droplets pass the lung or a patent foramen ovale to reach the brain (confusion) and skin, where petechiae over the chest, axillae, and conjunctivae reflect capillary damage and thrombocytopenia."},{"type":"flow","title":"Fat embolism syndrome","steps":["Long bone fracture forces marrow fat into torn veins","Droplets lodge in pulmonary capillaries","Lipase releases free fatty acids; platelets aggregate","Endothelial injury: hypoxemia, possible respiratory distress","Systemic droplets: confusion and petechiae, 1 to 3 days after injury"],"caption":"Mechanical lodging is immediate; the toxic free fatty acid stage explains the delay before symptoms."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An 80-year-old woman with an intertrochanteric fracture and a 78-year-old woman with a displaced subcapital femoral neck fracture both heal their fractures. Which is more likely to develop femoral head collapse a year later?","explain":"The femoral neck fracture, because it lies inside the capsule and tears the retinacular arteries that supply the head; the intertrochanteric fracture is distal to them.","check":"Compare with the femoral neck paragraph and the table in this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C03","obj":"MS-13-H.3","title":"Sprain, strain, contusion, and dislocation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Soft tissue injuries are named by the tissue that fails. A sprain is a stretch or tear of a ligament, which joins bone to bone; it is graded from microscopic fiber tearing with a stable joint (grade I) through partial tear (grade II) to complete rupture with instability (grade III). A strain is a stretch or tear of muscle or tendon. It usually occurs at the myotendinous junction, the weakest link, during eccentric contraction (the muscle lengthening while contracting), so muscles crossing two joints, such as the hamstrings, are most often affected."},{"type":"p","text":"A contusion is a bruise: a blunt blow crushes tissue without breaking the skin, and blood from ruptured capillaries spreads into it. The color change of a bruise follows heme breakdown by macrophages: red-purple hemoglobin, then green biliverdin made by heme oxygenase, then yellow bilirubin and brown hemosiderin. A dislocation is complete loss of contact between articular surfaces, whereas a subluxation is partial loss. Because capsule and ligaments must tear to allow it, a dislocation can stretch nearby nerves and vessels, as with the axillary nerve in anterior shoulder dislocation."},{"type":"table","title":"Soft tissue injuries compared","cols":["Injury","Tissue that fails","Typical mechanism","Key finding"],"rows":[["Sprain","Ligament","Joint forced beyond its range (ankle inversion)","Joint pain and swelling; laxity if severe"],["Strain","Muscle or tendon","Eccentric overload","Pain on active contraction and on stretch"],["Contusion","Soft tissue capillaries","Direct blunt blow","Bruise, tender swelling"],["Dislocation","Joint congruity (capsule and ligaments)","Force driving the joint past its restraints","Deformity and loss of motion; risk to nearby nerves and vessels"]],"caption":"Name the tissue that failed, then predict the finding."},{"type":"p","text":"Ligaments and tendons are dense regular connective tissue: parallel bundles of type I collagen with few fibroblasts, little blood flow, and a low metabolic rate. After a tear, healing passes through inflammation, a proliferative phase in which fibroblasts lay down disorganized type III collagen, and a long remodeling phase. The repair tissue has thinner fibrils, more type III collagen, and fewer mature cross-links, so it stays weaker and more lax than the original for a year or more, which explains recurrent sprains and chronic instability. Intra-articular ligaments heal worst: synovial fluid dilutes and breaks down the clot that should form a scaffold, and the torn ends retract, which is why a torn anterior cruciate ligament does not heal on its own while the extra-articular medial collateral ligament usually does. Muscle heals better because satellite cells beneath the basal lamina can regenerate fibers, although a large tear still leaves scar."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C04","obj":"MS-13-H.4","title":"Compartment syndrome and Volkmann ischemic contracture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Muscles of the limbs lie in compartments bounded by bone and deep fascia, which barely stretches. Any added volume inside a compartment therefore raises its pressure steeply. Normal resting compartment pressure is about 0 to 8 mmHg. Volume rises from bleeding after a fracture (most often the tibia, then the forearm), swelling after crush injury, edema when blood flow returns after a period of ischemia (reperfusion), or external restriction by a tight cast or circumferential burn eschar."},{"type":"p","text":"Tissue pressure opposes the pressure inside thin-walled vessels. Veins and capillaries collapse first because their pressures are lowest, so venous outflow and capillary perfusion fail while the higher arterial pressure still drives a pulse through the compartment. Ischemic capillaries leak more fluid, which raises compartment pressure further and closes the cycle. Perfusion of a compartment depends on the difference between arterial pressure and compartment pressure, so hypotension lowers the compartment pressure at which ischemia begins."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The pressure cycle in compartment syndrome","steps":["Bleeding or edema adds volume to a closed compartment","Compartment pressure exceeds venous and capillary pressure","Muscle and nerve become ischemic","Ischemic capillaries leak more fluid","Added fluid raises compartment pressure further"],"caption":"The loop is self-sustaining: each turn raises pressure further."},{"type":"p","text":"The findings follow the order in which tissues fail. Ischemic muscle is intensely painful, so the earliest sign is pain out of proportion to the injury. Passively stretching the muscles of the compartment (for example, extending the toes for the deep posterior leg) lengthens ischemic fibers and raises pressure, sharply worsening the pain. Nerves then lose function, causing paresthesia, starting with the nerve that runs through the compartment, such as numbness of the first web space from the deep fibular nerve in the anterior leg. Paralysis comes later. Pallor and pulselessness appear late or not at all, so a palpable pulse does not exclude the diagnosis. Nerve conduction fails within about an hour, and muscle necrosis becomes irreversible after about 6 hours of ischemia. Dead muscle can release myoglobin and potassium, linking this condition to rhabdomyolysis (C05)."},{"type":"example","title":"Why the pulse persists","text":"A man with a tibial shaft fracture has a compartment pressure of 45 mmHg and blood pressure 120/70 mmHg. Capillary pressure is roughly 10 to 30 mmHg, highest at the arterial end, so a tissue pressure of 45 mmHg collapses capillaries and venules and stops nutrient flow. Arterial pressure (70 to 120 mmHg) still exceeds 45 mmHg, so the artery stays open and the dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable. The difference between diastolic and compartment pressure is 70 minus 45, or 25 mmHg; a difference of about 30 mmHg or less is commonly taken to mean inadequate perfusion. If his blood pressure fell to 90/50 mmHg, the difference would shrink to 5 mmHg, and ischemia would worsen without any change in compartment pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"Volkmann ischemic contracture is the late result of untreated forearm compartment syndrome, classically after a supracondylar humerus fracture. The deep flexors of the volar compartment (flexor digitorum profundus and flexor pollicis longus) are most vulnerable because they lie deepest, against bone. Necrotic muscle is replaced by fibrous tissue that shortens, producing a flexed wrist, flexed finger joints (often with extended metacarpophalangeal joints), and a pronated forearm, with sensory loss from median and ulnar nerve ischemia. Unlike Dupuytren contracture (MS-13-E), the problem lies in dead muscle rather than in the palmar fascia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a forearm fracture in a cast has severe pain on passive finger extension, a normal radial pulse, and normal capillary refill. Is compartment syndrome excluded?","explain":"No. Arterial pressure stays above compartment pressure until late, so the pulse persists while capillary perfusion to muscle has already failed. Pain with passive stretch is the earliest reliable sign.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the order in which tissues fail and the worked example."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C05","obj":"MS-13-H.5","title":"Rhabdomyolysis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rhabdomyolysis is breakdown of skeletal muscle with release of its contents into the blood. The common pathway is ATP depletion or direct membrane damage. Without ATP the Na+/K+-ATPase and the Ca2+ pumps fail, cytosolic calcium rises, and calcium activates proteases and phospholipases, sustains contraction, and damages mitochondria, so the cell breaks down. Causes include crush injury and prolonged immobilization on a hard surface, extreme exertion (especially in heat, with hypokalemia, or with sickle cell trait), seizures, alcohol and cocaine, metabolic myopathies such as McArdle disease, and statins, whose muscle toxicity is thought to involve reduced coenzyme Q10 and protein prenylation and rises when a second drug slows statin clearance."},{"type":"flow","title":"From muscle injury to acute kidney injury","steps":["Crush, exertion, or toxin depletes muscle ATP or tears the sarcolemma","Cytosolic calcium rises; proteases and phospholipases destroy the cell","CK, myoglobin, potassium, phosphate, and urate enter the blood","Fluid sequestered in swollen muscle lowers renal perfusion","Filtered myoglobin forms casts and its heme injures tubules","Acute kidney injury with hyperkalemia and acidosis"],"caption":"Kidney injury combines renal hypoperfusion, tubular obstruction, and direct heme toxicity."},{"type":"p","text":"Creatine kinase (CK) is the most sensitive marker. Rhabdomyolysis is usually defined as CK above about 5 times the upper reference limit, and values in the tens of thousands of U/L are common. CK peaks at about 1 to 3 days and falls slowly, whereas myoglobin, a small 17 kDa protein, is filtered and cleared within hours, so myoglobinuria may already have cleared when CK is still very high."},{"type":"p","text":"The urine dipstick detects the peroxidase-like activity of heme, and it cannot tell intact red cells from free heme protein. A strongly positive blood reaction with few or no red cells on microscopy therefore means myoglobin or free hemoglobin, and the urine is often red-brown or tea-colored. In the kidney, myoglobin precipitates with uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall protein) in acidic tubular fluid to form pigmented casts, its heme iron drives oxidative injury to tubular cells, and free heme scavenges nitric oxide, constricting renal vessels."},{"type":"table","title":"Expected laboratory pattern","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["CK very high","Released from damaged muscle cytosol"],["Dipstick positive for blood, few red cells","Filtered myoglobin has heme peroxidase activity"],["Hyperkalemia","Potassium released from muscle, worsened by kidney injury; can cause arrhythmia"],["Hyperphosphatemia and hyperuricemia","Phosphate and purines released from muscle"],["Early hypocalcemia, later hypercalcemia","Calcium deposits in necrotic muscle, then is released during recovery"],["Anion gap metabolic acidosis","Organic acids from muscle and reduced renal acid excretion"],["Rising creatinine with pigmented granular casts","Acute tubular injury"]],"caption":"Hemolysis also gives a heme-positive dipstick without red cells, but CK is normal and plasma may be pink."},{"type":"example","title":"Myoglobin or hemoglobin?","text":"Two patients have urine dipsticks 3+ for blood and 0 to 2 red cells per high-power field. Patient A ran a marathon in heat: CK 48,000 U/L, potassium 6.2 mmol/L, plasma clear. Patient B has a mechanical heart valve: CK normal, LDH high, haptoglobin low, plasma pink. In both, free heme pigment explains the dipstick. In A, myoglobin is small and loosely bound in plasma, so it is filtered and cleared quickly and plasma stays clear, while CK confirms muscle as the source. In B, free hemoglobin first saturates haptoglobin and stays in plasma, coloring it pink, and the low haptoglobin and high LDH point to red cell destruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-13-08-C06","obj":"MS-13-H.6","title":"Myositis ossificans and its distinction from osteosarcoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myositis ossificans is a reactive, benign formation of bone within skeletal muscle or nearby soft tissue. It typically follows a contusion or repeated injury in an athletic adolescent or young adult, most often in the quadriceps or the brachialis, although some patients recall no injury. Injured muscle releases inflammatory signals and bone morphogenetic proteins that drive local mesenchymal progenitor cells to become osteoblasts, so bone forms where muscle healed with hemorrhage and granulation tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesion evolves predictably. In the first weeks it is a painful, enlarging soft tissue mass. Faint mineralization appears after about 3 to 4 weeks, and by about 6 to 8 weeks imaging shows a well-defined peripheral rim of bone. Over months the mass matures, often shrinks, and becomes painless. It is usually separated from the adjacent cortex by a thin lucent cleft."},{"type":"p","text":"The key feature is zonation. The center contains loosely arranged, proliferating fibroblasts that can show mitoses; the middle zone holds osteoid and woven bone rimmed by osteoblasts; and the periphery is mature lamellar bone. The lesion matures from the outside in. Osteosarcoma shows the reverse: malignant cells with marked atypia and atypical mitoses lay down lace-like osteoid directly, the most immature and atypical tissue lies at the expanding edge, and that edge infiltrates and destroys surrounding bone and muscle, often lifting the periosteum. An early biopsy of myositis ossificans that samples only the cellular center can look alarming, so the pattern across the whole lesion is what matters."},{"type":"table","title":"Myositis ossificans versus osteosarcoma","cols":["Feature","Myositis ossificans","Osteosarcoma (MS-13-F)"],"rows":[["Nature","Reactive, benign","Malignant neoplasm"],["Zonation","Immature center, mature bone at periphery","Most immature, atypical tissue at periphery"],["Margin","Well defined; lucent cleft from cortex","Infiltrative; arises from and destroys bone"],["Cells","Proliferating fibroblasts without marked atypia","Pleomorphic malignant cells, atypical mitoses"],["Course","Matures and often shrinks over months","Progressive growth"]],"caption":"Direction of maturation separates a reactive lesion from a malignant one."},{"type":"p","text":"Post-traumatic myositis ossificans should not be confused with fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva, a rare autosomal dominant disease caused by an activating mutation in ACVR1, a BMP type I receptor. In that disease, minor injury triggers progressive ossification of muscles, tendons, and ligaments throughout the body, often with malformed great toes from birth. Heterotopic ossification in general also follows hip surgery, spinal cord injury, and burns."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A biopsy from a thigh mass in a 17-year-old soccer player shows proliferating fibroblasts with mitoses in the center and mature trabecular bone around the edge. Is this more consistent with osteosarcoma or myositis ossificans?","explain":"Myositis ossificans, because its most mature bone lies at the periphery around an immature center; osteosarcoma has its least mature, most atypical tissue at the infiltrating edge.","check":"Compare with the zonation paragraph and the comparison table."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy predicts most fracture complications. The scaphoid and the femoral head share a blood supply that reaches them from one direction, so a fracture across that path causes avascular necrosis, and the supracondylar humerus lies directly behind the brachial artery and median nerve. Physiology explains compartment syndrome: capillaries fail before arteries because their pressure is lower, which is why pain and passive-stretch pain come before loss of pulses. Biochemistry and renal pathology link crushed muscle to the kidney through ATP depletion, calcium overload, myoglobin casts, and heme toxicity, and the urine dipstick reads heme, not red cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Repair biology connects the rest. Bone regenerates by repeating endochondral and intramembranous ossification, provided blood vessels and limited motion allow it; ligaments heal slowly with weaker type III collagen scar; and injured muscle can either regenerate from satellite cells, scar and contract after ischemia, or ossify. Timings and pressure thresholds in this chapter are approximate and vary with the patient and the source, so they support reasoning rather than replace the whole clinical picture."}],"summary":["Secondary fracture healing runs from hematoma and inflammation to soft callus, hard callus by endochondral ossification, and remodeling to lamellar bone.","Healing needs blood vessels and limited motion; poor supply, instability, infection, smoking, diabetes, and malnutrition cause delayed union or nonunion.","Scaphoid waist and displaced femoral neck fractures cut a one-directional blood supply, causing avascular necrosis of the proximal pole or femoral head.","Extension-type supracondylar humerus fractures injure the brachial artery and median (anterior interosseous) nerve and can cause forearm compartment syndrome.","Fat embolism syndrome appears 1 to 3 days after long bone fracture as hypoxemia, confusion, and petechiae, from free fatty acid injury to endothelium.","Sprain is ligament, strain is muscle or tendon, contusion is bleeding from a blow, and dislocation is loss of joint contact; ligaments heal slowly because they are hypocellular and poorly vascularized.","In compartment syndrome capillaries collapse before arteries, so pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch precede loss of pulses; Volkmann contracture is the late forearm result.","Rhabdomyolysis gives very high CK, a heme-positive dipstick without red cells, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, early hypocalcemia, and acute kidney injury.","Myositis ossificans matures from the periphery inward, leaving a shell of mature bone around an immature center; osteosarcoma has its most atypical tissue at the infiltrating edge."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors (fracture healing, osteonecrosis, myositis ossificans, osteosarcoma)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Hemodynamic Disorders, Thromboembolism, and Shock (fat embolism)"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb and Lower Limb (scaphoid, supracondylar humerus, and femoral neck fractures; fascial compartments)"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone (fracture repair) and Connective Tissue"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: capillary fluid exchange"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Renal Disease, acute kidney injury"}]},"MS-2":{"unit":"MS-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01","title":"Muscle, Bone, and Joint Histology","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the microscopic structure of the musculoskeletal system: the sarcomere, the three muscle types, compact and spongy bone, the cells that build and remove bone, the epiphyseal growth plate, and the three classes of joints. Board questions rarely ask for a label alone. They ask you to predict what changes when a filament slides, a receptor is overactive, a mineral is missing, or a tissue has no blood supply. Each structure here is explained by the job it does, so that achondroplasia, rickets, osteopetrosis, and cartilage that heals poorly follow directly from normal histology.","prerequisites":["Basic tissue types and the structure of collagen and proteoglycans","Cell junctions: gap junctions, desmosomes, and adherens junctions","Calcium and phosphate balance with PTH and vitamin D (EN-5)","Endochondral and intramembranous ossification (MS-1)"],"connections":[{"to":"Muscle contraction (MS-7) and cardiac muscle contraction (CV-5)","how":"The sarcomere, triads, and dyads described here are the structures that excitation-contraction coupling uses."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal connective tissue (MS-6)","how":"Type I collagen in bone and ligament and type II collagen with aggrecan in cartilage explain the different strengths of each tissue."},{"to":"Endocrine function and control (EN-5)","how":"PTH, calcitriol, FGF23, estrogen, and growth hormone act on the bone cells and growth plate chondrocytes described in this chapter."},{"to":"Metabolic and congenital musculoskeletal disease (MS-13-C, MS-13-D)","how":"Osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, rickets, and achondroplasia are each a defect in one cell or one zone named here."},{"to":"Joints: structure and function (MS-4)","how":"Joint class sets how much a joint moves and how its cartilage is fed, which shapes how arthritis develops."}],"terms":[{"term":"Sarcomere","def":"The contractile unit of striated muscle, extending from one Z line to the next."},{"term":"Triad","def":"A T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum, found at the A band to I band junction in skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Intercalated disc","def":"The step-shaped junction between cardiac muscle cells that contains fascia adherens, desmosomes, and gap junctions."},{"term":"Dense body","def":"A cytoplasmic or membrane-attached plaque rich in alpha-actinin that anchors thin filaments in smooth muscle, the functional equivalent of a Z line."},{"term":"Osteon","def":"The structural unit of compact bone: concentric lamellae arranged around a central (Haversian) canal."},{"term":"Osteoid","def":"Newly secreted, unmineralized bone matrix, mostly type I collagen."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"A ligand on osteoblast-lineage cells that binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their formation and activation."},{"term":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG)","def":"A soluble decoy receptor made by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents osteoclast activation."},{"term":"Howship lacuna","def":"The shallow pit on a bone surface that an active osteoclast excavates beneath its ruffled border."},{"term":"Growth plate (physis)","def":"The disc of hyaline cartilage between epiphysis and metaphysis whose chondrocytes drive lengthening of long bones."},{"term":"Synchondrosis","def":"A primary cartilaginous joint in which bones are joined by hyaline cartilage."},{"term":"Symphysis","def":"A secondary cartilaginous joint in which bones are joined by fibrocartilage, usually in the midline."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C01","obj":"MS-2.1","title":"The sarcomere and its bands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle look striated because their myofibrils are built of repeating sarcomeres lined up in register across the cell. A sarcomere runs from one Z line (Z disc) to the next. Thin filaments of actin, with tropomyosin and troponin, are anchored at the Z line by alpha-actinin and project toward the center. Thick filaments of myosin sit in the center, held in place at the M line and tethered to the Z line by the giant elastic protein titin."},{"type":"p","text":"The bands are defined by which filaments are present. The A band (anisotropic: birefringent in polarized light, dark in stained sections) is the full length of the thick filaments, including the parts that overlap thin filaments. The I band (isotropic, light) contains thin filaments only and is bisected by the Z line, so each I band belongs to two neighboring sarcomeres. The H zone is the central part of the A band where thick filaments are not overlapped by thin filaments, and the M line runs through its middle, where proteins such as myomesin cross-link the thick filaments."},{"type":"table","title":"Sarcomere landmarks and what happens to them","cols":["Landmark","Contains or defined by","During contraction"],"rows":[["Z line","Alpha-actinin anchoring thin filaments; sarcomere boundary","Z lines move closer together"],["I band","Thin filaments only","Shortens"],["A band","Whole length of thick filaments","Stays constant"],["H zone","Thick filaments only, no overlap","Shortens, may disappear"],["M line","Cross-linking proteins in the center of the thick filaments","Stays at the center"],["Sarcomere (Z to Z)","One contractile unit","Shortens"]],"caption":"Only regions defined by the absence of overlap change length; the A band is set by the length of the thick filament itself."},{"type":"p","text":"These changes follow from the sliding filament mechanism. Neither actin nor myosin filaments change length. Myosin heads on each half of the bipolar thick filament pull their thin filaments toward the M line, so overlap increases. The non-overlap regions (I band and H zone) therefore narrow, the Z lines approach each other, and the A band is unchanged. When a muscle is passively stretched the opposite occurs: the I band and H zone widen while the A band still stays constant."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating band widths","text":"Take thick filaments of about 1.6 micrometers and thin filaments of about 1.0 micrometer extending from each Z line, ignoring Z line thickness. At a sarcomere length of 2.4 micrometers, the A band is 1.6, the I band is 2.4 minus 1.6, or 0.8, and the H zone is 2.4 minus 2 times 1.0, or 0.4. If the sarcomere shortens to 2.0 micrometers, the A band is still 1.6, the I band falls to 0.4, and the H zone falls to 0. The I band and H zone each lost 0.4 micrometers, exactly the amount the sarcomere shortened, because the thin filaments slid that distance."},{"type":"p","text":"The amount of overlap also sets force. Near the length where every myosin head can reach actin and thin filaments do not collide, tension is maximal; at very long lengths few cross-bridges form, and at very short lengths filaments collide and interfere. This length-tension relationship (MS-7) is the sarcomere basis of the Frank-Starling mechanism in the heart."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A muscle fiber is stretched passively well beyond its resting length. Which bands change, and in which direction?","explain":"The I band and H zone widen because the thin filaments are pulled out of overlap, and the Z lines move apart. The A band does not change because thick filament length does not change.","check":"The landmark table and the sliding filament paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C02","obj":"MS-2.2","title":"Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle on histology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each muscle type is built around how it receives its signal to contract and how its cells work together. Recognizing them on a slide comes down to four questions: is the tissue striated, where are the nuclei, how many are there, and how are the cells joined."},{"type":"p","text":"A skeletal muscle fiber is a single huge cell formed by fusion of myoblasts, so it is multinucleate, with many nuclei pressed flat against the sarcolemma. In cross section fibers are polygonal with peripheral nuclei. Because each fiber is wide and must contract uniformly along its length, T tubules (inward extensions of the sarcolemma) dive into the fiber at every A band to I band junction and pair with two terminal cisternae of sarcoplasmic reticulum to form triads, giving rapid, uniform calcium release. Mature fiber nuclei do not divide; repair depends on satellite cells lying between the sarcolemma and the basal lamina. Centrally placed nuclei in skeletal muscle are therefore abnormal and indicate regeneration or a myopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac muscle cells are separate cells that must contract as one unit. They are branched, with one or occasionally two central nuclei, often surrounded by a pale perinuclear zone that collects lipofuscin with age. Intercalated discs join cells end to end. Their transverse portions carry fascia adherens, which anchor actin filaments, and desmosomes, which link desmin intermediate filaments and keep cells from pulling apart; their lateral portions carry gap junctions (mainly connexin 43) that let depolarization spread from cell to cell so the myocardium behaves as a functional syncytium. Cardiac T tubules are wider and lie at the Z lines, usually pairing with a single SR cisterna as a dyad, which fits the cardiac reliance on calcium entering through L-type channels to trigger SR calcium release. Adult cardiomyocytes have very limited capacity to divide, so injury heals by fibrous scar and increased workload causes hypertrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"Smooth muscle cells are fusiform with one central, cigar-shaped nucleus that may look corkscrewed in a contracted cell. Actin and myosin are not arranged in register, so there are no striations; thin filaments attach to dense bodies in the cytoplasm and on the membrane, and desmin intermediate filaments link the dense bodies into a network that pulls the cell into a shorter, rounder shape. There are no T tubules; caveolae and a sparse SR serve the small cell. Single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle is coupled by gap junctions. Smooth muscle can divide, so organs such as the uterus in pregnancy enlarge by both hypertrophy and hyperplasia."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three muscle types","cols":["Feature","Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth"],"rows":[["Shape","Long cylindrical fiber","Branched cell","Fusiform (spindle)"],["Nuclei","Many, peripheral","One or two, central","One, central"],["Striations","Yes","Yes","No"],["Junctions between cells","None (each fiber innervated)","Intercalated discs: fascia adherens, desmosomes, gap junctions","Gap junctions in single-unit muscle"],["T tubule arrangement","Triads at A to I junction","Dyads at Z line, wider T tubules","No T tubules; caveolae"],["Thin filament anchor","Z line","Z line","Dense bodies"],["Response to injury or load","Satellite cell regeneration; hypertrophy","Scar; hypertrophy","Hyperplasia and hypertrophy"]],"caption":"Peripheral nuclei plus striations means skeletal; central nuclei plus striations and intercalated discs means cardiac; central nucleus without striations means smooth."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C03","obj":"MS-2.3","title":"Compact bone, spongy bone, and woven versus lamellar bone","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone matrix is type I collagen hardened by hydroxyapatite crystals. Mineral gives stiffness and collagen gives resistance to fracture, but mineralized matrix blocks diffusion, so every osteocyte must stay within a short distance of a blood vessel. The architecture of bone is largely the solution to that supply problem."},{"type":"p","text":"Compact (cortical) bone forms the shafts of long bones and the outer shell of all bones. Its unit is the osteon (Haversian system), a cylinder of concentric lamellae running along the long axis of the bone around a central Haversian canal that carries capillaries and nerves. Collagen fibers in adjacent lamellae run in alternating directions, which resists loads from several directions. Osteocytes sit in lacunae between lamellae and extend processes through tiny channels called canaliculi; the processes meet through gap junctions, so nutrients and signals pass from the central canal outward cell by cell. Volkmann (perforating) canals run across the bone, roughly perpendicular to osteons, and connect Haversian canals to each other, to the periosteum, and to the marrow cavity; they are not surrounded by concentric lamellae. Each osteon is bounded by a cement line. Between osteons lie interstitial lamellae, the remnants of older, partly resorbed osteons, and circumferential lamellae line the outer and inner surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"The periosteum covers the outer surface. Its outer fibrous layer is anchored by collagen (Sharpey) fibers, and its inner layer holds osteoprogenitor cells that allow growth in width and fracture repair. A thinner endosteum lines the marrow cavity and trabeculae."},{"type":"p","text":"Spongy (cancellous, trabecular) bone, found in vertebral bodies, the ends of long bones, and the interior of flat bones, is a lattice of thin plates and struts called trabeculae with marrow in the spaces between them. Trabeculae are thin enough to be nourished from the marrow surface, so they lack osteons and Haversian canals. Their large surface area makes trabecular bone turn over faster than cortical bone, which is why trabecular-rich sites such as vertebrae, the distal radius, and the femoral neck lose mass early in osteoporosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Woven versus lamellar bone","cols":["Feature","Woven (primary) bone","Lamellar (secondary) bone"],"rows":[["Collagen arrangement","Random, interlacing","Parallel sheets in ordered lamellae"],["Osteocytes","Many, large, irregular","Fewer, evenly spaced"],["Speed of formation","Fast","Slow"],["Strength","Weak","Strong"],["Normal setting","Fetal skeleton, early fracture callus","Nearly all of the adult skeleton"],["Abnormal setting in adults","Paget disease, fibrous dysplasia, bone tumors, severe hyperparathyroidism","Not applicable"]],"caption":"Woven bone trades strength for speed and is normally remodeled into lamellar bone; its presence in adult bone signals rapid abnormal formation."},{"type":"p","text":"Paget disease shows why the distinction matters. Disordered, excessive osteoclast resorption followed by disorganized osteoblast repair forms woven bone early and later a mosaic of lamellar bone with irregular cement lines, so the bone is thicker yet weaker and prone to bowing and fracture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C04","obj":"MS-2.4","title":"Osteoblasts, osteocytes, and osteoclasts","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone is remodeled throughout life. Formation and resorption are coupled because the osteoblast lineage controls the osteoclast, and most hormones that change bone mass act at that point."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal stem cells under the transcription factors RUNX2 and osterix, with Wnt and beta-catenin signaling promoting their differentiation. They are cuboidal cells with basophilic cytoplasm, rich in rough ER, lining bone surfaces. They secrete osteoid, which is mostly type I collagen with noncollagenous proteins such as osteocalcin, and they express alkaline phosphatase, which raises local phosphate and breaks down pyrophosphate, an inhibitor of mineralization, so hydroxyapatite can form in the osteoid. Serum bone-specific alkaline phosphatase therefore rises when bone formation is active, as in growth, fracture healing, Paget disease, and rickets. Osteoblasts carry the PTH receptor and vitamin D receptor and secrete both RANKL and OPG."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteocytes are osteoblasts walled in by their own matrix; they are the most numerous bone cells and connect through canaliculi. Fluid flow in the canaliculi during loading lets them act as mechanosensors. Unloading increases their secretion of sclerostin, which inhibits Wnt signaling in osteoblasts and reduces formation, while loading suppresses sclerostin. Osteocytes are also the main source of FGF23, which lowers serum phosphate (see the cycle below)."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteoclasts come from the monocyte and macrophage lineage, not from mesenchyme. M-CSF and RANKL drive precursors to fuse into large multinucleated cells with acidophilic cytoplasm. An active osteoclast seals onto bone with integrin alpha-v beta-3 at a ring-shaped sealing zone. Inside the ring, its ruffled border pumps H+ through a vacuolar H+ ATPase, with H+ supplied by carbonic anhydrase II and chloride following through the ClC-7 channel; the resulting acid dissolves hydroxyapatite, and the protease cathepsin K then digests the exposed collagen. The pit left behind is a Howship lacuna. Osteoclasts express tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP) and calcitonin receptors."},{"type":"flow","title":"Coupled remodeling","steps":["PTH or local signals raise RANKL relative to OPG","RANKL binds RANK on monocyte-lineage precursors (with M-CSF)","Precursors fuse into multinucleated osteoclasts","Ruffled border releases acid and cathepsin K","Howship lacuna forms; matrix factors recruit osteoblasts","Osteoblasts lay osteoid that then mineralizes","Some osteoblasts are trapped and become osteocytes"],"caption":"PTH acts on osteoblast-lineage cells, which signal osteoclast precursors through RANKL; osteoclasts lack PTH receptors."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Osteocyte FGF23 and phosphate","steps":["Serum phosphate or calcitriol rises","Osteocytes increase FGF23 secretion","Kidney reduces proximal phosphate reabsorption","FGF23 also lowers 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol falls","Serum phosphate falls, reducing FGF23 drive"],"caption":"Excess FGF23, as in X-linked hypophosphatemia from PHEX mutation, causes renal phosphate wasting and rickets or osteomalacia."},{"type":"p","text":"This control explains several classic findings. Continuously high PTH raises RANKL relative to OPG and causes net resorption, whereas estrogen restrains RANKL signaling and shortens osteoclast life, so its loss at menopause speeds resorption. If osteoclasts cannot acidify their lacuna, bone is not removed, producing osteopetrosis: dense, brittle bone that crowds the marrow and narrows cranial foramina. Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency adds renal tubular acidosis, and cathepsin K deficiency causes pycnodysostosis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A mouse lacks the gene for osteoprotegerin. What happens to its bone mass, and why?","explain":"Bone mass falls. Without the decoy receptor, RANKL binds RANK unopposed, so osteoclast formation and resorption outpace formation.","check":"The RANKL and OPG steps in the remodeling flow."},{"type":"example","title":"Dense bones that break","text":"A child has fractures after minor falls, anemia, metabolic acidosis with an inappropriately high urine pH, and uniformly dense bones on radiographs. Dense bone means bone is made but not removed, so osteoclasts are failing. The marrow cavity shrinks, causing anemia, and old microdamaged matrix is never replaced, so bone is dense but brittle. A defect shared by bone and renal tubules points to the enzyme both use to generate secreted H+: carbonic anhydrase II. The diagnosis is osteopetrosis with renal tubular acidosis from carbonic anhydrase II deficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C05","obj":"MS-2.5","title":"The epiphyseal growth plate, achondroplasia, and rickets","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Long bones lengthen by endochondral ossification at the growth plate (physis), a disc of hyaline cartilage between the epiphysis and the metaphysis. Bone does not grow by expansion of existing bone; cartilage is added on the epiphyseal side and replaced by bone on the metaphyseal side. The plate keeps a fairly constant thickness during growth because the two processes run at matched rates."},{"type":"flow","title":"Growth plate zones, epiphysis to diaphysis","steps":["Reserve zone: scattered resting chondrocytes supply new cells","Proliferative zone: chondrocytes divide into stacked columns","Hypertrophic zone: cells swell, make type X collagen and VEGF","Calcification zone: matrix mineralizes, chondrocytes die","Ossification zone: vessels invade, osteoblasts lay bone on cartilage"],"caption":"Proliferation and hypertrophy produce length; mineralization of cartilage and vascular invasion let bone replace it."},{"type":"p","text":"Length comes from two steps: division in the proliferative zone and enlargement of chondrocytes in the hypertrophic zone, which accounts for much of the gain. Hypertrophic chondrocytes release VEGF and matrix vesicles; once their matrix calcifies they die, and the calcified cartilage becomes a scaffold. Capillaries and osteoprogenitor cells from the marrow invade, and osteoblasts deposit bone on the calcified cartilage, producing mixed spicules with a cartilage core and a bone covering that are later remodeled. Proliferation is regulated by growth hormone through IGF-1, thyroid hormone, a local Indian hedgehog and PTHrP feedback loop, and FGFR3 signaling, which acts as a brake. Estrogen in both sexes eventually closes the plate. The hypertrophic zone, with large cells and little matrix, is the mechanically weakest layer, so physeal fractures and slipped capital femoral epiphysis usually pass through it."},{"type":"p","text":"Achondroplasia is a gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3, autosomal dominant but usually a new mutation, often linked to advanced paternal age. The overactive receptor suppresses chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy, so the proliferative zone is thin with short, disorganized columns. Bones formed by endochondral ossification are short, especially the proximal limb segments, while bones formed by intramembranous ossification, such as the skull vault, grow normally. The result is short limbs with a near-normal trunk, a relatively large head with frontal bossing, and a small cranial base that can narrow the foramen magnum."},{"type":"p","text":"Rickets is defective mineralization of the growing skeleton, from vitamin D deficiency or from phosphate loss. Low phosphate prevents the normal apoptosis of hypertrophic chondrocytes, and unmineralized cartilage cannot be invaded by vessels and replaced. Hypertrophic chondrocytes therefore pile up in long, disordered columns, the plate becomes wide and irregular, and osteoid accumulates without mineral. Radiographs show widened plates with frayed, cupped metaphyses. Clinically, enlarged costochondral junctions form the rachitic rosary, and soft bones bow under weight bearing. In adults, whose plates have closed, the same mineral defect causes osteomalacia, with excess osteoid on bone surfaces but no plate changes."},{"type":"table","title":"Growth plate in achondroplasia and rickets","cols":["Feature","Achondroplasia","Rickets"],"rows":[["Primary defect","Too little chondrocyte proliferation (FGFR3 activation)","Too little mineralization (low vitamin D or phosphate)"],["Zone most affected","Proliferative zone thin","Hypertrophic zone expanded and disordered"],["Plate width","Thin","Wide and irregular"],["Bone mineral","Normal","Low, with excess osteoid"],["Skull vault","Normal size (intramembranous)","Soft (craniotabes), delayed fontanelle closure"],["Typical serum alkaline phosphatase","Normal","Elevated"]],"caption":"Achondroplasia makes too little cartilage; rickets makes cartilage that cannot be converted to bone."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-02-01-C06","obj":"MS-2.6","title":"Fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial joints","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Joints are classified by the tissue that binds the bones together, and that tissue determines how much movement is possible. Fibrous joints are joined by dense connective tissue and move little, cartilaginous joints by cartilage and move slightly, and synovial joints have a fluid-filled cavity and move freely."},{"type":"table","title":"Joint classes","cols":["Class and subtype","Binding tissue","Examples","Notes"],"rows":[["Fibrous: suture","Thin dense connective tissue","Sutures of the skull vault","Allow molding at birth and skull growth; may fuse with age"],["Fibrous: syndesmosis","Ligament or interosseous membrane","Distal tibiofibular joint; radius and ulna interosseous membrane","Slight movement"],["Fibrous: gomphosis","Periodontal ligament","Tooth in its alveolar socket","Peg in socket"],["Cartilaginous: synchondrosis (primary)","Hyaline cartilage","Epiphyseal plate; spheno-occipital; first sternocostal joint","Most are temporary and ossify"],["Cartilaginous: symphysis (secondary)","Fibrocartilage, with thin hyaline cartilage on the bone ends","Pubic symphysis; intervertebral disc; manubriosternal joint","Midline, slight movement, permanent"],["Synovial","Joint capsule enclosing a synovial cavity","Knee, hip, shoulder","Free movement; articular hyaline cartilage"]],"caption":"Synchondroses use hyaline cartilage and symphyses use fibrocartilage."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibrocartilage combines rows of chondrocytes with dense bundles of type I collagen in addition to type II, so it resists both compression and tension, suiting it to the pubic symphysis and the annulus fibrosus of the intervertebral disc. The disc's central nucleus pulposus is a gel derived from the notochord. When the annulus weakens, usually posterolaterally where the posterior longitudinal ligament is narrow, the nucleus can herniate and compress a nerve root."},{"type":"p","text":"A synovial joint is enclosed by a capsule with an outer fibrous layer and an inner synovial membrane. The synovium has no true epithelium and no basement membrane: one to a few layers of synoviocytes rest on vascular loose connective tissue. Type A synoviocytes are macrophage-like cells derived from bone marrow that clear debris; type B synoviocytes are fibroblast-like cells that secrete hyaluronan and lubricin. Synovial fluid is a filtrate of plasma enriched with these molecules. Hyaluronan makes the fluid viscous, and lubricin coats the cartilage surface to reduce friction."},{"type":"p","text":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage rich in type II collagen and aggrecan, whose negatively charged glycosaminoglycans hold water and resist compression. Unlike most hyaline cartilage it has no perichondrium, and it has no blood vessels, lymphatics, or nerves. Chondrocytes receive nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, helped by the pumping effect of cyclic loading. With no vessels to bring repair cells and no perichondrium to supply new chondroblasts, damaged articular cartilage repairs poorly, which underlies osteoarthritis. In rheumatoid arthritis, by contrast, the disease begins in the vascular synovium, which proliferates into a pannus that erodes cartilage and bone."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A full-thickness defect in articular cartilage reaches the subchondral bone. How will it heal compared with a defect confined to the cartilage?","explain":"A defect limited to cartilage barely heals because no vessels or perichondrium can supply repair cells. A defect reaching subchondral bone lets marrow cells and blood enter, but they form fibrocartilage, which is weaker and wears faster than hyaline cartilage.","check":"The paragraph on articular cartilage nutrition and repair."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology set the pattern. Endochondral bones grow from a cartilage model at growth plates while intramembranous bones form directly in mesenchyme, which is why FGFR3 mutations shorten limbs but spare the skull vault. Muscle structure mirrors its signaling: triads, dyads, and caveolae match how each muscle type raises cytosolic calcium (MS-7, CV-5), and the ability to divide (smooth muscle), regenerate from satellite cells (skeletal), or only scar (cardiac) predicts the outcome of injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, and pathology meet at the bone cell. Bone mass reflects the balance of RANKL and OPG on osteoblast-lineage cells, with PTH, estrogen, and mechanical load acting there; mineralization depends on vitamin D, phosphate, FGF23, and alkaline phosphatase; and collagen types I and II determine the behavior of bone and cartilage. Histology explains appearance and mechanism but not every clinical detail, because the same pattern can have several causes: a wide growth plate is rickets from vitamin D deficiency or from renal phosphate wasting, and only serum chemistry separates them."}],"summary":["During contraction the I band, H zone, and sarcomere shorten while the A band, set by thick filament length, stays constant; no filament changes length.","Skeletal muscle is multinucleate with peripheral nuclei and triads; cardiac muscle is branched with central nuclei, dyads, and intercalated discs; smooth muscle is fusiform, nonstriated, and anchors actin to dense bodies.","Intercalated disc gap junctions make the myocardium a functional syncytium, while fascia adherens and desmosomes hold cells together.","Compact bone osteons keep osteocytes near Haversian vessels through canaliculi and gap junctions; Volkmann canals cross between osteons.","Spongy bone has no osteons and a large surface area, so it turns over faster and is lost first in osteoporosis; woven bone in adults signals abnormal rapid formation.","Osteoblasts (mesenchymal) make type I collagen osteoid and alkaline phosphatase and control osteoclasts through RANKL and OPG; osteocytes sense load and secrete sclerostin and FGF23.","Osteoclasts (monocyte lineage) dissolve mineral with acid from carbonic anhydrase II and digest collagen with cathepsin K in Howship lacunae; failure causes osteopetrosis.","Growth plate zones run reserve, proliferative, hypertrophic, calcification, ossification; achondroplasia thins the proliferative zone and rickets widens a disordered hypertrophic zone.","Fibrous joints are sutures, syndesmoses, and gomphoses; synchondroses are hyaline and symphyses are fibrocartilage.","Articular cartilage lacks vessels and perichondrium and is fed by synovial fluid, whose hyaluronan and lubricin come from type B synoviocytes, so it repairs poorly."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Muscle Tissue"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Cartilage"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview and basic concepts, joints"}]},"MS-3":{"unit":"MS-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01","title":"Axial and Appendicular Skeleton","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the bones of the spine, skull, and limbs and explains how their shapes follow from the work each region does. It relates regional vertebral features to movement and load, the normal spinal curves to their abnormal forms and to breathing, the skull openings to the nerves and vessels that pass through them, and the limb bones to the nerves and arteries that lie against them. Board questions usually describe a fracture, a curve, or a cranial nerve deficit and ask which structure is involved or what function is lost. If you know where each nerve touches bone and why each region is shaped as it is, most of these questions can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Bone structure, ossification, and joint types (MS-1, MS-2)","Cranial nerve functions and the trigeminal divisions (NE-8)","Brachial and lumbosacral plexus branches and the muscles they supply (NE-8, MS-5)","Meningeal layers and the epidural and subdural spaces (NE-4)","Lung volumes and spirometry (PU-6)"],"connections":[{"to":"Joints and muscles (MS-4, MS-5)","how":"The bony features in this chapter are the attachment sites and articular surfaces that set each joint's range and each muscle's action."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal trauma and degeneration (MS-13-H, MS-13-E)","how":"Fracture sites, osteoporotic vertebral collapse, and avascular necrosis follow directly from bone shape and blood supply."},{"to":"Cranial nerves and CNS trauma (NE-8, NE-20-H)","how":"Skull foramina explain grouped cranial nerve palsies, and the pterion explains the epidural hematoma."},{"to":"Peripheral nerve injury (NE-20-I)","how":"The axillary, radial, ulnar, and common fibular nerves are injured where they lie directly on bone."},{"to":"Restrictive lung disease (PU-14-A)","how":"Severe thoracic scoliosis is a chest wall cause of restriction that can progress to hypoventilation and cor pulmonale."}],"terms":[{"term":"Transverse foramen","def":"An opening in each cervical transverse process; those of C6 to C1 normally carry the vertebral artery."},{"term":"Dens","def":"The upward peg of the axis (C2), developmentally the body of C1, around which the atlas rotates."},{"term":"Costal facet","def":"An articular surface on a thoracic vertebral body or transverse process that receives the head or tubercle of a rib."},{"term":"Lordosis","def":"A sagittal curve of the spine that is concave posteriorly, normal in the cervical and lumbar regions."},{"term":"Kyphosis","def":"A sagittal curve of the spine that is concave anteriorly, normal in the thoracic and sacral regions."},{"term":"Scoliosis","def":"Lateral curvature of the spine of at least 10 degrees combined with rotation of the vertebrae."},{"term":"Cobb angle","def":"The angle between the end plates of the most tilted vertebrae at the top and bottom of a curve, used to measure its size."},{"term":"Pterion","def":"The H-shaped junction of the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones on the side of the skull, overlying the middle meningeal artery."},{"term":"Epidural hematoma","def":"Blood collected between the skull and the dura, usually arterial and usually from the middle meningeal artery."},{"term":"Surgical neck of the humerus","def":"The narrow region below the greater and lesser tubercles, a common fracture site where the axillary nerve winds around the bone."},{"term":"Avascular necrosis","def":"Death of bone after its blood supply is cut off, typical of the proximal scaphoid and the femoral head after fracture."},{"term":"Pes planus","def":"Flat foot, a low or collapsed medial longitudinal arch."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C01","obj":"MS-3.1","title":"Regional features of the vertebrae and what each region does","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A typical vertebra has a body in front for weight bearing and a vertebral arch behind (pedicles and laminae) enclosing the vertebral foramen, with transverse and spinous processes as muscle levers and paired articular processes forming the facet (zygapophyseal) joints. Body size, which rises with load, and facet orientation best explain what each region can do."},{"type":"p","text":"Cervical vertebrae carry only the head, so their bodies are small, and their oblique facets allow the widest range of flexion, extension, lateral flexion, and rotation. Each transverse process has a transverse foramen. The vertebral artery, the first branch of the subclavian artery, usually enters at C6 and rises through the foramina of C6 to C1 before entering the skull through the foramen magnum; C7's foramen usually carries only small veins. The bony canal shields the artery as the neck moves, but cervical fractures can tear or dissect it, causing brainstem or cerebellar stroke. C3 to C6 have short, usually bifid spines that anchor the ligamentum nuchae, and C7 (vertebra prominens) has the long single spine felt at the base of the neck."},{"type":"p","text":"The atlas (C1) is a ring of anterior and posterior arches and two lateral masses with no body and no spinous process; its superior facets cradle the occipital condyles, and the atlanto-occipital joints allow nodding. During development the body that would have belonged to C1 joins C2 as the dens, a peg that projects into the ring of the atlas. The atlas pivots around the dens at the atlantoaxial joints, which supply about half of cervical rotation. The transverse ligament of the atlas holds the dens against the anterior arch; if rheumatoid arthritis erodes it, ligament laxity in Down syndrome weakens it, or the dens fractures, C1 can slip forward on C2 and the dens can compress the upper cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Thoracic vertebrae carry the ribs. A typical rib head articulates with two adjacent bodies, so typical thoracic bodies have upper and lower costal facets (demifacets), and the transverse processes of T1 to T10 have facets for the rib tubercles. Near-coronal facets allow rotation, but the rib cage splints the region and the long, overlapping spines limit extension. Lumbar vertebrae carry the whole upper body, so they have the largest, kidney-shaped bodies and no transverse foramina or costal facets. Their curved, near-sagittal facets interlock, allowing flexion and extension but nearly blocking rotation. Because the cord ends at about L1 to L2 in adults, the lumbar canal holds the cauda equina, and the heavily loaded L4 to L5 and L5 to S1 discs are the commonest levels of herniation."},{"type":"table","title":"Regional vertebral features and their functional results","cols":["Region","Distinctive features","Facets and motion","Function served"],"rows":[["Cervical (C3 to C7)","Small bodies; transverse foramina; bifid spines C3 to C6","Oblique; widest range","Head mobility; vertebral artery protected"],["Atlas (C1)","No body or spinous process; arches and lateral masses","Atlanto-occipital: flexion and extension (nodding)","Carries the skull"],["Axis (C2)","Dens, held by the transverse ligament","Atlantoaxial: rotation","Pivot for turning the head"],["Thoracic","Costal facets on bodies and T1 to T10 transverse processes","Near coronal; rotation, little extension","Rib attachment; stable thoracic cage"],["Lumbar","Largest bodies; no transverse foramina or costal facets","Near sagittal; flexion and extension, little rotation","Weight bearing"]],"caption":"Body size rises with load; facet orientation sets which movements each region allows."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis develops neck pain, brisk reflexes, and weakness in all four limbs. Which structure has failed?","explain":"Synovitis has eroded the transverse ligament of the atlas, so C1 slides forward on C2 and the dens compresses the upper cervical cord, giving upper motor neuron signs in all four limbs.","check":"The atlas, dens, and transverse ligament paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C02","obj":"MS-3.2","title":"Normal spinal curves, scoliosis, and hyperkyphosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Seen from the side, the adult column has four curves: cervical lordosis, thoracic kyphosis, lumbar lordosis, and sacral kyphosis. The kyphoses are primary curves, the remnants of the flexed C-shape of the fetus, and come mainly from the shape of the vertebral bodies. The lordoses are secondary curves that come mainly from wedge-shaped discs; they begin before birth and become obvious when the infant holds up its head (cervical) and when the child sits, stands, and walks (lumbar). The alternating curves absorb axial shock like a spring, and the lordoses keep the center of gravity over the hips so standing needs little muscle work."},{"type":"p","text":"Excessive thoracic kyphosis (hyperkyphosis) in older adults most often follows osteoporotic compression fractures. Vertebral bodies are mostly trabecular bone, which osteoporosis thins early, and under flexion load the front of the body collapses into a wedge, so height falls and the back rounds. In adolescents, wedging of several adjacent vertebrae gives a rigid kyphosis (Scheuermann disease), and spinal tuberculosis destroys vertebral bodies to give a sharp angular gibbus. Excessive lumbar lordosis follows a forward shift of the center of gravity, as in late pregnancy."},{"type":"p","text":"Scoliosis is a lateral curve of at least 10 degrees (Cobb angle) combined with vertebral rotation, so it is a three-dimensional deformity, not simple side bending. The bodies rotate toward the convex side and the spinous processes toward the concavity, and the attached ribs are pushed backward on the convex side, producing a rib hump when the patient bends forward. A structural curve has fixed rotation and persists on bending; a functional curve from a short leg or muscle spasm has no fixed rotation and corrects when the cause is removed. About 80% of structural cases are idiopathic, mostly in adolescents, and progressive curves are far more common in girls. Other causes are hemivertebrae, neuromuscular disease (cerebral palsy, Duchenne muscular dystrophy), and Marfan syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Severe thoracic scoliosis restricts the lungs from outside. The rotated ribs crowd together on the concave side, the chest wall stiffens (low compliance), and the inspiratory muscles work at a mechanical disadvantage. The lungs and airways themselves are normal, so total lung capacity, vital capacity, and FVC fall while FEV1 falls in proportion, leaving FEV1/FVC normal or high. Function is usually clearly impaired once a thoracic curve passes about 70 degrees. Patients take small, rapid breaths, which raises the dead space fraction; alveolar hypoventilation, worst in sleep, causes hypercapnia and hypoxemia, and chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Curves beginning before about age 5 also limit alveolar growth."},{"type":"table","title":"Normal and abnormal spinal curves","cols":["Curve","Plane","Normal or abnormal","Mechanism or typical cause"],"rows":[["Thoracic and sacral kyphosis","Sagittal, concave anteriorly","Normal primary","Fetal flexion; vertebral body shape"],["Cervical and lumbar lordosis","Sagittal, concave posteriorly","Normal secondary","Wedge-shaped discs; head control, walking"],["Hyperkyphosis","Sagittal, exaggerated","Abnormal","Osteoporotic wedge fractures; spinal tuberculosis"],["Hyperlordosis","Sagittal, exaggerated","Abnormal","Forward center of gravity: pregnancy, obesity"],["Scoliosis","Coronal curve plus axial rotation","Abnormal","Idiopathic; congenital; neuromuscular"]],"caption":"Kyphosis and lordosis are front to back curves; scoliosis is a side to side curve with rotation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From severe thoracic scoliosis to cor pulmonale","steps":["Thoracic curve with vertebral and rib rotation","Chest wall stiffens; inspiratory muscles disadvantaged","TLC and vital capacity fall; FEV1/FVC preserved","Alveolar hypoventilation: high PaCO2, low PaO2","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction; pulmonary hypertension","Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale)"],"caption":"Restriction arises in the chest wall, not the lung tissue, so airflow ratios stay normal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 19-year-old with a 95 degree thoracic scoliosis has spirometry. Predict FVC, FEV1, and FEV1/FVC, and say how the pattern differs from COPD.","explain":"FVC and FEV1 both fall, FEV1/FVC is normal or high, and TLC is low (extrapulmonary restriction). COPD lowers FEV1 more than FVC, so the ratio falls and TLC is normal or high.","check":"The paragraph on scoliosis and the lungs, and the flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C03","obj":"MS-3.3","title":"Skull foramina, their contents, and the pterion","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cranial base steps down in three fossae. Openings in the sphenoid lead to the orbit, face, and jaw; posterior fossa openings carry the lower cranial nerves, the great veins, and the brainstem."},{"type":"p","text":"All three nerves that move the eye and the ophthalmic nerve, which carries sensation from the eyeball and forehead, enter the orbit through the superior orbital fissure, between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid: the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), ophthalmic (V1), and abducens (VI) nerves, with the superior ophthalmic vein draining back to the cavernous sinus. A lesion here causes ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, loss of pupillary constriction, and forehead and corneal numbness, but spares vision because the optic nerve runs separately through the optic canal. The maxillary nerve (V2) runs forward through the foramen rotundum to the pterygopalatine fossa, and the mandibular nerve (V3), the only trigeminal division with motor fibers, drops through the foramen ovale to the infratemporal fossa. Just behind the ovale, the foramen spinosum admits the middle meningeal artery."},{"type":"p","text":"In the posterior fossa, the facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves enter the internal acoustic meatus. VII then runs through the facial canal and exits at the stylomastoid foramen, so a vestibular schwannoma in the meatus causes one-sided hearing loss and tinnitus before facial weakness. The jugular foramen transmits IX, X, and XI with the internal jugular vein, and the foramen magnum transmits the medulla, the vertebral arteries, and the spinal roots of XI, so tonsils herniating through it compress the medullary respiratory centers."},{"type":"table","title":"Major skull openings and their contents","cols":["Opening","Contents","Deficit when damaged"],"rows":[["Optic canal","CN II, ophthalmic artery","Monocular vision loss"],["Superior orbital fissure","CN III, IV, V1, VI; superior ophthalmic vein","Ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, numb forehead"],["Foramen rotundum","CN V2","Numb cheek and upper lip"],["Foramen ovale","CN V3 (motor and sensory)","Weak chewing, numb jaw"],["Foramen spinosum","Middle meningeal artery","Epidural hematoma if torn"],["Internal acoustic meatus","CN VII, CN VIII, labyrinthine artery","Hearing loss, vertigo, facial weakness"],["Jugular foramen","CN IX, X, XI; internal jugular vein","Dysphagia, hoarseness, lost gag, weak shrug"],["Hypoglossal canal","CN XII","Tongue deviates to lesion side"],["Foramen magnum","Medulla, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of XI","Medullary compression"]],"caption":"Nerves sharing an opening fail together, so a grouped deficit points to one site."},{"type":"p","text":"The pterion is the H-shaped junction where the frontal, parietal, and squamous temporal bones meet the greater wing of the sphenoid, a short distance above the zygomatic arch. The bone is thin, and on its inner surface, in a groove or sometimes a bony canal, runs the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery that entered through the foramen spinosum. A blow to the temple fractures this thin plate, and the fracture edge tears the artery. Arterial blood strips the periosteal dura from bone, but that layer is fused firmly at the sutures, so the collection is biconvex (lens-shaped) and does not cross suture lines, unlike the crescent of a venous subdural hematoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"From pterion fracture to herniation","steps":["Blow to the temple fractures the thin pterion","Fracture edge tears the middle meningeal artery","Lens-shaped hematoma, bounded by the sutures","Uncal herniation compresses the same-side CN III"],"caption":"Arterial bleeding enlarges the hematoma quickly."},{"type":"example","title":"Reasoning from a temple injury","text":"A 17-year-old struck on the left temple by a baseball is briefly unconscious, then lucid. Three hours later he is drowsy, with a dilated, unreactive left pupil and right-sided weakness. The temple overlies the pterion, so the likely injury is a fracture tearing the middle meningeal artery. The brief unconsciousness is concussion; the hematoma then needs time to strip dura from bone and reach a damaging size, hence the lucid interval. The growing mass pushes the left uncus over the tentorium, compressing the left oculomotor nerve (superficial parasympathetic fibers first) and the left cerebral peduncle, whose corticospinal fibers serve the right side."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C04","obj":"MS-3.4","title":"Bones of the upper limb and the nerves at risk in humeral fractures","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The upper limb's only bony link to the trunk is the clavicle at the sternoclavicular joint. The S-shaped clavicle is a strut that holds the limb away from the trunk and passes force to the axial skeleton; it often fractures, after which the sternocleidomastoid lifts the medial fragment and the limb's weight drops the shoulder. The scapula glides on the chest wall; its acromion joins the clavicle, and its shallow glenoid gives the shoulder great range but little bony stability. The humerus has a head, anatomical neck, greater and lesser tubercles for rotator cuff tendons, and the narrower surgical neck below them; the shaft bears the deltoid tuberosity and radial groove, and the distal end the capitulum (for the radius), trochlea (for the ulna), and two epicondyles."},{"type":"p","text":"In the forearm the radius lies laterally (thumb side) and the ulna medially. The ulna is large at the elbow, where the olecranon and trochlear notch grip the trochlea of the humerus as a hinge, and small at the wrist. The radius is small at the elbow, where its head spins in the annular ligament during pronation and supination, and large at the wrist, where it carries the scaphoid and lunate. The carpals form two rows (proximal: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform; distal: trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate) arched into the carpal tunnel. The scaphoid bridges both rows and is the carpal most often fractured; its vessels enter mainly distally, so a waist fracture can leave the proximal pole without blood, causing nonunion and avascular necrosis. The lunate, the carpal most often dislocated, can compress the median nerve in the tunnel."},{"type":"p","text":"Three nerves lie directly on the humerus, so the fracture site predicts the nerve. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck, which often breaks when an older adult with osteoporosis falls; injury weakens the deltoid and teres minor (abduction beyond the first 15 degrees) and numbs skin over the lower deltoid. The radial nerve spirals along the radial groove on the back of the shaft, so a midshaft fracture paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors (wrist drop) and numbs the back of the hand near the first web space; triceps is usually spared because most of its branches leave the nerve above the groove. The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle, so a fracture there weakens most intrinsic hand muscles, giving weak finger abduction and adduction, clawing of the ring and little fingers, and numbness of the medial one and a half digits. The clawing is often milder than with a wrist lesion because an elbow lesion also weakens the medial flexor digitorum profundus, which would otherwise flex the distal joints."},{"type":"table","title":"Humeral and carpal fracture sites and the structures at risk","cols":["Fracture site","Structure at risk","Main deficit or result"],"rows":[["Surgical neck of humerus","Axillary nerve, posterior circumflex humeral artery","Weak abduction beyond 15 degrees; numb lower deltoid skin"],["Midshaft humerus (radial groove)","Radial nerve, deep brachial artery","Wrist drop; numb dorsal first web space; triceps usually spared"],["Supracondylar humerus","Median nerve, brachial artery","Weak thumb opposition and flexion; forearm ischemia"],["Medial epicondyle","Ulnar nerve","Weak intrinsic hand muscles; ring and little finger claw"],["Scaphoid waist","Retrograde blood supply to proximal pole","Nonunion, avascular necrosis"],["Lunate dislocation","Median nerve in carpal tunnel","Numb lateral digits; weak thenar muscles"]],"caption":"Each nerve is injured where it lies directly against bone."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the nerve from the fracture","text":"A 45-year-old falls from a ladder and has a midshaft fracture of the humerus. He cannot extend his wrist or fingers but can extend his elbow, and the back of the hand near the thumb is numb. The radial nerve lies in the radial groove on the back of the midshaft, so it is the nerve at risk. Wrist and finger extensors are supplied below the groove and are lost; triceps branches mostly leave the nerve higher, so elbow extension survives."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-03-01-C05","obj":"MS-3.5","title":"Bones of the lower limb, the foot arches, and the common fibular nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each hip bone forms from the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which meet in the acetabulum and are joined by a Y-shaped (triradiate) cartilage until they fuse in adolescence. With the sacrum and pubic symphysis, the hip bones form a ring that carries weight from the spine to the femurs in standing and to the ischial tuberosities in sitting. The deep acetabulum and its labrum hold most of the femoral head, trading range for stability."},{"type":"p","text":"The femur is the longest and strongest bone. Its neck meets the shaft at about 125 degrees in adults, giving hip muscles leverage, and the shaft angles medially so the knees sit under the body. Most blood reaches the adult femoral head through retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery running up the neck inside the capsule, so a displaced neck fracture risks avascular necrosis of the head. The tibia carries body weight from the femur to the talus and, because its anteromedial surface lies under the skin, often has open fractures; the slender fibula bears little weight, anchors muscles, and forms the lateral malleolus."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular (peroneal) nerve, a terminal branch of the sciatic nerve, winds around the fibular neck under only skin and fascia. A fibular neck fracture, a tight cast, or habitual leg crossing can injure it. Its deep branch supplies the ankle dorsiflexors and toe extensors and the skin of the first web space, and its superficial branch supplies the evertors (fibularis longus and brevis) and most skin of the dorsum of the foot. Injury therefore causes foot drop with a high-stepping gait, loss of eversion, and numbness over the dorsum of the foot, while plantar flexion and inversion by tibialis posterior, both supplied by the tibial nerve, remain."},{"type":"p","text":"The foot bones form arches that spread weight between heel and metatarsal heads and let the foot act as a shock absorber in stance and a rigid lever at push-off. The medial longitudinal arch is the highest, with the head of the talus as its keystone. It is held passively by the plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament, which cradles the talar head, and by the plantar aponeurosis, which ties heel to toes like a bowstring, and actively by the tibialis posterior tendon. When the toes dorsiflex at push-off, the aponeurosis winds around the metatarsal heads, shortening the foot and raising the arch."},{"type":"p","text":"Pes planus (flat foot) is a low or collapsed medial longitudinal arch. Infants look flat-footed because of a plantar fat pad, and a flexible flat foot whose arch reappears on tiptoe is usually painless. Acquired adult flat foot most often follows tibialis posterior tendon failure (below); a rigid flat foot, as with fused tarsals (tarsal coalition), has a structural cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Foot arches and their supports","cols":["Arch","Bones","Main supports"],"rows":[["Medial longitudinal","Calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuneiforms, metatarsals 1 to 3","Spring ligament, plantar aponeurosis, tibialis posterior"],["Lateral longitudinal","Calcaneus, cuboid, metatarsals 4 and 5","Long and short plantar ligaments, fibularis longus, plantar aponeurosis"],["Transverse","Cuboid, cuneiforms, metatarsal bases","Fibularis longus tendon crossing the sole, intrinsic muscles"]],"caption":"The high medial arch is the one that collapses in pes planus."},{"type":"flow","title":"From tibialis posterior failure to acquired flat foot","steps":["Tibialis posterior tendon degenerates and lengthens","Spring ligament takes the load and stretches","Talar head drops medially and toward the sole","Medial longitudinal arch collapses","Heel tilts into valgus; forefoot abducts","Medial pain; weak single-heel rise"],"caption":"Dynamic support fails first, then passive support."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a tight below-knee cast, a patient catches his toes when walking. Which movements are weak, which are preserved, and where is skin numb?","explain":"Pressure at the fibular neck injures the common fibular nerve. Dorsiflexion and toe extension (deep branch) and eversion (superficial branch) are weak; plantar flexion and inversion by tibialis posterior (tibial nerve) remain. The dorsum of the foot is numb.","check":"The common fibular nerve paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and histology explain much of the anatomy. The dens is the displaced body of C1, primary and secondary curves record fetal flexion and later upright posture, and hemivertebrae from defective somite segmentation (MS-1) cause congenital scoliosis. Vertebral bodies, the femoral neck, and the distal radius are rich in trabecular bone, which osteoporosis thins first (MS-13-C), so they are where fragility fractures cluster. Blood supply that enters bone from one end, as in the scaphoid and femoral head, explains avascular necrosis after fracture, and infection or inflammation that erodes ligaments, such as rheumatoid destruction of the transverse ligament of the atlas (MS-11), turns a stable joint into a threat to the spinal cord."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology follow from position. A nerve or vessel that lies on bone or passes through a foramen is injured when that bone breaks or the opening narrows, so a grouped deficit points to one site: III, IV, V1, and VI to the superior orbital fissure; IX, X, and XI to the jugular foramen; extensors with spared triceps to the radial groove. Severe scoliosis links the skeleton to respiratory and cardiac physiology through restriction, hypoventilation, and hypoxic pulmonary hypertension. Anatomical variation limits these rules: the vertebral artery sometimes enters above C6, and the middle meningeal artery may run in a bony canal, so the expected structure is the most likely one, not a certainty."}],"summary":["Cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina for the vertebral artery (usually C6 to C1) and bifid spines; the atlas has no body, and the dens of the axis is the pivot for head rotation.","Thoracic costal facets carry the ribs, and the rib cage limits thoracic motion; large lumbar bodies bear weight and sagittal lumbar facets permit flexion and extension but block rotation.","Thoracic and sacral kyphoses are primary curves; cervical and lumbar lordoses are secondary curves formed with head control and walking.","Scoliosis is lateral curvature with vertebral rotation; hyperkyphosis is an exaggerated sagittal curve, often from osteoporotic wedge fractures.","Severe thoracic scoliosis stiffens the chest wall, lowering TLC and vital capacity with normal FEV1/FVC, and can progress to hypoventilation and cor pulmonale.","Superior orbital fissure: III, IV, V1, VI. Rotundum: V2. Ovale: V3. Spinosum: middle meningeal artery. Internal acoustic meatus: VII and VIII. Jugular foramen: IX, X, XI and internal jugular vein. Foramen magnum: medulla and vertebral arteries.","A fracture of the thin pterion tears the middle meningeal artery, producing a biconvex epidural hematoma that does not cross sutures and can cause uncal herniation.","Humeral surgical neck fractures injure the axillary nerve, midshaft fractures the radial nerve (wrist drop), and medial epicondyle fractures the ulnar nerve.","Scaphoid waist and femoral neck fractures risk avascular necrosis because blood reaches the vulnerable fragment from one direction only.","Fibular neck fractures injure the common fibular nerve (foot drop, lost eversion); collapse of the medial longitudinal arch, often from tibialis posterior failure, causes pes planus."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: vertebral column, regional vertebrae, and curvatures"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: cranium, cranial base foramina, and meninges"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb: bones of the upper limb and their clinical box notes"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb: bones of the lower limb, common fibular nerve, and arches of the foot"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors; Central Nervous System, section on epidural hematoma"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Pulmonary Disease: restrictive lung disease"}]},"MS-4":{"unit":"MS-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01","title":"Joints: Structure, Innervation, and Function","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how joints are built, how they are innervated, and how three clinically important joints, the shoulder, knee, and temporomandibular joint, stay stable while they move. Joint classification follows from the tissue between the bones and the shape of the articular surfaces. Hilton law explains joint position sense, protective reflexes, and referred joint pain. Board questions usually describe a mechanism of injury, a positive examination test, a lost movement, or pain felt away from its source, and ask you to name the structure or nerve responsible.","prerequisites":["Connective tissue types: dense regular tissue, hyaline cartilage, and fibrocartilage","Brachial and lumbar plexus organization and the cords and branches that leave them","Muscle actions at the shoulder, hip, knee, and jaw, with their nerve supply","Sensory receptors and the dorsal horn pathways for proprioception and pain","Branches of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Bone and cartilage histology (MS-2)","how":"Avascular articular cartilage depends on synovial fluid for nutrition, which explains why it heals poorly once injured."},{"to":"Muscles of the limbs (MS-5)","how":"Muscles that cross a joint both move it and stabilize it, and their nerves also supply its capsule."},{"to":"Neuroscience: somatosensory pathways","how":"Convergence of joint and skin afferents on shared spinal neurons is the basis of referred joint pain."},{"to":"Rheumatology and joint pathology","how":"Synovium, cartilage, and capsule are the targets of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, crystal disease, and septic arthritis."},{"to":"Head and neck anatomy","how":"The TMJ shares its nerve supply with the masseter and the skin of the ear and temple, so jaw disease can present as ear pain."}],"terms":[{"term":"Synarthrosis","def":"A joint that permits essentially no movement, such as a cranial suture."},{"term":"Amphiarthrosis","def":"A joint that permits slight movement, such as the pubic symphysis or an intervertebral disc."},{"term":"Diarthrosis","def":"A freely movable joint; all diarthroses are synovial joints."},{"term":"Synovial membrane","def":"The vascular connective tissue lining of a joint capsule whose cells secrete hyaluronan and lubricin into synovial fluid."},{"term":"Degrees of freedom","def":"The number of independent axes about which a joint can rotate: one for a hinge or pivot, two for condyloid and saddle joints, three for a ball and socket."},{"term":"Hilton law","def":"The nerves supplying the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint capsule and the skin over the muscles' insertions."},{"term":"Referred pain","def":"Pain perceived at a site other than its source because both sites send afferents to the same spinal cord neurons."},{"term":"Rotator cuff","def":"Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, whose tendons blend with the capsule and press the humeral head into the glenoid."},{"term":"Glenoid labrum","def":"A fibrocartilage rim attached to the margin of the glenoid fossa that deepens the socket slightly."},{"term":"Menisci","def":"Crescent-shaped fibrocartilage pads between the femoral condyles and tibial plateau that improve fit and spread load."},{"term":"Valgus and varus","def":"Valgus angulation points the distal segment away from the midline (knee drawn inward); varus points it toward the midline (knee bowed outward)."},{"term":"Articular disc","def":"A plate of dense fibrous tissue or fibrocartilage inside a synovial joint, such as the TMJ, that divides the cavity into two compartments."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C01","obj":"MS-4.1","title":"Classifying joints by function and synovial joints by shape","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A joint is any place where two or more bones (or a bone and cartilage) meet. How much it moves depends on what fills the space between the bones. When dense fibrous tissue or cartilage binds the bones directly, the surfaces cannot slide on one another, and motion is absent or slight. When the bones are separated by a fluid-filled cavity enclosed in a capsule, the surfaces are free to move. Two classifications follow from this: a structural one (fibrous, cartilaginous, synovial) and a functional one based on mobility."},{"type":"table","title":"Functional classes and the structures that produce them","cols":["Functional class","Mobility","Structural basis","Examples"],"rows":[["Synarthrosis","Immovable","Fibrous (suture, gomphosis) or primary cartilaginous (synchondrosis of hyaline cartilage)","Cranial sutures, tooth in its socket, epiphyseal growth plate"],["Amphiarthrosis","Slightly movable","Secondary cartilaginous (symphysis of fibrocartilage) or a fibrous syndesmosis","Pubic symphysis, intervertebral disc, distal tibiofibular joint"],["Diarthrosis","Freely movable","Synovial: capsule, synovial membrane, cavity, articular cartilage","Shoulder, hip, knee, elbow, TMJ"]],"caption":"Every diarthrosis is synovial. Synchondroses are usually temporary and ossify after growth ends."},{"type":"p","text":"A synovial joint has a fibrous capsule continuous with the periosteum, an inner synovial membrane that lines everything in the cavity except the articular cartilage, and a thin film of synovial fluid. Synoviocytes secrete hyaluronan, which makes the fluid viscous, and lubricin, which reduces friction at the cartilage surface. Articular cartilage is usually hyaline, has no blood vessels or nerves, and receives its nutrients by diffusion from synovial fluid, aided by the cyclic compression of movement. Some synovial joints (TMJ, sternoclavicular) contain an articular disc, and the knee contains menisci."},{"type":"p","text":"Among synovial joints, the shape of the articular surfaces sets the axes of motion. A cylinder in a trough (hinge) or a peg in a ring (pivot) allows rotation about one axis. An oval convex surface in an oval concavity (condyloid, also called ellipsoid) allows flexion, extension, abduction, and adduction but not axial rotation. A saddle joint has two surfaces each convex in one direction and concave in the other, giving two axes. A sphere in a cup (ball and socket) allows three axes, including rotation. Flat surfaces (plane joints) only glide, with motion limited by ligaments."},{"type":"table","title":"Synovial joint types","cols":["Type","Axes","Movement","Examples"],"rows":[["Hinge","1","Flexion and extension","Humeroulnar (elbow), interphalangeal, talocrural (ankle); knee is a modified hinge"],["Pivot","1","Rotation about a long axis","Median atlantoaxial (dens in ring of atlas and transverse ligament), proximal radioulnar (radial head in annular ligament)"],["Condyloid (ellipsoid)","2","Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, circumduction","Radiocarpal (wrist), metacarpophalangeal, atlanto-occipital"],["Saddle","2","Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction; opposition with conjunct rotation","First carpometacarpal (trapezium and first metacarpal)"],["Ball and socket","3","All of the above plus medial and lateral rotation","Glenohumeral, hip"],["Plane","Gliding","Sliding of flat surfaces","Acromioclavicular, intercarpal, zygapophysial (facet) joints"]],"caption":"Number of axes rises with the curvature of the articular surfaces."},{"type":"p","text":"Two examples are tested often. At the median atlantoaxial joint the dens of C2 sits in a ring formed by the anterior arch of the atlas and the transverse ligament; the atlas and skull turn around the dens, giving roughly half of cervical rotation. Rupture of the transverse ligament, as in rheumatoid arthritis, lets the dens move back toward the spinal cord. At the first carpometacarpal joint the saddle shape lets the thumb flex across the palm while it rotates medially, so the thumb pad can meet each fingertip in opposition. In general, mobility costs stability: the shallow ball and socket of the shoulder moves most and dislocates most."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C02","obj":"MS-4.2","title":"Hilton law, articular sensation, and referred joint pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hilton law states that the nerves supplying the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint itself and the skin over those muscles' insertions. Anatomically, articular branches usually arise from the nerves to nearby muscles as those nerves cross the joint. The muscle, the joint it moves, and the overlying skin therefore share nerves and spinal segments, which lets the joint report its state directly to the reflex circuits that control the muscles around it."},{"type":"p","text":"Articular nerves are richest in the fibrous capsule, ligaments, and synovial membrane. Articular cartilage has no nerves, so cartilage loss in osteoarthritis is painless in itself; the pain comes from subchondral bone, synovium, and the stretched capsule. Joint receptors work with muscle spindles to give position and movement sense. Joint receptors signal mainly near the ends of the range, and spindles supply most mid-range position sense."},{"type":"table","title":"Articular receptors","cols":["Receptor","Location","Adaptation","Signal"],"rows":[["Ruffini endings","Fibrous capsule","Slow","Joint position and capsular tension"],["Pacinian corpuscles","Capsule and fat pads","Rapid","Onset of movement and acceleration"],["Golgi-type endings","Ligaments","Slow","Tension near the extremes of motion"],["Free nerve endings (A-delta and C fibers)","Capsule, ligaments, synovium, fat pads","Variable; sensitized by inflammation","Nociception from stretch, injury, and inflammatory mediators"]],"caption":"Inflammatory mediators such as bradykinin and prostaglandins lower the threshold of joint nociceptors, so normal movement becomes painful."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the same afferents feed motor reflexes, capsular stretch or inflammation reflexly inhibits the muscles around the joint. Swelling of the knee, for example, inhibits the quadriceps (arthrogenic muscle inhibition), and a painful joint is held in the position of greatest capsular volume. When articular sensation is lost, as in diabetic neuropathy, tabes dorsalis, or syringomyelia, the joint is repeatedly overloaded without warning and can be destroyed (neuropathic or Charcot joint)."},{"type":"p","text":"Shared innervation also explains referred pain. The hip capsule is supplied by the femoral nerve anteriorly, the obturator nerve inferomedially, the nerve to quadratus femoris posteriorly, and the superior gluteal nerve superiorly. The femoral and obturator nerves (L2 to L4) also supply the knee and the skin of the anterior and medial thigh. Afferents from the hip and from these skin areas converge on the same dorsal horn neurons, and the brain, more used to signals from skin, projects the pain to the thigh and knee. A child or adolescent with knee pain, such as from slipped capital femoral epiphysis or Legg-Calve-Perthes disease, needs a hip examination."},{"type":"flow","title":"How hip disease is felt in the knee","steps":["Hip capsule is inflamed or stretched","Femoral and obturator nociceptors fire","Afferents enter L2 to L4 dorsal horn","Converge on neurons that also serve thigh and knee","Brain maps the signal to the familiar skin area","Pain is felt in the medial thigh and knee"],"caption":"Convergence on shared spinal neurons, not nerve damage, produces the referral."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An obese 13-year-old limps and complains only of left knee pain. The knee examination is normal. Which joint should be examined next, and through which nerves is the pain referred?","explain":"The left hip. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis stretches the hip capsule, and its nociceptive input travels in the obturator and femoral nerves, which also supply the knee and medial thigh, so pain is projected there.","check":"The hip innervation paragraph and the referral flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C03","obj":"MS-4.3","title":"Glenohumeral stability, anterior dislocation, and the axillary nerve","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glenohumeral joint is the most mobile joint in the body and the one most often dislocated. Its bony fit is poor: the glenoid fossa is shallow and covers only about a quarter to a third of the humeral head at any moment, and the fibrocartilage labrum deepens it only slightly. The capsule is loose enough to allow wide range, so it is taut only near the extremes of motion. Stability through most of the range therefore comes from muscle rather than from bone or ligament."},{"type":"table","title":"Stabilizers of the glenohumeral joint","cols":["Structure","Type","Nerve","How it stabilizes"],"rows":[["Glenoid labrum","Static","None (passive)","Deepens the socket and adds a suction seal"],["Capsule and glenohumeral ligaments","Static","None (passive)","Inferior glenohumeral ligament resists anterior translation in abduction and external rotation"],["Supraspinatus","Dynamic","Suprascapular (C5, C6)","Compresses the head into the glenoid; initiates abduction (about 0 to 15 degrees)"],["Infraspinatus","Dynamic","Suprascapular (C5, C6)","Lateral rotation; resists posterior and superior translation"],["Teres minor","Dynamic","Axillary (C5, C6)","Lateral rotation; depresses the head"],["Subscapularis","Dynamic","Upper and lower subscapular (C5, C6)","Medial rotation; anterior wall of the joint"]],"caption":"The four cuff tendons blend with the capsule superiorly, anteriorly, and posteriorly, but not inferiorly."},{"type":"p","text":"The rotator cuff works by concavity compression. Pulling the humeral head into the glenoid keeps its center fixed while it rotates. This is essential during abduction, because the deltoid's line of pull at low angles would otherwise slide the head upward against the acromion. The cuff and deltoid act as a force couple: the deltoid elevates the arm while the cuff holds the fulcrum in place. A torn cuff therefore causes weakness, superior migration of the head, and impingement even when the deltoid is intact."},{"type":"p","text":"The inferior capsule has no cuff tendon over it and is the weakest part of the joint. With the arm abducted and externally rotated, as in blocking a throw or falling on an outstretched hand, the head is levered forward and down through the anteroinferior capsule and comes to rest below the coracoid process. The shoulder looks squared off because the head no longer fills the deltoid beneath the acromion. Anterior dislocation may tear the anteroinferior labrum (Bankart lesion) or impact the posterolateral humeral head on the glenoid rim (Hill-Sachs lesion), both of which favor recurrence. Posterior dislocation is uncommon and classically follows a seizure or electric shock, when the strong medial rotators overpower the lateral rotators."},{"type":"p","text":"The axillary nerve (C5, C6, posterior cord) passes through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery and winds around the surgical neck of the humerus, directly below the joint. The displaced head stretches it, as can a fracture of the surgical neck. The result is predictable from what the nerve supplies: the deltoid, teres minor, and skin over the lower deltoid through the superior lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm. Numbness over this patch is the quickest bedside sign of axillary nerve injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Anterior dislocation to axillary nerve palsy","steps":["Arm abducted and externally rotated under force","Head levered through weak anteroinferior capsule","Head lies below the coracoid process","Axillary nerve stretched around the surgical neck","Deltoid and teres minor denervated","Weak abduction past 15 degrees; numb lower deltoid skin"],"caption":"Supraspinatus can still start abduction, and infraspinatus masks most of the lateral rotation loss."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After reduction of an anterior dislocation, a patient can begin abducting the arm but cannot raise it further, and lateral rotation is only slightly weak. Which nerve is injured, and why is the first part of abduction spared?","explain":"The axillary nerve. Supraspinatus, supplied by the suprascapular nerve, initiates abduction and is intact, but the deltoid, the main abductor from about 15 to 90 degrees, is paralyzed. Infraspinatus, also suprascapular, still performs most lateral rotation, so loss of teres minor is subtle.","check":"The stabilizer table and the axillary nerve paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C04","obj":"MS-4.4","title":"Knee ligaments, menisci, and the tests that examine them","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The knee is a modified hinge: rounded femoral condyles sit on a nearly flat tibial plateau, so bone shape adds little stability, and flexion and extension come with a small amount of rotation. Four main ligaments and two menisci keep it aligned. Each ligament resists one direction of displacement, so each test applies that displacement and looks for excess movement or a soft end point."},{"type":"p","text":"The cruciate ligaments lie inside the capsule but outside the synovial cavity and are named for their tibial attachments. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) runs from the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia upward, backward, and laterally to the medial surface of the lateral femoral condyle; it stops the tibia sliding forward on the femur and limits hyperextension. The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), stronger and thicker, runs from the posterior intercondylar area upward and forward to the lateral surface of the medial femoral condyle; it stops the tibia sliding backward and is the main stabilizer of the flexed, weight-bearing knee. The medial (tibial) collateral ligament is a broad flat band whose deep fibers attach to the capsule and medial meniscus. The lateral (fibular) collateral ligament is a round cord to the fibular head, separated from the lateral meniscus by the popliteus tendon."},{"type":"table","title":"Knee structures and their tests","cols":["Structure","Resists","Test","Positive finding"],"rows":[["ACL","Anterior tibial translation","Lachman (knee at 20 to 30 degrees); anterior drawer (90 degrees)","Excess forward glide of the tibia with a soft end point"],["PCL","Posterior tibial translation","Posterior drawer (90 degrees); posterior sag","Tibia pushes or sags backward"],["MCL","Valgus force (opening of the medial side)","Valgus stress at 20 to 30 degrees","Medial joint line gapes"],["LCL","Varus force (opening of the lateral side)","Varus stress at 20 to 30 degrees","Lateral joint line gapes"],["Menisci","Load spreading; torn by compression with rotation","McMurray; joint line tenderness","Painful click or catch on rotation"]],"caption":"At 20 to 30 degrees of flexion a collateral can be judged alone; gaping in full extension implies cruciate or capsular injury too."},{"type":"p","text":"Lachman is more sensitive than the anterior drawer, particularly soon after injury. At 90 degrees, hamstring guarding pulls the tibia back and the posterior horn of the medial meniscus wedges against the femoral condyle, blocking forward glide; at 20 to 30 degrees neither happens."},{"type":"p","text":"The menisci are fibrocartilage wedges that deepen the plateau and spread load; only their outer rim is vascular, so inner tears heal poorly. The C-shaped medial meniscus is fixed to the deep MCL and capsule, so it cannot shift away when the femur rotates and compresses it, and the medial compartment also carries more load. The nearly circular lateral meniscus is free of the LCL and is pulled back by popliteus during flexion. The medial meniscus therefore tears more often. Swelling timing helps localize injury: the ACL carries a branch of the middle genicular artery, so its rupture causes hemarthrosis within hours, whereas an isolated meniscal tear swells more slowly."},{"type":"flow","title":"The unhappy triad from a lateral blow","steps":["Blow to lateral knee, foot planted, knee slightly flexed","Valgus force opens the medial side","MCL tears","Continued valgus with rotation ruptures the ACL","Medial meniscus, fixed to the MCL, may also tear"],"caption":"In noncontact pivoting ACL injuries, the lateral meniscus is often the one torn."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a knee examination","text":"A skier twists a planted knee, hears a pop, and the knee swells within 2 hours. At 25 degrees of flexion the tibia glides forward with no firm end point, while the anterior drawer at 90 degrees is equivocal. Valgus stress at 25 degrees opens the medial joint line. Rapid swelling means a vascular structure bled, most often the ACL, and the positive Lachman confirms it; guarding and the meniscal block masked the drawer. Medial gapping means a torn MCL, and medial joint line tenderness with a painful McMurray would add the medial meniscus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-04-01-C05","obj":"MS-4.5","title":"The temporomandibular joint and disc displacement","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a synovial joint between the head (condyle) of the mandible and the mandibular fossa and articular eminence of the temporal bone. Unlike most synovial joints, its articular surfaces are covered by fibrocartilage rather than hyaline cartilage. An articular disc of dense fibrous tissue divides the cavity into two separate synovial compartments. The disc is biconcave, with thick anterior and posterior bands and a thin intermediate zone on which the condyle normally sits. Behind it, a vascular, richly innervated retrodiscal tissue (bilaminar zone) attaches it to the temporal bone and condyle, and in front the superior head of the lateral pterygoid inserts into the disc and capsule. The lateral (temporomandibular) ligament thickens the capsule and limits posterior displacement of the condyle."},{"type":"table","title":"The two TMJ compartments","cols":["Compartment","Between","Movement","Role"],"rows":[["Lower (inferior)","Condyle and disc","Hinge rotation","Early mouth opening"],["Upper (superior)","Disc and temporal bone","Gliding (translation) forward onto the eminence","Wide opening, protrusion, and side-to-side grinding"]],"caption":"Wide opening requires both compartments working in sequence."},{"type":"flow","title":"Opening the mouth at the TMJ","steps":["Digastric and other suprahyoid muscles depress the mandible","Condyle rotates under the disc (lower compartment)","Lateral pterygoid pulls condyle and disc forward","Disc and condyle glide onto the eminence (upper compartment)","Full opening; condyle sits under the eminence"],"caption":"Closing reverses the sequence, driven by temporalis, masseter, and medial pterygoid, with posterior temporalis fibers retracting the condyle."},{"type":"p","text":"The joint is supplied by the auriculotemporal nerve, the main supply to the posterior and lateral capsule, and by the masseteric nerve anteriorly, both branches of CN V3. This fits Hilton law: the masseteric nerve supplies the masseter that moves the joint. The auriculotemporal nerve also supplies the skin of the temple, tragus, and external acoustic meatus, so TMJ pain is commonly felt in front of or inside the ear. Because the lateral pterygoids pull the condyles forward, a unilateral CN V3 lesion makes the jaw deviate toward the paralyzed side on opening."},{"type":"p","text":"Disc displacement is usually anterior or anteromedial, in the direction of the lateral pterygoid's pull, once the retrodiscal tissue has been stretched. With the disc ahead of the condyle, the condyle rests on the retrodiscal tissue, whose compression and inflammation cause pain. In displacement with reduction, the condyle jumps over the thick posterior band onto the intermediate zone during opening, which produces a click, and the disc slips off again near the end of closing, sometimes with a softer second click (reciprocal clicking). In displacement without reduction, the disc stays in front and blocks forward translation, so the click disappears, opening is limited (closed lock), and the jaw deviates toward the affected side because that condyle cannot glide. Dislocation is different: the condyle itself passes in front of the eminence, often during a yawn, and the mouth is locked open."},{"type":"example","title":"Interpreting a click and a lock","text":"A patient has had a painless click on opening the right jaw for months. One morning the click is gone, the mouth opens only partway, and the jaw swings to the right as it opens. Before, the disc was displaced anteriorly but recaptured each time the condyle crossed its posterior band, so the click marked reduction. Now the disc no longer reduces, so it blocks forward gliding of the right condyle. The left condyle still translates, which turns the mandible toward the right. Pain in front of the ear reflects compressed retrodiscal tissue supplied by the auriculotemporal nerve."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain how joints fail. Avascular, aneural articular cartilage and the avascular inner menisci heal poorly, so mechanical injury tends to progress to osteoarthritis, while the vascular, innervated synovium, capsule, and retrodiscal tissue are where pain, swelling, and inflammatory arthritis arise. Rheumatoid synovitis loosens ligaments such as the transverse ligament of the atlas, and loss of articular sensation in neuropathy removes the protective reflexes that Hilton law describes, leading to neuropathic joint destruction."},{"type":"p","text":"Neuroscience explains the clinical pattern. Joint, muscle, and skin afferents share segments, so the examiner must look beyond the painful region: knee pain may come from the hip, and ear pain from the TMJ. Examination tests are directional reasoning, not memorized lists, but they have limits: guarding, effusion, and combined injuries reduce their accuracy, and imaging confirms what the history and mechanism suggest."}],"summary":["Mobility depends on the tissue between bones: fibrous and cartilaginous joints are synarthroses or amphiarthroses, and all diarthroses are synovial.","Surface shape sets the axes: hinge and pivot one, condyloid and saddle two, ball and socket three, plane joints glide; the median atlantoaxial joint is a pivot and the first carpometacarpal joint a saddle.","Hilton law: nerves to the muscles that move a joint also supply the joint and overlying skin, linking articular sensation to protective muscle reflexes.","Hip pain referred to the knee travels in the femoral and obturator nerves (L2 to L4), which converge with knee and thigh afferents in the dorsal horn.","The shallow glenoid makes the shoulder depend on rotator cuff compression; the uncovered inferior capsule allows anteroinferior dislocation in abduction and external rotation.","Anterior dislocation stretches the axillary nerve: deltoid weakness beyond about 15 degrees of abduction, mild lateral rotation loss, and numbness over the lower deltoid.","ACL and PCL stop anterior and posterior tibial translation (Lachman and anterior drawer; posterior drawer); MCL and LCL resist valgus and varus stress.","The medial meniscus tears more often because its attachment to the MCL and capsule keeps it from moving out of the way.","The TMJ has fibrocartilage surfaces and a disc creating a lower hinge and upper gliding compartment; it is supplied by the auriculotemporal and masseteric branches of CN V3.","Anterior disc displacement with reduction clicks on opening; without reduction it causes closed lock, limited opening, and deviation toward the affected side."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Overview and Basic Concepts, section on joints"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb, glenohumeral joint and axillary nerve"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb, hip joint and knee joint"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, temporomandibular joint"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, section on joints and synovial membrane"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Sensory transduction, somatic sensory receptors and proprioception"}]},"MS-5":{"unit":"MS-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01","title":"Muscles: Origin, Insertion, Action, and Innervation","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter teaches the muscles that board questions return to most often: the rotator cuff, the muscle groups served by the major limb nerves, the cranial nerve muscles of the head and neck, and the trunk muscles of posture, breathing, and the pelvic floor. The emphasis is on reasoning rather than memorizing lists. If you know where a muscle pulls, which compartment it lives in, and which nerve serves that compartment, you can work backward from a weakness, a deformity, or an abnormal gait to the injured nerve and the level of injury. Most exam items give exactly that kind of clue.","prerequisites":["Bones and bony landmarks of the scapula, humerus, pelvis, femur, tibia, and fibula","Brachial and lumbosacral plexus organization: roots, trunks or divisions, cords, and terminal branches","Joint movements and planes: flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation, inversion, eversion","Upper versus lower motor neuron signs","Pharyngeal arch and somite derivatives (embryology)"],"connections":[{"to":"Neuroscience: peripheral nerves and spinal cord","how":"Root, plexus, and peripheral nerve lesions each leave a distinct pattern of weakness, sensory loss, and reflex change."},{"to":"Embryology of the pharyngeal arches and somites","how":"A muscle keeps the nerve of the arch or somite it came from, which explains V3 to the chewing muscles, VII to the face, and XII to the tongue."},{"to":"Respiratory physiology","how":"The diaphragm generates most of the quiet tidal volume, so phrenic nerve and high cervical cord injuries present as ventilatory failure."},{"to":"Joints and connective tissue (musculoskeletal units)","how":"Rotator cuff tendinopathy and tears are degenerative tendon disorders shaped by the anatomy of the subacromial space."},{"to":"Reproductive and gastrointestinal systems","how":"The levator ani supports the pelvic organs and maintains the anorectal angle, linking pelvic floor injury to prolapse and incontinence."}],"terms":[{"term":"Origin","def":"The usually proximal, more fixed attachment of a muscle."},{"term":"Insertion","def":"The usually distal, more mobile attachment that the muscle pulls toward its origin."},{"term":"Compartment","def":"A group of muscles enclosed by fascia that share a function and, as a rule, a single motor nerve."},{"term":"Force couple","def":"Two muscles pulling in different directions on a bone so that their combined effect is rotation rather than translation."},{"term":"Rotator cuff","def":"The supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, whose tendons blend with the shoulder joint capsule and hold the humeral head in the glenoid."},{"term":"Subacromial space","def":"The gap between the humeral head and the coracoacromial arch, through which the supraspinatus tendon and subacromial bursa pass."},{"term":"Claw hand","def":"Hyperextension of the metacarpophalangeal joints with flexion of the interphalangeal joints, caused by loss of the lumbricals and interossei."},{"term":"Trendelenburg sign","def":"Drop of the pelvis on the unsupported side during single-leg stance, caused by weakness of the hip abductors on the stance side."},{"term":"Foot drop","def":"Inability to dorsiflex the ankle, so the toes catch the ground during the swing phase of gait."},{"term":"Dorsal ramus","def":"The posterior branch of a spinal nerve, supplying the deep (intrinsic) back muscles and the skin over them."},{"term":"Pelvic diaphragm","def":"The levator ani and coccygeus, which together form the muscular floor of the pelvic cavity."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C01","obj":"MS-5.1","title":"The rotator cuff","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The glenohumeral joint trades stability for range: a large humeral head sits on a shallow glenoid fossa, deepened only slightly by the glenoid labrum. The loose capsule cannot hold the head in place through the full arc of motion, so stability depends on four muscles whose tendons fuse with the capsule on its superior, posterior, and anterior sides. Together they form the rotator cuff (supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis). Each muscle produces its own movement, but their shared job is to press the humeral head into the glenoid while larger muscles move the arm. The capsule is not reinforced by the cuff inferiorly, which is why most dislocations are anterior and inferior."},{"type":"table","title":"Rotator cuff muscles","cols":["Muscle","Origin","Insertion","Main action","Nerve (roots)"],"rows":[["Supraspinatus","Supraspinous fossa of scapula","Superior facet of greater tubercle","Initiates abduction (about the first 15 degrees)","Suprascapular (C5, C6)"],["Infraspinatus","Infraspinous fossa","Middle facet of greater tubercle","Lateral rotation","Suprascapular (C5, C6)"],["Teres minor","Middle part of lateral border of scapula","Inferior facet of greater tubercle","Lateral rotation","Axillary (C5, C6)"],["Subscapularis","Subscapular fossa (anterior surface)","Lesser tubercle","Medial rotation","Upper and lower subscapular (C5, C6)"]],"caption":"Three muscles reach the greater tubercle from behind; only subscapularis inserts on the lesser tubercle from the front."},{"type":"p","text":"Abduction shows why the cuff matters. With the arm at the side, the deltoid (axillary nerve) pulls almost straight upward along the humerus, so on its own it would slide the head up into the acromion rather than swing the arm out. The supraspinatus pulls the head medially into the glenoid, and the infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis pull it downward. This force couple converts the deltoid's pull into rotation. Classic teaching gives the supraspinatus the first 15 degrees and the deltoid the range to about 90 degrees; throughout the movement the trapezius and serratus anterior rotate the scapula upward, and that rotation is what allows the arm to rise above about 90 degrees."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the supraspinatus is the cuff tendon that fails","steps":["Tendon runs under the coracoacromial arch","Abduction and overhead use narrow the subacromial space","Tendon is compressed against the acromion repeatedly","Age-related degeneration weakens the tendon near its insertion","Tendinopathy, then partial or full-thickness tear","Painful arc; weak initiation of abduction"],"caption":"Impingement plus degeneration explains why supraspinatus tears are most common and rise with age."},{"type":"p","text":"Injury patterns follow the nerves. The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch beneath the superior transverse scapular ligament, then around the spinoglenoid notch; entrapment at the first site weakens both abduction initiation and lateral rotation, while entrapment at the second weakens lateral rotation alone and wastes only the infraspinatus. The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck of the humerus, so a surgical neck fracture or anterior dislocation weakens the deltoid and teres minor and numbs the skin over the lower deltoid, sparing the supraspinatus. Infraspinatus remains a strong lateral rotator in that case, so lateral rotation is only mildly weak. Subscapularis loss is tested by the patient's inability to push the hand away from the lower back (lift-off test)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient cannot start abducting the arm from the side, but if the examiner lifts the arm to 30 degrees, the patient can continue to raise it. Which muscle is likely torn?","explain":"The supraspinatus. Once the arm is partly abducted, the deltoid has a better line of pull and can continue the movement even without the supraspinatus.","check":"The abduction paragraph and the force couple."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C02","obj":"MS-5.2","title":"Upper limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each upper limb compartment has a principal nerve: anterior arm (musculocutaneous), posterior arm and forearm (radial), most of the anterior forearm and thenar eminence (median), and most intrinsic hand muscles (ulnar). The lesion site along the nerve decides which branches are lost."},{"type":"table","title":"Upper limb nerves, muscles, and deficits","cols":["Nerve (roots)","Key muscles","Classic lesion and sign","Sensory loss"],"rows":[["Musculocutaneous (C5 to C7)","Biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis","Weak elbow flexion and supination; lost biceps reflex","Lateral forearm"],["Radial (C5 to T1)","Triceps, brachioradialis, extensors, supinator","Midshaft humerus fracture: wrist drop","Dorsum of hand over first web space"],["Median (C6 to T1)","Most forearm flexors, thenar muscles, lateral two lumbricals","Wrist: ape hand. Elbow: also hand of benediction","Palmar lateral three and a half digits"],["Ulnar (C8, T1)","Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial profundus, hypothenar, interossei, medial lumbricals, adductor pollicis","Elbow or wrist: claw of digits 4 and 5, weak finger abduction","Medial one and a half digits"],["Long thoracic (C5 to C7)","Serratus anterior","Axillary surgery: winged scapula","None (purely motor)"]],"caption":"Root values vary slightly between texts."},{"type":"p","text":"Elbow flexion is weakened rather than abolished because the brachioradialis (radial nerve) still flexes the elbow, and supination survives through the supinator (radial nerve). The radial nerve spirals in the radial groove of the humerus, so a midshaft fracture paralyzes the wrist and finger extensors and the wrist hangs flexed (wrist drop); the triceps is usually spared because its branches leave above the groove. Compression in the axilla, as from a crutch, also weakens the triceps."},{"type":"p","text":"The median nerve enters the hand through the carpal tunnel and sends its recurrent branch to the thenar muscles. Compression there (carpal tunnel syndrome) or a wrist laceration weakens or paralyzes opposition and thumb abduction, the thenar eminence wastes, and the thumb lies in line with the fingers (ape hand). In carpal tunnel syndrome palm sensation is spared because the palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum. A lesion at the elbow, as in a supracondylar fracture, also paralyzes the flexor digitorum superficialis and the lateral half of the profundus, so on attempting a fist the index and middle fingers stay extended (hand of benediction)."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ulnar nerve loss produces a claw hand","steps":["Medial lumbricals and interossei are paralyzed","MCP flexion and IP extension of digits 4 and 5 are lost","Extensor digitorum hyperextends the MCP joints unopposed","Long flexors flex the IP joints","Claw of the ring and little fingers"],"caption":"Digits 2 and 3 are spared because the median nerve supplies their lumbricals."},{"type":"p","text":"The ulnar nerve also supplies all the interossei, so a card cannot be gripped between the fingers, and loss of adductor pollicis makes the patient flex the thumb tip with flexor pollicis longus (median nerve) to pinch paper (Froment sign). Clawing is paradoxically worse with a lesion at the wrist than at the elbow, because a distal lesion spares the medial flexor digitorum profundus, which then flexes the distal joints strongly. The long thoracic nerve lies on the surface of the serratus anterior in the medial axillary wall, exposed during axillary node dissection. The serratus holds the scapula against the chest wall and rotates it upward, so its loss lifts the medial border away when the patient pushes against a wall and limits abduction above the horizontal."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an ulnar nerve lesion","text":"A cyclist has clawing of digits 4 and 5, weak finger abduction, and a positive Froment sign, with numbness of the palmar little finger but normal sensation over the dorsal medial hand and strong ulnar wrist flexion. The dorsal cutaneous branch and the branch to flexor carpi ulnaris leave the nerve in the forearm, so sparing both places the lesion at the wrist (Guyon canal). The marked claw fits, because the medial flexor digitorum profundus is intact. An elbow lesion would add weak ulnar wrist flexion and dorsal hand numbness, with a milder claw."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C03","obj":"MS-5.3","title":"Lower limb nerve injuries","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lower limb deficits are also read by compartment. The femoral nerve (L2 to L4, posterior divisions of the lumbar plexus) supplies the anterior thigh; the obturator nerve (L2 to L4, anterior divisions) supplies the medial thigh; the sciatic nerve divides into the tibial nerve for most of the posterior thigh, the posterior leg, and the sole, and the common fibular nerve for the anterior and lateral leg. The superior gluteal nerve (L4 to S1) supplies the hip abductors."},{"type":"table","title":"Lower limb nerves, muscles, and deficits","cols":["Nerve","Key muscles","Deficit","Typical cause"],"rows":[["Femoral","Quadriceps femoris, sartorius, iliacus","Weak knee extension, lost patellar reflex, numb anterior thigh and medial leg","Pelvic surgery, retroperitoneal hematoma, femoral catheterization"],["Obturator","Adductor longus, brevis, and magnus (adductor part), gracilis","Weak adduction; leg swings outward; numb medial thigh","Pelvic surgery, pelvic tumor, difficult labor"],["Superior gluteal","Gluteus medius and minimus, tensor fasciae latae","Trendelenburg sign; lurching gait","Misplaced gluteal injection, hip surgery"],["Common fibular","Anterior compartment (dorsiflexors) and lateral compartment (evertors)","Foot drop, steppage gait, weak eversion, numb dorsum of foot","Fibular neck fracture, tight cast, leg crossing"],["Tibial","Gastrocnemius, soleus, tibialis posterior, long toe flexors, sole muscles","Weak plantarflexion and inversion, cannot rise on toes, lost ankle reflex, numb sole","Popliteal fossa trauma; tarsal tunnel (distal branches)"]],"caption":"The nerve's compartment predicts the movement lost; its course predicts the cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Femoral injury makes the knee buckle on stairs or uneven ground, because the quadriceps can no longer lock it; the saphenous branch carries sensation from the medial leg. Hip flexion is only mildly affected because the psoas major receives direct branches from the lumbar plexus."},{"type":"p","text":"During walking, each limb in turn bears the body weight alone, and that weight falls medial to the stance hip. The gluteus medius and minimus of the stance limb pull on the pelvis to keep it level. When they are paralyzed by a superior gluteal nerve injury, the pelvis tilts down on the side of the lifted leg. The pelvis therefore drops on the side opposite the lesion, while the lesion is on the stance side."},{"type":"flow","title":"Trendelenburg sign from a right superior gluteal nerve injury","steps":["Patient stands on the right leg; left foot lifts","Body weight acts medial to the right hip","Right gluteus medius and minimus cannot hold the pelvis","Pelvis drops on the left (unsupported) side","Trunk lurches right to shift weight over the hip"],"caption":"Positive sign: pelvis drops opposite the weak abductors. The lurch is the compensation."},{"type":"p","text":"The common fibular nerve winds around the neck of the fibula just under the skin, which makes it the most often injured nerve of the lower limb. Its deep branch supplies the dorsiflexors and its superficial branch the evertors, so the foot hangs plantarflexed and inverted, the toes drag, and the patient lifts the knee high to clear them (steppage gait). Inversion is preserved because tibialis posterior is supplied by the tibial nerve. The tibial nerve lies deep in the popliteal fossa and is injured much less often."},{"type":"example","title":"Foot drop: common fibular nerve or L5 root?","text":"Both lesions cause foot drop and numbness over the dorsum of the foot, because the L5 root supplies the dorsiflexors through the common fibular nerve. The difference lies in muscles that receive L5 through other nerves. Tibialis posterior (tibial nerve, L4 and L5) inverts the foot, and the gluteus medius (superior gluteal nerve, L4 to S1) abducts the hip. In an L5 radiculopathy both are weak, so inversion and hip abduction are reduced, and back pain often radiates down the leg. In a fibular neck lesion inversion and hip abduction are normal, and the history usually involves the fibular neck itself (fracture, cast, prolonged leg crossing). Testing inversion is the key step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C04","obj":"MS-5.4","title":"Head and neck muscles and their cranial nerves","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Head and neck innervation follows embryologic origin. Muscles that develop from a pharyngeal arch keep that arch's nerve: first arch muscles are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3), and second arch muscles by the facial nerve (CN VII). The tongue muscles come instead from occipital somites and migrate forward carrying the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius are supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI)."},{"type":"table","title":"Muscles of mastication (first arch, CN V3)","cols":["Muscle","Origin","Insertion","Action"],"rows":[["Masseter","Zygomatic arch","Lateral ramus and angle of mandible","Elevates mandible (closes jaw)"],["Temporalis","Temporal fossa","Coronoid process","Elevates; posterior fibers retract"],["Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid plate (medial side)","Medial ramus and angle","Elevates; helps protrude and grind"],["Lateral pterygoid","Sphenoid wing and lateral pterygoid plate","Neck of mandible, TMJ disc and capsule","Protrudes and opens jaw; grinding"]],"caption":"Three muscles close the jaw; the lateral pterygoid opens it, aided by the suprahyoids and gravity."},{"type":"p","text":"Because each lateral pterygoid pulls its own condyle forward, a CN V3 lesion makes the chin deviate toward the paralyzed side on opening, as the intact muscle advances only the healthy condyle. The masseter and temporalis waste on that side, and the jaw jerk is lost. CN V3 also supplies the other first arch muscles (mylohyoid, anterior digastric, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini)."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscles of facial expression insert into skin. Key members are the frontalis (raises the eyebrows), orbicularis oculi (closes the eye), orbicularis oris (closes the lips), buccinator (keeps the cheek against the teeth so food stays between them), and platysma. CN VII also supplies the stapedius, stylohyoid, and posterior belly of digastric. A lower motor neuron lesion such as Bell palsy paralyzes the whole half of the face, including the forehead; the eye cannot close, food collects in the cheek, and loss of stapedius makes sounds seem too loud. An upper motor neuron lesion, such as a cortical stroke, spares the forehead because the part of the facial nucleus serving the upper face receives corticobulbar fibers from both hemispheres."},{"type":"p","text":"The tongue has intrinsic muscles that change its shape and extrinsic muscles that move it: genioglossus (protrudes), hyoglossus (depresses), styloglossus (retracts and elevates), and palatoglossus (elevates the back of the tongue). All are supplied by CN XII except palatoglossus, which develops with the palate muscles and is supplied by the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why the tongue deviates toward a CN XII lesion","steps":["Each genioglossus pulls the tongue forward and toward the opposite side","Both sides acting together protrude it in the midline","One genioglossus is paralyzed","Intact genioglossus pushes the tongue toward the weak side","Lower motor neuron lesion: weak side atrophies and fasciculates"],"caption":"The protruded tongue points toward the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the manubrium and medial clavicle to the mastoid process. Acting alone it turns the face to the opposite side and tilts the head toward its own shoulder; together they flex the neck. The trapezius elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula and, with the serratus anterior, rotates it upward for overhead reach. CN XI arises from the upper cervical cord, enters the skull through the foramen magnum, and leaves through the jugular foramen. Injury in the posterior triangle, often at lymph node biopsy, spares the sternocleidomastoid (already supplied) but paralyzes the trapezius, so the shoulder droops and the arm cannot be raised fully overhead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient cannot shrug the right shoulder and has difficulty turning the head to the left. Where along CN XI is the lesion likely to be?","explain":"Proximal, at or near the jugular foramen. The right sternocleidomastoid turns the face to the left, so its weakness plus trapezius weakness means the nerve was injured before it supplied the sternocleidomastoid. A posterior triangle lesion would spare head turning.","check":"The sternocleidomastoid and CN XI paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-05-01-C05","obj":"MS-5.5","title":"Trunk muscles: back, diaphragm, abdominal wall, and pelvic floor","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Trunk muscles fall into two developmental groups. Epaxial muscles form behind the vertebral column and are supplied by dorsal rami; hypaxial muscles form the body wall in front and are supplied by ventral rami. The erector spinae is the main epaxial group. Its three columns (iliocostalis laterally, longissimus, spinalis medially) arise by a common tendon from the posterior iliac crest, sacrum, and lumbar spinous processes and insert on the ribs, vertebrae, and skull. Acting together they extend the spine; acting on one side they bend it laterally. Superficial back muscles such as the trapezius and latissimus dorsi are limb muscles that migrated onto the back and are not supplied by dorsal rami."},{"type":"table","title":"Trunk muscles at a glance","cols":["Muscle","Main action","Innervation"],"rows":[["Erector spinae","Extends and laterally flexes the spine","Dorsal rami of spinal nerves"],["Diaphragm","Main muscle of inspiration","Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)"],["External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Compress abdomen; flex and rotate trunk","Thoracoabdominal (T7 to T11) and subcostal (T12); L1 branches to the lowest internal oblique and transversus"],["Rectus abdominis","Flexes trunk; compresses abdomen","Thoracoabdominal and subcostal nerves"],["Levator ani (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus)","Supports pelvic viscera; maintains anorectal angle","Nerve to levator ani (S4) and pudendal branches"]],"caption":"Dorsal rami supply the deep back; ventral rami supply the rest."},{"type":"p","text":"The diaphragm arises from the xiphoid process, the lower six costal cartilages, and the lumbar vertebrae (through the crura and arcuate ligaments), and inserts into its own central tendon. When it contracts, the dome descends and the lower ribs are raised, enlarging the thorax, lowering intrapleural pressure, and drawing air in. In quiet breathing it generates most of the tidal volume. Its motor supply is the phrenic nerve from C3 to C5, mainly C4, because its muscle precursors arise at cervical levels and descend with their nerve. The phrenic nerve is also sensory to the central diaphragm, so irritation there refers pain to the shoulder (C3 and C4 dermatomes)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cervical cord level and breathing","steps":["Complete injury at or above C3: phrenic output lost, apnea","Injury at C4 to C5: diaphragm partly spared, ventilation impaired","Injury below C5: diaphragm works, intercostals and abdominals lost","Below C5: weak cough and forced expiration"],"caption":"Unilateral phrenic palsy instead gives an elevated hemidiaphragm that moves paradoxically upward on a sniff."},{"type":"p","text":"The anterolateral abdominal wall has three flat layers with crossing fiber directions (external oblique running inferomedially, internal oblique superomedially, transversus abdominis horizontally) and the vertical rectus abdominis in its sheath. Contracting together, they raise intra-abdominal pressure for forced expiration, coughing, defecation, and childbirth. Their nerves are the continuations of the lower intercostal nerves (thoracoabdominal, T7 to T11) and the subcostal nerve (T12), with the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1) to the lowest fibers. These run between the internal oblique and transversus, so supply and skin sensation are segmental, with T10 at the umbilicus."},{"type":"p","text":"The levator ani is a thin muscular sheet stretching from the pubis, the tendinous arch over the obturator internus fascia, and the ischial spine to the perineal body, anococcygeal body, and coccyx. Its tone supports the bladder, uterus, and rectum, and it contracts reflexly whenever intra-abdominal pressure rises. The puborectalis slings the anorectal junction forward, holding the anorectal angle that contributes to fecal continence; it relaxes during defecation. Stretching or tearing of the levator ani and its nerves during vaginal delivery is a major cause of pelvic organ prolapse and stress urinary incontinence."},{"type":"stop","predict":"During a hard cough, why does urine leak in a woman with a weakened pelvic floor but not in one with a healthy pelvic floor?","explain":"The abdominal wall and diaphragm raise intra-abdominal pressure. Normally the levator ani contracts at the same moment, supporting the bladder neck and urethra so urethral closure pressure rises with bladder pressure. Without that support, bladder pressure exceeds urethral pressure and urine leaks.","check":"The levator ani paragraph and the abdominal wall actions."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology together explain innervation. Arch muscles keep the arch nerve (V3, VII, and the vagus for the palate and pharynx), somite-derived tongue muscles keep CN XII, epaxial back muscles keep dorsal rami, and the diaphragm keeps the cervical phrenic nerve after descending. In the limbs, compartments developed as dorsal and ventral muscle masses supplied by posterior and anterior divisions of the plexuses, which is why one nerve serves one functional group and why a single deficit localizes so well. Neuroscience adds the next layer: a peripheral nerve lesion affects one nerve's muscles and skin, a root lesion affects every muscle sharing that segment across several nerves, and an upper motor neuron lesion weakens movements with spasticity and brisk reflexes rather than wasting."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology supplies the mechanisms of injury: compression at fixed tunnels (carpal tunnel, suprascapular notch, fibular neck), traction or laceration during fractures and surgery, degeneration of poorly vascularized tendons, and stretching of the pelvic floor in childbirth. Physiology explains the consequences, from the lost force couple in a rotator cuff tear to hypoventilation after phrenic injury. Real anatomy varies, so the classic patterns described here are the starting point for reasoning, and electrodiagnostic testing or imaging confirms the site in practice."}],"summary":["The rotator cuff holds the humeral head in the shallow glenoid; supraspinatus initiates abduction, infraspinatus and teres minor rotate laterally, and subscapularis rotates medially.","Suprascapular nerve: supraspinatus and infraspinatus. Axillary nerve: teres minor and deltoid. Supraspinatus is torn most often because it is compressed under the coracoacromial arch.","Upper limb: musculocutaneous loss weakens elbow flexion, radial loss causes wrist drop, median loss at the wrist causes ape hand, and ulnar loss causes clawing of digits 4 and 5.","Claw hand results from paralyzed lumbricals and interossei, which leaves MCP joints hyperextended and IP joints flexed.","Long thoracic nerve injury paralyzes serratus anterior and wings the scapula; it is a classic complication of axillary surgery.","Lower limb: femoral loss weakens knee extension, obturator loss weakens adduction, common fibular loss causes foot drop with weak eversion, and tibial loss weakens plantarflexion and inversion.","In a Trendelenburg sign the pelvis drops on the lifted side; the weak gluteus medius and minimus are on the stance side.","CN V3 supplies mastication, CN VII facial expression, CN XII the tongue except palatoglossus (CN X), and CN XI the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius.","Diaphragm: phrenic nerve C3 to C5; complete cord injury at or above C3 causes apnea. Abdominal wall: T7 to T12 and L1 in a segmental pattern.","Erector spinae extends the spine on dorsal rami; levator ani supports the pelvic organs, and puborectalis holds the anorectal angle."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Upper Limb: shoulder region, brachial plexus, and nerve injuries"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Lower Limb: gluteal region, thigh, leg, and nerve injuries"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head and Neck: muscles of mastication, face, tongue, and the accessory nerve"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back, Abdomen, and Pelvis and Perineum: intrinsic back muscles, diaphragm, anterolateral abdominal wall, and pelvic floor"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Head and Neck (pharyngeal arches and tongue); Muscular System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Pulmonary Ventilation"}]},"MS-6":{"unit":"MS-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Connective Tissue: Origins, Collagen, Cartilage, and Elastic Fibers","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains where the connective tissues of bone, cartilage, tendon, and ligament come from, which collagen types build them, how collagen and elastic fibers are assembled, and why cartilage and tendon heal slowly. Board questions usually name a vitamin, mineral, gene, or embryonic tissue and ask which structure or step fails. If you can place each defect on the collagen pathway, explain how charged proteoglycans hold water, and describe how fibrillin-1 controls TGF-beta, you can reason through most questions on scurvy, osteogenesis imperfecta, Menkes disease, osteoarthritis, and Marfan syndrome.","prerequisites":["Germ layers, somites, and neural crest formation (MS-1)","Histology of bone, cartilage, and dense connective tissue (MS-2)","Protein synthesis in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus, and secretion","Autosomal dominant, autosomal recessive, and X-linked inheritance","Structure of the vertebral column and synovial joints (MS-3, MS-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal biochemistry and nutrition (MS-8)","how":"Vitamin C, iron, and copper are cofactors for collagen modification, so dietary deficiency becomes a connective tissue disease."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal genetics (MS-9)","how":"COL1A1, COL1A2, ATP7A, and FBN1 mutations show how the type of mutation (null allele or dominant negative) sets disease severity."},{"to":"Degenerative musculoskeletal diseases (MS-13-E)","how":"Osteoarthritis begins with proteoglycan loss and collagen network damage in avascular articular cartilage that cannot repair itself."},{"to":"Vascular conditions (CV-18-I)","how":"Fibrillin-1 and type III collagen defects weaken the arterial wall, causing aortic root dilation, dissection, and arterial rupture."},{"to":"Renal basement membrane disease","how":"Type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane is the target in Alport syndrome and Goodpasture syndrome."}],"terms":[{"term":"Mesenchyme","def":"Loosely organized embryonic connective tissue whose cells migrate and differentiate into fibroblasts, chondroblasts, and osteoblasts."},{"term":"Sclerotome","def":"The ventromedial part of each somite that surrounds the notochord and neural tube and forms the vertebrae and ribs."},{"term":"Neural crest","def":"Cells that leave the edges of the neural folds, migrate widely, and in the head form most of the facial skeleton and connective tissue."},{"term":"Procollagen","def":"The secreted triple helical precursor of collagen that still carries its nonhelical propeptides at both ends."},{"term":"Tropocollagen","def":"The collagen molecule left after the propeptides are cleaved, which self-assembles into fibrils."},{"term":"Lysyl oxidase","def":"A copper-dependent extracellular enzyme that converts lysine side chains to aldehydes, which then form covalent cross-links in collagen and elastin."},{"term":"Glycosaminoglycan (GAG)","def":"A long unbranched polysaccharide of repeating disaccharides, usually sulfated and strongly negatively charged."},{"term":"Aggrecan","def":"The large cartilage proteoglycan whose core protein carries many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains and binds hyaluronan."},{"term":"Fibrocartilage","def":"Cartilage in which dense type I collagen bundles alternate with rows of chondrocytes, resisting both tension and compression."},{"term":"Fibrillin-1","def":"A large glycoprotein that forms the microfibrils on which elastin is deposited and that anchors latent TGF-beta in the matrix."},{"term":"Desmosine","def":"A cross-link formed from four lysine side chains that joins elastin molecules and lets the fiber recoil after stretch."},{"term":"Dominant negative mutation","def":"A mutation whose abnormal product interferes with the normal product from the other allele, producing a worse phenotype than loss of one allele."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01-C01","obj":"MS-6.1","title":"Embryonic origins of bone, cartilage, and tendon","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every skeletal connective tissue begins as mesenchyme, which condenses where a bone, cartilage, or tendon will form. Three sources supply this mesenchyme in different body regions: the somatic (parietal) layer of lateral plate mesoderm in the limbs, the sclerotome of the somites in the axial skeleton, and neural crest in most of the face. Because the sources are separate, a defect in one can damage a single region while the rest of the skeleton is normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Sources of musculoskeletal connective tissue","cols":["Source","Skeletal and connective tissue formed","Ossification of bones formed"],"rows":[["Lateral plate mesoderm, somatic layer","Limb bones and cartilage, limb tendons and ligaments, limb dermis and fascia, sternum","Mostly endochondral (cartilage model first)"],["Paraxial mesoderm, sclerotome","Vertebrae, ribs, annulus fibrosus, occipital part of the skull base","Endochondral"],["Paraxial mesoderm, myotome","Skeletal muscle fibers of the trunk and limbs (not their tendons in the limb)","Not applicable"],["Notochord","Nucleus pulposus of each intervertebral disc","Not applicable"],["Cranial neural crest","Most facial bones, pharyngeal arch cartilages, parts of the cranial vault, facial dermis and tendons","Mostly intramembranous (face and vault); arch cartilages are endochondral or persist as cartilage"]],"caption":"Each region of the skeleton has its own mesenchymal source, which is why regional malformations occur."},{"type":"h","text":"Limbs: skeleton and muscle meet after migration"},{"type":"p","text":"The limb bud is a core of somatic lateral plate mesoderm under a cap of ectoderm. This core condenses into hyaline cartilage models that later ossify, and it also forms the tendons, ligaments, and connective tissue sheaths that pattern each muscle. The muscle cells themselves do not arise here. Myoblasts migrate into the bud from the somites, so a limb muscle and its tendon come from different sources and join only after migration, guided by the lateral plate connective tissue."},{"type":"h","text":"Axial skeleton: the sclerotome and resegmentation"},{"type":"p","text":"Sonic hedgehog from the notochord and neural tube floor plate induces the ventromedial somite to become sclerotome. Each vertebra forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome fused with the cranial half of the next, a process called resegmentation, shown below. The shift places each segmental muscle across an intervertebral joint so it can move the spine, and lets the spinal nerve exit near the disc. Failure of segmentation fuses vertebrae (in the neck, Klippel-Feil syndrome, with a short neck, low hairline, and limited motion), and failure of one half to form gives a hemivertebra and congenital scoliosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From somite to vertebra","steps":["Notochord and floor plate secrete Sonic hedgehog","Ventromedial somite becomes sclerotome","Sclerotome cells surround the notochord and neural tube","Each sclerotome splits into cranial and caudal halves","Caudal half fuses with the next cranial half","Fused halves form the vertebral body; intervening cells form annulus fibrosus","Notochord persists between bodies as nucleus pulposus"],"caption":"Resegmentation makes vertebrae intersegmental, so muscles from one somite span two vertebrae."},{"type":"h","text":"Head: neural crest builds the face"},{"type":"p","text":"Cranial neural crest cells migrate into the frontonasal region and the pharyngeal arches. There they form most of the viscerocranium (maxilla, mandible, zygomatic, nasal, and palatine bones) by intramembranous ossification, and the cartilages of the first two arches, including Meckel cartilage, the malleus, incus, and stapes. Paraxial mesoderm supplies the occipital region and much of the posterior skull base. When neural crest cells are too few, as in Treacher Collins syndrome, the mandible, zygomatic bones, and ears are hypoplastic while the limbs and spine are normal, which the origins in the table predict."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant has fused cervical vertebrae, a normal face, and normal limbs. Which embryonic tissue failed, and why are the face and limbs spared?","explain":"The sclerotome failed to segment. The face comes from neural crest and the limb skeleton from somatic lateral plate mesoderm, so neither depends on sclerotome segmentation.","check":"The source table and the resegmentation flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01-C02","obj":"MS-6.2","title":"Collagen types and the tissues they build","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Collagens are a family of proteins built from three alpha chains wound into a triple helix. The fibrillar collagens (types I, II, III, and V) lose their end domains after secretion and pack into banded fibrils, while type IV keeps its nonhelical domains and forms a network. Each tissue uses the type whose architecture fits the load it carries, so knowing the load predicts the collagen."},{"type":"table","title":"Collagen types to know","cols":["Type","Structure","Main tissues","Why it suits them","Linked disorder"],"rows":[["I","Thick fibrils in parallel bundles; two alpha1(I) chains and one alpha2(I) chain","Bone, tendon, ligament, dermis, fibrocartilage (annulus fibrosus, menisci, pubic symphysis), dentin, cornea, mature scar","Highest tensile strength; about 90% of body collagen","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],["II","Thin fibrils dispersed in proteoglycan gel; three alpha1(II) chains","Hyaline and elastic cartilage, nucleus pulposus, vitreous body","Restrains proteoglycan swelling so cartilage resists compression","Type II collagenopathies (chondrodysplasias)"],["III","Fine fibrils forming branched reticular fibers","Reticular stroma of lymph nodes, spleen, marrow, and liver; blood vessels; uterus; skin; early granulation tissue","Flexible support in distensible organs and early repair","Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (COL3A1)"],["IV","Sheetlike network, not fibrils","Basement membranes, including the external lamina of each muscle fiber and the glomerular basement membrane","Supports epithelium and muscle fibers and filters","Alport syndrome; Goodpasture syndrome"],["V","Fibrillar, regulates type I fibril diameter","With type I in skin, tendon, and other tissues","Controls fibril size","Classical Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],["X","Short network-forming chain","Hypertrophic chondrocytes of the growth plate","Prepares cartilage for calcification","Schmid metaphyseal chondrodysplasia"]],"caption":"Types I to IV are the core exam set; types V and X are added because they appear in musculoskeletal disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Type I collagen dominates tissues pulled in tension. In tendon and ligament its fibrils lie parallel to the line of pull, and in bone it forms the organic scaffold (osteoid) on which hydroxyapatite deposits, giving bone tensile strength while the mineral resists compression. Fibrocartilage is a hybrid: dense type I bundles resist tension, and a small amount of proteoglycan around rows of chondrocytes resists compression. In the intervertebral disc the outer annulus fibrosus is mainly type I, while the gel-like nucleus pulposus is type II with abundant proteoglycan."},{"type":"p","text":"Type II fibrils are thin and do not bundle, and their refractive index is close to that of the surrounding matrix, so hyaline cartilage looks glassy and fiber-free on light microscopy. Its matrix stains basophilic because of the sulfated GAGs, not the collagen. Type III fibrils are heavily glycosylated, which is why reticular fibers are periodic acid-Schiff positive and stain black with silver (argyrophilic) rather than with H&E. In wound healing, type III is laid down first in granulation tissue and is gradually replaced by type I, which explains why a scar gains strength over months."},{"type":"p","text":"Type IV collagen keeps interruptions in its Gly-X-Y sequence and its terminal NC1 domains, so molecules link end to end and side to side into a mesh that binds laminin. In the glomerulus, alpha3, alpha4, and alpha5 chains form this mesh: Alport syndrome (most often X-linked COL4A5) causes hematuria, sensorineural hearing loss, and eye defects, and Goodpasture syndrome is caused by autoantibodies against the NC1 domain of the alpha3 chain."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01-C03","obj":"MS-6.3","title":"Collagen synthesis and the diseases of each step","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fibroblasts, osteoblasts, and chondrocytes modify and fold collagen inside the cell, then trim and cross-link it outside. Each step has its own requirement, so each defect can be placed on the pathway."},{"type":"flow","title":"Collagen synthesis","steps":["RER ribosomes make pro-alpha chains with Gly-X-Y repeats","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases act (vitamin C, Fe2+, O2)","Some hydroxylysines are glycosylated","C-propeptide disulfides align three chains; helix zips toward N-terminus","Procollagen passes through the Golgi and is secreted","Procollagen peptidases cleave both propeptides, forming tropocollagen","Tropocollagen self-assembles into staggered fibrils","Lysyl oxidase (copper) forms covalent cross-links"],"caption":"Steps 1 to 5 occur inside the cell and steps 6 to 8 outside it."},{"type":"p","text":"Glycine occupies every third position because only its hydrogen side chain fits in the crowded center of the helix. Proline in the X and Y positions stiffens each chain, and hydroxyproline adds hydrogen bonding that keeps the helix stable at body temperature, 37 °C (98.6 °F). The hydroxylases are iron enzymes that use O2 and alpha-ketoglutarate, and ascorbate keeps their iron in the reduced Fe2+ state. Hydroxylysine provides sites for glycosylation and for the most stable cross-links."},{"type":"p","text":"Folding starts at the C-terminal propeptides, which also keep procollagen soluble inside the cell. Once they are cleaved outside, tropocollagen molecules pack in a quarter stagger, leaving regular gaps that give the fibril its banding and seed bone mineral. Lysyl oxidase then oxidizes lysine and hydroxylysine side chains to aldehydes that bond covalently with neighboring molecules, converting a weak fibril into one that resists tension."},{"type":"table","title":"Defects by step","cols":["Step","Defect","Disease","Findings explained by mechanism"],"rows":[["Chain sequence","COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutation","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Fractures with minor trauma, blue sclerae (thin sclera shows the choroid), hearing loss, dentinogenesis imperfecta"],["Hydroxylation","Vitamin C deficiency","Scurvy","Unstable helices are degraded: perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums, poor wound healing, subperiosteal hemorrhage"],["Lysine hydroxylation","Lysyl hydroxylase (PLOD1) mutation","Kyphoscoliotic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Weak cross-links: hypotonia, progressive scoliosis"],["Propeptide cleavage","Loss of N-propeptide cleavage (ADAMTS2, or COL1A1 or COL1A2 cleavage site)","Dermatosparaxis and arthrochalasia Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Disordered fibrils: fragile skin or joint dislocation"],["Cross-linking","ATP7A mutation, low copper delivery","Menkes disease","Weak collagen and elastin: tortuous arteries, bladder diverticula, lax skin"]],"caption":"The step that fails predicts which tissues fail."},{"type":"p","text":"Scurvy shows first where collagen turns over quickly and supports small vessels, so skin and gum capillaries bleed and wounds stay weak. Osteogenesis imperfecta is usually autosomal dominant: a null allele halves normal collagen and gives the mild form, while a glycine substitution gives severe or lethal forms, as the example below shows. Menkes disease is X-linked; ATP7A normally pumps copper out of enterocytes and into the Golgi for copper enzymes, so copper is trapped in the gut, serum copper and ceruloplasmin are low, and lysyl oxidase, tyrosinase (hypopigmentation), and cytochrome c oxidase (hypotonia, seizures, neurodegeneration) all fail; the brittle, kinky hair reflects poor copper-dependent cross-linking."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a glycine substitution in COL1A1 is worse than a null allele","text":"Type I collagen has two alpha1 chains and one alpha2 chain. With one null COL1A1 allele, the cell makes about half the normal amount of collagen, but every molecule is normal: mild osteogenesis imperfecta. With a glycine substitution, half the alpha1 chains are abnormal and both kinds are used. A molecule is normal only if both of its alpha1 chains are normal, a probability of 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4. About three quarters of the molecules carry at least one defective chain and are degraded or weaken the fibril. This is the dominant negative effect."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with scurvy and a child with Menkes disease both have fragile vessels. Where on the pathway does each defect lie, and which one also affects hair and the brain?","explain":"Scurvy blocks hydroxylation inside the cell; Menkes blocks cross-linking outside it because lysyl oxidase lacks copper. Only Menkes also starves tyrosinase and cytochrome c oxidase.","check":"The synthesis flow and the defects table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01-C04","obj":"MS-6.4","title":"How articular cartilage resists compression, and why cartilage and tendon heal slowly","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage about 70% to 80% water by weight, with sparse chondrocytes in lacunae, a type II collagen network, and a large amount of aggrecan. It has no blood vessels, nerves, or lymphatics, and unlike most hyaline cartilage it has no perichondrium. Its job is to spread load and give a nearly frictionless surface, and both depend on how its matrix handles water."},{"type":"p","text":"Aggrecan is a core protein carrying many chondroitin sulfate and keratan sulfate chains. Many aggrecan molecules (often 100 or more) bind noncovalently to a single strand of hyaluronan, stabilized by link protein, forming enormous aggregates trapped in the collagen mesh. The sulfate and carboxyl groups give the matrix a high density of fixed negative charge. These charges draw in Na+ and other cations, and water follows by osmosis, so the matrix tries to swell. The type II collagen network resists that swelling and is held in tension, much as the fabric of a tire holds in air pressure. The result is a pre-stressed cushion that pushes back when compressed."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Load and recovery in articular cartilage","steps":["Joint load compresses the matrix","Fluid pressure rises and carries most of the load","Water escapes slowly through tiny matrix pores","Fixed charges concentrate; osmotic resistance rises","Load is removed","Fixed charges draw water and solutes back in"],"caption":"Low permeability lets fluid pressure bear load, and cyclic loading pumps nutrients from synovial fluid to chondrocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Because cartilage is avascular, chondrocytes receive nutrients and oxygen by diffusion from synovial fluid, aided by this pumping, and they work at low oxygen tension. They are few, cannot migrate through the dense matrix, and divide little in adults. Injury therefore brings no clot, no inflammatory cells, and no new matrix-forming cells. In osteoarthritis, proteoglycan loss lowers the fixed charge density, so cartilage loses stiffness, the collagen network fibrillates, and the damage spreads rather than heals."},{"type":"p","text":"Tendons are dense regular connective tissue: parallel type I collagen bundles with rows of flattened tenocytes. Cells are sparse and the blood supply is poor, reaching the tendon from the muscle, the bone insertion, and the surrounding paratenon or synovial sheath, with relatively avascular zones in the supraspinatus tendon near its insertion and in the Achilles tendon a few centimeters above the calcaneus. Healing passes through inflammation, a proliferative phase rich in type III collagen, and remodeling toward type I over many months, and the repaired tendon rarely regains full strength."},{"type":"table","title":"Blood supply and repair","cols":["Tissue","Blood supply","Repair"],"rows":[["Articular hyaline cartilage","None; diffusion from synovial fluid","Little or none; defects reaching bone fill with fibrocartilage"],["Meniscus (fibrocartilage)","Peripheral rim only","Peripheral tears can heal; inner tears usually do not"],["Tendon and ligament","Poor, with watershed zones","Slow scar formation, type III then type I; strength stays below normal"],["Bone","Rich","Regenerates true bone without a scar"]],"caption":"Repair capacity tracks blood supply and the number of cells able to make new matrix."},{"type":"example","title":"Two cartilage injuries","text":"A shallow defect in the articular cartilage of the femoral condyle never reaches bone. No vessels are injured, no clot forms, and nearby chondrocytes cannot migrate into it, so it persists or enlarges. A deeper defect that penetrates the subchondral bone bleeds. The clot brings marrow mesenchymal cells, which fill the defect with fibrocartilage rich in type I collagen. This tissue resists tension but lacks the aggrecan-rich, type II matrix that resists compression, so it wears down under joint loading. Vascular access, not the size of the injury, decides whether any repair occurs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-06-01-C05","obj":"MS-6.5","title":"Elastic fibers, fibrillin-1, and TGF-beta","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Elastic fibers let tissues stretch and spring back. Each fiber has an amorphous core of elastin, about 90% of the fiber, deposited on a scaffold of fibrillin-1 microfibrils about 10 nm wide. Cells secrete tropoelastin, a soluble precursor rich in glycine, proline, valine, and alanine with lysine-containing cross-linking domains. It contains little hydroxyproline, no hydroxylysine, and is not glycosylated. The microfibrils come first and guide tropoelastin into place, and lysyl oxidase, the same copper enzyme that cross-links collagen, joins four lysine side chains into desmosine and isodesmosine cross-links."},{"type":"p","text":"Elastin's hydrophobic segments are disordered. Stretching straightens them and lowers their entropy; releasing the force lets them return to disorder, so recoil is passive and needs no energy. The cross-links keep the molecules joined so the fiber returns to its original length. Elastic fibers stain poorly with H&E and are shown with special stains such as Verhoeff or orcein."},{"type":"table","title":"Collagen fibers versus elastic fibers","cols":["Feature","Collagen fibers (type I)","Elastic fibers"],"rows":[["Main protein","Triple helical collagen","Elastin core on fibrillin-1 microfibrils"],["Hydroxylysine and glycosylation","Present","Absent"],["Cross-links","Lysyl oxidase aldehyde cross-links","Desmosine and isodesmosine (lysyl oxidase)"],["Mechanical role","Resists stretch with little give","Stretches and recoils"],["Examples","Tendon, ligament, bone","Ligamentum flavum, elastic cartilage of the ear and epiglottis"]],"caption":"Both fibers depend on copper, which is why Menkes disease weakens arteries and skin."},{"type":"p","text":"The ligamenta flava join the laminae of adjacent vertebrae and form part of the posterior wall of the vertebral canal. Their high elastic content keeps them taut in every position. They stretch during flexion and recoil to help restore the upright posture, and their tension prevents them from buckling into the canal during extension. With age, elastic fibers are lost and the ligament thickens and folds inward, which contributes to lumbar spinal stenosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Fibrillin-1 microfibrils also store growth factor. TGF-beta is secreted in an inactive large latent complex: the mature cytokine bound to its latency-associated peptide, which is linked to latent TGF-beta binding protein. That binding protein attaches to fibrillin-1, holding TGF-beta in the matrix until integrins or proteases release it. In Marfan syndrome, an autosomal dominant FBN1 mutation on chromosome 15 leaves the microfibrils defective and unable to hold the complex, so TGF-beta is released and signals excessively through SMAD proteins. The mechanical weakness and the excess signaling together explain the phenotype: ectopia lentis (the lens zonules are microfibrils, and the lens usually shifts upward), mitral valve prolapse, aortic root dilation with cystic medial degeneration, tall stature with long limbs and arachnodactyly, and joint hypermobility. Loeys-Dietz syndrome, caused by TGF-beta receptor mutations, produces similar aortic disease, which supports the role of TGF-beta signaling."},{"type":"flow","title":"Marfan syndrome pathogenesis","steps":["FBN1 mutation produces defective fibrillin-1","Microfibrils are weak and reduced","Latent TGF-beta is not held in the matrix","Excess TGF-beta signaling disrupts smooth muscle and matrix","Elastic lamellae of the aortic media fragment","Aortic root dilates; dissection risk rises"],"caption":"Structural weakness of the microfibrils and excess TGF-beta signaling act together on the aortic wall."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a fibrillin-1 defect dislocate the lens, while an elastin gene deletion (as in Williams syndrome) narrows the aorta above the valve but leaves the lens in place?","explain":"Lens zonules are made of fibrillin microfibrils without elastin, so only fibrillin defects weaken them. Elastin loss affects elastic arteries, where too little elastin leads to smooth muscle overgrowth and supravalvular aortic stenosis.","check":"The elastic fiber structure and the Marfan paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology, histology, and biochemistry explain the same tissues from three directions. The embryonic source decides where a malformation appears, the collagen type decides what load a tissue can carry, and the synthetic step decides which nutrient or gene matters. Vitamin C and iron act inside the cell and copper outside it, so scurvy and Menkes disease both weaken vessels but only Menkes, which starves many copper enzymes, damages the brain and hair. Elastic fibers and collagen share lysyl oxidase but differ in scaffold, which is why fibrillin defects dislocate the lens and elastin defects do not."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology follows blood supply and cell number. Avascular articular cartilage and poorly perfused tendon cannot mount a full repair, so degenerative disease and tendon injury tend to persist, while well-vascularized bone regenerates. Immunology joins in at the basement membrane, where autoantibodies to type IV collagen cause Goodpasture syndrome. The limits of these rules matter: Ehlers-Danlos syndrome has many types with different genes, Marfan features overlap with other connective tissue disorders, and TGF-beta excess is one contributor to aortic disease rather than the whole explanation."}],"summary":["Limb bones, cartilage, and tendons come from somatic lateral plate mesoderm, limb muscle from somites, the axial skeleton from sclerotome, and most of the face from neural crest.","Each vertebra forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next; the notochord persists as the nucleus pulposus.","Type I collagen resists tension in bone, tendon, ligament, and fibrocartilage; type II restrains proteoglycan swelling in hyaline cartilage and the nucleus pulposus.","Type III forms reticular fibers and early granulation tissue; type IV forms the basement membrane network targeted in Alport and Goodpasture syndromes.","Glycine at every third position allows the triple helix; hydroxylation needs vitamin C and Fe2+; cross-linking by lysyl oxidase needs copper.","Scurvy fails at hydroxylation, osteogenesis imperfecta at type I chain structure or amount, and Menkes disease at copper-dependent cross-linking.","A glycine substitution acts dominant negatively, leaving only about one quarter of type I molecules normal, so it is worse than a null allele.","Aggrecan's fixed negative charges draw in cations and water, and the type II network holds that swelling, so cartilage resists compression.","Avascular cartilage and poorly perfused tendon heal slowly or poorly; cartilage defects reaching bone fill with weaker fibrocartilage.","Elastic fibers are elastin cross-linked by desmosine on fibrillin-1 microfibrils; FBN1 mutation in Marfan syndrome releases TGF-beta and weakens the aorta and lens zonules."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Third to eighth weeks: somites and neural crest; skeletal system; limbs; head and neck"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Connective Tissue; Cartilage"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycosaminoglycans, Proteoglycans, and Glycoproteins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic disorders: Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos syndromes; Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Back: vertebral column and ligamenta flava"}]},"MS-7":{"unit":"MS-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01","title":"Muscle Contraction","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle turn an electrical or chemical signal into force, and how each type grades that force. It follows Ca2+ from its release to its removal, works through the cross-bridge cycle and the mechanics of length and load, and contrasts the three muscle types and the two main skeletal fiber types. Board questions usually change one step (a channel, a pump, a kinase, ATP supply, or a training pattern) and ask what happens to contraction or relaxation. If you can say where the Ca2+ comes from, what it binds, and what removes it in each muscle type, you can reason through most of them.","prerequisites":["Resting membrane potential and the action potential (NE-13)","Neuromuscular junction transmission by acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors","Sarcomere structure: thick and thin filaments, Z lines, A and I bands (MS-2)","G protein signaling: Gs, Gq, cAMP, IP3, and protein kinases A and G","ATP production by phosphocreatine, glycolysis, and oxidative phosphorylation"],"connections":[{"to":"Cardiac muscle contraction and the cardiac cycle (CV-5, CV-6)","how":"Calcium-induced Ca2+ release and the length dependence of force underlie contractility and the Frank-Starling mechanism."},{"to":"Hemodynamics and blood flow regulation (CV-11)","how":"Vascular smooth muscle tone, set by the balance of MLCK and MLCP and by nitric oxide, determines arteriolar resistance."},{"to":"Muscle and nerve interactions (MS-10)","how":"The motor neuron sets motor unit size, recruitment order, firing rate, and fiber type."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal biochemistry and genetics (MS-8, MS-9)","how":"ATP supply limits contraction and relaxation, and RyR1, SERCA1, and myophosphorylase variants produce characteristic contraction disorders."},{"to":"Exercise and the cardiovascular system (CV-12)","how":"Fiber type and training adaptations explain differences in endurance and strength."}],"terms":[{"term":"Excitation-contraction coupling","def":"The sequence that links a muscle action potential or receptor signal to a rise in cytosolic Ca2+ and cross-bridge cycling."},{"term":"Triad","def":"A T tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the site of coupling in skeletal muscle."},{"term":"Dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR)","def":"The L-type Ca2+ channel of the T tubule, which in skeletal muscle acts mainly as a voltage sensor."},{"term":"Ryanodine receptor (RyR)","def":"The Ca2+ release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum; RyR1 in skeletal muscle and RyR2 in cardiac muscle."},{"term":"SERCA","def":"The sarcoplasmic and endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase, which pumps Ca2+ back into the SR and allows relaxation."},{"term":"Cross-bridge cycle","def":"The repeated binding, power stroke, and release of myosin heads on actin, driven by ATP binding and hydrolysis."},{"term":"Motor unit","def":"One alpha motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates."},{"term":"Tetanus (physiological)","def":"Fused, sustained maximal contraction produced when action potentials arrive faster than the fiber can relax."},{"term":"Phospholamban","def":"An SR membrane protein that inhibits SERCA2a until it is phosphorylated, mainly by protein kinase A."},{"term":"Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)","def":"The Ca2+-calmodulin dependent enzyme that phosphorylates the myosin regulatory light chain and switches on smooth muscle contraction."},{"term":"Latch state","def":"Maintenance of smooth muscle force by slowly cycling dephosphorylated cross-bridges at low ATP cost."},{"term":"Calcium sensitization","def":"An increase in smooth muscle force at a constant Ca2+ concentration, usually through inhibition of myosin light chain phosphatase."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C01","obj":"MS-7.1","title":"Skeletal excitation-contraction coupling","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A skeletal fiber contracts only when its motor neuron fires. Acetylcholine opens nicotinic cation channels at the end plate, and the resulting depolarization fires a sarcolemmal action potential that lasts only about 1 to 5 ms. Excitation-contraction coupling converts that brief electrical event into a rise in cytosolic Ca2+, and the Ca2+ into force."},{"type":"p","text":"The action potential travels into the fiber along transverse (T) tubules, which in mammalian skeletal muscle lie at each junction of the A and I bands. Each T tubule is flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), forming a triad. The T tubule membrane contains the dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR), an L-type Ca2+ channel (Cav1.1) that here works mainly as a voltage sensor. Its cytoplasmic loop is physically linked to ryanodine receptor 1 (RyR1), the SR release channel. Depolarization moves the DHPR voltage sensors, the conformational change is transmitted directly, and RyR1 opens. Because the link is mechanical, skeletal muscle keeps contracting for a time in Ca2+-free bathing fluid; Ca2+ entry through the DHPR is small and slow and is not needed for contraction."},{"type":"p","text":"Released Ca2+ raises cytosolic concentration about 100-fold or more from its resting level of about 0.1 micromolar. Troponin has three subunits: troponin C (TnC) binds Ca2+, troponin I (TnI) inhibits actin-myosin interaction, and troponin T (TnT) anchors the complex to tropomyosin. Ca2+ binding to TnC pulls TnI off actin, so tropomyosin rolls deeper into the actin groove and uncovers the myosin-binding sites. Cross-bridges then cycle (C02)."},{"type":"p","text":"Relaxation requires active removal of Ca2+. SERCA1, the fast-fiber SR Ca2+-ATPase, pumps two Ca2+ into the SR for each ATP hydrolyzed, and calsequestrin binds Ca2+ inside the SR so the store holds a large amount at a modest free concentration. As cytosolic Ca2+ falls, Ca2+ leaves TnC, tropomyosin covers the binding sites, and tension declines. Relaxation therefore costs ATP just as contraction does."},{"type":"flow","title":"Skeletal excitation-contraction coupling","steps":["Action potential spreads along sarcolemma and down T tubules","DHPR voltage sensors change shape","Mechanical link opens RyR1 in the SR","Ca2+ binds troponin C; tropomyosin uncovers actin","Cross-bridges cycle and generate force","SERCA1 pumps Ca2+ back into the SR; fiber relaxes"],"caption":"No extracellular Ca2+ is required; the voltage signal reaches the SR through protein-to-protein contact."},{"type":"table","title":"Proteins of skeletal coupling and their disorders","cols":["Protein","Location","Role","Disorder when altered"],"rows":[["DHPR (Cav1.1)","T tubule membrane","Voltage sensor linked to RyR1","Hypokalemic periodic paralysis type 1; some malignant hyperthermia"],["RyR1","SR terminal cisternae","Ca2+ release channel","Malignant hyperthermia; central core disease"],["Troponin C","Thin filament","Ca2+ sensor that moves tropomyosin","Not a classic skeletal disorder"],["Calsequestrin","SR lumen","Ca2+ storage buffer","Not a classic skeletal disorder"],["SERCA1","SR membrane of fast fibers","Ca2+ reuptake, relaxation","Brody disease: slow relaxation after exercise"]],"caption":"Each step of coupling has a protein whose variants change contraction or relaxation."},{"type":"p","text":"Malignant hyperthermia shows the system failing. An autosomal dominant RyR1 variant makes the channel open excessively when exposed to volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine. Uncontrolled Ca2+ release causes sustained rigidity, and ATP use by myosin and SERCA produces heat, CO2, and lactate; as fibers break down, K+ and myoglobin leak out (hyperkalemia, rhabdomyolysis). Dantrolene blocks RyR1 release and halts the process, which shows that the defect lies at the SR channel."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Severe hypocalcemia causes muscle cramps and carpopedal spasm. Does this mean skeletal contraction depends on extracellular Ca2+?","explain":"No. Skeletal coupling uses SR Ca2+ released through the mechanical DHPR to RyR1 link. Low extracellular Ca2+ makes nerve membranes more excitable, because voltage-gated Na+ channels open more easily, so motor axons fire spontaneously and drive the spasm.","check":"The DHPR and RyR1 paragraph, last sentence on Ca2+-free fluid."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C02","obj":"MS-7.2","title":"The cross-bridge cycle and rigor mortis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Force comes from myosin II heads on the thick filament pulling the thin actin filaments toward the center of the sarcomere (the M line). The filaments slide past each other without changing length, which is why the band pattern changes the way it does during shortening."},{"type":"table","title":"Sarcomere bands during shortening","cols":["Region","What it contains","Change with shortening"],"rows":[["A band","Full length of the thick filaments","No change"],["I band","Thin filaments only, bisected by the Z line","Narrows"],["H zone","Thick filaments only, center of the A band","Narrows"],["Z line to Z line","One sarcomere","Shortens"]],"caption":"Constant A band width is the key evidence that filaments slide rather than shorten."},{"type":"p","text":"Each myosin head has an actin-binding site and an ATPase site, and the nucleotide in the ATPase site decides how tightly the head holds actin. The cycle is easiest to follow from the rigor state, in which a head with no nucleotide is bound tightly to actin at the end of its stroke. ATP binding lowers the head's affinity for actin, so it detaches. The head then hydrolyzes ATP to ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pi), keeps both products bound, and uses the energy released to re-cock into its high-energy, pre-stroke position. If Ca2+ has uncovered the binding sites, the cocked head binds actin, weakly at first. Release of Pi converts this to strong binding and triggers the power stroke, a lever-arm rotation that moves the thin filament roughly 5 to 10 nm. ADP then leaves, returning the head to rigor until another ATP arrives."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The cross-bridge cycle","steps":["Rigor: nucleotide-free head bound tightly to actin","ATP binds; head releases actin","ATP hydrolyzed; head cocks with ADP and Pi bound","Cocked head binds an exposed actin site","Pi released; power stroke pulls thin filament","ADP released; head returns to rigor"],"caption":"Ca2+ controls whether step 4 can occur; ATP controls whether step 2 can occur."},{"type":"p","text":"ATP binding, not its hydrolysis, causes detachment, and the power stroke is driven by energy stored when the head was cocked and released with Pi. Heads cycle out of step with one another, so some are always attached and tension is smooth. ATP is also consumed by SERCA and the Na+/K+-ATPase. A fiber stores enough ATP for only a second or two of maximal work, so ATP is regenerated from phosphocreatine through creatine kinase, then by glycolysis and oxidative phosphorylation (MS-8)."},{"type":"p","text":"Rigor mortis follows directly from this cycle. After death, ATP production stops, Ca2+ is no longer pumped back into the SR, and heads that bind actin cannot detach without ATP. Muscles stiffen over the first several hours and soften again over roughly the next day or two as proteolytic enzymes degrade the contractile proteins, with timing that depends strongly on temperature. The same principle explains the electrically silent contracture of myophosphorylase deficiency (McArdle disease): during intense exercise, fibers that cannot break down glycogen run short of ATP, cannot release actin or re-sequester Ca2+, and lock in a painful cramp without action potentials on electromyography."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C03","obj":"MS-7.3","title":"Grading force: recruitment, summation, length, and load","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A motor unit is one alpha motor neuron and all the fibers it supplies, all of the same type. Because neuromuscular transmission normally has a large safety factor, one motor neuron action potential makes every fiber in the unit twitch, so force cannot be graded within a unit by weakening the signal. The nervous system grades whole-muscle force by recruiting more units and by firing each unit faster. Muscles needing fine control (extraocular, intrinsic hand) have units of a few fibers; large limb muscles have units of several hundred or more."},{"type":"h","text":"Recruitment and the size principle"},{"type":"p","text":"Units are recruited from smallest to largest. A small motor neuron has less membrane area and therefore higher input resistance, so the same synaptic current produces a larger depolarization (V = IR) and reaches threshold first. Small neurons supply small units of fatigue-resistant type I fibers, and the largest neurons supply type II units that produce high force but fatigue quickly (C06). Posture and light tasks use mainly slow units, and fast units join as demand rises. Because each added unit is larger than the last, each increment of force stays roughly proportional to the force already present, which keeps control precise at every level of effort."},{"type":"h","text":"Summation and tetanus"},{"type":"p","text":"The action potential lasts a few milliseconds, but the twitch lasts tens of milliseconds or longer because Ca2+ release, cycling, and reuptake take time. A second action potential that arrives before relaxation adds force (summation). At high firing rates cytosolic Ca2+ stays elevated, twitches fuse into a smooth tetanus, and force reaches several times twitch force. A single twitch is weaker because Ca2+ is removed before the contractile elements have fully stretched the series elastic components (tendon and other elastic structures in series with the sarcomeres)."},{"type":"h","text":"Length-tension and force-velocity"},{"type":"p","text":"Active tension depends on how many myosin heads can reach actin, which depends on filament overlap. Passive tension, from titin and connective tissue, rises steeply when a relaxed muscle is stretched beyond its resting length; total tension is the sum of the two. Skeletal muscles in the body rest near optimal length, while cardiac muscle works on the ascending limb, so stretch increases its force (Frank-Starling, CV-6)."},{"type":"table","title":"Active force across sarcomere length","cols":["Sarcomere length","Filament overlap","Active force"],"rows":[["About 3.6 micrometers or more","No thick and thin overlap","Zero"],["About 2.2 to 3.6 micrometers","Overlap falls as length rises","Falls roughly linearly"],["About 2.0 to 2.2 micrometers","All heads can reach actin","Maximal (plateau)"],["Below about 2.0 micrometers","Thin filaments overlap each other; thick filaments approach Z lines","Falls, steeply below about 1.65 micrometers"]],"caption":"Values are approximate and derive mainly from classic single-fiber studies."},{"type":"p","text":"Shortening velocity falls as load rises. With no load, velocity is maximal (Vmax), set by the cross-bridge cycling rate of the fiber's myosin isoform. Heavier loads require more heads attached at once, so shortening slows until, at a load equal to maximal isometric force (P0), velocity is zero. A load above P0 stretches the active muscle (eccentric contraction), and force then exceeds P0 because attached heads are pulled backward before detaching. Power (force times velocity) peaks at roughly one-third of Vmax and P0."},{"type":"example","title":"Combining length and load","text":"Why is active force zero at about 3.6 micrometers? Two thin filaments of about 1.0 micrometer and a thick filament of about 1.6 micrometers span that distance, so no head overlaps actin. Between 2.2 and 3.6 micrometers force falls roughly linearly, so at 2.9 micrometers, halfway along that limb, a fiber develops about half its maximal isometric force. Now give that stretched fiber a load equal to 40% of its optimal-length P0. The load is about 80% of what it can generate at 2.9 micrometers, so it shortens slowly. At 2.2 micrometers the same load is only 40% of its P0, so it shortens much faster."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C04","obj":"MS-7.4","title":"Cardiac muscle: calcium-induced calcium release and contractility","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cardiac myocytes share the troponin and tropomyosin switch and the cross-bridge cycle with skeletal fibers, but they couple excitation differently and grade force differently. Gap junctions make the myocardium a functional syncytium, so every cell contracts on every beat; there are no motor units to recruit and no summation. Force is graded instead by how much Ca2+ reaches the myofilaments and how sensitive the filaments are to it."},{"type":"p","text":"During the plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential, L-type Ca2+ channels (Cav1.2) carry an inward Ca2+ current. Unlike skeletal muscle, there is no mechanical link to the SR. The trigger Ca2+ entering through L-type channels binds and opens ryanodine receptor 2 (RyR2), which releases a much larger amount from the SR: calcium-induced calcium release. Because the trigger controls release, anything that changes L-type current or SR Ca2+ content changes the strength of the beat, and cardiac muscle stops contracting within a few beats in Ca2+-free fluid. In human ventricle about 70% of the activating Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR by SERCA2a and most of the rest is extruded by the sarcolemmal Na+/Ca2+ exchanger (NCX, three Na+ in for one Ca2+ out); at steady state, NCX removes as much Ca2+ as entered."},{"type":"diagram","name":"ventricular-ap","caption":"Phases 0 to 4 of the ventricular myocyte action potential; L-type Ca2+ entry during the phase 2 plateau triggers SR release.","img":"notes-web/fig/MS-7-3.png"},{"type":"p","text":"Fast Na+ channels inactivate during the upstroke and cannot recover until phase 3 repolarization. The effective refractory period therefore lasts nearly as long as the action potential, about 250 ms in ventricle, and by then the twitch is well into relaxation. A new action potential cannot arrive until the previous contraction is largely over, so twitches cannot fuse and tetany is impossible. This protects the pump, because a tetanized ventricle could not relax and fill."},{"type":"table","title":"Skeletal versus cardiac coupling","cols":["Feature","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"],"rows":[["T tubule junction","Triad, at the A-I junction","Dyad, at the Z line"],["DHPR to RyR link","Mechanical (DHPR to RyR1)","Ca2+ entry triggers RyR2"],["Needs extracellular Ca2+","No","Yes"],["Main Ca2+ removal","SERCA1","SERCA2a, then NCX"],["Grading of force","Recruitment and summation","Amount of Ca2+ released and myofilament sensitivity"],["Tetany","Possible","Prevented by long refractory period"]],"caption":"The two muscles share troponin C regulation but differ in how voltage releases Ca2+ and how force is graded."},{"type":"p","text":"Sympathetic stimulation increases contractility (inotropy) and speeds relaxation (lusitropy). Norepinephrine acts on beta-1 receptors, and protein kinase A (PKA) phosphorylates three key targets. Phospholamban, a small SR membrane protein, inhibits SERCA2a by lowering its Ca2+ affinity while it is dephosphorylated. PKA phosphorylation removes this brake, so SERCA2a pumps faster: cytosolic Ca2+ falls sooner, preserving diastolic filling time at high heart rates, and the SR stores more Ca2+, so the next release is larger."},{"type":"flow","title":"Sympathetic increase in contractility","steps":["Norepinephrine binds beta-1 receptors","Gs activates adenylyl cyclase; cAMP rises","Protein kinase A is activated","L-type channels phosphorylated: more trigger Ca2+","Phospholamban phosphorylated: SERCA2a disinhibited","Troponin I phosphorylated: Ca2+ leaves TnC faster","Stronger, faster contraction with faster relaxation"],"caption":"More trigger Ca2+ and a fuller SR raise force; faster reuptake and lower TnC affinity speed relaxation."},{"type":"p","text":"Disease alters the same steps: failing myocardium expresses less SERCA2a, so relaxation slows and SR loading falls, and RyR2 variants that leak Ca2+ in diastole cause catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CV-7)."},{"type":"example","title":"Why inhibiting the Na+/K+-ATPase strengthens the beat","text":"Cardiac glycosides partially inhibit the Na+/K+-ATPase, so intracellular Na+ rises slightly. NCX depends on the inward Na+ gradient to push Ca2+ out, and a smaller gradient means less Ca2+ is extruded between beats. More of each beat's Ca2+ is therefore left for SERCA2a to pump into the SR, SR content rises, and the next calcium-induced release is larger, so force increases. SR content, not only trigger size, sets release, and NCX links Na+ handling to contractility."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C05","obj":"MS-7.5","title":"Smooth muscle: MLCK, MLCP, latch, and nitric oxide","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Smooth muscle in vessels, airways, gut, bladder, and uterus has no sarcomeres and no troponin. Thin filaments anchor to cytoplasmic dense bodies and membrane dense plaques, which contain alpha-actinin like the Z line, so cells shorten in several directions and generate force over a wide range of lengths. Regulation is on the thick filament: myosin cannot cycle until its regulatory light chain is phosphorylated."},{"type":"p","text":"Cytosolic Ca2+ rises by two routes. Depolarization by action potentials, slow waves, or stretch opens L-type Ca2+ channels (electromechanical coupling). Agonists on Gq-coupled receptors, such as alpha-1 adrenergic, angiotensin II type 1, and endothelin receptors, activate phospholipase C; IP3 then opens SR IP3 receptors, often with little change in membrane potential (pharmacomechanical coupling). Membrane potential and Ca2+ are graded, so force is graded rather than all-or-none."},{"type":"p","text":"Ca2+ binds calmodulin, and Ca2+-calmodulin activates myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), which phosphorylates the 20 kDa regulatory light chain of myosin and switches on cross-bridge cycling. Myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) removes the phosphate. Force therefore reflects the balance of kinase and phosphatase activity, not Ca2+ alone. Agonists that activate RhoA and Rho kinase phosphorylate the regulatory subunit of MLCP and inhibit it, and protein kinase C inhibits it through the protein CPI-17, so force rises at the same Ca2+ (Ca2+ sensitization). Relaxation needs Ca2+ removal by SERCA, the plasma membrane Ca2+-ATPase, and NCX, which inactivates MLCK, together with MLCP activity."},{"type":"flow","title":"Smooth muscle contraction","steps":["L-type Ca2+ entry or IP3 receptor release from SR","Ca2+ binds calmodulin","Ca2+-calmodulin activates MLCK","MLCK phosphorylates myosin regulatory light chain","Myosin ATPase activates; cross-bridges cycle","MLCP dephosphorylates; force falls or enters latch"],"caption":"Rho kinase inhibition of MLCP raises force without more Ca2+."},{"type":"p","text":"Smooth muscle myosin cycles far more slowly than skeletal myosin. During sustained stimulation, Ca2+ and light chain phosphorylation fall partway, yet force is maintained, because heads dephosphorylated while still attached detach very slowly (latch bridges). The latch state lets vascular and sphincter muscle hold tone for long periods at a small fraction of the ATP that skeletal muscle would spend for the same force."},{"type":"p","text":"Nitric oxide (NO) is the main endothelium-derived relaxing factor. Endothelial NO synthase (eNOS), a Ca2+-calmodulin dependent enzyme, makes NO from L-arginine when agonists such as acetylcholine (on endothelial M3 receptors) and bradykinin raise endothelial Ca2+; shear stress activates it mainly by phosphorylation through Akt. NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase by binding its heme, and the cGMP it produces activates protein kinase G (PKG), which lowers Ca2+ and activates MLCP. Phosphodiesterase 5 ends the signal by hydrolyzing cGMP, so blocking it prolongs relaxation. Without intact endothelium, acetylcholine instead contracts many vessels through smooth muscle muscarinic receptors, and reduced NO availability is the endothelial dysfunction of atherosclerosis, hypertension, and diabetes. A parallel path uses cAMP: beta-2 receptors activate PKA, which lowers Ca2+ and reduces MLCK affinity for Ca2+-calmodulin."},{"type":"flow","title":"Nitric oxide relaxation","steps":["Acetylcholine (via Ca2+) or shear stress activates eNOS","eNOS makes NO from L-arginine","NO diffuses into smooth muscle","NO activates soluble guanylyl cyclase: GTP to cGMP","cGMP activates protein kinase G","PKG lowers Ca2+ and activates MLCP","Light chains dephosphorylate; muscle relaxes"],"caption":"PKG lowers Ca2+ by reducing entry and IP3 release, increasing SR uptake, and opening K+ channels that hyperpolarize the cell."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A compound inhibits MLCP. What happens to force at unchanged Ca2+, and to relaxation after Ca2+ falls?","explain":"Light chain phosphorylation rises, so force increases at the same Ca2+ (sensitization). After Ca2+ falls, MLCK stops, but light chains stay phosphorylated, so relaxation is slow and incomplete.","check":"The MLCK and MLCP paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-07-01-C06","obj":"MS-7.6","title":"Skeletal fiber types and training adaptations","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Skeletal fibers differ chiefly in the myosin heavy chain isoform they express, which sets ATPase rate and shortening velocity, and in how they make ATP. Type I fibers express slow (beta) myosin, the isoform that also predominates in human ventricle. Type II fibers express fast isoforms; human muscle contains type IIa (fast oxidative-glycolytic) and type IIx (fast glycolytic) fibers. All fibers in a motor unit share one type, and most muscles are mosaics whose proportions reflect genetics and use."},{"type":"table","title":"Skeletal muscle fiber types","cols":["Feature","Type I","Type IIa","Type IIx"],"rows":[["Contraction and myosin ATPase","Slow","Fast","Fastest"],["Main ATP source","Oxidative phosphorylation","Oxidative and glycolytic","Glycolysis"],["Mitochondria, capillaries, myoglobin","Many (red)","Moderate to many","Few (white)"],["Glycogen","Low","Intermediate","High"],["Fiber diameter and force","Small, low","Intermediate","Large, high"],["Fatigue","Resistant","Moderately resistant","Rapid"],["Motor unit","Small, recruited first","Intermediate","Large, recruited last"],["Example","Soleus, postural muscles","Mixed limb muscles","Muscles for brief, powerful movement"]],"caption":"Boards often simplify to type I (slow, oxidative, red) versus type II (fast, glycolytic, white)."},{"type":"p","text":"The properties cluster for a reason. A fiber that works continuously must regenerate ATP as fast as it uses it, which only oxidative phosphorylation can sustain, so type I fibers have many mitochondria, dense capillaries, and abundant myoglobin, the red pigment that stores O2 and speeds its diffusion to the mitochondria. Slow myosin also uses less ATP for each unit of force held. Type II fibers generate ATP rapidly from phosphocreatine and glycogen through glycolysis, and an extensive SR with SERCA1 lets them contract and relax quickly. Glycolysis cannot keep pace for long, and accumulating Pi and H+ depress cross-bridge force and SR Ca2+ release, so these fibers fatigue within seconds to minutes."},{"type":"p","text":"The motor neuron largely determines fiber type through its firing pattern: slow neurons fire steadily at low rates, fast neurons in brief high-frequency bursts, and cross-reinnervation experiments show that changing the nerve shifts the fiber toward the new type."},{"type":"table","title":"Training adaptations","cols":["Feature","Endurance training","Resistance training"],"rows":[["Main signals","AMP-activated protein kinase, Ca2+-dependent kinases, PGC-1 alpha","mTORC1 and myofibrillar protein synthesis"],["Mitochondria and oxidative enzymes","Increase markedly","Little change"],["Capillary density","Increases","Little change per fiber"],["Fiber size","Little change","Increases, mostly in type II fibers"],["Fiber type shift","IIx toward IIa; little type II to type I conversion","IIx toward IIa"],["Functional result","Fatigue resistance, more fat oxidation","Greater force and power"]],"caption":"Training mainly remodels existing fibers rather than converting type II fibers to type I."},{"type":"p","text":"Endurance training activates PGC-1 alpha, the coactivator that drives mitochondrial biogenesis, so fibers of every type become more oxidative and fatigue resistant. Resistance training adds myofibrils in parallel, enlarging fiber cross-sectional area, and satellite cells fuse with fibers to supply new nuclei; fiber number changes little in humans. Strength rises in the first weeks largely through neural adaptation (fuller recruitment and higher firing rates) before hypertrophy is measurable."},{"type":"p","text":"These adaptations reverse with disuse. Immobilization and weightlessness atrophy postural, type I rich muscles such as the soleus, whereas aging and glucocorticoid excess preferentially atrophy type II fibers. After denervation, surviving axons sprout and reinnervate orphaned fibers, converting them to their own type, so biopsy shows fiber type grouping, a sign of neurogenic disease (MS-10)."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Muscle contraction ties several disciplines to one sequence. Neuroscience supplies the trigger (motor neuron firing and acetylcholine at the end plate), biochemistry supplies the ATP that both detaches myosin and pumps Ca2+, and genetics explains disorders at single steps: RyR1 variants in malignant hyperthermia and central core disease, SERCA1 loss in Brody disease, myophosphorylase deficiency in McArdle disease, and RyR2 variants in catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. Pathology follows the same logic, since uncontrolled Ca2+ release or ATP failure leads to contracture, heat production, and rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria."},{"type":"p","text":"The three muscle types answer the same problem in different ways. Skeletal muscle needs speed and voluntary grading, so it uses a mechanical voltage link and recruits motor units. The heart must beat reliably and refill, so it uses trigger Ca2+ and a long refractory period and grades force through Ca2+ handling under sympathetic control. Smooth muscle must hold tone cheaply, so it regulates myosin by phosphorylation and uses latch."}],"summary":["In skeletal muscle, T tubule depolarization moves the DHPR, which mechanically opens RyR1; no extracellular Ca2+ is required.","Ca2+ binding to troponin C moves tropomyosin off actin; SERCA pumps Ca2+ back into the SR, so relaxation also costs ATP.","ATP binding detaches myosin, hydrolysis cocks the head, and Pi release drives the power stroke; without ATP, heads stay bound (rigor mortis, McArdle contracture).","Skeletal force is graded by recruitment from small to large motor units (size principle) and by firing rate, from summation to fused tetanus.","Active force is maximal at sarcomere lengths of about 2.0 to 2.2 micrometers; shortening velocity falls as load rises and is zero at maximal isometric force.","In cardiac muscle, L-type Ca2+ entry triggers RyR2 release (calcium-induced calcium release), and SR content plus trigger size set the strength of the beat.","The cardiac refractory period lasts nearly as long as contraction, so the heart cannot tetanize and always relaxes to fill.","Beta-1 stimulation via PKA phosphorylates L-type channels, phospholamban, and troponin I, increasing force and speeding relaxation.","Smooth muscle contracts when Ca2+-calmodulin activates MLCK and relaxes when MLCP dephosphorylates the light chain; latch holds tone cheaply, and NO relaxes through cGMP and PKG.","Type I fibers are slow, oxidative, red, and fatigue resistant; type II fibers are fast, glycolytic, and fatigable; endurance training raises oxidative capacity and resistance training enlarges type II fibers."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Contraction of Skeletal Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation of Skeletal Muscle: Neuromuscular Transmission and Excitation-Contraction Coupling"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Excitation and Contraction of Smooth Muscle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Cardiac Muscle; The Heart as a Pump and Function of the Heart Valves"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Cellular physiology of skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"}]},"MS-8":{"unit":"MS-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Biochemistry and Nutrition","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the chemistry that keeps bone mineralized and muscle supplied with ATP. It traces vitamin D from skin to kidney, explains how PTH, calcitriol, calcitonin, and FGF23 hold serum calcium and phosphate in range, follows muscle fuel use from the first seconds of effort to hours of exercise, and uses McArdle disease and CPT II deficiency to show what happens when one fuel pathway fails. It ends with the minerals and cofactors that bone and muscle depend on. Board questions usually give a lab pattern or an exercise history and ask for the hormone, enzyme, or cofactor that explains it.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and their ATP yields","Mitochondrial beta-oxidation and oxidative phosphorylation","Bone cells and remodeling: osteoblasts, osteocytes, osteoclasts (MS-1)","Nephron segments and proximal tubule transport","Nuclear receptors and G protein coupled receptor signaling"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The proximal tubule is where 1-alpha-hydroxylation occurs and where PTH and FGF23 regulate phosphate reabsorption, so kidney disease disturbs both."},{"to":"Endocrine system (parathyroid and thyroid)","how":"Parathyroid chief cells and thyroid C cells sense ionized calcium and release the hormones that defend it."},{"to":"Metabolic bone disease (rickets, osteomalacia, osteoporosis)","how":"Low calcium times phosphate product impairs mineralization, while a high RANKL to OPG ratio increases resorption."},{"to":"Skeletal muscle physiology (MS-3)","how":"Fiber type determines whether a muscle relies on glycogen or fatty acids, which predicts which metabolic myopathy affects it."},{"to":"Connective tissue biochemistry","how":"Collagen hydroxylation and cross-linking need vitamin C and copper, linking nutrition to bone strength."}],"terms":[{"term":"Calcidiol","def":"25-hydroxyvitamin D, the main circulating and storage form of vitamin D made in the liver."},{"term":"Calcitriol","def":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, the active hormone made mainly in the renal proximal tubule."},{"term":"Calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR)","def":"A Gq coupled receptor on parathyroid and renal cells that detects ionized calcium and suppresses PTH release when calcium is high."},{"term":"RANKL","def":"Receptor activator of nuclear factor kappa B ligand, made by osteoblasts and osteocytes, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors to drive their maturation and activity."},{"term":"Osteoprotegerin (OPG)","def":"A soluble decoy receptor secreted by osteoblasts that binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK."},{"term":"FGF23","def":"Fibroblast growth factor 23, a phosphate-lowering hormone secreted by osteocytes that acts on the kidney with the coreceptor Klotho."},{"term":"Phosphocreatine","def":"A high-energy phosphate store in muscle that creatine kinase uses to regenerate ATP from ADP in one step."},{"term":"Cori cycle","def":"The circuit in which muscle lactate is converted to glucose by hepatic gluconeogenesis and returned to muscle."},{"term":"Myoglobinuria","def":"Myoglobin in the urine after muscle breakdown, giving dark urine that tests positive for heme without red cells."},{"term":"Second wind","def":"Improved exercise tolerance after about 6 to 10 minutes in McArdle disease, as bloodborne glucose and fatty acids replace unavailable muscle glycogen."},{"term":"Carnitine shuttle","def":"The CPT I, translocase, and CPT II system that carries long-chain fatty acids across the inner mitochondrial membrane."},{"term":"Gamma-carboxylation","def":"Vitamin K dependent addition of a second carboxyl group to glutamate residues, creating calcium-binding Gla residues."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C01","obj":"MS-8.1","title":"Vitamin D activation and why 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures status","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D is a prohormone. Neither the form made in skin nor the form absorbed from food binds the vitamin D receptor well, so two hydroxylations in two organs are needed to make the active hormone. Where each step happens, and how tightly it is regulated, explains which blood test reflects body stores."},{"type":"p","text":"In the epidermis, ultraviolet B light (about 290 to 315 nm) opens the B ring of 7-dehydrocholesterol, a cholesterol precursor in the plasma membrane of keratinocytes, to form previtamin D3. Body heat then isomerizes previtamin D3 to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3), which enters the circulation bound to vitamin D binding protein. Prolonged sun exposure cannot cause toxicity, because excess UVB converts previtamin D3 and vitamin D3 into inactive photoproducts. Melanin, high latitude, winter, sunscreen, and age (less 7-dehydrocholesterol in older skin) all reduce synthesis. Diet supplies vitamin D3 from animal foods and ergocalciferol (vitamin D2) from fungi and fortified foods; both are fat soluble and absorbed in chylomicrons, so fat malabsorption causes deficiency."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin D activation","steps":["7-dehydrocholesterol in skin plus UVB","Previtamin D3 isomerizes to cholecalciferol (D3)","Diet adds D2 and D3 via chylomicrons","Liver 25-hydroxylase forms 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Renal proximal tubule 1-alpha-hydroxylase forms calcitriol","Calcitriol binds the nuclear vitamin D receptor","24-hydroxylase inactivates both 25-OH D and calcitriol"],"caption":"The liver step depends on supply; the kidney step is the regulated control point."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver 25-hydroxylase (mainly CYP2R1) is driven by substrate supply and is barely regulated, so 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol) rises and falls with sun exposure and intake. It circulates at high concentration bound to vitamin D binding protein and has a half-life of about 2 to 3 weeks, so a single value integrates supply over recent weeks to months."},{"type":"p","text":"The renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule cells is the control point. PTH and low phosphate increase it; FGF23, high phosphate, and calcitriol itself suppress it, and calcitriol also induces the 24-hydroxylase (CYP24A1) that inactivates both metabolites. Calcitriol circulates at roughly one thousandth the concentration of calcidiol and has a half-life of only hours. In early deficiency, falling calcium absorption raises PTH, which drives 1-alpha-hydroxylase harder, so calcitriol stays normal or even high until stores are severely depleted. That is why serum calcitriol is a poor test of status and serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the correct one."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcitriol acts through the vitamin D receptor, which pairs with the retinoid X receptor and changes gene transcription. In the duodenum it increases the apical calcium channel TRPV6, the carrier protein calbindin, and the basolateral calcium pump, and it raises phosphate absorption. It also suppresses PTH gene transcription. Calcitriol measurement is useful in a different situation: activated macrophages in granulomas (for example sarcoidosis) express 1-alpha-hydroxylase that PTH does not regulate, producing high calcitriol, hypercalcemia, and suppressed PTH. Advanced chronic kidney disease does the opposite, lowering calcitriol despite normal stores."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with low intake and little sun has a low 25-hydroxyvitamin D but a normal calcitriol. Is the normal calcitriol reassuring?","explain":"No. Secondary hyperparathyroidism is driving the remaining substrate through 1-alpha-hydroxylase at a high rate, so the active hormone is defended at the cost of high PTH, phosphate wasting, and bone resorption.","check":"The regulation of 1-alpha-hydroxylase and the half-lives of the two metabolites."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C02","obj":"MS-8.2","title":"Hormonal control of serum calcium and phosphate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Low ionized calcium is sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells, which release PTH within minutes. Total serum calcium is about 8.5 to 10.5 mg/dL (2.1 to 2.6 mmol/L). About 45% is ionized, 40% albumin bound, and the rest complexed to anions. Only ionized calcium (about 4.5 to 5.3 mg/dL, 1.1 to 1.3 mmol/L) is sensed and biologically active. Low albumin lowers total but not ionized calcium; alkalosis increases albumin binding and lowers ionized calcium. Adult serum phosphate is about 2.5 to 4.5 mg/dL (0.8 to 1.45 mmol/L)."},{"type":"p","text":"In the kidney PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule (through the apical channel TRPV5) and thick ascending limb, removes the sodium phosphate cotransporters NaPi-IIa and NaPi-IIc from proximal tubule membranes, and induces 1-alpha-hydroxylase. The phosphaturia matters because calcium released from bone comes with phosphate, which would otherwise complex ionized calcium."},{"type":"p","text":"Mature osteoclasts lack PTH receptors, so PTH acts on bone indirectly. Osteoblasts and osteocytes respond by raising RANKL and lowering osteoprotegerin (OPG). RANKL binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and, with M-CSF, drives their fusion into multinucleated osteoclasts that dissolve mineral with acid and matrix with cathepsin K. OPG, secreted by osteoblasts, is a decoy receptor that binds RANKL and blocks this step, so the RANKL to OPG ratio sets the resorption rate. Estrogen increases OPG, which is why its loss at menopause speeds resorption. Continuous PTH excess favors resorption, whereas intermittent PTH exposure is anabolic."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcitonin, from thyroid parafollicular C cells, is released when calcium is high and binds receptors directly on osteoclasts to inhibit resorption. Its role in adults is minor: neither thyroidectomy nor the high calcitonin of medullary thyroid carcinoma disturbs serum calcium. FGF23 from osteocytes is the phosphate-lowering hormone. High phosphate and calcitriol stimulate it; acting with Klotho, it removes proximal tubule phosphate transporters, suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase, induces 24-hydroxylase, and reduces PTH secretion. Excess FGF23 (X-linked hypophosphatemia, tumor-induced osteomalacia) causes phosphate wasting with low or inappropriately normal calcitriol."},{"type":"table","title":"Hormones that regulate calcium and phosphate","cols":["Hormone","Source and stimulus","Bone","Kidney","Gut","Net serum effect"],"rows":[["PTH","Parathyroid chief cells; low ionized calcium","Resorption via osteoblast RANKL","Ca reabsorption, P excretion, and 1-alpha-hydroxylase up","Indirect, through calcitriol","Ca up, P down"],["Calcitriol","Proximal tubule; PTH, low phosphate","Supplies mineral for mineralization","Minor reabsorption","Ca and P absorption up","Ca up, P up"],["Calcitonin","Thyroid C cells; high calcium","Direct osteoclast inhibition","Minor","None","Ca down slightly"],["FGF23","Osteocytes; high phosphate, calcitriol","Little direct effect","P excretion up; 1-alpha-hydroxylase down","Less absorption via lower calcitriol","P down"]],"caption":"Ca, calcium; P, phosphate. PTH and FGF23 both cause phosphaturia but have opposite effects on calcitriol."},{"type":"table","title":"Lab patterns that test these relationships","cols":["Condition","Calcium","Phosphate","PTH","Calcitriol"],"rows":[["Primary hyperparathyroidism","High","Low","High","High or normal"],["Vitamin D deficiency","Low or normal","Low","High","Normal, high, or low"],["Chronic kidney disease","Low or normal","High","High","Low"],["Hypoparathyroidism","Low","High","Low","Low"],["FGF23 excess (X-linked hypophosphatemia)","Normal","Low","Normal or mildly high","Low or inappropriately normal"]],"caption":"Calcium separates primary from secondary hyperparathyroidism; phosphate separates vitamin D deficiency from kidney disease."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a calcium and phosphate pattern","text":"A patient with chronic kidney disease has calcium 8.2 mg/dL, phosphate 6.1 mg/dL, and a high PTH. Start with phosphate: the failing kidney cannot excrete it, so phosphate and FGF23 rise. FGF23 and lost renal mass lower 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so calcitriol and gut calcium absorption fall. Low calcium, low calcitriol, and high phosphate all stimulate the parathyroid (secondary hyperparathyroidism). In primary hyperparathyroidism, calcium is high and phosphate low, because a healthy kidney responds to PTH by wasting phosphate."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If osteoblasts made no OPG, what would happen to bone mass at normal PTH levels?","explain":"RANKL would act unopposed, so osteoclast formation and resorption would outpace bone formation and bone would be lost early.","check":"The paragraph on how PTH acts on bone through osteoblasts."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C03","obj":"MS-8.3","title":"Muscle fuel use across exercise, the Cori cycle, and creatinine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Muscle can raise its ATP turnover more than a hundredfold, yet its free ATP would last only a few seconds of maximal contraction. It therefore uses three systems that trade speed of ATP supply for capacity. The fastest systems have the smallest capacity, so the fuel mix shifts as exercise continues."},{"type":"table","title":"Energy systems in skeletal muscle","cols":["System","Reaction","ATP yield","Dominant period","Limit"],"rows":[["Phosphagen","Phosphocreatine + ADP to creatine + ATP (creatine kinase)","1 ATP per phosphocreatine","First seconds, up to about 10 s maximal","Small store"],["Anaerobic glycolysis","Glycogen to pyruvate to lactate (lactate dehydrogenase)","3 ATP per glucosyl unit from glycogen; 2 per blood glucose","About 10 s to 2 min of hard effort","Lactate and H+ accumulate; glycogen store"],["Oxidative, carbohydrate","Glucose and glycogen to CO2 via TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation","About 30 to 32 ATP per glucose","Sustained moderate to high intensity","Oxygen delivery; glycogen depletion"],["Oxidative, fat","Fatty acids to acetyl-CoA by beta-oxidation, then TCA cycle","About 106 ATP per palmitate","Prolonged low to moderate intensity, fasting","Slower maximal rate; more oxygen per ATP"]],"caption":"Higher intensity shifts the mix toward carbohydrate; longer duration shifts it toward fat."},{"type":"p","text":"Creatine kinase transfers phosphate from phosphocreatine to ADP in one step, buffering ATP at the start of contraction. Glycogen breakdown begins immediately: calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum activates phosphorylase kinase, AMP allosterically activates glycogen phosphorylase, and epinephrine adds cAMP and protein kinase A. Phosphorylase releases glucose 1-phosphate without spending ATP, so glycogen yields 3 ATP per glucosyl unit anaerobically. Muscle lacks glucose 6-phosphatase, so its glycogen serves only the muscle. When glycolysis outruns mitochondria, lactate dehydrogenase reduces pyruvate to lactate to regenerate NAD+ so glycolysis can continue."},{"type":"p","text":"As exercise continues, AMP-activated protein kinase and contraction move GLUT4 to the membrane without insulin, and adipose lipolysis supplies fatty acids. In prolonged low to moderate exercise and in fasting, especially in oxidative type I fibers, fatty acids become the major fuel, sparing glycogen. Fat yields more ATP per carbon but needs more oxygen per ATP and is oxidized at a slower maximal rate, so very intense effort still depends on carbohydrate."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The Cori cycle","steps":["Muscle glycolysis makes lactate from glucose (+2 ATP)","Lactate enters blood","Liver oxidizes lactate to pyruvate","Hepatic gluconeogenesis makes glucose (uses 6 ATP equivalents)","Glucose returns to muscle"],"caption":"Net cost of about 4 ATP per turn, paid by hepatic fatty acid oxidation; the glucose-alanine cycle also carries amino nitrogen."},{"type":"p","text":"The Cori cycle moves the metabolic burden from working muscle to the liver, which spends 6 high-energy phosphates (4 ATP and 2 GTP) to rebuild one glucose from 2 lactate while muscle gained only 2 ATP. It also explains lactic acidosis when hepatic gluconeogenesis fails or perfusion falls."},{"type":"p","text":"Creatine is made from arginine and glycine in the kidney (guanidinoacetate) and methylated by S-adenosylmethionine in the liver, then taken up by muscle. Creatine and phosphocreatine cyclize nonenzymatically to creatinine at a nearly constant rate of about 1.5 to 2% of the pool per day. Daily creatinine production therefore tracks muscle mass, and creatinine is freely filtered with little tubular secretion, so serum creatinine (about 0.6 to 1.2 mg/dL, 53 to 106 micromol/L) reflects both muscle mass and GFR. A frail older adult can have a normal serum creatinine despite a reduced GFR, and a muscular person a mildly high one with normal GFR. Creatine kinase, unlike creatinine, rises only when damaged muscle leaks it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C04","obj":"MS-8.4","title":"McArdle disease: muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V) is an autosomal recessive deficiency of myophosphorylase, the muscle isoform of glycogen phosphorylase, caused by mutations in the PYGM gene. Liver phosphorylase is a separate isoform and is normal, so hepatic glycogenolysis and fasting blood glucose are preserved. The defect is confined to the one fuel that muscle needs most at the start of intense work."},{"type":"p","text":"Phosphorylase is the first enzyme of glycogenolysis, cleaving glucose 1-phosphate from glycogen chains. Without it, the fiber cannot use its own glycogen, which accumulates modestly in subsarcolemmal deposits that stain with periodic acid-Schiff. At rest and during light activity, muscle runs on bloodborne glucose and fatty acids and the patient feels normal. In the first minutes of brief, intense, or isometric exercise (sprinting, lifting, carrying), demand outruns blood fuel delivery, the phosphocreatine store is exhausted, and ATP falls."},{"type":"flow","title":"From missing myophosphorylase to myoglobinuria","steps":["Intense exercise begins","Muscle glycogen cannot be broken down","Phosphocreatine is spent; ATP falls","Calcium reuptake and ion pumps fail","Contracture and early fatigue","Membrane breakdown releases CK and myoglobin","Dark urine; risk of acute kidney injury"],"caption":"Symptoms begin within minutes of intense exercise, when muscle normally depends on its own glycogen."},{"type":"p","text":"Low ATP explains each sign. Relaxation needs ATP for SERCA to pump calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum and for myosin to release actin, so fibers stay contracted in painful cramps that are electrically silent on electromyography (contractures). Failing Na+/K+-ATPase activity and membrane damage lead to rhabdomyolysis: creatine kinase is often elevated even at rest and rises sharply after attacks, and myoglobin colors the urine red-brown. The urine dipstick is positive for heme while microscopy shows few or no red cells. Filtered myoglobin can injure tubules and cause acute kidney injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Blood lactate fails to rise with exercise because no glucosyl units enter glycolysis from glycogen. In the classic forearm exercise test, lactate stays flat while ammonia still rises, often excessively, because ATP depletion pushes AMP through AMP deaminase in the purine nucleotide cycle. A normal lactate rise argues against a glycogenolytic or glycolytic defect."},{"type":"p","text":"The second wind is a hallmark. After about 6 to 10 minutes of moderate exercise, heart rate falls and the patient can continue, because increased muscle blood flow and hepatic glycogenolysis now deliver glucose, and adipose lipolysis delivers fatty acids. Glycolysis downstream of phosphorylase is intact, so blood glucose can still be used."},{"type":"table","title":"McArdle disease compared with related glycogen disorders","cols":["Feature","McArdle (type V)","Phosphofructokinase deficiency (type VII)","Liver phosphorylase deficiency (type VI)"],"rows":[["Enzyme","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Muscle phosphofructokinase","Liver glycogen phosphorylase"],["Fasting hypoglycemia","No","No","Mild, with hepatomegaly"],["Exercise lactate rise","Absent","Absent","Normal"],["Second wind","Yes","Not typical; blood glucose is also blocked at PFK","Not applicable"],["Other","Contractures, myoglobinuria","Also hemolysis and hyperuricemia","Liver-limited disease"]],"caption":"The step blocked determines whether blood glucose can rescue exercising muscle."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C05","obj":"MS-8.5","title":"Carnitine shuttle defects and CPT II deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Long-chain fatty acids (about 14 to 20 carbons) are oxidized in the mitochondrial matrix, but their acyl-CoA esters cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane. The carnitine shuttle carries them across. Short- and medium-chain fatty acids enter the matrix without carnitine, which is why carnitine shuttle defects spare their use."},{"type":"flow","title":"The carnitine shuttle","steps":["Acyl-CoA synthetase activates fatty acid on outer membrane","CPT I transfers acyl group to carnitine","Translocase moves acylcarnitine into matrix, carnitine out","CPT II regenerates acyl-CoA on inner membrane","Beta-oxidation yields acetyl-CoA, NADH, FADH2"],"caption":"CPT I is the regulated step: malonyl-CoA, made in the fed state, inhibits it."},{"type":"p","text":"CPT I is the rate-limiting step. Malonyl-CoA, produced by acetyl-CoA carboxylase when insulin is high, inhibits CPT I so newly made fatty acids are not oxidized. In fasting and prolonged exercise, glucagon, epinephrine, and AMP-activated protein kinase lower malonyl-CoA and open the shuttle. Skeletal muscle, especially oxidative type I fibers, and heart then depend heavily on fatty acid oxidation."},{"type":"p","text":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency is an autosomal recessive disorder and the most common inherited disorder of fatty acid oxidation affecting skeletal muscle in adults. In the common myopathic form, enough enzyme activity remains to meet normal needs, so patients are well between attacks with normal strength and often normal CK. Attacks come when demand for fatty acid oxidation is high and glycogen is depleted: prolonged exercise, fasting, cold exposure, fever, or infection. The fiber then runs short of ATP, cannot maintain ion gradients and membrane integrity, and breaks down. The result is muscle pain, weakness, and exertional rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria, high CK, and risk of acute kidney injury."},{"type":"p","text":"The timing separates it from McArdle disease. Short intense exercise uses glycogen and is tolerated, there is no second wind, and the forearm exercise test shows a normal lactate rise. Long-chain acylcarnitines (for example C16 and C18:1) build up behind the block and can be detected in plasma. Severe neonatal and infantile forms affect liver and heart and cause hypoketotic hypoglycemia, because the liver cannot make ketones from fatty acids and gluconeogenesis lacks the acetyl-CoA, ATP, and NADH that fatty acid oxidation normally provides."},{"type":"table","title":"Defects of carnitine-dependent fatty acid transport","cols":["Defect","Main tissues","Key features"],"rows":[["CPT II deficiency, myopathic form","Skeletal muscle","Episodic rhabdomyolysis after prolonged exercise, fasting, cold, or illness"],["CPT I deficiency (liver isoform)","Liver","Hypoketotic hypoglycemia with fasting; muscle usually spared"],["Primary carnitine deficiency (carnitine transporter OCTN2)","Heart, muscle, liver","Low plasma carnitine, cardiomyopathy, weakness, hypoketotic hypoglycemia"]],"caption":"Which isoform or transporter is defective determines whether liver, muscle, or heart fails first."},{"type":"example","title":"Distinguishing two exercise-induced myoglobinurias","text":"Patient A develops cramps and dark urine within minutes of lifting heavy boxes, but can keep walking if he slows down for a few minutes. Patient B finishes a 3-hour hike without breakfast, then develops aching weakness and dark urine that evening. A fails early, when muscle relies on its own glycogen, and improves once blood glucose and fatty acids arrive: McArdle disease, with a flat lactate response. B fails late, after glycogen is spent and muscle depends on long-chain fatty acids: CPT II deficiency, with a normal lactate rise and raised long-chain acylcarnitines."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-08-01-C06","obj":"MS-8.6","title":"Minerals and cofactors in bone and muscle","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bone strength comes from two components: a type I collagen scaffold that resists tension and bends without breaking, and a mineral phase that resists compression. By weight, mature bone matrix is roughly two thirds mineral and one third organic material, about 90% of which is type I collagen. Several nutrients act at specific steps in building each component."},{"type":"p","text":"The mineral is calcium hydroxyapatite, Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2, deposited as small crystals in the gaps between collagen fibrils. Bone holds about 99% of body calcium and about 85% of body phosphate. Osteoblasts release matrix vesicles and express tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase, which cleaves pyrophosphate (a crystal inhibitor) and frees phosphate. Mineralization therefore needs an adequate calcium times phosphate product; when either is low, as in vitamin D deficiency or phosphate wasting, unmineralized osteoid accumulates (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults). Fluoride can replace hydroxyl ions to form fluorapatite, which is more resistant to acid."},{"type":"p","text":"Collagen requires vitamin C for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylation inside the cell, and copper for cross-linking outside it. Lysyl oxidase, a copper-dependent extracellular enzyme, oxidatively deaminates selected lysine and hydroxylysine residues to reactive aldehydes, which condense with neighboring residues to form covalent cross-links between collagen molecules (and elastin). Without cross-links, fibrils have little tensile strength. Copper deficiency, including Menkes disease (X-linked ATP7A defect in copper transport), causes osteoporosis, fragile vessels, and lax connective tissue."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin K is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which adds a second carboxyl group to specific glutamate residues to make gamma-carboxyglutamate (Gla). The two adjacent carboxyl groups chelate calcium. Osteocalcin, made by osteoblasts, needs its Gla residues to bind hydroxyapatite; matrix Gla protein uses the same modification to inhibit calcification of soft tissue. The reaction oxidizes vitamin K to its epoxide, and vitamin K epoxide reductase (the enzyme warfarin inhibits) recycles it."},{"type":"p","text":"Magnesium (serum about 1.7 to 2.2 mg/dL, 0.7 to 0.9 mmol/L) is needed for both PTH secretion and PTH action. Mild hypomagnesemia can stimulate PTH, but severe hypomagnesemia (often below about 1 mg/dL) impairs PTH release and causes end-organ resistance, partly because low intracellular magnesium enhances calcium-sensing receptor signaling. The resulting hypocalcemia does not correct with calcium alone until magnesium is restored. Magnesium is also bound to ATP in every kinase and ATPase reaction in muscle."},{"type":"table","title":"Mineral and cofactor roles","cols":["Nutrient","Enzyme or role","Product or process","Deficiency effect"],"rows":[["Calcium and phosphate","Substrates for hydroxyapatite","Bone mineral","Rickets or osteomalacia"],["Vitamin C","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase","Stable collagen triple helix","Scurvy, weak osteoid"],["Copper","Lysyl oxidase","Collagen and elastin cross-links","Osteoporosis, vascular fragility"],["Vitamin K","Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase","Gla residues on osteocalcin and matrix Gla protein","Undercarboxylated osteocalcin"],["Magnesium","PTH secretion and action; Mg-ATP","Calcium homeostasis; muscle energetics","Refractory hypocalcemia"]],"caption":"Vitamin C and copper build the collagen scaffold; calcium, phosphate, and vitamin K govern its mineral."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with chronic diarrhea has low calcium that does not rise with calcium replacement, and PTH is inappropriately low. What should be measured?","explain":"Serum magnesium. Severe hypomagnesemia suppresses PTH release and blunts PTH action, so calcium stays low until magnesium is replaced.","check":"The magnesium paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Calcium balance links four organs through one set of hormones. The skin and liver supply vitamin D according to sun and diet, the kidney decides how much becomes calcitriol and how much phosphate to keep, bone acts as both reservoir and sensor through osteoblasts and osteocytes, and the parathyroid closes the loop. Pathology follows from where the loop breaks: deficient supply gives secondary hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia, renal failure gives high phosphate, high FGF23, and low calcitriol, and granulomas escape PTH control altogether. Biochemistry adds the scaffold side, since vitamin C, copper, and vitamin K act on proteins rather than on mineral, so a normal calcium does not guarantee normal bone."},{"type":"p","text":"In muscle, timing is the diagnostic clue. Brief intense effort depends on phosphocreatine and glycogen, so glycogenolytic and glycolytic defects fail early and show a flat lactate response; prolonged effort and fasting depend on long-chain fatty acids, so carnitine shuttle defects fail late with a normal lactate response. Both end in the same pathology, ATP depletion followed by rhabdomyolysis, and the same renal risk from myoglobin. Creatinine and creatine kinase come from the same muscle chemistry but answer different questions: creatinine reflects muscle mass and filtration, while creatine kinase reflects injury."}],"summary":["Vitamin D needs hepatic 25-hydroxylation (supply driven) and renal 1-alpha-hydroxylation (regulated by PTH, phosphate, FGF23, and calcitriol) to become active.","Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D measures status because it is abundant, long lived, and unregulated; calcitriol can stay normal in deficiency because PTH drives its production.","PTH raises calcium by increasing distal tubular reabsorption, calcitriol synthesis, and osteoblast-mediated osteoclast activation, and lowers phosphate by proximal tubule phosphaturia.","The RANKL to OPG ratio sets bone resorption; OPG is a decoy receptor that limits osteoclast activation.","FGF23 from osteocytes lowers phosphate and calcitriol; calcitonin inhibits osteoclasts directly but has a minor role in adult calcium balance.","Muscle uses phosphocreatine first, then anaerobic glycolysis, then oxidative metabolism, with fat dominating prolonged moderate exercise; the Cori cycle costs about 4 ATP per turn.","Creatinine forms nonenzymatically at a steady rate from creatine, so it reflects muscle mass as well as GFR.","McArdle disease fails early in intense exercise, with contractures, myoglobinuria, flat lactate, and a second wind; CPT II deficiency fails late with prolonged exercise, fasting, or cold.","Bone mineral is calcium hydroxyapatite; copper (lysyl oxidase) and vitamin C build the collagen scaffold, vitamin K carboxylates osteocalcin, and magnesium is needed for PTH secretion and action."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Parathyroid Hormone, Calcitonin, Calcium and Phosphate Metabolism, Vitamin D, Bone, and Teeth"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Sports Physiology"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Endocrine regulation of calcium and phosphate"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Glycogen Metabolism; Fatty Acid, Ketone Body, and Triacylglycerol Metabolism; Vitamins; Fibrous Proteins"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic and Pediatric Diseases: glycogen storage diseases; Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors: metabolic bone disease"}]},"MS-9":{"unit":"MS-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01","title":"Musculoskeletal Genetics","subject":"Musculoskeletal System (MUSC)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains how genes build bone, cartilage, and muscle, and how particular kinds of mutation produce particular diseases. It covers the transcription factors that direct skeletal development, the receptor gain of function behind achondroplasia, dominant negative collagen defects, the dystrophin reading frame, repeat expansion, mitochondrial inheritance, and the tumor suppressor and fusion gene changes that drive bone sarcomas. Board questions rarely ask you to recall a gene in isolation. They describe a family, a mutation type, or a biopsy and ask you to predict severity, inheritance, or the protein defect, so the mechanism is what must be learned.","prerequisites":["Transcription, RNA splicing, the triplet genetic code, and nonsense-mediated mRNA decay","Mendelian inheritance patterns and pedigree analysis","Endochondral and intramembranous ossification and the growth plate zones (MS-1)","Type I collagen synthesis and post-translational modification","Skeletal muscle structure, the sarcolemma, and excitation-contraction coupling"],"connections":[{"to":"Musculoskeletal embryology (MS-1)","how":"RUNX2, SOX9, and HOX genes are the molecular switches behind the ossification and limb patterning events described there."},{"to":"Biochemistry of collagen","how":"The Gly-X-Y repeat and hydroxylation steps of collagen synthesis explain why one glycine substitution can cripple the whole triple helix."},{"to":"Cardiovascular conduction (CV-7)","how":"Myotonic dystrophy and the dystrophinopathies damage the heart, causing conduction block and dilated cardiomyopathy."},{"to":"Neoplasia and cell cycle (general pathology)","how":"RB1 and TP53 are the model tumor suppressors of the two-hit hypothesis, and EWSR1-FLI1 is a model oncogenic fusion."},{"to":"Neurology","how":"Mitochondrial disorders such as MELAS and MERRF affect brain and muscle together because both depend heavily on oxidative phosphorylation."}],"terms":[{"term":"Haploinsufficiency","def":"Disease caused because one working copy of a gene cannot make enough product for normal function."},{"term":"Gain of function","def":"A mutation that makes a protein more active, active at the wrong time, or able to do something new."},{"term":"Dominant negative","def":"A mutation whose abnormal product interferes with the normal product of the other allele, often by joining the same multimeric complex."},{"term":"Null allele","def":"An allele that produces no functional protein, often because a premature stop codon triggers mRNA decay."},{"term":"Reading frame","def":"The grouping of mRNA nucleotides into consecutive triplet codons, set by the start codon."},{"term":"Anticipation","def":"Earlier onset or greater severity of a disorder in successive generations, usually because an unstable repeat expands."},{"term":"Heteroplasmy","def":"The presence of both normal and mutant mitochondrial DNA within one cell or tissue."},{"term":"Threshold effect","def":"The principle that a mitochondrial disorder appears in a tissue only when the fraction of mutant mtDNA exceeds the level that tissue can tolerate."},{"term":"Two-hit hypothesis","def":"Knudson's model in which both alleles of a tumor suppressor gene must be inactivated before a cell becomes neoplastic."},{"term":"Loss of heterozygosity","def":"Loss of the remaining normal allele in a cell that already carries one mutant allele, a common second hit."},{"term":"Fusion gene","def":"A hybrid gene formed when a chromosomal translocation joins parts of two genes, producing a chimeric protein."},{"term":"Rhizomelia","def":"Disproportionate shortening of the proximal limb segments, the humerus and femur."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C01","obj":"MS-9.1","title":"Transcription factors that build the skeleton: RUNX2, SOX9, and HOX genes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every skeletal cell type arises from mesenchyme, and the choice between becoming a chondrocyte or an osteoblast is made by transcription factors, proteins that bind DNA and switch on whole programs of target genes. Because they sit at the top of these cascades, a modest drop in their dose can change an entire tissue. This explains why many skeletal dysplasias are autosomal dominant through haploinsufficiency rather than through an abnormal protein."},{"type":"h","text":"RUNX2 and the osteoblast"},{"type":"p","text":"RUNX2 (also called CBFA1) is the master regulator of osteoblast differentiation. It commits mesenchymal cells to the osteoblast lineage, induces the downstream factor osterix (SP7), and activates bone matrix genes such as type I collagen and osteocalcin. It is required for both forms of ossification, and in the growth plate it also drives chondrocyte hypertrophy. Mice lacking both copies form a cartilage skeleton with no bone at all."},{"type":"p","text":"In humans, losing one copy causes cleidocranial dysplasia, an autosomal dominant disorder. With half the RUNX2 dose, bones that ossify directly from mesenchyme are hit hardest. The clavicles, which ossify largely by the intramembranous route, are hypoplastic or absent, so affected people can bring their shoulders together in front of the chest. The skull vault ossifies late, leaving wide sutures, open fontanelles, and extra (wormian) bones. RUNX2 also acts in the dental lamina, so primary teeth are retained, permanent teeth erupt late, and supernumerary teeth are common. Short stature and delayed ossification of the pubic bones complete the picture."},{"type":"h","text":"SOX9 and the chondrocyte"},{"type":"p","text":"SOX9 is the corresponding switch for cartilage. With SOX5 and SOX6 it turns condensing mesenchyme into chondrocytes and activates type II collagen (COL2A1) and aggrecan. It keeps chondrocytes proliferating and restrains their hypertrophy, opposing RUNX2 within the growth plate. Haploinsufficiency causes campomelic dysplasia, with bowed long bones and a small chest. SOX9 is also required downstream of SRY for Sertoli cell differentiation, so many 46,XY infants with campomelic dysplasia have female or ambiguous genitalia."},{"type":"h","text":"HOX genes and segment identity"},{"type":"p","text":"HOX genes encode homeodomain transcription factors arranged in four clusters (HOXA to HOXD, 39 genes). Genes are expressed along the head to tail axis in the same order as they lie on the chromosome, a property called colinearity, so each segment carries a combinatorial HOX code that tells it what to become. A HOX mutation does not change tissue type; it changes position or identity, for example a vertebra with the form of its neighbor or a cervical rib. In the limb, HOXD13 mutations cause synpolydactyly and HOXA13 mutations cause hand-foot-genital syndrome. Retinoic acid excess shifts HOX expression, which helps explain retinoid embryopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Skeletal transcription factors","cols":["Factor","Normal job","Effect of loss","Example disorder"],"rows":[["RUNX2","Commits mesenchyme to osteoblasts; drives chondrocyte hypertrophy","Poor ossification, worst in membranous bone and teeth","Cleidocranial dysplasia (autosomal dominant)"],["SOX9","Commits mesenchyme to chondrocytes; type II collagen and aggrecan","Defective cartilage templates; failed testis development","Campomelic dysplasia, often with 46,XY sex reversal"],["HOX genes","Assign positional identity along the body axis and limb","Structures form in the wrong number or identity","Synpolydactyly (HOXD13); hand-foot-genital syndrome (HOXA13)"]],"caption":"Dose-sensitive transcription factors make haploinsufficiency a common dominant mechanism in skeletal disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C02","obj":"MS-9.2","title":"Achondroplasia: FGFR3 gain of function and the paternal age effect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Achondroplasia is the most common skeletal dysplasia causing disproportionate short stature, occurring in about 1 in 25,000 births. It is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3) on chromosome 4p. FGFR3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase on growth plate chondrocytes, and its normal role is a brake: when FGF binds, the receptor dimerizes, autophosphorylates, and signals through STAT1 and the MAPK (ERK) pathway to slow chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"About 98% of cases carry the same substitution, glycine to arginine at position 380 (G380R) in the transmembrane domain. The mutant receptor signals without enough ligand and escapes normal downregulation, so the brake is pressed constantly. The proliferative and hypertrophic zones of every growth plate are thin, and bones that lengthen through a cartilage model stay short."},{"type":"flow","title":"From FGFR3 mutation to short limbs","steps":["G380R mutation in FGFR3 transmembrane domain","Receptor signals without adequate ligand","STAT1 and MAPK pathways persistently active","Chondrocyte proliferation and hypertrophy suppressed","Thin proliferative and hypertrophic zones","Short endochondral bones; membranous bone spared"],"caption":"The mutation exaggerates a normal growth-limiting signal rather than creating a new function."},{"type":"p","text":"The phenotype follows the type of ossification. Long bones are short, worst in the proximal segments (rhizomelia), and the hands are short with a gap between the middle and ring fingers (trident hand). The skull base forms in cartilage, so the foramen magnum is small and can compress the cervicomedullary junction, and a narrow spinal canal can cause stenosis in adults. The skull vault and facial bones grow by membranous ossification and are unaffected, so the head is large with frontal bossing beside a flat midface. The trunk is near normal in length, and intelligence is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"The inheritance is autosomal dominant with complete penetrance, but about 80% of affected children are born to parents of average stature, so most cases are new mutations. These arise almost entirely on the paternal allele, and their frequency rises with the father's age. Spermatogonial stem cells divide continuously from puberty onward, giving many replication cycles, and the G380R mutation also gives the spermatogonia that carry it a growth advantage, so mutant clones expand within the testis over time. Oocytes complete their mitotic divisions before birth, so maternal age has no such effect. Homozygosity for the mutation is usually lethal soon after birth, and more severe FGFR3 mutations cause thanatophoric dysplasia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people with achondroplasia plan a family. What are the outcomes for each pregnancy?","explain":"Each parent passes the mutant allele half the time, so 25% of children are unaffected, 50% are heterozygous with achondroplasia, and 25% are homozygous, a usually lethal severe form.","check":"The inheritance paragraph and the dominant gain-of-function mechanism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C03","obj":"MS-9.3","title":"Osteogenesis imperfecta: dominant negative versus null collagen alleles","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Type I collagen gives bone, skin, tendon, sclera, dentin, and the ossicles their tensile strength. Each molecule is a triple helix of two pro-alpha-1(I) chains, encoded by COL1A1 on chromosome 17, and one pro-alpha-2(I) chain, encoded by COL1A2 on chromosome 7. Every chain has the repeating sequence Gly-X-Y, where X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline. Glycine has only a hydrogen side chain, and it is the only residue small enough to sit in the crowded center of the helix where the three chains meet. The helix also folds like a zipper from the C-terminal end toward the N-terminal end."},{"type":"p","text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI) is a group of disorders of brittle bone, most of them autosomal dominant mutations in these two genes. The severity depends far more on the type of mutation than on which gene is hit, because what matters is what reaches the triple helix."},{"type":"p","text":"A null allele, usually a premature stop codon in COL1A1 that triggers nonsense-mediated mRNA decay, makes no chain at all. Every collagen molecule assembled is normal, but there is only about half as much. This quantitative defect causes OI type I, the mildest and most common form."},{"type":"p","text":"A glycine substitution makes a chain that is synthesized and incorporated into helices. The bulky residue blocks the zipper at that point, so folding stalls. While the chains wait unfolded in the endoplasmic reticulum, hydroxylases and glycosyltransferases keep modifying them, and the overmodified molecules are retained, degraded, or secreted as abnormal fibrils that mineralize poorly. Because the mutant chain spoils normal chains that join it, this is a dominant negative effect. Substitutions near the C-terminal end usually disrupt more of the helix and are more severe. These qualitative defects cause types II, III, and IV."},{"type":"flow","title":"How one glycine substitution poisons collagen","steps":["Mutant pro-alpha chain made at normal rate","Chain joins helices with normal partner chains","Bulky residue stalls C to N zipper folding","Unfolded chains overmodified in the ER","Abnormal molecules degraded or form weak fibrils","Most collagen abnormal: severe brittle bone"],"caption":"A mutant chain that is made is worse than a chain that is missing."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a COL1A1 substitution is worse than a null allele","text":"In a COL1A1 glycine substitution, half of all alpha-1 chains are mutant. Each molecule takes two alpha-1 chains at random, so the chance that both are normal is 1/2 times 1/2, or 1/4. Only about one quarter of molecules are normal and three quarters are abnormal. A COL1A2 substitution affects the single alpha-2 chain, so half the molecules are abnormal, which is one reason COL1A2 substitutions are on average milder. A COL1A1 null allele makes only half the alpha-1 chains, so half the usual number of molecules form, but all of them are normal. Half of normal collagen is compatible with mild disease; three quarters abnormal collagen disrupts the whole matrix."},{"type":"table","title":"Main types of osteogenesis imperfecta (Sillence classification)","cols":["Type","Usual defect","Severity","Key features"],"rows":[["I","COL1A1 null allele (quantitative)","Mild","Fractures in childhood, blue sclerae, hearing loss often from early adulthood, near normal stature"],["II","Glycine substitution (qualitative)","Perinatal lethal","Multiple intrauterine fractures, beaded ribs, small chest with respiratory failure"],["III","Glycine substitution (qualitative)","Severe, progressive deforming","Many fractures, bowing, very short stature, triangular face, dentinogenesis imperfecta"],["IV","Glycine substitution (qualitative)","Moderate","Fractures and bowing, normal or near normal sclerae, variable dentinogenesis imperfecta"]],"caption":"Blue sclerae occur because thin, collagen-poor sclera lets the underlying choroid show through."},{"type":"p","text":"Less often, OI is autosomal recessive, caused by mutations in genes for the enzymes and chaperones that modify and fold type I collagen. Because fractures with little trauma raise the question of child abuse, the history, sclerae, teeth, and family pattern matter when interpreting fractures in a child."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C04","obj":"MS-9.4","title":"Dystrophin and the reading frame rule: Duchenne and Becker","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dystrophin is a large cytoskeletal protein on the inner face of the sarcolemma. Its N-terminal end binds actin, a long central rod domain acts as a flexible spacer, and its C-terminal region binds beta-dystroglycan in the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex. Through alpha-dystroglycan, which binds laminin in the basement membrane, the complex links the contractile apparatus to the extracellular matrix and spreads the mechanical stress of contraction across the membrane."},{"type":"flow","title":"From frameshift to muscle loss in Duchenne","steps":["Out-of-frame deletion in DMD","Premature stop codon downstream","Nonsense-mediated decay; no dystrophin","Sarcolemma tears during contraction","Ca2+ influx, protease activation, fiber necrosis","Regeneration fails; fat and fibrous tissue replace muscle"],"caption":"Creatine kinase leaks from damaged fibers and is markedly elevated from early childhood."},{"type":"p","text":"The DMD gene at Xp21 is the largest known human gene, about 2.2 million base pairs with 79 exons. About two thirds of mutations are exon deletions clustered in a central hot spot; most others are duplications and point mutations. Disease severity is predicted by the reading frame rule, which holds in about 90% of cases. Ribosomes read mRNA in consecutive triplets from the start codon. If the deletion removes a number of coding nucleotides that is not a multiple of three, every downstream codon is misread and a premature stop codon soon appears, so no functional dystrophin is made. This is Duchenne muscular dystrophy. If the number is a multiple of three, the frame is preserved: a shorter protein lacking part of the rod domain is made, but it keeps the actin-binding and dystroglycan-binding ends and works partly. This is Becker muscular dystrophy."},{"type":"example","title":"Applying the reading frame rule","text":"Boy A has a deletion removing 1,000 coding nucleotides; boy B has one removing 3,600. Divide by three. For A, 1,000 / 3 = 333 with a remainder of 1, so the frame shifts by one nucleotide, a stop codon follows, and no dystrophin is made: Duchenne. For B, 3,600 / 3 = 1,200 exactly, so 1,200 amino acids are lost from the rod domain but the frame and both functional ends are kept: Becker, despite the larger deletion. The rule uses nucleotides in the mature mRNA, so a whole-exon deletion keeps the frame only when the exons on either side join in phase."},{"type":"table","title":"Duchenne and Becker compared","cols":["Feature","Duchenne","Becker"],"rows":[["Usual mutation","Out-of-frame deletion, nonsense mutation","In-frame deletion"],["Dystrophin","Absent","Reduced amount or shortened protein"],["Onset","Early childhood, usually by about 5 years","Later childhood to adulthood"],["Course","Loss of walking by early teens; death from respiratory or cardiac failure, often in the 20s or 30s","Slower; walking usually kept past 16"],["Shared features","Proximal weakness, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, high CK, dilated cardiomyopathy","Same pattern, milder; cardiomyopathy may dominate"]],"caption":"Both are X-linked recessive and both involve the heart; the difference lies in how much working dystrophin is made."},{"type":"p","text":"Gowers sign, pushing on the thighs to stand from the floor, reflects pelvic girdle weakness. Calf pseudohypertrophy is enlargement from fat and connective tissue replacing muscle, not true hypertrophy. Female carriers are usually unaffected, but skewed X inactivation can produce manifesting carriers with weakness or cardiomyopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Duchenne affects about 1 in 3,500 to 5,000 male births, and about one third of cases are new mutations. The huge gene is a large target, so its mutation rate per generation is among the highest known, and affected males rarely reproduce, so each lost allele must be replaced by new mutation to keep the frequency steady; for an X-linked lethal disorder the expected share of new cases at equilibrium is about one third. Mothers of an isolated case may also carry germline mosaicism, so recurrence risk is not zero even when maternal blood testing is normal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C05","obj":"MS-9.5","title":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1: CTG repeat expansion and toxic RNA","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1) is the most common muscular dystrophy beginning in adulthood. It is autosomal dominant and caused by expansion of a CTG trinucleotide repeat in the 3' untranslated region of the DMPK gene on chromosome 19q. Normal alleles carry about 5 to 34 repeats, alleles of about 35 to 49 are unstable but cause no symptoms, and about 50 or more cause disease. Larger expansions give earlier onset and more severe disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the repeat lies outside the coding sequence, the main problem is not a missing DMPK protein but the RNA itself, a toxic RNA gain of function. The expanded CUG repeat folds into hairpins that stay in the nucleus as foci and trap the splicing regulator muscleblind-like 1 (MBNL1), while another regulator, CELF1, becomes overactive. Many transcripts then revert to fetal splice forms, and the protein products work poorly in adult tissue. This one mechanism explains why the disease reaches muscle, heart, lens, testis, and endocrine tissue at once."},{"type":"flow","title":"From CTG expansion to multisystem disease","steps":["Expanded CTG repeat in DMPK 3' UTR","CUG repeat RNA forms nuclear hairpin foci","MBNL1 sequestered; CELF1 overactive","Fetal splice forms persist in adult tissues","Mis-spliced chloride channel, insulin receptor, cardiac proteins","Myotonia, insulin resistance, conduction block, weakness"],"caption":"One toxic RNA disturbs splicing of many unrelated genes."},{"type":"p","text":"Myotonia is delayed relaxation after voluntary contraction, seen as a slow release of a handgrip or a sustained dimple after percussion of the thenar eminence. It arises because mis-splicing of the skeletal muscle chloride channel ClC-1 lowers chloride conductance, which normally provides most of the resting conductance of the fiber. Without that stabilizing current, K+ that accumulates in the T-tubules during activity depolarizes the membrane, and the fiber keeps firing after the nerve stops."},{"type":"table","title":"Features of DM1 and their basis","cols":["Feature","Basis"],"rows":[["Myotonia, worse in hands","Reduced ClC-1 chloride conductance"],["Distal weakness of hands and feet, facial and neck weakness, ptosis, long thin face","Muscle fiber atrophy and degeneration; distal pattern unlike most dystrophies"],["Cardiac conduction defects (prolonged PR, AV block, arrhythmia, sudden death)","Degeneration and fibrosis of the conduction system with mis-spliced cardiac transcripts"],["Posterior subcapsular cataracts, often early and iridescent","Lens epithelial involvement"],["Frontal balding","Hair follicle involvement"],["Testicular atrophy with infertility","Primary hypogonadism"],["Insulin resistance","Mis-splicing toward the less active fetal insulin receptor isoform"]],"caption":"Distal weakness with myotonia, cataracts, and a conduction defect is the classic DM1 pattern."},{"type":"p","text":"The repeat is unstable in meiosis and tends to grow from parent to child, so onset becomes earlier and disease more severe in later generations. This is anticipation. The largest expansions, often over 1,000 repeats, arise almost entirely in the female germline, so congenital DM1, with severe hypotonia, respiratory failure, feeding difficulty, and intellectual disability, is nearly always inherited from the mother, who may be mildly affected and unaware of her diagnosis. Myotonic dystrophy type 2, a CCTG expansion in CNBP, is milder, mainly proximal, and shows no congenital form."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A floppy newborn needs ventilation, and testing shows a very large CTG expansion in DMPK. Which parent most likely transmitted it, and what might you find on examining that parent?","explain":"The mother. Massive expansions arise mainly in female meiosis. She may have mild myotonia, distal hand weakness, early cataracts, or a prolonged PR interval she has never had evaluated.","check":"The anticipation paragraph and the features table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C06","obj":"MS-9.6","title":"Mitochondrial myopathies: maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy, and ragged red fibers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each mitochondrion carries several copies of a small circular DNA of about 16,600 base pairs. It encodes 37 genes: 13 polypeptides of the oxidative phosphorylation complexes, 22 transfer RNAs, and 2 ribosomal RNAs. All other mitochondrial proteins, including most respiratory chain subunits, are nuclear encoded. A mutation in an mtDNA tRNA gene impairs translation of all 13 mtDNA-encoded proteins, so ATP production fails broadly."},{"type":"p","text":"Mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother. The oocyte contributes hundreds of thousands of mtDNA copies, while the few sperm mitochondria are destroyed after fertilization. An affected mother can therefore pass the disorder to all her children of either sex, and an affected father passes it to none."},{"type":"p","text":"Heteroplasmy explains why that transmission is so variable. Cells contain a mixture of normal and mutant mtDNA, and at each cell division mitochondria are distributed to daughter cells at random, so the proportion drifts between cell lines and tissues. During oogenesis only a small number of mtDNA molecules populate each developing egg, a genetic bottleneck, so siblings can inherit very different mutant loads from the same mother. Disease appears in a tissue only when the mutant fraction exceeds its threshold, and tissues with the highest dependence on oxidative phosphorylation, skeletal muscle, brain, heart, retina, and cochlea, cross it first. With oxidative phosphorylation blocked, NADH accumulates and pyruvate is converted to lactate, producing lactic acidosis that worsens with exercise."},{"type":"table","title":"Mitochondrial inheritance compared with Mendelian patterns","cols":["Pattern","Transmission","Severity within a family"],"rows":[["Autosomal dominant","Either parent; about 50% of children","Varies with expressivity and penetrance"],["X-linked recessive","Carrier mother to sons; no father to son transmission","Affected males similar"],["Mitochondrial","Mother to all children; never through the father","Highly variable because of heteroplasmy and threshold"]],"caption":"Many respiratory chain disorders are caused by nuclear genes and follow Mendelian rules; only mtDNA mutations show maternal inheritance."},{"type":"p","text":"The muscle biopsy shows the compensation. Muscle fibers that cannot make ATP proliferate mitochondria, which collect beneath the sarcolemma. With the modified Gomori trichrome stain these subsarcolemmal clumps stain red against blue-green myofibrils, giving the irregular ragged red fiber. Electron microscopy shows enlarged mitochondria with abnormal cristae and paracrystalline inclusions. Ragged red fibers point to a mitochondrial disorder but are not specific to one syndrome."},{"type":"table","title":"Two classic syndromes with ragged red fibers","cols":["Syndrome","Common mutation","Main features"],"rows":[["MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers)","m.8344A>G in the tRNA for lysine (about 80%)","Myoclonus, generalized seizures, ataxia, myopathy, hearing loss"],["MELAS (mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes)","m.3243A>G in the tRNA for leucine (about 80%)","Stroke-like episodes before 40 not confined to arterial territories, lactic acidosis, seizures, headache, short stature, diabetes, hearing loss"]],"caption":"Both are tRNA mutations, so translation of all mtDNA-encoded respiratory chain subunits suffers."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with MELAS asks about risk to his children, and his sister, also carrying the mutation, asks about hers. How do their risks differ?","explain":"The man cannot transmit mtDNA, so his children are not at risk from him. His sister can pass the mutation to any child, but the mutant load each child receives is unpredictable because of the oocyte bottleneck, so severity can range from none to severe.","check":"The maternal inheritance and heteroplasmy paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-MUSC-09-01-C07","obj":"MS-9.7","title":"Genetics of bone and soft tissue sarcomas: RB1, TP53, and EWSR1-FLI1","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sarcomas show the two main routes to cancer. Tumor suppressor genes cause disease through loss of function, so both alleles must be inactivated in a cell. Oncogenes cause disease through gain of function, so one altered allele is enough. Both RB1 and TP53 guard the G1 to S transition, which is why their loss is so common in osteosarcoma, a tumor of rapidly dividing bone-forming cells."},{"type":"h","text":"RB1 and osteosarcoma"},{"type":"p","text":"The RB protein, encoded by RB1 on chromosome 13q14, holds cells in G1. When hypophosphorylated, it binds the transcription factor E2F and blocks transcription of the genes needed for DNA synthesis. Growth signals raise cyclin D, and cyclin D with CDK4 or CDK6 phosphorylates RB, which releases E2F and lets the cell enter S phase. Without RB, the checkpoint is open permanently."},{"type":"cycle","title":"RB control of the G1 to S checkpoint","steps":["Hypophosphorylated RB binds E2F; cell held in G1","Growth signals raise cyclin D","Cyclin D with CDK4 or CDK6 phosphorylates RB","E2F released; S phase genes transcribed","Cell divides; RB dephosphorylated in the next G1"],"caption":"Loss of RB, or of CDK inhibitors such as p21 and p16, removes this brake."},{"type":"p","text":"Knudson's two-hit hypothesis came from retinoblastoma. In the hereditary form, a germline RB1 mutation is present in every cell, so only one somatic hit, often loss of heterozygosity, is needed. Tumors appear earlier, are often bilateral, and recur in other tissues: survivors have a markedly increased risk of osteosarcoma in adolescence, both within and outside radiation fields, and of soft tissue sarcomas and melanoma. Predisposition is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait even though the gene acts recessively in each cell."},{"type":"h","text":"TP53 and Li-Fraumeni syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"p53, encoded by TP53 on 17p13, senses DNA damage. Normally MDM2 keeps it low by targeting it for degradation; damage activates ATM and ATR kinases, which stabilize p53. p53 then induces p21, a CDK inhibitor that blocks RB phosphorylation and arrests the cell in G1 for repair, or triggers apoptosis through BAX and related proteins when damage is beyond repair. A germline TP53 mutation causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome, an autosomal dominant condition with a very high lifetime cancer risk at young ages: soft tissue sarcoma and osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, adrenocortical carcinoma, and leukemia. Several different early cancers in one family suggest the diagnosis."},{"type":"h","text":"EWSR1-FLI1 and Ewing sarcoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Ewing sarcoma is driven instead by a gain of function. In about 85% of cases the translocation t(11;22)(q24;q12) joins the 5' part of EWSR1 on chromosome 22 to the 3' part of FLI1 on chromosome 11. The fusion protein combines the strong transactivation domain of EWS with the ETS DNA-binding domain of FLI1, making an abnormal transcription factor that binds GGAA microsatellite repeats and switches on a growth program while repressing differentiation genes. A smaller share of tumors carry other EWSR1 fusions, such as EWSR1-ERG."},{"type":"table","title":"Osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma compared","cols":["Feature","Osteosarcoma","Ewing sarcoma"],"rows":[["Genetic basis","Loss of RB1 and TP53 pathways (tumor suppressors)","EWSR1-FLI1 fusion from t(11;22) (oncogenic transcription factor)"],["Usual age","Adolescents during the growth spurt; second peak in older adults, often with Paget disease","Children and adolescents, usually 10 to 20 years"],["Usual site","Metaphysis of long bones, especially around the knee","Diaphysis or metadiaphysis of long bones and the pelvis"],["Imaging","Codman triangle and sunburst periosteal reaction","Lytic lesion with onion-skin layered periosteal reaction"],["Histology","Malignant cells producing osteoid","Sheets of small round blue cells with membranous CD99"]],"caption":"Ewing sarcoma may cause fever and a high ESR, mimicking osteomyelitis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The common thread is that the type of mutation predicts the disease better than the name of the gene. Loss of one copy of a dose-sensitive transcription factor (RUNX2, SOX9) gives haploinsufficiency. Overactivity of a growth-limiting receptor (FGFR3) gives a dominant gain of function. An abnormal subunit of a multimeric structure (collagen) gives a dominant negative effect that is worse than having no subunit at all. In dystrophin the reading frame, not deletion size, decides whether any protein is made. In DM1 the gene product barely matters because the RNA itself is toxic, and in mitochondrial disease the dose of mutant DNA varies cell by cell. Sarcomas repeat the pattern at the level of the cell: tumor suppressors need two hits, while a fusion oncogene needs one event."},{"type":"p","text":"These mechanisms link anatomy, biochemistry, and pathology. Membranous versus endochondral ossification explains which bones suffer in cleidocranial dysplasia and achondroplasia; collagen folding explains OI; membrane mechanics and Ca2+ entry explain dystrophic necrosis; splicing and ion channel physiology explain myotonia; and oxidative phosphorylation explains lactic acidosis and ragged red fibers. The limits matter too: the reading frame rule has exceptions, heteroplasmy measured in blood may not reflect muscle, and the same syndrome can arise from several genes, so a phenotype points to a mechanism before it points to a single mutation."}],"summary":["RUNX2 drives osteoblast differentiation; its haploinsufficiency causes cleidocranial dysplasia with absent clavicles, open fontanelles, delayed suture closure, and extra teeth.","SOX9 drives chondrocyte differentiation (campomelic dysplasia, often with 46,XY sex reversal), and HOX genes assign segment identity in colinear order.","Achondroplasia is FGFR3 gain of function (usually G380R) that suppresses growth plate chondrocytes; endochondral bone is short while membranous bone is spared.","About 80% of achondroplasia is new mutation on the paternal allele, rising with paternal age through continuous spermatogonial division and clonal advantage.","In OI, a glycine substitution acts dominant negatively (only about 1 in 4 molecules normal with COL1A1), causing types II to IV; a null allele halves normal collagen, causing mild type I.","Out-of-frame DMD deletions abolish dystrophin (Duchenne); in-frame deletions leave a shortened, partly functional protein (Becker), whatever the deletion size.","The DMD gene is the largest human gene, so its mutation rate is high and about one third of Duchenne cases are new mutations.","DM1 is an autosomal dominant CTG expansion in DMPK whose toxic RNA disrupts splicing, causing myotonia, distal weakness, cataracts, balding, testicular atrophy, and conduction block, with maternal transmission of the congenital form.","mtDNA disorders pass only from mother to children; heteroplasmy, the oocyte bottleneck, and tissue thresholds make severity vary, and ragged red fibers appear on modified Gomori trichrome in MERRF and MELAS.","Germline RB1 loss raises osteosarcoma risk, germline TP53 loss causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome, and t(11;22) creates the EWSR1-FLI1 transcription factor of Ewing sarcoma."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders; Bones, Joints, and Soft Tissue Tumors; Peripheral Nerves and Skeletal Muscles"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Neoplasia, sections on tumor suppressor genes and the two-hit hypothesis"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Skeletal system and limbs, sections on ossification and HOX gene patterning"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Fibrous proteins: collagen structure and synthesis; mitochondrial DNA and oxidative phosphorylation"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Bone, sections on osteogenesis and the epiphyseal growth plate"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Genetic Disease"}]}});
